{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.743696+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ef2c33774ea34dd04d8356a3d3d45f7d22bcbb2b29ab7bdf3357eef1ea9e819","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2607.08632, 'Uncountably many extremal Series--Sinai states for the Ising model on Lobachevsky lattices'] [Discrete hyperbolic Schur theorem~\cite{FG97}]

    For \(\ell > 0\), set 
    \begin{equation}
        \alpha_\ell = 2 \arctan\left(\sinh\left(\frac{\ell}{2}\right)\right).
    \end{equation}
    Let us consider \(u_0,\,\ldots,\,u_n\) and \(v_0,\,\ldots,\,v_n\) points of \(\Hyp{2}\).
    Let us suppose that for all \(i\in\llbracket 0, n-1\rrbracket\), \(\hyperbolicDistance{u_i}{u_{i+1}} = \hyperbolicDistance{v_i}{v_{i+1}} = \ell\).
    Let us suppose that for all \(i\in\llbracket 1, n-1\rrbracket\), if we denote \(\theta_i = \angle(u_{i-1}, u_i, u_{i+1})\in[-\pi, \pi]\), then \(\angle(v_{i-1}, v_i, v_{i+1}) = \abs{\theta_i}\).
    If for all \(i\in\llbracket 1, n-1\rrbracket\), \(\abs{\theta_i} \ge \pi - \alpha_\ell\), we have
    \begin{equation}
        \hyperbolicDistance{v_0}{v_n}
        \le
        \hyperbolicDistance{u_0}{u_n}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.888886+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ef5427de801bdf0948a07896a44603bedef2ac2d7bd73db3718af80a76cacb6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:potential under tens and Ar of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $A\subset \mathbb{R}^m$ and $B\subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be finite subsets and let $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{>0}^A$ and $\beta\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^B$. For all $(x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^m\oplus\mathbb{R}^n$ we have 
  \begin{equation}
    \Phi_{\alpha\otimes \beta}(x,y)=\Phi_\alpha(x)+\Phi_\beta(y).
  \end{equation}
  Moreover, if $n=m$, then for all $x\in\mathbb{R}^m$ we have
  \begin{equation}
    \Phi_{\alpha\odot \beta}(x)=\Phi_\alpha(x)+\Phi_\beta(x).
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.613292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ef5f6b5060819d6e66637ab9a6931d94a58d6e0950435aa81b2a5fb542ee385","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 50 of arXiv:2607.08536, 'Soft edge limit of the Laguerre beta-ensemble at the lower edge'] Denote by $\ma{M}_n^{(T)}\equiv \ma{M}_n^{G,(T)} $ the upper-left $n_0\times n_0$ corner of $\ma{M}_n$. Set
    \begin{align*}
    \mathsf{K}_n^{(T)}(x,y)\equiv\mathsf{K}_n^{G,(T)}(x,y) = \sigma_n^{-1}m_n\Big[\big(\ma{M}_n^{(T)}\big)^{-1}\Big]_{\lceil x m_n\rceil, \lceil y m_n\rceil} \ind(0<x, y\le T),
\end{align*}
    then for any fixed $T>0$, there exists a coupling so that $\|\mathsf{K}_n^{(T)}-\mathsf{K}_{\Ai}^{(T)}\|_{2} \to 0$ a.s.~as $n\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.321179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5ef698e5ede595058fca967223b33199a622797adf4eca15107e26985ac45717","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:SRWoptimal of arXiv:2601.10624, 'Source localisation in simple random walks'] We have
    \begin{equation*}
