{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.833285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5efe6cd31e0e82e569bd25ee500a39a7bcbca467e567a1c4f67f51d5d730abfd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 (Coordinatewise stationary-fiber representation) of arXiv:2604.10792, 'Variable-Length Markov Chains on Finite Quivers: Boundary-Window Identifiability, Exact De'] Fix a pair $(y,a)$ consisting of a visible state $y\in\cS_m$ and an admissible appended edge $a$ from the terminal vertex of $y$, and write $y_a:=U_m(y,a)$. For each $z\in F_y$ define
\begin{equation}
\alpha_z(\theta):=P_{\theta,\mathrm{stat}}(Z_t^{(r)}=z\mid Z_t^{(m)}=y), \qquad \zeta_z(\theta):=q_{z,U_r(z,a)}^{(r)}(\theta).
\end{equation}
Then, for every $\theta$ near $\theta_0$,
\begin{equation}
q_{y,y_a}^{(m)}(\theta)=\sum_{z\in F_y}\alpha_z(\theta)\zeta_z(\theta).
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
Dq_{y,y_a}^{(m)}(\theta_0)|_{T_0} = \sum_{z\in F_y}\alpha_z(\theta_0)D\zeta_z(\theta_0)|_{T_0} +
\sum_{z\in F_y}\zeta_z(\theta_0)D\alpha_z(\theta_0)|_{T_0}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.060451+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0514eed7200f4e8f80bf7117219eae091aeacfa3058f9d65bf0e18b75305d6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 (Frequency-envelope variant) of arXiv:2606.16662, '2606.16662'] Fix \(0<T\le T_0\) and a finite family \(\mathcal M\) of mixed labels.
Assume the Fourier-diagonal covariance model \eqref{eq:conv-cov} together with the envelope coefficient bounds \eqref{eq:env-K}--\eqref{eq:env-A-increment}.  Let \((\beta_\tau,\zeta_\tau,\sigma)\) be such that, for every \(\tau\in\mathcal M\), the two quantities \(\mathfrak S_{\tau,H}^{\eps}(\beta_\tau,\sigma)\) and \(\mathfrak S_{\tau,B}^{\eps}(\zeta_\tau,\sigma)\) in \eqref{eq:env-SH}--\eqref{eq:env-SB} are finite, with \(\eps>0\) leaving room for the time-lift absorption \eqref{eq:time-lift-absorb}.  Then, for every finite \(p\ge2\), the centered cutoff operators satisfy
\begin{equation}
        B_\Lambda^\tau\longrightarrow B^\tau
        \quad\hbox{in}\quad
        L^p\bigl(\Omega;\mathcal L(X_T^\sigma,Y_T^{\beta_\tau,\zeta_\tau})\bigr)
\end{equation}
and almost surely in the corresponding operator norm.  If, in addition, a deterministic diagonal resolution has been chosen so that the retained branches satisfy \eqref{eq:env-retained-bound}, the localized source-tail bound \eqref{eq:env-X-localized}, and fixed-block convergence in \(Y_T^{\beta_\tau,\zeta_\tau}\), then
\begin{equation}
        T_\Lambda^{\tau,\mathfrak P}=T_\Lambda^\tau-\Ctr^\tau_\Lambda
        \longrightarrow \Ret^\tau+B^\tau
        \quad\hbox{in}\quad
        \mathcal L(X_T^\sigma\cap\mathcal X_T,Y_T^{\beta_\tau,\zeta_\tau})
\end{equation}
almost surely and in \(L^p(\Omega)\).  Fully Wick-subtracted branches are obtained by setting \(\Ret^\tau_\Lambda=0\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:44:12.647132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0aa15d7f8848a41547ca6dbd72778c836b796de7786dd84d95fd5d2b40eb30","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:pw-strict of arXiv:2512.04371, 'Constructive Approximation under Carleman's Condition, \\ with Applications to Smoothed An'] Let $r \ge 1$, $K > 0$ and $A > 0$ be constants.  
Suppose that the moment generating function of $\mu$ satisfies that
\[
  \|e^{x\xi}\|_{\mu} \;\le\; A \exp\bigl((|\xi|\,K)^r\bigr)
  \quad\text{for all }\xi\in\mathbb{R}.
\]
Then for any $\Omega\ge0$ and $D\ge r\,( K\Omega)^r$, it holds that 
\begin{align}
  \|r_D\|_{\mu}
  &\le \frac{A\,\|\hat f\|_{L^1([-\Omega,\Omega])}}{2\pi}
       \Bigl(\frac{r\,e}{D}\Bigr)^{D/r} \cdot (K\Omega)^{D}
    + \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb{R}\setminus[-\Omega,\Omega]}
       \bigl|\hat f(\xi)\bigr|\,d\xi.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.616437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0b43b5ac0a84c05bc4e26cab7cb4a2d34530eca8a0d25070612f00c80d5bab","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 48 of arXiv:2510.27589, 'Geodesic switches and exceptional times in dynamical Brownian last passage percolation'] Fix $\delta>0$. The following holds almost surely. For all $t\in \RR$ and $\theta\in [0,\infty]$ admitting a $\theta$-directed semi-infinite geodesic $\Gamma^{t}$, and for all $m$ large enough, we have 
  \begin{equation}

    \Gamma^t\cap \{m\}_{\RR}\subseteq [\theta m-m^{2/3+\delta}, \theta m+m^{2/3+\delta}].
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.491412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0dce013a9cd4d4be16cf2cc1bf1746f2a4ad64bd36470a6bba4e4ab5df41ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 228 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] If $H = 2$ and $\theta_{g,h} = \theta > 0$ for all $g,h \in \{1,2\}$, we have
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{F}(z_1,z_2) = \frac{1}{4\theta \sigma(z_1)\sigma(z_2)}\left(\bigg(\frac{\sigma(z_1) - \sigma(z_2)}{z_1 - z_2}\bigg)^2 - (z_1 + z_2)(z_1 + z_2 - \mathsf{e}_1) + 4c_{0,0} - \mathsf{e}_2\right).
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}

c_{0,0} = \frac{1}{4}\left(\alpha_1\beta_2 + \alpha_2\beta_1 + (\beta_2 - \beta_1)(\alpha_2 - \alpha_1)\frac{E(\mathsf{k})}{K(\mathsf{k})}\right)
\end{equation}
in terms of the complete elliptic integrals
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
E(\mathsf{k}) = \int_{0}^{1} \sqrt{\frac{1 - \mathsf{k}^2t^2}{1 - t^2}},\qquad K(\mathsf{k}) = \int_{0}^{1} \frac{\dd t}{\sqrt{(1 - t^2)(1 - \mathsf{k}^2t^2)}}.
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
specialized to
\[
\mathsf{k} = \sqrt{\frac{(\beta_2 - \alpha_1)(\alpha_2 - \beta_1)}{(\beta_2 - \beta_1)(\alpha_2 - \alpha_1)}} < 1,
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.264599+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0dfbaca031479c0f074c549e8870045cfb99790dd8f8a3b44d34276b471558","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2604.19924, 'Fluctuation theory for spectrally negative Lévy processes killed by additive functionals'] Let $q \geq 0$, $x \in (c,b)$, and let $f:\R \to \R$ be a bounded, non-negative measurable function. Then
	\begin{align}
		\E_x\left[\int_0^{\tau_b^+\wedge\tau_c^-} e^{-qt-A_t} f(X_t)\,dt\right]
		= \int_c^b f(y)\left(
		\frac{W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(x,c)}{W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(b,c)}
		W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(b,y)
		- W_{{\mathbf{T}}(\nu_A)}^{(q)}(x,y)
		\right)dy.
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.538350+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0e060db80bf653e9a9c26e6c026e0d7e1a25a28bf77d431d902d671494e5c0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 4 of arXiv:2603.28702, 'Distribution of the magnetization of the critical Ising model on sparse random graphs'] Consider the Ising model on $G(n,d/n)$ at the critical temperature and let
$$Z_n:=\SL{\sigma\in\{-1,1\}^n}\exp\left(\beta\SL{u\sim v}\sigma_u\sigma_v\right)$$
be its partition function. If we set
\begin{align*}
\Delta_n:=\log(Z_n)-n\log(2)-|E_n|\log\cosh(\beta)-\log\dfrac{\sqrt[4]{n}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}+\dfrac34,
\end{align*}
where $|E_n|$ is the number of edges of the graph, then
\begin{align*}
\Delta_n\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{(d)}&\SL{i=3}^\infty\left(\tilde{C}_i\log(1+d^{-i})-\frac{d^i+1}{2i}\right)\\&+\log\int_\R\exp\left(\frac{y^2}{\sqrt{2(d-1)}}X-\left(\dfrac{1}{12}+\dfrac{1}{4(d-1)}\right)y^4\right)\ \text{d}y
\end{align*}
where the $\tilde{C}_i\sim\text{Pois}(\frac{d^i}{2i})$ are independent, and also independent of $X\sim N(0,1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.250835+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f0e48a9de05dececc9a8a836c1028d1ae02c1fa4bd2ade5e86541c3123171c0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2605.22760, 'Extremes of Gaussian fields with a product term in the variance'] Let \(\delta>0\), \(\gamma>0\), and
\[
        I_\gamma(u)=\int_0^\delta\int_0^\delta
        e^{-\gamma u^2(x^\beta+y^\beta+x^ay^a)}\dd x\dd y .
\]
Then, as \(u\to\infty\),
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item if \(0<a<\beta/2\), then
\begin{equation}
        I_\gamma(u)
        \sim
        \frac{2(\beta-2a)\Gamma(1/a)}{a^2\beta\,\gamma^{1/a}}
        u^{-2/a}\log u;
\end{equation}
\item if \(a=\beta/2\), then
\begin{equation}
        I_\gamma(u)
        \sim
        u^{-4/\beta}\int_0^\infty\int_0^\infty
        e^{-\gamma(x^\beta+y^\beta+x^{\beta/2}y^{\beta/2})}\dd x\dd y;
\end{equation}
\item if \(a>\beta/2\), then
\begin{equation}
        I_\gamma(u)
        \sim
        \gamma^{-2/\beta}G_\beta^2u^{-4/\beta}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if \(0<\alpha<\beta\), \(a\ge a_0=\alpha\beta/(\alpha+\beta)\), \(R_u\to\infty\), and \(\log(R_u)=o(\log(u))\), then in the corresponding cases above
\begin{equation}
        \int_{R_uq_u}^\delta\int_{R_uq_u}^\delta
        e^{-\gamma u^2(x^\beta+y^\beta+x^ay^a)}\dd x\dd y
        \sim I_\gamma(u).
\end{equation}
Finally,
\begin{equation}
        \int_0^\delta e^{-\gamma u^2x^\beta}\dd x
        \sim
        \gamma^{-1/\beta}G_\beta u^{-2/\beta}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.669704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f14e0ff4c3ff06f0861746bce3df6750a3166054af43ecdc454467da852fc45","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] Let $ \pi : E \longrightarrow B $ be a fiber bundle with fiber $S^{k}$ ($ k \geq 1 $), over a connected base $B$. 
        	Assume for simplicity that the bundle is oriented, so that the fibers carry a consistent orientation. In the Gysin sequence 
