{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:55.638380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f5c8594ec033e9c0321d659839eff67a62a0057e20764776b2573137795684f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2605.23461, 'An almost sure invariance principle for the Takagi--van der Waerden class functions'] Suppose that \eqref{Assump2:A_n_to_infinite} and \eqref{Assump2:last_term} hold. Then, for any $p\in (0, 1)$, we have the following:
  \begin{enumerate}[label={(\roman*)}, ref={\roman*}, align=left, leftmargin=*]










    \descitem{(CLT)} \begin{equation}
    \lim_{n\to\infty} P\biggl(\frac{S_n}{s_n}\le y\biggr) = \int_{-\infty}^{y} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-t^2/2} dt.
  \end{equation}
    \descitem{(the compact LIL)} With probability one, the set of limit points of
  \begin{equation}
    \frac{S_n}{\sqrt{2 s_n^2 \log\log s_n^2}}
  \end{equation}
  coincides with the closed interval $[-1,1]$.
    \descitem{(the other LIL)}
    \begin{equation}
    \liminf_{n\to\infty} \sqrt{\frac{\log\log s_n^2}{s_n^2}}\max_{1\le k\le n} |S_k| = \frac{\pi}{\sqrt{8}} \quad \text{$P$-a.s.}
  \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.528130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f5d0cbbbc32b189ea6f6a57904ba90898dc880d934a44908ea9b3db84383746","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 (Talagrand's inequality) of arXiv:2608.18557, 'Randomly Permuted Orthogonal Products and Fast Dimension Reduction'] Let \(\xi\in\{-1,1\}^d\) be a Rademacher vector, and let
