{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.922419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f6934c1cbfa89044bfb8e325e85e4ac70f64b6d1fdf29fe2b182281949063fa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:>interface of arXiv:2505.09604, 'Exceptional force, uncountably many solutions in the KPZ fixed point'] The following holds on the event $\Omega_1$: for all $\xi \in \mathbb{R}, (x_0,s) \in \mathbb{R}^2$ the following is true: assume that there is a $t>s$ such that $\tau_{(x_0,s)}^{\xi,-}(r) < \tau_{(x_0,s)}^{\xi,+}(r)$ for all $r \in (s,t]$. Then, letting $\tau^{\scaleobj{0.8}{\boxempty}}_r = \tau_{(x_0,s)}^{\xi,-}(r)$, we have
       \begin{align}

           &g^{\xi-,R}_{(\tau_t^-,t)}(r) = g^{\xi+,R}_{(\tau_t^-,t)}(r)>\tau_r^- \qquad \text{ for all } r \in (s,t), \quad\text{and}\\

           &g^{\xi+,L}_{(\tau_t^+,t)}(r) = g^{\xi-,L}_{(\tau_t^+,t)}(r) <\tau_r^+ \qquad \text{ for all } r \in (s,t).
