{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.989325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f7cfc4f9c74bcf77fbe0b6c843c640c06ae36f547a7fa6e8854d3b7f3c87d55","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 79 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Let $U_1, U_2, \ldots, U_m$ be independent $U[0, 1]$ variables, and denote by $U(1), U(2), \ldots , U(m)$ the order statistics, i.e.\ $U(1) \leq U(2) \leq \ldots \leq U(m)$. If $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_{m+1}$  are $(m + 1)$ independent standard exponentials (also independent of the $U_i$, $1 \leq i \leq m$), then
