		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.588971+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_601b80bc2f2061f45b6f6a668bcdc122ce5907493c2666f68551c4b40e9f24e9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def eq: poincar\'e of arXiv:2412.04918, 'On one dimensional weighted Poincar\'e inequalities for Global Sensitivity Analysis'] A probability measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(a,b)$ satisfies a weighted Poincar\'e inequality with weight function $w \in \mathcal{W}(a,b)$ and constant $C>0$ if for every function $f\in H^1(\mu,w)$ such that $\int_a^b f \, d\mu =0$ (we say that $f$ is centered), it is true that
    \begin{equation}

    \scaleobj{1.0}{
        \int_{a}^b f^2 \, d\mu 
\leq C\int_a^b w \, (f')^2 \, d\mu .}
\end{equation}
The Poincar\'e constant, denoted $C_P(\mu,w)$, is the optimal (\textit{i.e.}, the smallest) constant $C$ for which ((eq: poincar\'e of the paper)) holds. If there exists some non-null function which realizes the equality in ((eq: poincar\'e of the paper)), we say that it saturates the weighted Poincar\'e inequality.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.786173+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_601ce14c6d8a02c31fae58e58912ed3b1c54b1ab17c1d5c3f4fbed310ae4ee9b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 21 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $p \in \mathbb{R}[z_1, z_2]$ be a real polynomial with bidegree $(n,m)$, without zeros in the right bihalfplane $\RHPtwo$. Then $p$ admits a determinantal representation 
\begin{equation} p(z_1,z_2)=c\det (A+z_1B_1+z_2B_2),
\end{equation}
where $A, B_1, B_2$ are real matrices of size $\deg p \times \deg p$ such that $S_A \coloneqq \frac{1}{2}(A+A^T) \geq 0$, $B_1, B_2 \geq 0$ and $B_1+B_2=I$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.030492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_601e10d79179d2fc5bc98ea77a7b2f0610a26f1f7aa5a542014855f0d217bdbb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2608.15616, '\textbf{Compact Support Property of Super-Brownian Motion with Irregular Drift}'] For any $T>0$ we have 
		\begin{equation}
			\sup_{n\geq 1} \E_f[R^n_T] \leq \E_f[R^0_T] +T^{1/2} \sqrt{2\pi b_1 \constFromEq{c}{eq:est_v_infty_1}}\sqrt{\E_f[R^0_T]}+ \sqrt{\E_f[R_T^0]} \sum_{i=1}^\infty a_i(T) <\infty, 
		\end{equation}
		where 
		\begin{equation}
			a_i(T):=   \sqrt{\frac{4\pi \constFromEq{c}{eq:est_v_infty_1}}{3} \constFromEq{c}{eq:est_W_time_int}^ib_1^{i+1} \Big( \prod_{k = 2}^i w_{k+2} \Big)T^\frac{i+2}{2}},
		\end{equation}
    with the convention that $\prod_{k=2}^1w_{k+2} =1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.819315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_601fd91262da6ce648b61ebbc78bba0cb2574a5f29afa78c25c2d89c05870d8a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: MILT and SILT d=1 of arXiv:2607.17393, 'Optimal Covariance Estimates for Schr\"odinger Semigroups with White Noise in $d=1,2$'] For $d=1$ and $t>0$ the self-intersection local time of a Brownian Bridge $B_t^{x,x}$ is defined as 
\begin{align}
    \beta_t(B_t^{x,x}) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty (L_t^a(B_t^{x,x}))^2da = \inn{L_t(B_t^{x,x}),L_t(B_t^{x,x})} = \norm{L_t(B_t^{x,x})}_2^2
\end{align}
and for $s,t>0$ the mutual intersection local time of the (independent) Brownian Bridges $B_t^{x,x}$ and $\overline{B}_s^{y,y}$ is defined as
\begin{align}
    \alpha_{t,s}(B_t^{x,x},\overline{B}_s^{y,y}) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty  L_t^a(B^{x,x}_t)L_s^a(\overline{B}^{y,y}_s)da = \inn{L_t^a(B_t^{x,x}),L_s^a(\overline{B}_s^{y,y})}
\end{align}
respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.744925+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_602412f286bd7eb18f88b14f0731eee5de709486eb8f2cd6f65e133ff1a26b72","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop positiveSignFeatureLaplace of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] Fix $\beta>0$ and assume that
\begin{align}
2\beta\|T_q\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1,
\qquad
\sup_{M\ge1}{\mathbb{E}}\brak*{e^{\beta\mathsf W_M(q)}}<\infty,
\qquad
\int_{{\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d}} e^{aD(x)}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\mu(x)<\infty
\end{align}
for some $a>0$.  Then there is a finite constant $C$ such that, for every $N\ge1$,
\begin{align}
\left|
{\mathbb{E}}\brak*{e^{\frac\beta2\mathsf W_N(q)}}
-\mathrm{det}_2\pa*{\mathrm{Id}-\beta T_q}^{-1/2}
\right|
\le C N^{-1/2}.
\end{align}
For fixed $\beta$ and $a$, the constant may be chosen to depend polynomially on $1+\int_{{\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d}} e^{aD(x)}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\mu(x)$, with coefficients depending only on the subcritical margin $1-2\beta\|T_q\|_{\mathrm{op}}$, $\|T_q\|_{\mathrm{HS}}$, and the uniform exponential moment in (equation eq:positiveSignFeatureAssumptions of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.863821+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6026ddc65bf4ab67d4cda264b0d12849ff3e45648d86555344ec84b47e20953f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2609.02426, 'Deformations of the $\osp(n|2)$-action on the superspace\\ of symbols of differential oper'] For all $1\leq i\leq n$ and all $a\in C^\infty(\R^{1|n})$:
\begin{align}
\eta_i\,\Ec&=(-1)^{i-1}(-\partial_x)\,\Ehat{i},\qquad
\Ec\,\eta_i=(-1)^{n-i}(-\partial_x)\,\Ehat{i},\qquad \eta_i\Ec=(-1)^{n+1}\Ec\,\eta_i,\\
\Ec\circ(a\,\cdot)&=(-1)^{na}a\,\Ec+(-1)^{(n-1)a}\sum_{i=1}^n(-1)^{i-1}\eta_i(a)\,\Ehat{i}
\qquad\text{whenever }\eta_j\eta_i(a)=0\ (i\neq j).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.588074+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60285a6d2a5936d74cfb24a4c1ec50f7f4aad4cd643a49a3f6bb3d32c4d41a55","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.01346, '2609.01346'] Fix $\rho_*>1$ and define
