{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.316798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60622eae747a086003eeec33599a62e3c0d44c4c6afbf052e10f5a92ebb06066","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2604.09248, '\bfseries Limit laws for longest edges in empty region graphs'] Let $\eta_t$ be a stationary Poisson process on $\RR^d$ with intensity $t\geq 2$. To each pair of distinct points $x,y\in\RR^d$ we associate a compact set $S(x,y)$ such that $(x,y)\mapsto S(x,y)$ is measurable and the following assumptions for pairs of distinct points $(x,y),(u,v)\in(\RR^d)^2$ are satisfied:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] $\vol(S(x,y))=\gamma\|x-y\|^d$ for some $\gamma\in(0,\infty)$,
    \item[(ii)] $S(x,y)\subseteq B^d({x+y\over 2},\alpha\|x-y\|)$ for some {$\alpha\in(\frac{1}{2},\infty)$,}
    \item[(iii)] $\vol(S(x,y)\setminus S(u,v)) \geq \beta\|{x+y\over 2}-{u+v\over 2}\|\|x-y\|^{d-1}$ for some $\beta\in(0,\infty)$ if $\|x-y\|\geq\|u-v\|$ and $S(x,y)\cap S(u,v)\neq\varnothing$,
    \item[(iv)] $S(x,y)=S(y,x)$.
\end{itemize}
Define the point process
$$
\xi_t := {1\over 2}\sum_{(x,y)\in\eta_{t,\neq}^2}\mathbbm{1}\{(\eta_t-\delta_x-\delta_y)(S(x,y))=0\}\delta_{\big({x+y\over 2},\,\gamma t\|x-y\|^d-\log(t)-\log(\kappa_d/(2\gamma)),\,\mathrm{span}\{x-y\}\big)}
$$
on $\RR^d\times\RR\times\LL^d$, denote by $\zeta$ a Poisson process on $\RR^d\times\RR\times\LL^d$ with intensity measure $\vol\otimes\mu\otimes\nu$, and let $W\in\mathcal{B}(\RR^d)$ with $\vol(W)<\infty$ and $b\in\RR$. Then, for any $t\geq 2$,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{KR}}(\xi_t|_{W\times[b,\infty)\times\LL^d},\zeta|_{W\times[b,\infty)\times\LL^d}) \leq \begin{cases}
        c\log^{-(d-2)}(t), & d \ge 3,\\
        c\log^{-1/2}(t), & d=2,
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $c\in(0,\infty)$ is a constant only depending on $d$, $\vol(W)$, $b$, $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$. {Moreover, one has $\xi_t\overset{d}{\longrightarrow} \zeta$ as $t\to\infty$.} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.068368+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60628a16f762dc95c732d6bbf4503f76afc980a3a59831af2c24a4bee2f730c3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma simple_recursive_biadapted of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \\ and Wasserstein Distances'] A function $T : \mathcal{X}_1 \times \mathcal{X}_2 \to \mathcal{Y}_1 \times \mathcal{Y}_2$ given by $T(x_1,x_2) = (T_1(x_1), T_2(x_1, x_2))$ is biadapted if and only if 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $T_1 : \mathcal{X}_1 \to \mathcal{Y}_1$ is bijective;
        \item $T_2^{x_1} : \mathcal{X}_2 \to \mathcal{Y}_2$ is bijective for all $x_1 \in \mathcal{X}_1$.
    \end{enumerate}
    In this case, the inverse of $T$ is given by $S(y_1, y_2) = (T_1^{-1}(y_1), \brackets{T_2^{T_1^{-1}(y_1)}}^{-1}(y_2))$ i.e. $S_1 = T_1^{-1}$ and $S_2^{y_1} = \brackets{T_2^{x_1}}^{-1}$ for $x_1 = T_1^{-1}(y_1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.668740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60644b50e6006a3134d73f52e465657877f19fca203e423e6ef07fd8f634cbd4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 18 of arXiv:2604.24189, 'Malliavin calculus and densities for chaos-driven stochastic differential equations'] The one-dimensional Hermite process $Z^{H,q}=\left(Z_t^{H,q}\right)_{t \in \R_+}$ of order $q\geq 1$ and self-similarity parameter $H \in\left(\frac{1}{2}, 1\right)$ is defined by
\begin{align}
	Z^{H,q}_t=I_q(L_t^{H, q}) \qquad \text{where} \qquad L_t^{H, q}(\xi)=c(H, q) \int_0^t \prod_{j=1}^q\left(s-\xi_j\right)_{+}^{H_0-\frac{3}{2}} ds,
\end{align}
$$
H_0=1+\frac{H-1}{q} \in\left(1-\frac{1}{2 q}, 1\right), \quad\text{and}\quad c(H, q)=\sqrt{\frac{H(2 H-1)}{q!\,\beta^q\left(H_0-\frac{1}{2}, 2-2 H_0\right)}}.
$$
Here $\beta$ stands for the Beta function and we use the convention, for each $(\theta, \alpha) \in \R^2$,
		\[\theta^{\alpha}_+:=\left\{ \begin{array}{rl}\theta^{\alpha}, & \text{if }\theta>0,\\ 0, &  \text{otherwise}. \end{array}\right. \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.729279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6069dae4e423e82268cb4b94ce40f5f6d534ecf8975135b79b54e79dc76b144e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm_1 of arXiv:2503.22334, 'Probabilistic analysis of optimal multi-pivot QuickSort'] Let $K\in {\mathbb N}$ and $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$. For the normalization $Y_n:=(X_n-{\mathbb E}\left[X_n\right])/n$ as $n\to\infty$ we have 
\begin{align*}
   Y_n&\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} Z_K,\\
   {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Y_n)\right] &\longrightarrow {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Z_K)\right]<\infty,
\end{align*}
where the distribution of $Z_K$ is determined as the unique centered, square integrable probability measure such that
\begin{align}
Z_K \overset{\text{d}}{=} \sum_{i=0}^{K} \kl*{D_i Z_K^{(i)}+\alpha_K D_i \ln\left(D_i\right)}+l_{\mathrm{opt}}(D),
\end{align}
where $D,Z_K^{(0)},\dots,Z_K^{(K)}$ are independent and $Z_K^{(i)}$ has the same distribution as $Z_K$ for all $i=0,\dots,K$.
Moreover, we have moments convergence, i.e., all moments of $Y_n$ converge to the moments of $Z_K$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:13:21.261654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_606bbe2f20bf6e5d22fc4bcaed75e2a6d41323909b5ba3bef92a1ea581531c7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 of arXiv:2609.01473, 'Keller sequences of exact $\infty$-categories'] Given a nullcomposite sequence
