\Xi_q\le \frac{C(\log\log n)^C}{\log n}
\right)
\ge 1-C(\log n)^{-p}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:55:18.920663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607140eaa1d336025c0ec96ccf303f43816d0488194c7f73cd66b27db83ba61e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:FH10.5 of arXiv:2601.21762, 'Navier-Stokes with a fractional transport noise as a limit of multi-scale dynamics'] Let $(X,Y)=(X^i,Y^i)_i$ be a centered Gaussian process on $[0,T]$, such that $(X^i,Y^i)$ is independent of 
    $(X^j, Y^j)$ when $i \neq j$. Assume that there exists $\rho \in [1,2)$, and $\{M_i\}\in \ell^1$, such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \norm{R_{X^i}}_{\rho,[s,t]^2} &\leq M_i |t-s|^{\frac{1}{\rho}}, \qquad
        \norm{R_{Y^i}}_{\rho,[s,t]^2} \leq M_i |t-s|^{\frac{1}{\rho}}, \quad 
        \norm{R_{X^i-Y^i}}_{\rho,[s,t]^2} &\leq \epsilon^2 M_i |t-s|^{\frac{1}{\rho}},
    \end{align*}
for all $i\geq 1$ and $0\leq s \leq t \leq T.$ Then
    \begin{itemize}
        \item [a)] For every $q\in [1,\infty)$ and for all $0\leq s \leq t \leq T$,
       $$
            \mathbb{E} \left[\norm{Y_{s,t} - X_{s,t}}^q \right]^{\frac{1}{q}} \lesssim \epsilon \sqrt{\left\| M \right\|_{\ell^1}}
            |t-s|^{\frac{1}{2\rho}}, \qquad 
            \mathbb{E} \left[\norm{\mathbb{Y}_{s,t} - \mathbb{X}_{s,t}}^q \right]^{\frac{1}{q}} \lesssim \epsilon \left\| M \right\|_{\ell^1}
            |t-s|^{\frac{1}{\rho}}.
$$
        \item [b)] For any $\alpha < \frac{1}{2\rho}$ and $q\in [1,\infty)$, one has
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{E} \left[\left\| Y-X \right\|_\alpha^q\right]^{\frac{1}{q}} &\lesssim \epsilon \sqrt{\left\| M \right\|_{\ell^1}}, \qquad
            \mathbb{E} \left[\norm{\mathbb{Y}-\mathbb{X}}_{2\alpha}^q\right]^{\frac{1}{q}} \lesssim \epsilon \left\| M \right\|_{\ell^1}.
        \end{align*}
        \item[c)] For $\rho \in [1, \frac{3}{2})$ and any $\alpha \in (\frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{2\rho})$ , $q<\infty$ one has 
       $$ \mathbb{E}\left[\rho_\alpha(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{Y})^q \right]^{\frac{1}{q}} \lesssim \epsilon. $$
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.674161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6072baee99818c0387fb0699d620decc9fb14fc20d7c4f1d1a8ae37dd01703ce","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 40 (Equivalence of intrinsic and fixed-chart norms) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] Fix \(n\), \(m\geq2\), \(\sigma\in(0,1)\), \(\Lambda\geq1\), and \(L>0\).
Suppose that
\(\PBG_m(s;\Lambda;[a,b])\) holds and \(b-a\leq L\).  Let
\(\|\cdot\|_{X_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}\) and
\(\|\cdot\|_{Y_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}\) denote the suprema of the Euclidean
component norms in the fixed lifted atlas supplied by
Lemma~\ref{lem:uniform-parabolic-atlas}.  Then there is
\(C=C(n,m,\sigma,\Lambda,L)\) such that every deck-invariant tensor \(U\)
satisfies
\begin{align}
 C^{-1}\|U\|_{X_s^m[a,b]}
 &\leq \|U\|_{X_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}
 \leq C\|U\|_{X_s^m[a,b]},                                      \\
 C^{-1}\|U\|_{Y_s^m[a,b]}
 &\leq \|U\|_{Y_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}
 \leq C\|U\|_{Y_s^m[a,b]}.                                      
\end{align}
The analogous comparison holds for every spatial
\(C^{k,\sigma}\)-norm with \(0\leq k\leq m\).  For tensors on the universal
cover that are not deck-invariant, the same local comparison holds with the
fixed-chart side interpreted as the uniformly local norm.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.846309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60736a80f6936f77a7bcd3554a3b9db486fff528b84d4be4b78789b341e3e7de","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: bounds for integrals of hazard rate of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] For each $\varepsilon > 0$, there exist $K_\varepsilon \geq 0$ and compact sets $A_\varepsilon \subset B_\varepsilon \subset U_g$ such that $A_\varepsilon$ is inside the interior in $\mathbbm{R}_+^2$ of $B_\varepsilon$ and the following properties hold:
	\begin{equation*}
		\int_{A_\varepsilon} g(x, y)dxdy \geq 1 - \varepsilon \quad \text{and} \quad h_g^x(x, y) + h_g^y(x, y) \leq K_\varepsilon \quad \text{for all} \quad (x, y) \in B_\varepsilon.
	\end{equation*}
	Suppose that $\psi$ is continuous and $\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{(x, y) \in A_\varepsilon\right\}} \leq \psi(x, y) \leq \mathbbm{1}_{\left\{(x, y) \in B_\varepsilon\right\}}$ for all $x, y \in \mathbbm{R}_+$, then
	\begin{equation*}
		\max\left\{E\left|\bar\boldsymbol{D}_t^N((1 - \psi)\varphi)\right|, E\left|\bar\boldsymbol{D}_t^{N, c}((1 - \psi)\varphi)\right|\right\} \leq \varepsilon \left(\bar M + \frac{E\expectarg*{\mathcal{R}^N(t)}}{N}\right) \left|\left|\varphi\right|\right|_\infty.
	\end{equation*}
	for all $t \geq 0$ and $\varphi \in C_b(\mathbbm{R}_+^3)$. This inequality in fact holds if $0 \leq \psi \leq 1$ and $\psi = 1$ in~$A_\varepsilon$. In particular, the condition involving $B_\varepsilon$ is not needed to prove the inequality, but will be used when we invoke the lemma in the sequel.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.778456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6074c0a8c7da2f8741d4816a411b8e24b633eec0306550675788baca7cab1ccf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop planarity of arXiv:2609.02346, 'Regularised double shuffle relations for planar \AZVs{}'] Let $(S,T)\in\mathcal{PT}_{\N^*}^{\rm conv} \times \mathcal{PT}_{\N^*}^{\rm conv}$ (resp. $(S,T)\in\mathcal{PT}_{\{x,y\}}^{\rm conv} \times \mathcal{PT}_{\{x,y\}}^{\rm conv}$). Then
    \begin{equation*}
        F_{\N^*}(S)=F_{\N^*}(T)~\Longrightarrow~\zeta^T(S)=\zeta^T(T)\quad\left(\text{\normalfont{resp. } }F_{\{x,y\}}(S)=F_{\{x,y\}}(T)~\Longrightarrow~\zeta_\shuffle^T(S)=\zeta_\shuffle^T(T)~\right).
    \end{equation*}
    We call these relations the \emph{planarity relations}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.643924+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607509264e1e94f79c50fa828e1a24f4caee1653d5149a21e8f2d4985890c220","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop EHI:p:SbmCounterex of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0} = (B_{S_t})_{t \geq 0}$ be a subordinate Brownian motion. For all $s>0$, let $T^{\{s\}} := \inf\{ t : \Delta S(t) > s \}$. Suppose there exist sequences $(s_n)$ and $(r_n)$ of positive numbers such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $s_n \ll r_n^2$.
        \item $1 \gg \mathbb{P}_0(\tau_{B(0, r_n)} < T^{\{s_n\}}) \gg \mathbb{P}_0 (|\Delta X(T^{\{s_n\}})| \leq 36 r_n)$.
    \end{itemize}
    Then $X$ does not satisfy $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}$. Furthermore, if $r_n \to 0$ along a subsequence then $X$ does not satisfy $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \leq 1)$, and if $r_n \to \infty$ along a subsequence then $X$ does not satisfy $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \geq 1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.635439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607786131d062e6e255f54a00381d8cebf6e6f431360a5f34b6b65d3c315896c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2605.31088, 'On couplings for kinetic Langevin diffusions'] Let $(Z_k,\tilde Z_k)_{k\in \N}$ be given by the iterated one-shot coupling defined in Section~\ref{sssec: iterated one-shot} applied to the Markov chains~\eqref{eq: linear Markov chain}. For $k\in \N$ consider the event $\{Z_{k-1}\neq\tilde Z_{k-1}\}$, i.e.\ the event that the chains have not met before the $k^{\text{th}}$ step. On this event we have that
     \begin{equation*}
         e_{k}=\frac{B_{k}^{-1}
         \Pi_{k}\Delta z}{\l|B_{k}^{-1}\Pi_{k}\Delta z\r|},
     \end{equation*}
    where $\Pi_{k}\coloneqq A_{k}A_{k-1}\dots A_1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.565621+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6079c680287d538599fc7f99e34f628eec7eb475e3c065ca8f9422f22d2de4e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: Yu replace sum by min of arXiv:2411.17109, 'On the maximal correlation of some stochastic processes'] Let $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n$ be i.i.d. real-valued random variables. Let $1\leq \ell+1\leq m\leq n$. Then we have that
\begin{equation}
R\left(\min_{i:1\leq i\leq m}X_i,\min_{j:\ell+1\leq j\leq n}X_j\right)\leq\frac{m-\ell}{\sqrt{m(n-\ell)}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.961988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607a3d0e8ad8da71f6d2a696faf50255dbb7d99b9236e2244e5bf2e212958b7a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 (Potter bound at $0$) of arXiv:2604.00642, 'Quantitative central limit theorem for an integrated periodogram via the fourth moment the'] For any slowly varying $L$ at $0$ and any $\varepsilon>0$, there exist $C_\varepsilon$ and $\delta>0$ such that
