{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:48:00.184472+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60da6528d34a5e323ab88a07f3fa1be155d146ed5da4a4976fcf3ef1aeef8c82","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro FourierHilbert of arXiv:2609.02882, 'A new proof that more than $2/3$ of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function are simple and '] Let $\lambda>0$ be a real number and $\eta\in L^2(\mathbb{R})$ be a real valued even function with $\textup{supp} (\eta) \subset (-\lambda, \lambda) $, such that $\widehat{\eta^2}(0)=1$. Let $\mathcal{Z}$ be a non-empty finite multiset of complex numbers which is invariant under complex
conjugation. For $z\in \mathcal{Z}$ we let $m_z$ be its multiplicity in $\mathcal{Z}$.  Then we have 
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\substack{z\in \mathcal{Z}\cap \mathbb{R}\\ m_z=1}}1\geqslant 2\sum_{z\in \mathcal{Z}} 1 -
 \sum_{z,s\in\mathcal Z}K(z-s)^2,  
\end{equation}
where $K$ is the kernel $K(\xi):= \widehat{\eta^2}(\xi).$ Moreover, the number of distinct elements of $\mathcal{Z}$ is 
\begin{equation}
 \geqslant \frac{3}{2}\sum_{z\in \mathcal{Z}} 1 -
 \frac12\sum_{z,s\in\mathcal Z}K(z-s)^2.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.203898+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60dc2c69687f9884f4750df243935214c895bd268c33aa49a25ced124e5a4df1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2604.26898, '\bf Stochastic Scaling Limits and Synchronization \\by Noise in Deep Transformer Models'] Under the assumptions of Proposition~\ref{prop:euler_maruyama}, for any $0 \leq u \leq t \leq T$ and any particle $i \in \{1, \dots, N\}$,
