{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.794220+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60dfd359ddd6905f61b9fd057ec9a15a13f26b93d62924b1e2eb411a7cd9b6af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:two-edges of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] For the geometric inhomogeneous random graph  with $0\leq\gamma<\frac{1}{2}$ and either a hard profile and $\gamma\not=0$ or a soft profile with $\delta>2$ and with intensity \smash{$0< \lambda < \widehat{\lambda}_c$}, we have for all $\epsilon>0$, 
  \begin{align}
      \theta_{r, R} & \asymp \pi_{r, R}^{(2)}
      & \text{ on } \{  r \geq  R^\epsilon\},
       \\[2mm]
      \theta_{(u), R} & \asymp 
                 \pi_{(u), R}^{(2)} 
