\end{equation}
The \textit{effective edge set} is $\bar E:=\{(M_1,M_2)\in\mathcal M\times \mathcal M \,:\, \bar r_N(M_1,M_2)>0 \}\,$ and we call $(\mathcal M, \bar E, \bar r_N)$ the \textit{effective weighted graph}.
This is the transition graph of an \textit{effective Markov chain} $\bar X^{N}_t$ on $\mathcal M$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.304584+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_610d2b3cfe4a20169f464a9a66ceeee7457017065fabe9d05bfb37aaad9acc7e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 56 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d\ge 2$.
Suppose that $F:\mathbb [0,\infty)\to [0,\infty)$ 
    is a decreasing function of exponential decay at infinity. 
    Let $K > 0$. Then there exists some $\epsilon>0$ and a constant $C = C(K, F) >0$ such that uniformly over $|\varsigma|<\epsilon$, $   a_1,   a_2\in I$, and all $0 = r_0 < r_1 \leq \cdots \leq r_m$, we have  \begin{align*}
    \mathbf E^{(\varsigma,2)}_{(   a_1,   a_2)} \bigg[ \prod_{j=1}^{m} F(|   R^1_{r_j}-   R^2_{r_j}|)\bigg] \leq C \left( 1+ r_1^{-1/2} |   a_1 -    a_2|\right)^{-K} \prod_{j=1}^{m} 1 \wedge (r_j - r_{j-1})^{-d/2}. 
\end{align*}
In particular, for fixed $r\in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}$ we have that \begin{equation}\sum_{1\le s_1\le ...\le s_{m-1} \le r}\mathbf E^{(\varsigma,2)}_{(   a_1,   a_2)} \bigg[ F(|R^1_r -R^2_r|) \prod_{j=1}^{m} F(|   R^1_{r_j}-   R^2_{r_j}|)\bigg] \leq \frac{C^m}{\omega_r(d)^{m-1} } r^{-d/2} (1+r^{-1/2} |   a_1-   a_2| \big)^{-K} .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.402545+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_61107a82a2cd8523a1fa700de5ae3213e3f3bdcb6b40ecc3dfb28dccc641b706","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem hd of arXiv:2412.06480, 'A SIR epidemic model on a refining spatial grid \\ II-Central limit theorem'] For any $T>0$, there exists a positive constant $C$ such that
	\begin{align}
	\sup_{0\le t\le T}\left(\|\nabla^+_\varepsilon S_\varepsilon(t)\|_\infty\vee\|\nabla^+_\varepsilon I_\varepsilon(t)\|_\infty\vee\|\nabla^+_\varepsilon R_\varepsilon(t)\|_\infty\vee\|\nabla^+_\varepsilon f_\varepsilon(t)\|_\infty\vee\|\nabla^+_\varepsilon g_\varepsilon(t)\|_\infty\right)&\le C,\\
	\int_{0}^{T} \left( \big\Vert \Delta_{\varepsilon} \mathcal{S}_{\varepsilon}(t) \big\Vert_{L^2}^2 +\big\Vert \Delta_{\varepsilon} \mathcal{I}_{\varepsilon}(t) \big\Vert_{L^2}^2+ \big\Vert \Delta_{\varepsilon} \mathcal{R}_{\varepsilon}(t) \big\Vert_{L^2}^2\right)dt &\le C,\\
