{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.232992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6118bae412e8eadbcedaa8e3011eb5db4a271b63da48bbdaacfcb7a37829ee4a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 63 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [$v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$]
Given $Cone\ A \in CONE(H_0)$. A one-to-one mapping
\begin{equation*}
v_{{\partial}Cone\ A} :\ H_0 \rightarrow {\partial}Cone\ A
\end{equation*}
is uniquely defined by the equation
\begin{equation*}
{\partial}Cone\ A \cap \pi^{-1}(x) = \{ v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x) \}.
\end{equation*}
In particular, ${\pi}(v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x))=x$. $v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$ is called the \textit{surface map} of $Cone\ A$. In the following, \underline{we often identify $Cone\ A$ with $v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$}.","status":"unverified"}

