{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.822274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_614cfbbac241a15c008629da3f2873d93d6a6179e2c5375f093c1c592fefc1c8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def: symmetry factor of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] The \emph{symmetry factor} of a tree is defined recursively by imposing $s(\bullet)=1$ and,
		for distinct $\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_k\in{\mathcal T}$, $n_1,\ldots, n_k\in\mathbb{N}$ and considering the tree $\tau=[\tau_1^{\cdot n_1}\cdots\tau_k^{\cdot n_k}]$, by
		\begin{equation}
			s(\tau):=\prod_{i=1}^ks(\tau_i)^{n_i}n_i!\,.
		\end{equation}
		It extends to forests by identifying $s(\tau_1^{\cdot n_1}\ldots\tau_k^{\cdot n_k})$ with (equation Eq: symmetry factor of the paper).
		We also define, for the forest $w=\tau_1^{n_1}\cdots\tau_k^{n_k}\in{\mathcal F}$, the \emph{permutation factor} 
		\begin{equation}
			\pi(w):=\prod_{i=1}^kn_i!\,,
		\end{equation}
		which counts the number of permutations of identical trees in the forest $w$. Note that $\pi\equiv 1$ on trees. 
		In addition, we denote by $\pi(\tau_i;w)$ the number of times the tree $\tau_i$ appears in the forest $w$,  i.e. for $w=\hat\tau^{\ell_1}_1\cdots\hat\tau_r^{\ell_r}$ and $\hat\tau_i\ne\hat\tau_j$ for $i\ne j$, we have $\pi(\tau_i;w)=\ell_i$.
Finally $w\setminus\tau_i$ is the forest obtained by removing an instance of $\tau_i$ in $w$.","status":"unverified"}

