{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.237355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6183063c854a04bbdfd9523f284aa5a34d78b8838c05595a6e8663b5f2e76099","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.29147, 'The crossing matrix and the Burau matrix of pure braids'] Let $A=[a_{ij}]$ be an $m \times m$ symmetric matrix with zero diagonal. 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] The following diagonal matrix $D_A \in M_m(\mathbb{Z})$ is called the {\it degree matrix of $A$}. 
\begin{align*}
D_A= 
\begin{bmatrix}
\sum_{j\neq 1}a_{1 j}  \\
 & \sum_{j\neq 2}a_{2 j}          &        & \text{\Huge{0}}   \\
 &                 & \ddots                     \\
  & \text{\Huge{0}} &        & \ddots            \\
&                 &        &           & \sum_{j\neq m}a_{m j}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
\item[(2)] The matrix $L_A=D_A-A \in M_m(\mathbb{Z})$ is called the {\it Laplacian of $A$}. 
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

