{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.060278+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62093e0932eb4a4167537223bf9554e1b84fedfbf9b1e63ce1f17efd5ca1fe8d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:explpp of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Let $\{ \omega_{\mathbf v} : \mathbf v \in {\mathbb N}^2\}$ be a collection of i.i.d.\@ exponential random variables of mean $1$. 
The last passage time from $\mathbf p$ to $\mathbf q$ is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathbf p}(\mathbf q) = \max_{\pi: \mathbf p \to \mathbf q} E(\pi), 
    \qquad E(\pi)= \sum_{\mathbf v\in \pi} \omega_{\mathbf v}, 
\end{equation*}
where the maximum is taken over all up/right paths from $\mathbf p$ to $\mathbf q$. 
When $\mathbf p=(1,1)$, we write $\mathcal{L}_{(1,1)}(\mathbf q)= \mathcal{L}(\mathbf q)$. 
We call the 2-dimensional random field $\mathcal{L}= \{\mathcal{L}(\mathbf q) : \mathbf q \in {\mathbb N}^2\}$ the exponential last passage percolation, or simply exponential LPP.  
For $(\alpha, \beta)\in {\mathbb R}_+^2$, we define
\begin{equation} 
    \mathcal{L}(\alpha, \beta)  = \mathcal{L}(\lceil\alpha\rceil, \lceil\beta\rceil)
\end{equation}
where $\lceil\alpha \rceil$ denotes the smallest integer that is greater than or equal to $\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

