{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.884176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6225cbf639f5db88c8281d3beb3c1b5c1fd6f318e7946fddcf653fd0d7eafd32","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:main_claim1-4 of arXiv:2605.09377, 'A factorization formula for the partition function in the semi-discrete parabolic Anderson'] For an inverse temperature $\beta$ sufficiently small, the following is true: There is $\theta = \theta(\sigma) > 0$, independent of $\beta$, such that 
\begin{align}
	\lim_{t \to \infty} t^{\theta} \sup_{\|y\| \leq t^{\sigma}} \frac{1}{p_t^y} \left \langle \left \lvert \mathbf{E}  \left[ q^y_{n_t} \left(F_2^{y,t} - (T_{0,0}^t - 1) (T^{y,t}_0 - 1 ) \right) \right] \right \rvert \right \rangle &= 0,  \\

	\lim_{t \to \infty} t^{\theta} \sup_{\|y\| \leq t^{\sigma}} \frac{1}{p_t^y}\left \langle \left \lvert \mathbf{E} \left[ q^y_{n_t} \left(F_1^{y,t} - (T^{y,t}_0 - 1) \right)\right] \right \rvert \right \rangle &= 0,  \\

