{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.496328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62283335531778908b7b0875ad7d606a5246a9b5b4ecab396955bc1bfb25cb4e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2605.28801, 'Microscopic Weak Selection Principle for the Logistic Branching Brownian Motion with selec'] Let $(\boldsymbol{X}^c(t))_{t\geq 0}$ denote the Log-BBM($1,c$) process. Given $\varepsilon_1 \sim$ Exp$(1)$, define the times 
	\[
	T_1 := \inf \{t\geq 0: N^c_t = 1\} \quad \text{ and } \quad
	T_2 := \inf \{ t \geq T_1 + \varepsilon_1: N_t^c = 1 \},
	\]
	which are the hitting time of one and the first return time to one of the process $N_t^c$, respectively. Set $T:=T_2 - T_1$, and we define 
	\begin{equation*}
		\gamma := \max\limits_{t \in [0,T]}\Big|\max\limits_{u \in \mathcal{N}^c_t} X^c_u(t) \Big|.
	\end{equation*}
	Then $\mathbb{E}_{(0)}(\gamma)<\infty$, where $\mathbb{E}_{(0)}$ denote expectation under the law of the process starting from one particle at position zero. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.065445+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6228701dcd3e67f5929331263ab5b0ef6b4e9ed3e1ce89004f053521c9746734","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem informal_thm2 of arXiv:2606.28575, 'Global smooth solutions by high mode Lie-Transport noise for Logarithmically Hyperdissipat'] Let $R>0,\ \varepsilon\in (0,1)$. There exist $\nu>0,\ \theta(t)\in \ell^2(\mathbb{Z}^3_0)$ such that for all zero mean, divergence-free vector field $\omega_0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \|\omega_0\|_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^3;\mathbb{R}^3)}\leq R,
    \end{align*}
    there exists a unique smooth solution to the (equation main_strat of the paper) that is global in time with probability larger than $1-\varepsilon$. Moreover, $\theta(t)$ can be chosen so that there exists a sequence of deterministic times $\{\overline{T}_i\}_{i\geq 0}$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    \overline{T}_0=0,\quad \overline{T}_{i+1}-\overline{T}_{i}\geq 1 \quad \text{for each } i\geq 0,
\end{align*}
and $\theta(t)$ is constant on $[\overline{T}_i,\overline{T}_{i+1})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:19:46.234750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62293f3f94f1680a1152b4ff8b5c1276d8eaf2a05fee14599cf904bcb4c6c867","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 76 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] Let $\phi \in \B(\A,\rmM_k)_+$ be unital, and let $\cal{O}$ be any base of neighbourhoods around $\phi$ in $\S_k^\rm{u}(\A)$.  Then
\begin{equation}
\rmh^\ann_{\bspi}(\phi) = \inf_{O\in \cal{O}} \limsup_{n \to\infty} \frac{1}{d_n}\log \bbE m_{\bf{U}(k,d_n)}\X(\pi_n,O).
\end{equation}
The analogous formula holds for $\ul{\rmh}^\ann_{\bspi}$ if `$\limsup$' is replaced by `$\liminf$'. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.929993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_622b1f1b21690acacd061ce4d607cc2661e498d467dc4b13b26ca7ef1ead6870","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2505.21774, 'The friendship paradox for trees'] {\bf [A sufficient criterion for negative correlation]}

Let $\mathcal{T}_\infty$ be the infinite Galton-Watson tree with offspring distribution $p$ satisfying \eqref{eq:pcond}. Let $S_k$ be as defined in Theorem \ref{thm:densities-on-infinite-trees}. If, for each $\tk$ in the support of $\tilde{p}$, 
\begin{equation}

k \mapsto \PP\big(\tk + S_k - k(k+1)>0\big) \text{ is non-increasing on the support of $p$},
\end{equation}
then $f^{++} \leq \tf^+ f^+$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.598176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_622c5d39cf46fa652837f0bdec8144185d852195cf547e47025e231fd9c21c52","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary C:exp-quadrangle of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For $i = 1, 2$, consider points $u_i = (x_i, n) \le v_i = (y_i, m) \in \mathbb{Z}^2_\ge, i = 1, 2$ with $x_1 \le x_2$ and $y_1 \le y_2$. Then:
\begin{align*}
    X_\infty(u_1; v_1) + Y(u_2; v_2) &\le Y(u_1; v_1) + X_\infty(u_2; v_2) \\
    X_\beta(u_1; v_1) + X_\alpha(u_2; v_2) &\le X_\alpha(u_1; v_1) + X_\beta(u_2; v_2), \qquad \text{ for any } \alpha \le \beta \in \mathbb{R}_+\cup\{\infty\}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.029274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_622f9a765cbb054c2890f4c217ee43b5488adc6da596d5ae664eecdb86d6871f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 ($(m,n)$-good) of arXiv:2506.09609, 'Schramm-Loewner evolution contains a topological Sierpi\'nski carpet when $\kappa$ is clos'] We define the \textbf{$(m,n)$-good} property for points in $V_n$ and integers $m \geq 0$. If a point is not $(m,n)$-good, we say that it is \textbf{$(m,n)$-bad}.
    The definition is given inductively on $m$. For $m = 0$ and $n \geq 0$, we say $x \in V_n$ is \textbf{$(0,n)$-good} if and only if $x$ is $n$-good.

    For $m \geq 1$, given the definition of $(j,n)$-good for $0 \leq j \leq m-1$, we define the $(m,n)$-good property for $x \in V_n$ as follows. Let $J_x$ be the set of points in $B(x,\epsilon^n) \cap V_{n+1}$ that are either $(m-1,n+1)$-bad or $(0, n+1)$-bad. Consider the collection of closed boxes $\{ \overline{B(w, \epsilon^{n+1})} \}_{w \in J_x}$ and the connected components formed by their union on $\mathbb{C}$, where two boxes that share exactly one point are also viewed as connected. We say that $x$ is \textbf{$(m,n)$-good} if all these components have $|\cdot|_\infty$-diameter at most $\frac{1}{4} \epsilon^n$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:32:58.303032+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_622fdc83d838f4564241d2350de35e437b6149109a7096dbaba547149a5f6feb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:SRW_transition_prob_estimate of arXiv:2505.10256, 'Stochastic Oscillators out of equilibrium:\\ scaling limits and correlation estimates'] Let $\{Y_t^{(1)}: t\ge0 \}$ be the simple symmetric random walk on $\mathbb T_N$ with jump rate $N^2$ to all directions, starting from $x\in\mathbb T_N$ and let $P_x$ be the associated probability measure.
There exists a constant $C>0$ such that 
