{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.855574+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62b654e4df844f5d165741bc029488a47efe35e45d69cbcf0a39344016ce966a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop ch2-incluU of arXiv:2410.23995, 'Sample path properties of parabolic SPDEs with non constant coefficients'] Assume that $\mu$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure. Then the set
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{S}^\prime_\mu(\mathbb{R}^k)  = \left\{ \Gamma \in \mathcal{S}^\prime(\mathbb{R}^k): \text{$\mathcal{F}\Gamma$ is a function and } \int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}} \mu(d\xi)\, \vert \mathcal{F} \Gamma(\xi)\vert^2 < \infty\right\}
\end{equation*}
is included in $U$, and for such $\Gamma$,
\begin{equation}

\Vert \Gamma\Vert_ U^2 = \int_{\mathbb{R}^{k}} \mu(d\xi) \, \vert \mathcal{F} \Gamma(\xi)\vert^2.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.097852+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62bd9606f4c1179b984af6ebe7c4633b0ab8eafb71b6cdd346f7f0b096709a6a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 (Tail Bound on the Leakage Probability) of arXiv:2608.28198, 'Performative Privacy: When Differential Privacy Maximizes Utility'] Let $Z_{t}$ be the dataset at the beginning of round $t$ and $z \in Z_{t}$ be a target record. Let $o_t \sim \mathcal{M}(Z_{t})$ be the candidate output. Define the factual and counterfactual output distributions as $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}}(\cdot) = \Pr[\mathcal{M}(Z_{t}) = \cdot \mid z \in Z_{t}]$ and $P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}}(\cdot) = \Pr[\mathcal{M}(Z_{t} \setminus \{z\}) = \cdot]$. 

For the log-likelihood ratio $\Lambda(o_t|z) = \ln \left( \frac{P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}}(o_t)}{P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}}(o_t)} \right)$ evaluated at output $o_t$, and for any threshold $\tau > 0$ and order $\alpha > 1$, the tail probability of leakage is bounded by:
\begin{align*}
\Pr_{o_t \sim P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}}}(\Lambda(o_t|z) > \tau) \le \exp \Big( -(\alpha - 1) \big( \tau - D_\alpha(P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}} \parallel P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}}) \big) \Big) 
\end{align*}
where $D_\alpha(P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{in}} \parallel P^{\mathcal{M}}_{\text{out}})$ is the Rényi divergence of order $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.333718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62be09d203a7287a8e185a1f1f8f755629894752fa967a88631fb5eff6e7bfb7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition of arXiv:2606.16609, 'On stability of outliers from the circular law'] Let $K$ be a compact such $K \subset \{\lvert z \lvert >1\}$. Then, a.s,
     \begin{equation*}
         \sup_{z\in K}\lvert Q_N(z)-q(z)\lvert \rightarrow0.
     \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.049235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62bf5d9fe0b36a69bfbbf28b7d88a890f6f000ec813f821d2fbf081b93e89f38","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2606.16083, 'Eyring--Kramers asymptotics for infinite-dimensional stochastic gradient systems'] Let $x \in \mathcal S_0$ and $B_{r_x}(x)$ be as in \eqref{Eq:Open_Convex}. Then, for all $0<r<r_x$, there exists a function $\Phi_\beta$ with $\|\Phi_\beta\|_{L^2(\mu_\beta)}=1$ and $\mathrm{supp}(\Phi_\beta)\subseteq B_{r}(x)$ and constants $C, \beta_0>0$
     such that for $\beta>\beta_0$
    \begin{align}

         \frac{1}{\beta} \int_B \|\nabla \Psi\|_H^2 \, d\mu_\beta   \ge C\|\Psi\|^2_{L^2(\mu_\beta)}
\end{align}
for all $\Psi\perp_{L^2(\mu_\beta)} \Phi_\beta$ with $\mathrm{supp}(\Psi)\subseteq B_{r_x}(x)$. Furthermore, there exists $C,\kappa>0$ such that
\begin{align}

     \frac{1}{\beta} \int_B \|\nabla \Phi_\beta\|_H^2 d\mu_\beta \leq C e^{-\beta \kappa}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.843809+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62c4b9e428ff7e38a2b9189c64a1686db373f87d604b18b3d5765698eb0d7f19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:inverse of arXiv:2608.23742, 'Second-order fusion asymptotics for $\Sine_\beta$ correlation functions'] Let $\beta>0$, $m\ge2$, and $m\beta>1$. Let $X_{\beta,m\beta/2}$ denote the zero set of $\xi^{\beta,m\beta/2}$, equivalently the $\mathsf{HP}_{\beta,m\beta/2}$ point process. Then
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E\sum_{x\in X_{\beta,m\beta/2}}\frac1{x^2}
=\frac{m\beta}{4(m\beta-1)(2m+1)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.509845+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62c532dbdf493e018ab27bcd91b38b836ea88a3367e15c70aee07dd4230f333c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Let $T<\infty$, $b>2$, and $p>4$. For every $w_0\in H^b$ and every
$y^\circ\in L^p(0,T;H^b)$, the equation
\begin{align}
 v(t)=e^{\nu t\Delta}w_0-
 \int_0^t e^{\nu(t-s)\Delta}
 B(v(s)+y_s^\circ,v(s)+y_s^\circ)\,\dd s
\end{align}
has a unique solution $v\in C([0,T];H^b)$. Moreover, for every
$R<\infty$,
\begin{align}
 \sup_{\substack{\|w_0\|_{H^b}
 +\|y^\circ\|_{L^p(0,T;H^b)}\le R}}
 \|v\|_{C([0,T];H^b)}<\infty.
\end{align}
Consequently, since
$H^\sigma(0,T;H^b)\hookrightarrow L^{p_\sigma}(0,T;H^b)$ and
$p_\sigma>4$, the equation is globally well posed for every
$(w_0,y)\in H^b\times\mathsf Y_T$.","status":"unverified"}

