{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.872086+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_62ff899970791e8658044f1418a8df379f3087aa3074e2a2fa6363f50cf4d63a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main of arXiv:2411.00629, '\Large Transformation of Stochastic Recursions and Critical Phenomena in the Analysis of t'] Let $\alpha>1$ and define $R_\alpha\subset \mathbb{R}$ by
\begin{equation}
R_\alpha=\left[0, \, \max \left\{1,  \frac{2\alpha-1}{4}-\frac{6\alpha^2-15\alpha+4}{4(\alpha-1)(2\alpha-1)}\right\}\right)\cup\{1\}. 
\end{equation}
Then, for any $u_0\in R_\alpha$ and $\lambda>0$, there exists a solution $u_\lambda$ to (equation diffalpha-ricc of the paper) such that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{1-u_\lambda(t)}{t^{-\gamma}}={\lambda},
\end{equation}
where $\gamma>0$ is given by (equation gma of the paper). Consequently, there are infinitely many solutions converging to 1 with an algebraic rate 
$t^{-\gamma}$ as $t\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.363772+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63000eb8f0ab559e9023ef9f5268684d597d3bd9941afc62520124d985dbada0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop-well-posedness-approx of arXiv:2507.09922, 'A scaling limit for Vlasov equations with electrostatic fluctuations'] Assume that $f^\delta_0\in C_c^\infty (\mathbb{T}^3\times \mathbb{R}^3, \mathbb{R}_+)$. Then for any $\delta>0$, the approximate equation (equation stoch-Vlasov-approx of the paper) admits a unique probabilistically strong solution $f^\delta$, represented as
  $$f^\delta_t(x,v)= f^\delta_0 \big(\Phi^{\delta,-1}_t(x,v) \big), $$
where $\big\{\Phi^{\delta,-1}_t \big\}_{t\ge 0}$ is the inverse flow associated to the stochastic equations
  \begin{equation}
  \left\{ \aligned
  d X^\delta_t &= V^\delta_t\, dt, \\
  d V^\delta_t &= \big[E_{\rho^\delta_t}\big(X^\delta_t \big) + B V^\delta_t\times \bm{e}_3 \big]\, dt + \sum_k \sigma_k\big(X^\delta_t \big)\circ dW^k_t
  \endaligned \right.
  \end{equation}
with initial condition $\big(X^\delta_0, V^\delta_0 \big)= (x,v)\in \mathbb{T}^3\times \mathbb{R}^3$. Moreover, $\|f^\delta_t \|_{L^p_{x,v}} = \|f^\delta_0 \|_{L^p_{x,v}}$ for any $p\ge 1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:57:51.765267+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63046ad74444e29c4efa99bc8ad8b247a1abcefac87c089800132bc4bb1037c7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (Uniform compactly supported half-space quasimodes) of arXiv:2608.30211, '2608.30211'] There are $R_0,C>0$ such that, for every $R\geq R_0$ and
