	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.828107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_634bcd1bf745bb12321519823c986b367629329192971fc4923cee1cb4ad02b0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2604.10705, '#1'] A functional $F$ is \emph{left-continuous} (in time) at $(t,x) \in [0,T]\times D([0,T],\mathbb{R}^d)$ if for all $\epsilon > 0$, there exists $\delta > 0$ such that for all $y \in \cadlag$,
    \begin{align}
        d_*((t,x_{\wedge t}), (s, y_{\wedge s})) <\delta,\, s\leq t \implies |F(t,x_{\wedge t})-F(s,y_{\wedge s})|< \epsilon.\nonumber
    \end{align}
A functional that is left-continuous for all $(t,x)\in [0, T]\times \cadlag$ is said to be left-continuous in time. Right-continuity in time is defined analogously, and a functional is continuous in time if it is both left and right continuous in time.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.129378+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6353ea068432bc556ce1b28029f79e4b4c4fc13a68b7f7eb29317669e2ce7cf3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:interp of arXiv:2411.16058, 'Gaussian deconvolution on $\mathbb R^d$ \\ with application to self-repellent Brownian mot'] Let $d\ge 1$.
Let $h : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{C}$ be a function such that
\begin{equation} 
h(x) \in L^1 \cap L^2(\mathbb{R}^d), 
\qquad
\abs x^{2} h(x) \in L^1(\mathbb{R}^d) .
\end{equation}
Let $ 2 \le b \le a \le d+2$ and write $p_a^* = \frac d {d - a + 2} \in [1,\infty]$. Then
\begin{equation} 
\abs x^a h(x) \in L^{p_a} \cap L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)
\qquad
\text{for some $1 \le p_a \le p_a^*$} 
\end{equation}
implies
\begin{equation} 
\abs x^b h(x) \in L^{p_b} \cap L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)
\qquad
\text{for some $1 \le p_b \le p_b^*$} . 
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $b > 2$ and (equation eq:h_hyp of the paper) holds with some $p_a < p_a^*$, then (equation eq:h_conc of the paper) holds with some $p_b < p_b^*$.","status":"unverified"}

