 \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:52.232505+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63cdb7c5c3078dd3b8380439521eec79d341a0e080c2845a353fcdb862c8861f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2512.10199, 'Explicit correlation functions for the six-vertex model in the free-fermion regime'] Let $\rho \in \pure_n$ have disjoint cycle decomposition $\rho = c_1 c_2\dots c_r$. For a cycle $c$ on $\midedge_n$, define its \textit{horizontal} and \textit{vertical winding numbers} by, respectively,
        \begin{equation*}
            q_1(c) = \frac{A(c) + \tfrac{1}{2}C(c)}{n}, \qquad q_2(c) = \frac{B(c) + \tfrac{1}{2}C(c)}{n}.
        \end{equation*}
        Then there exist coprime non-negative integers $q_1,q_2$ such that $q_i(c_j) = q_i$ for all $i = 1,2$ and $1 \leq j \leq r$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.778597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63ce1ed36b18abb1e8bacccfaa52a7318adce4abaa4c349dab9b1e74d909f218","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 (Uniform height-dependent survival) of arXiv:2609.02257, 'Aperiodic random walks: uniform estimates, local limits and invariance principles'] There is a constant \(C>0\) such that, for all sufficiently large \(N\)
