{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:16:46.121714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63d00c10fd4d6c68d917678f7e31fd67d0fa41b2bc79184cf9767b353354067a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lm:bdry of arXiv:2604.12963, 'Shocks, instability, and the twenty networks\\ of infinite geodesics in the Directed Lands'] Let $\omega\in\Omega_0$, $\theta\in\Theta^\omega$, and  $(x,s)\in\mathbb{R}^2$. For $t\in\mathbb{R}$, let $\gamma(t)=(I^{\theta-}_{(x,s)}(t),t)$ and $\tau(t)=(I^{\theta+}_{(x,s)}(t),t)$. 
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\alph*)}, ref={\rm\alph*}] \itemsep=1pt
    \item $\gamma\preceq\tau$ and $(x,s)$ is weakly between the two paths. If $\gamma\ne\tau$, then the connected components of $\{(z,r):\gamma(r)<(z,r)<\tau(r)\}$ are all stability islands.
    \item Suppose $(x,s)$ is in the closure of some stability island. Let $t_1=\sup\{r\le s:\gamma(r)=\tau(r)\}$ and $t_2=\inf\{r\ge s:\gamma(r)=\tau(r)\}$. Then $-\infty<t_1<t_2<\infty$, $s\in[t_1,t_2]$, $\gamma(t_1) = \tau(t_1)$, $\gamma(t_2) = \tau(t_2)$, for any $r\in(t_1,t_2)$, $\gamma(r) < \tau(r)$, and the island is given by
    \begin{align}
    \{(z,r):\gamma(r)<(z,r)<\tau(r),\,t_1<r<t_2\}.
    \end{align}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.464957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63d1d4aebc091af7ef4e93663911c93f2fecf4f6f4695947fcc4e879dedf67d2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2606.29131, '2606.29131'] Let $\Lambda_\e^2 f(x,y)$  be defined in \eqref{e1-5a}. For every $\beta>0$, there exists a positive constant $C_3:=C_3(\beta)$ such that for every $f\in \M_{\beta}$,  $x\in \R^d$ and $y \in \T^d$,
