{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.648480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63d5fd3139d41bb60d82b65dce2df416479a14b717d98e1edb25a3dcaeec227c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2608.30736, 'Stability results for distribution-dependent stochastic Volterra equations'] Let $T>0$ and let kernels $K_b$, $K_\sigma$ satisfy Assumption~\ref{ass:kernel}. Let $A$ and $M$ be continuous processes such that
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $A$ is a finite variation process satisfying $A_t = A_0 + \int_0^t \alpha_s \dd s$ with $\bE[\int_0^T |\alpha_s|^q \dd s]<\infty$ for some $q \geq 2 + \frac{4}{\epsilon}$, and
	\item $M$ is a continuous local martingale satisfying $M_t = \int_0^t \beta_s \dd B_s$ for some Brownian motion $B$ with $\bE[\int_0^T |\beta_s|^p \dd s]<\infty$
  \end{itemize}
  and define $X$ by
  \begin{equation*}
    X_t := A_0 + \int_0^t K_b(s,t) \dd A_s + \int_0^t K_\sigma(s,t) \dd M_s, \qquad t \in [0,T].
  \end{equation*}
  Then
  \begin{itemize}
	\item there exists a measurable map
	\begin{equation*}
      \Phi \colon C([0,T];\bR^d) \times C([0,T];\bR^d) \to C([0,T];\bR^d)
	\end{equation*}
	s.t. $X = \Phi(A,M)$ a.s.
	\item If $(\hat{A},\hat{M},\hat{X}) \stackrel{\mathscr{D}}{\sim} (A,M,X)$ in $C([0,T];\bR^d \times \bR^d \times \bR^d)$, then $\hat{X}= \Phi(\hat{A},\hat{M})$ a.s.
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.956746+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63d6a42ab1db564ce30403415f0cdd8a5a22d8ef57dbabcc4850f52fcba3b5e9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2607.21931, 'Lorenz and convex ordering of parasite burden distributions with density-dependent deaths'] The {\em Lorenz curve} $L: [0,1] \to [0,1] $ for the distribution $F$ on $[0,\infty)$ with finite mean $ \mu$ is given by
    \begin{equation*}
        L(u) = \frac{\int_{0}^{u} F^{-1}(y)\, dy}{\mu}, 
    \end{equation*}
    where $ F^{-1}$ is the quantile function
    \begin{equation*}
        F^{-1}(y) = \inf \{ x : F(x) \geq y \} \quad \text{for } y \in (0,1).
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:09:59.308232+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63d8d358ccc965cffea74c8e42cc6cac8e289023695486bb3f560e3508c5f583","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma_phi_martingale_on_stochastic_interval of arXiv:2608.08299, 'Non-linear optimal stopping with Bermudan strategies: the infinite horizon case'] Let $\rho$ satisfy the consistency property (v).
    Let $\phi=(\phi(\nu))$ be a given non-negative admissible family.
    Let $S$ $\in$ $\Theta$ and $\tau$ $\in$ $\Theta$ be such that $S \leq \tau$ a.s. 
    We assume that for any  $\sigma$ $\in$ $\Theta$ such that $S \leq \sigma \leq \tau$ a.s., it holds 
    \begin{equation}
        \rho_{\sigma,\tau}[\phi(\tau)] = \phi(\sigma) \,\,\,\, a.s.
    \end{equation}
    Then, $\phi$ is a \emph{$(\Theta, \rho)$}-martingale on the stochastic interval $[S,\tau]$, that is, for any  $\nu_1$ $\in$ $\Theta$, and $\nu_2$ $\in$ $\Theta$, such that $S \leq \nu_1 \leq \nu_2 \leq \tau$ a.s.,
    \begin{equation}
          \rho_{\nu_1,\nu_2}[\phi(\nu_2)] = \phi(\nu_1) \,\,\,\, a.s.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.475485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63dafd9183f5860de8d603a5c681d2ee937ddd22582d37c480e1e07023cb2cea","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 70 (Deviation and almost-sure convergence) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] In the symmetric same-field setting of
Theorem~\ref{thm:dominated-stability}, let $2\le r<\infty$ and suppose
$K_N\to K$ with
\[
 \norm{(\Prof_{m,r}(K_{N;i}-K_i))_i}_{E(I)}\le\varepsilon_N.
\]
For every $t\ge2$,
\begin{equation}

 \Prob\!\left(
  \norm{I_m(K_N-K)}_{\Sch_r}
  >eA_mC_{E,r}(t+r)^{m/2}\varepsilon_N
 \right)
 \le e^{-t}.
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}

 \sum_{N=1}^\infty
 \norm{(\Prof_{m,r}(K_{N+1;i}-K_{N;i}))_i}_{E(I)}<\infty,
\end{equation}
then $I_m(K_N)$ converges almost surely in $\Sch_r$ and the successive
differences are almost surely absolutely summable.
If instead
\begin{equation}

 \varepsilon_N\bigl(\log(N+1)+r\bigr)^{m/2}\longrightarrow0,
\end{equation}
then $I_m(K_N)\to I_m(K)$ almost surely in $\Sch_r$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.638668+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63dc3bbbef34416f7c92b71a6935df705c2e4c4670d6809cef90555e87d7868c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2609.00687, 'A unification of Euler-Maclaurin and Euler-Boole summation formulas'] Let $n,q\in\mathbb{N}$ and $x,a\in\mathbb{R}$ with $a>0$. Let $\chi$ be a Dirichlet character modulo $q$. If $q=1$, then
\begin{equation}
L(1-n,x,a,\chi)=-\frac{\beta_{n}(a,e^{\frac{2\pi \mathrm{i}x}{q}})}{n},
\end{equation}
and if $q\geqslant2$, then
\begin{equation}
L(1-n,x,a,\chi)=-\frac{\beta_{n,\chi}(a,e^{\frac{2\pi \mathrm{i}x}{q}})}{n},
\end{equation}
where $\beta_{n,\chi}(x,\lambda)$ are the generalized Apostol-Bernoulli polynomials, defined for $\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$ and for a Dirichlet character $\chi$ modulo $q\in\mathbb{N}$, by the generating function (see, e.g., \cite[Definition 2.1]{he})
\begin{equation}
\sum_{r=1}^{q}\frac{\chi(r)\lambda^{r}te^{(r+x)t}}{\lambda^{q}e^{qt}-1}=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\beta_{n,\chi}(x,\lambda)\frac{t^{n}}{n!}\quad(|t+\log\lambda|<2\pi/q).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:52:17.597871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63df3ac5c6ba533597b49f25fb7d06e062ea85bfec0352da9f94adaf386ac20c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Lem2.2 of arXiv:2411.15724, 'Wasserstein Convergence Rates for Empirical Measures of Random Subsequence of $\{nα\}$'] Given $\theta > 1$ and $\tau > 0$, then for any irrational number $\alpha$ associated with convergents $( p_k/q_k )_{k \in \mathbb N}$ and every $k \geq 3$,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{q_k \leq m < q_{k + 1}} \frac{1}{m^{\theta} \| m \alpha \|^\tau} \, = \,  O \bigg( \frac{1}{q_k^\theta \| q_k \alpha \|^\tau} \bigg) \, + \,
\begin{cases}
O\big( q_k^{-(\theta - 1)} \big), & \textrm{if }0 < \tau < 1;\\
O\big( q_k^{-(\theta - 1)} \log q_k \big), & \textrm{if } \tau = 1;\\
O\big( q_k^{-\theta + \tau} \big), & \textrm{if }\tau > 1,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where the implied constants only depend on $\theta$ and $\tau$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.262001+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63df542a13800208623e8d33c995207ba4d35ba3aabc8b58ccf947fdb14ff96d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_h_limit of arXiv:2411.16452, 'Interface scaling limit for the critical planar Ising model perturbed by a magnetic field'] Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}$ be a bounded and simply connected domain with two marked boundary points $a,b \in \partial \Omega$. Let $\eta > 0$ and define $\Omega^{-}(\eta):=\{ z \in \Omega: \operatorname{dist}(z,\partial \Omega^{-}) \leq \eta \}$. Then
