    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.570516+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_640b1a3d0246198f208eeb7ff3d9ed6f30f89a249722ddd749848eec5f5c0642","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2605.30869, 'Approximate Transitivity of Young Translation on Rough Paths'] For any \( \mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{C}^{p\text{-var}}_{\mathbf{1}} \)  there exists  a sequence of paths $(h^n)\subset  C^{\infty}_0$ such that $(\mathbf X\boxplus h^n)$ converges uniformly to $\mathbf{1}$ and for any $p'>p$ one has
	 \begin{equation}
\rho_{p'\text{-var}}((\mathbf X\boxplus h^n),\mathbf 1)\to 0 \,.
	\end{equation}
	Moreover if $\mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{C}^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{1}}$ the same result holds with the metric $\rho_{\alpha'} $ for any $\alpha'<\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.870547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_640d79cc70204dfdf9e9cd57478afa65e20edfe7766bc4960f8d9bd9ba1798b6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 (Exact legwise rule) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Writing $U_S=\bigotimes_{j\in S}U_j$, one has
\begin{equation}

 \Flat_S(\Theta_{U,V,W}K)
 =(U_{S^c}\otimes W)\Flat_S(K)(\conj{U_S}\otimes V)^*.
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

 \Cost(\Theta_{U,V,W})
 \le\norm V\norm W\prod_{j=1}^m\norm{U_j}.
\end{equation}
Unitary leg maps preserve every cut norm exactly.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.086242+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64142e595236fdca06e7e51fdf30341f09afacde6d3eb9f2d1d135a57ca1c110","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-Jordan of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] Let $\xi$ be a random signed measure. Then, there exists two unique random measures $\xi_+$ and $\xi_-$ such that for every $\omega\in\Omega$ and every $B\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$
	\begin{align*}
		\xi_+(\omega,B)&=\sup_{E\in \mathcal{S}, E\subset B}\xi(\omega,E),\quad
		\xi_-(\omega,B)=\sup_{E\in \mathcal{S}, E\subset B}-\xi(\omega,E),
	\end{align*}
	\begin{equation*}
		and\quad\xi(\omega,B)=\xi_+(\omega,B)-\xi_-(\omega,B).
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.302915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_641755351a38a2e2274e43cdf77e9112b83b4a675f08bc40105b8db9bac8d5fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 (BRW tree process) of arXiv:2606.24056, 'Decorated stable $p$-adic self-similar processes with stationary increments'] Let \(0<\alpha\le2\), \(H>0\), and let
\[
  E=\bigsqcup_{k=0}^{\infty}\Z/p^{k+1}\Z
\]
with control measure \(\mu\) equal to the counting measure on each
level $k$.  Let \(M_\alpha\) be a real symmetric \(S\alpha S\) random measure on
\(E\) with control measure \(\mu\).  Define the (stable) BRW tree process as
\begin{align}
  \begin{split}
      X_n=\int_{E}p^{-kH}
  \left(\mathbf 1_{\{r\equiv n\!\!\!\pmod {p^{k+1}}\}}
        -\mathbf 1_{\{r\equiv0\!\!\!\pmod {p^{k+1}}\}}\right)
  &M_\alpha(dk,dr),\\
  &\quad n\in\N_0.
  \end{split}
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.828319+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64190849fdc9c6bdd3b8957ae7d7559f77301c0f8005691fdb4292c83d2906eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:marcus-restores of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] At level two the Marcus correction
\begin{equation}
\frac12\sum_{0<u\le T}\Delta X_u\otimes\Delta X_u
\end{equation}
adds one half of the bracket to each ordering. Consequently, the Marcus lift satisfies the shuffle identity at level two, and its completed-graph construction gives a weakly geometric rough path at all levels [chevyrev2019,frizshekhar2017]. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.865968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64199c6895dee64a16bba21b0a52a891ba2a3078499c5a7dbc2d41c64c04f17f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:3 of arXiv:2509.21253, '\bf Capacity in high dimensional percolation'] \rm{([KN11])}
