{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.421759+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_647f2e7d53bb5b8b39437103a862bd47d75c4fbb6272801c82da989196ffbd3c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma asymptotics of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] Let $C$ be a test-graph whose underlying graph is a cycle of length $c$ and $L$ be a test-graph whose underlying graph is a line of length $l$. Let $V_N$ be a uniform random permutation matrix. By a direct computation, one has
	       \begin{align*}
		      \mathbb{E}\left[\mathrm{Tr}^{0}(C(V_{N}))\right]&=N\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{U}_{N}\in\mathrm{Cyc}_{c})=1,\\
            \mathbb{E}\left[\mathrm{Tr}^{0}(L(V_{N}))\right]&=N\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{U}_{N}\in\mathrm{Cyc}_{\geq l})=N(1-\frac{l-1}{N}),
	       \end{align*}
        where $\mathcal{U}_{N}$ is a uniform random variable on $\{1,\cdots,N\}$ and the notations $\mathcal{U}_{N}\in\mathrm{Cyc}_{c}$ and $\mathcal{U}_{N}\in\mathrm{Cyc}_{\geq l}$ correspond to "$U_{N}$ belongs to a cycle of length $c$ of $V_{N}$" and "$U_{N}$ belongs to a cycle of length at least $l$ of $V_{N}$" respectively. 

        Moreover, if a test-graph $T$ has an underlying graph which is not a cycle nor a directed line, then, $\mathbb{E}\left[\mathrm{Tr}^{0}(T(V_{N}))\right]=0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

