{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.488078+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64b41f71258337778385b6446face8c07ea78f563df1317729a753df983397d6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Deff def:mild of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Fix numbers $r \in [2,\infty), \theta \in \mathbb{R}$, functions $F: B \to \mathcal{B}_{-1+\theta, r}$, $G: B \to \gamma(\mathcal{U},\mathcal{B}_{-1/2+\theta, r})$, a stopping time $\tau$, and assume $x \in L^p(\Omega;C([0,T];B))$.  
     A process $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ to (equation eq:gefx of the paper) up to time $\tau$




    if there is some $\epsilon > 0$ such that for all $T > 0$
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^{T \wedge \tau} \|F(x_t)\|_{-1+\theta + \epsilon, r} + \|G(x_t)\|_{-1/2+\theta +\epsilon}^2dt\Big] < \infty \,,
