    \end{equation}
    almost surely on the event $\{t \leq \tau\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.305245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64b4bdbbaae9cdef212db8fa1d6785319c5e2f95408f0ab85b2fc4e6003490e4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2609.02385, 'Kinetic Fokker--Planck Equations with Drifts in a Supercritical Range'] Let $Q\subset\mR^{1+2d}$ be a bounded domain. A function $u\in\sV_Q\cap\mL_Q^\infty$ is called a weak sub-solution of PDE \eqref{kineticpde} in $Q$ if for any nonnegative $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(Q)$,
\begin{align}
-\int_Q u\p_t\varphi\le\int_Q u\Delta_v \varphi-\int_Q u(v\cdot\nn_x\varphi)+\int_Q(b\cdot\nn_v u)\varphi+\int_Q f\varphi.
\end{align}
We say that $u$ is a weak solution to \eqref{kineticpde} in $Q$ if \eqref{weaksubsolineq} holds with equality for every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(Q)$. If $u\in\mL_{\rm loc}^\infty\cap\sV_{\rm loc}$ is a weak solution in every bounded domain $Q$, then we call $u$ a global weak solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:53:51.338113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64b579254cb43da690b27115bcb9bb0f83b568b2292236abc98e07aed32e1280","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:height_boundary of arXiv:2605.03896, 'Dimer models on astroidal zig-zag graphs'] Let~$\partial \mathcal D\subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a simple polygon and assume there is an order-preserving bijection~$v\mapsto \bm p_v$ from~$V(N)$ to~$V(\partial \mathcal D)$. Assume further that there is a continuous piecewise linear function~$\mathfrak h_0:\partial \mathcal D\to \mathbb{R}$ such that, for all~$v\in V(N)$,
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak h_0(x,y)=(x,y)\cdot(s^v,t^v)-d_v
\end{equation}
along the two line segments of $\partial \mathcal D$ adjacent to~$\bm p_v$, for some constant~$d_v$. Then the enclosed region~$\mathcal D$ is an astroidal domain~$\mathcal D_c$ with
\begin{equation}
c(e)=\frac{d_{v_+}-d_{v_-}}{|e|_{\mathbb{Z}}}
\end{equation}
for~$e=[v_-,v_+]\in E(N)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

