{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.457817+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_64c88188539357c4708ea0aba8ee8240e08645af27ed026e8abcc4e1709c5940","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:charprop of arXiv:2411.16572, 'Optimal decay of eigenvector overlap for non-Hermitian random matrices'] Fix $\Lambda_0$ with $\lVert \Lambda_0\rVert\lesssim 1$, and let $\Lambda_t$ be the solution of (equation eq:charflowmat of the paper) with initial condition $\Lambda_0$. Define $M_t:=M^{z_t}(\mathrm{i}\eta_t)$ and $\rho_t:=\pi^{-1}|\mathrm{Im}\, m_t|$, then for any $0\le s\le t\le T^*(\eta_0,z_0)$ we have
	\begin{equation}

		z_t= e^{-(t-s)/2}z_s, \qquad M_t=e^{(t-s)/2}M_s, \qquad \frac{|\eta_t|}{\rho_t}=e^{s-t}\frac{|\eta_s|}{\rho_s}-\pi(1-e^{s-t}).
	\end{equation}
	Additionally, the maps $t\mapsto |\eta_t|$, $t\mapsto |\eta_t|\rho_t$, and $t\mapsto|\eta_t|/\rho_t$ are strictly decreasing, and for any $\alpha>1$ there is a constant $C_\alpha$ such that
	\begin{equation}

		\int_s^t \frac{1}{|\eta_r|^\alpha}\,\mathrm{d} t\le \frac{C_\alpha}{|\eta_t|^{\alpha-1}\rho_t}, \qquad\quad \int_s^t \frac{\rho_r}{|\eta_r|}\,\mathrm{d} r=\frac{1}{\pi}\left[\log\left(\frac{\eta_s}{\eta_t}\right)-\frac{t-s}{2}\right].
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

