  \item Any path $\xi\subseteq B_{\delta_2 r}(V_r(z))$ from $B_{\delta_2 r}(I_v)$ to $B_{\delta_2 r}( (\mathcal{M}\setminus S_v)\cup S_u\cup \mathcal{A}_{u_+}\cup \mathcal{A}_{u_-})$ must necessarily intersect $B_{\delta_2 r}(\mathcal{A}_{v_-}\cup \mathcal{A}_{v_+})$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.545135+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6575b6048f377c592c686655cd725387cf8c4de521dfe4588b03631c88de0325","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: Non-extremality of mu_A of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] Consider a $\mathbb{Z}_q$-valued nearest-neighbor clock model with Hamiltonian given in (equation def: Hamiltonian of the paper) and a potential defined in (equation eq: Potential ferr n.n. model of the paper) fulfilling the $u,U,d$-bounds in (equation eq: u,U-bounds of the paper). Furthermore, let $A\subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_q$ with $|A|\geq 2$ and $\beta$ be large enough. Then, the Edwards-Anderson parameter, defined in (equation def: Edwards-Anderson parameter of the paper), corresponding to the $A$-localized state $\mu_A$ for this model, see Proposition (prop: Bounds A-loc. states of the paper), is bounded as follows 
    \begin{equation}
        q^{\mu_A}_{\text{EA}}\geq \frac{1}{2q}\bigg((1-\epsilon_1(\beta))^2(1-2|A|\epsilon_2(\beta))-\frac{|A|-1}{|A|}\bigg)
    \end{equation}
    where $\epsilon_1(\beta)\downarrow 0$ and $\epsilon_2(\beta)\downarrow 0$ as $\beta\uparrow \infty$ and the quantities are given in Lemma (lem: Key Lemma of the paper). In particular, we obtain 
    \begin{equation*}
        q^{\mu_A}_{\text{EA}}>0
    \end{equation*}
    for large enough $\beta$ and thus the non-extremality of $\mu_A$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.049608+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_657711e3004e509c1b0825430409ddf8d18567bc29f9915bc8ca9b3b5e780383","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mylem 8 (Corollary 1.4 in \cite{RV_imrn}) of arXiv:2607.00495, 'Rank deficiency of Bernoulli random matrices for growing corank'] Consider a random vector $X = (X_1,...,X_n)$ where $X_i$ are real-valued independent random variables. Let $t, b \ge  0$ be such that
