{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.330353+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_658f56332a0504b9f2052dc4f76fea57e08a8891f1029e1ba9d682a1d396ba5c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\xi,\Xi)$ be the underlying MAP of $Z$ from (\ref{item_a}) with respect to $\|\cdot\|_1$. The jump kernel of $(\xi,\Xi)$ is given by
	\begin{equation*}
		L(\boldsymbol{\theta},d\boldsymbol{\phi},dy)=\sum_{j=1}^d \Big(c_1^{(j)}\mathbbm{1}_{(0,\infty)}(y)+c_2^{(j)}\mathbbm{1}_{\big(\log(1-\theta^{(j)}),0\big)}(y)\Big)\delta_{\boldsymbol{v}_{j,y}^{\boldsymbol{\theta}}}(d\boldsymbol{\phi})\frac{{\rm e}^y}{|{\rm e}^y-1|^{1+\alpha}}dy,	,
	\end{equation*}
for $\boldsymbol{\theta}=(\theta^{(1)},\ldots,\theta^{(d)})\in\mathcal{S}^{d,+}_{1}$, $(\boldsymbol{\phi},y)\in\mathcal{S}^{d,+}_{1}\times\mathbb{R}$, where the vector $\boldsymbol{v}^{\boldsymbol{\theta}}_{j,y}$ is as defined in (\ref{frequent_vector}), and $\delta_{\boldsymbol{x}}$, for any $\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^d$, denotes the classic Dirac measure on $\mathbb{R}^d.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

