{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.351827+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_65f30b351ed44439a612c5b68bc3e722bbb3e404e21cc19d4afa4a0013bb8609","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2607.15903, 'Uniqueness for nonlinear Fokker-Planck equations with general diffusion terms and their as'] \rm (mild solution)
  A continuous function $u: [0, \infty) \rightarrow L^{1}$ is called a mild solution to NFPE (\ref{1}) if, for each $T>0$,
\begin{equation}
    u(t)=\displaystyle\lim_{h \rightarrow 0}u_{h}(t) \ \text{in} \ L^{1}, \quad \text{uniformly\; on} \; [0,T], \notag    
\end{equation}
where $u_{h}:[0,T] \rightarrow L^{1}$ is the step function
\begin{equation}
    u_{h}(t)=u_{h}^{k}, \ \forall t \in [kh,(k+1)h), k=0,1, \cdots , N_{h}=[\frac{T}{h}],\notag
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    u_{h}^{k+1}+hA(u_{h}^{k+1})=u_{h}^{k}, \ \forall k=0,1, \cdots ,N_{h}; \ u_{h}^{0}=u_{0}. \notag
\end{equation}
Here, $A$ is the operator defined as follows
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    &A(u)=-\sum\limits_{i,j=1}^{d} D^{2}_{ij}(a_{ij}(x)\beta (x,u))+ \text{div}(b(x,u)u),\ \forall u \in D(A), \\&
    D(A)=\left\{u \in L^{1}; A(u) \in L^{1}\right\},\notag
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.613731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_65f58fb0c7bdec46fe792e417481b7a742c709c447ea64f906ff7eaec1acf6bb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop-Rotation of arXiv:2512.13200, 'Martingales on a Euclidean manifold with a boundary and reflected BSDEs in non-convex doma'] {Let Assumption (ass:3 of the paper) hold.}
    Then, for any geodesic $\gamma$, its velocity function $\dot \gamma$ {(which is well-defined for every $t$)} is $\frac{|\gamma_0-\gamma_1|^2}{R_0}$-Lipschitz and hence absolutely continuous on $[0,1]$, and $|\ddot\gamma| \leqslant \frac{|\gamma_0-\gamma_1|^2}{R_0}$ where $R_0$ is defined in (equation ineq:extsphere of the paper).

    In addition, for any $(x,y) \in \bar{\mathcal{D}}\times \bar{\mathcal{D}}$, we have $|\theta(x,y)| \leqslant \frac{|x-y|}{R_0}$, and there exists $C>0$ such that, for all $(x,y) \in \bar{\mathcal{D}}\times \bar{\mathcal{D}}$,
     \begin{equation}

     \| I- R(\theta(x,y))\| \leqslant C|x-y|
     \end{equation}
     and
     \begin{equation}

      \gamma_t^{x,y} = \gamma_s^{x,y} +\overrightarrow{xy}(t-s) + O(|y-x|^2), \quad \forall \, 0 \leqslant s \leqslant t \leqslant 1,
     \end{equation}
    where $O()$ is uniform in $(s,t)$. In particular, the above estimate implies that 
     \begin{equation}

     \overrightarrow{xy}=y-x + O(|y-x|^2),
     \end{equation}
     {uniformly in $(x,y)\in \bar\mathcal{D}\times\bar\mathcal{D}$.}

     Finally, the following Taylor expansion holds:
     \begin{equation}

        \gamma_t^{x,y} = x + \overrightarrow{xy}t + \langle \gamma_t^{x,y}-x,v \rangle v + O(t^3),
     \end{equation}
     where $v$ is a unit vector orthogonal to $\overrightarrow{xy}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

