{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.477338+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_660b97c3a3db46a73ca86a25759ff0816ee3ede1a8cf408b8455b210342322fc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] Let $\mathbb{N}:=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$ and $\mathbb{N}_0:=\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}$. 
The convex set $K$ is 
\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\emph{linearly compact} 
[kendall62,klee63-compact] if $\ell\cap K$ is a compact line 
segment (possibly degenerated to a singleton) for every line 
$\ell$ in $V$ that meets $K$.

\item 
\emph{$k$-neighborly}\footnote{

The term \emph{$k$-neighborly} extends its homonym in the theory of 
polytopes [gruenbaum03,sturmfels88,ziegler95].}

if the convex hull of any $k$ or less extreme points of 
$K$ is a face of $K$, $k\in\mathbb{N}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:27.007963+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_660d15f42056dc4d87bfb377de184dbc5f68e033d87435f9506b501ab2b374a3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thmx leC of arXiv:2505.13382, 'The localization transition for the directed polymer in a random environment is smooth'] There exists positive \textbf{continous} functions $\beta\mapsto m(\beta)$ and $\beta \mapsto M(\beta)$ such that for every $\beta>0$ we have almost surely
 \begin{equation}
    -m(\beta) \mathfrak{f}(\beta) \le  \liminf_{n\to \infty} \mathrm{EP}^{\beta,\omega}_n \le \limsup_{n\to \infty} \mathrm{EP
    }^{\beta,\omega}_n \le -M(\beta) \mathfrak{f}(\beta).
\end{equation}
If the environment is standard Gaussian ($\omega_{1,0}\sim {\ensuremath{\mathcal N}} (0,1)$)  then we have
\begin{equation}
 -\mathfrak{f}'(\beta+)\le  \beta \liminf_{n\to \infty} {\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} \left[ \mathrm{OV}_n^{\beta,\omega} \right]\le \beta \limsup_{n\to \infty} {\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} \left[ \mathrm{OV}^{\beta,\omega}_n \right]\le- \mathfrak{f}'(\beta-)
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{f}'(\beta-)$ and $\mathfrak{f}'(\beta+)$ denote the left and right derivative of $\mathfrak{f}$. Since $\mathfrak{f}(\beta)+\lambda(\beta)$ is convex  they exist for all $\beta$ and coincide for all except possibly countably many $\beta$s. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:23:58.617976+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_660d3a0481addde388e849915cde66a495bf7b8cfa0fbd98d7ff1fa99552b55d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 156 (Covariant divergence identity for spacetime vectorfields) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] Let $\mathcal{J}$ be a spacetime vectorfield.
