exists and is
\begin{equation*}
  \boxed{
  \mathfrak M_{g,\eta}(\boldsymbol k)
  =2C_2^m
  \sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor g\rfloor}
  \binom{i}{\eta-(2g-2i)}
  \mathbf B_{m-1+i,\,2g-2i}^{\mathrm{conn}}
  \left[\frac{\mu_+\mathfrak c_2}{2}\boldsymbol k\right],
  }
\end{equation*}
where the binomial coefficient is zero unless its lower argument belongs to
$\{0,\ldots,i\}$.  Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
  \mathfrak M_{g,\eta}(\boldsymbol k)
  =[b^\eta]\operatorname{Hom}_{\,3g-3+\frac32m}
  \left\{
    2(1+b)^{1-m}C_2^m
    \mathbf L_b^{\mathrm{conn}}
    \left[\frac{\mu_+\mathfrak c_2}{2}\boldsymbol k\right]
  \right\}.

\end{equation}
For fixed $(g,\eta,m)$, both formulas contain only finitely many nonnegative
Brownian integrals.","status":"unverified"}

