 where the supremum ranges over $x \in [\max(\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}},\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}}),\min(\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}},\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}})]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.434826+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66bdae9b3a815da78914d6c392d8109e59e1790f2fded64c9e20517a74aabf9e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm:delocheightanealedgauss of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $V : \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}} \to \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ be an annealed Gaussian potential. Then there exists an inverse temperature $\beta_V \in (0 , \infty)$ (depending on $V$) and a constant $c_0 > 0$ (universal) such that, for any $\beta \geq \beta_V$ and any $L \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}^*$,
