{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.690998+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66e32ef629fe9e368b302159e3b3a73ef07cffb9cde38c4c68b68260a338cd06","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2609.02193, 'State-law dynamics of McKean-Vlasov stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on $\mathbb R^'] For every $\epsilon\in[0,\epsilon_0]$, $T>0$ and $u_0\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal F_0;H)$, equation~\eqref{Eq1} has a unique variational solution $u$ on $[0,T]$. The solutions obtained on different finite intervals are consistent and therefore define a global solution on $[0,\infty)$. Moreover, $t\mapsto\mathcal L u(t)$ belongs to $C([0,\infty);P)$ and
	\begin{align}
		&\mathbb E\sup_{0\leq r\leq T}\|u(r)\|^2
		+\mathbb E\int_0^T
		\big(\|u(r)\|_V^2+\|u(r)\|_p^p\big)dr
		\leq
		C_T\big(1+\mathbb E\|u_0\|^2\big),
	\end{align}
	where $C_T>0$ depends only on $T$ and the structural data and is independent of $\epsilon\in[0,\epsilon_0]$ and $u_0$. If $u_1,u_2$ are two solutions driven by the same Brownian motion with initial data $u_{0,1},u_{0,2}\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal F_0;H)$, then
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb E\|u_1(t)-u_2(t)\|^2
		\leq
		e^{(L_G-\bar\gamma)t}\mathbb E\|u_{0,1}-u_{0,2}\|^2,
		\qquad t\geq0.
	\end{align}
	Consequently, the law of the solution is determined by the initial law $\mathcal L u_0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.714238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66e6fef4def5dbb41acf377ed884b8051a3576662c367127f11243d283e247fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop rest3 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] If $\gamma$ is chosen small enough, then, given any $0 < \eta' < \eta < \frac{1}{2}$ small enough \mbox{(depending only on $\gamma$),} for any fixed $u \geq 0$ we have
