{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.813134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66eeac43ab731bb9d0f98e1957ebbe4546f0a5e408777a1667cf61f214ead9db","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:geodesics of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] For $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, a \emph{geodesic}  from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$ in the directed landscape
    is a continuous function $\Pi:[s,t]\to \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Pi(s)=x,\Pi(t)=y$, and
    \begin{align}
        \mathcal{L}(s,x;t,y) = \int_s^t d\mathcal{L}\circ \Pi.
    \end{align}
    Equivalently, $\Pi$ is a geodesic if for all $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and all $s=s_0<s_1<\cdots<s_k=t$,
    \begin{align}
        \int_s^t d\mathcal{L}\circ \Pi
        =\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \mathcal{L}(s_i,\Pi(s_i); s_{i+1}, \Pi(s_{i+1})).
    \end{align}
    By [dov], it holds almost surely that for every $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, there exists a \emph{rightmost} geodesic $\Pi_{(s,x),(t,y)}$ from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$, 
    in the sense that $\Pi_{(s,x),(t,y)} \geqslant \Pi'$ pointwise for any other geodesic $\Pi'$ from $(s,x)$ to $(t,y)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.962397+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66efa479471ba8671a3c3a9290c1b56b13a095bd35adb2966c662bda854e180c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 13 (Graphical computation) of arXiv:2605.28776, 'Limiting distributions of triangle counts in linear preferential attachment models'] For $m\ge 2$ and $\delta>-m$ let $\beta:=(m+\delta)/(2m+\delta)$.
 Let $H=([k],E)$ be a finite connected (undirected) multigraph that does not contain self-loops but might contain multi-edges. Then the expectation of the $H$-subgraph count in $\pam(m,\delta)$ is given by
 \begin{align}
 &\E \sum_{1\le i_1< i_2< \cdots< i_k\le n} \prod_{(a,b)\in E\atop a>b}\theta_{i_b i_a}\cong 
 F_k\biggl(d_1-\beta \ell_1,d_2-\beta (\ell_2-\ell_1),\ldots, d_{k}-\beta(\ell_{k}-\ell_{k-1})\biggr),
 \end{align}
 where
 \begin{itemize}
 \item $d_j$ is the in-degree of vertex $j$ (\ie~the number of edges $(j,k)\in E$ with $k>j$),
 \item $\ell_j$ is the number of edges $(u,v)\in E$ such that $v\le j< u$,
 \item $F_k(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_k):=
 \sum_{1\le i_1< i_2< \cdots< i_k\le n} 
 \left(\prod_{j=1}^k i_j^{a_j}\right)^{-1}$.
 \end{itemize}

 Moreover, the function $F_k$ satisfies
 \begin{align*}
 F_k(&a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_k) \\
 &\cong F_{t-1}(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_{t-1})\cdot
 \begin{cases}
 n^{k-t+1-(a_t+a_{t+1}+\cdots+a_k)} & \text{ if } a_t+a_{t+1}+\cdots+a_{k}< k-t+1\\
 \log n &\text{ if } a_t+a_{t+1}+\cdots+a_{k}= k-t+1
 \end{cases},
 \end{align*}
 where $t:=\max\{i\in [k]\mid a_i+a_{i+1}+\cdots+a_{k}\le k-i+1\}$ and 
 \begin{align*}
 F_k(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_k) 
 \cong 1 \text{ if no such $t$ exists.}
 \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.425633+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66f5c656c9327fa00dc80fdcef04219022efb20f28448472a0bb4cdf28051d59","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma CountingSimpleSets of arXiv:2605.18744, 'Lattice random-field Widom--Rowlinson models'] We have the following contour properties for $d\ge 2$ and finite $\Lambda\in  \mathfrak{A}_2$. 
