{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.283283+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67235c0e05c448a58174daa63d71c77aa15f10a8fb247afd13420c28ea58dc5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{H1}--\textbf{H3} and \textbf{A4}. As \(\epsilon\to 0\), \(x^\epsilon\) weakly converges to \(\bar x\), that is 
\begin{equation}
\dd \bar z_t = D\bar g(\bar x_t) \bar z_t \dd t+Df(\bar x_t) \bar z_t \dd B^H_t+ \bar V^{\frac{1}{2}}(\bar x_t)\dd \hat W_t,\ \bar x_0 = x,
\end{equation}
where \(\bar x\) satisfies the ODE \eqref{averaged-system}, and \(V(x,y):=(D_y \Phi \sigma)(D_y \Phi \sigma)^{*}(x,y)\) with $\bar V^{\frac{1}{2}}(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}} V^{\frac{1}{2}}(x,y)\mu^{x}(dy)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.338082+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6725284ec95c07e4c7b3d6a90fce7b0c5865cbd19eed05e286ac5005cdf311c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 of arXiv:2512.05687, '$L^p$-Boltzmann-Gibbs principle \\ via Littlewood-Paley-Stein inequality'] Assume
\begin{align} 
V\in C^2(\R) \quad \text{and} \quad 0<c_-\le V''\le c_+<\infty,
\end{align}
for some positive constants $c_+$ and $c_-$.
Let $\ell_0 \ge 1$, $p\ge 4$, and let $f$ be a local $L^{p'}(\mu_{u_0})$ function 
on $\mathcal{X}_N=\R^N$ for some $p'>p$
depending only on $\eta_{\La_{\ell_0}}=(\eta(x))_{x\in \La_{\ell_0}}$
such that $ \tilde{f}(u_0)  =0$ and 
$\tilde{f}' (u_0)=0$.  Then, there exists a constant $C=C(u_0,\ell_0)>0$, 
which is uniform in $N, \ell, \ga\in \R$, such that, for $T>0$, 
$\ell\geq \ell_0$ and a function $h$ on $[0,T]\times \T_N$, we have
\begin{align}  
& E_{\mu_{u_0}}\bigg[ \; \sup_{0\leq t\leq T}\Big| \int_0^t ds \; \sum_{x\in\T_N} 
   h(s,x)  r_s^{N,\ell;2}(x)  \Big|^p\; \bigg]\\
& \hskip 3mm \notag
    \le  C \Big( T^{(p-2)/2} N^{-p/2}\ell^{p/2} +T^{p-1} N^p \ell^{-3p/2}\Big)
        \|f\|^p_{L^{p'}(\mu_{u_0})} \int_0^T \frac{1}{N}\sum_{x\in{\T_N}}|h(t,x)|^p dt,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}  
r_s^{N,\ell;2}(x)=f(\tau_x \eta^{N}_s)  - \frac{1}{2} \tilde{f}''(u_0)
\Big\{\Big(\big(\eta^{N}_s\big)^{(\ell)}(x)-u_0\Big)^2    
  -\frac{{\rm var}(u_0)}{2\ell+1} \Big\}.
\end{align}
and 
${\rm var}(u)=u'(\la(u)) = E^{\mu_u}[(\eta(0)-u)^2]$ is the variance of $\mu_u$;
see \eqref{eq:u-deri}.
Recall \eqref{eq:eta-ell-B} for $\eta^{(\ell)}(x)$ and we take $\eta_s^N$ 
for $\eta$.
On the other hand, when only $\tilde{f}(u_0)=0$ is known, we have
\begin{align}  
& E_{\mu_{u_0}}\bigg[\; \sup_{0\leq t\leq T}\Big| \int_0^t  ds \; \sum_{x\in{\T_N}} 
  \; h(s,x) r_s^{N,\ell;1}(x)\Big|^p\; \bigg]\\
&\hskip 3mm \notag
  \le  C \Big( T^{(p-2)/2} N^{-p/2}\ell^{p} +T^{p-1} N^p \ell^{-p}\Big)
        \|f\|^p_{L^{p'}(\mu_{u_0})} \int_0^T \frac{1}{N}\sum_{x\in{\T_N}}|h(t,x)|^p dt,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}  
r_s^{N,\ell;1}(x) = f(\tau_x \eta^{N}_s) 
  - \tilde{f}'(u_0)\Big\{\big(\eta^{N}_s\big)^{(\ell)}(x) - u_0\Big\}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:29:21.940706+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6726545195ae6264caff93625dce7f8b7eecb066fe8df45e993d62c068ce6125","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 (Integrated slow variation) of arXiv:2608.01674, '\textbf{Limit Theorems for Tempered Linear Processes with Innovations in the Domain of Att'] Under \cref{ass:filter}, $L$ is slowly varying,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\ell(x)}{L(x)}\longrightarrow0, ~~~~\text{as} ~~x\to\infty,

\end{equation}
and, for every $c>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{L(cx)-L(x)}{\ell(x)}\longrightarrow\log c.

\end{equation}
More generally, if $0<a<b<\infty$ and $f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$,
then as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{\ell(N)}
 \sum_{aN<j\le bN}\frac{\ell(j)}{j}f(j/N)
 \longrightarrow
 \int_a^b\frac{f(u)}{u}\dd u.

\end{equation}
The same conclusion holds with $a=0$ whenever
$f(u)=O(u^\rho)$ as $u\downarrow0$ for some $\rho>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.480422+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6727c5dc76291a564005a1e3f7127bdf883eaafed02f36a82a73f6213c8715fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 3 (Corollary \ref{Cor psi Vandermonde}) of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Let $n\geq 1$
