{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.159400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67331513bdea13037a7e5b95a5b51ca560e3aec82b748970b33fa2092b95cf17","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2607.02406, 'On the range of competing random walks'] If $X^1,\dots,X^N$ are in the domain of attraction of a $\beta$-stable process $U$ with scale function $b(n)$ and that $d/\beta\ge3/2$, then there is an explicit constant $\sigma>0$ such that
