{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.335448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_673aa67036c42d22d6814abfab5370a36762be53f889a98df5806ea7c0aa0a55","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 (Exploration of the right part) of arXiv:2512.05466, 'Stochastic Flows and Marked Stable Processes'] Almost surely: for every  $t\ge 0$ and every spindle $\cT$,  setting $(a,r)$ and $(c,z)$ for its bottom and top points,  if $t> t_{\cT}$ and $\varrho_t\in \cT$, then there exist $t_0<t<t_1$ and $r_0\in (X_t,z)$ such that:

    (i)  $\varrho_{t_0}=\varrho_{t_1}=(\cB_{r,{r_0}}(a),r_0)$;

    (ii) by time $t$, $\varrho$ has visited all points $((\mathcal{B}_{r,x}(a), x)$ for $x \in [r_0, z]$, but has not visited any points $(\mathcal{B}_{r,x}(a), x)$ for $x \in (r, r_0)$; 

