{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.644288+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_674f19850e9c223138fd0182ba0093b027a8cdb6bc2012f5b373d80a422a75b6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2605.13518, 'The inertial It\^{o} drift and its applications to particle collision'] For every $\alpha \in\,(0,\infty)$ and $x \in\,\mathbb{R}^d$, system \eqref{smf4} has a unique invariant measure  given by the centered Gaussian measure $\mu_{\alpha,x}$ having covariance matrix
\begin{equation}Q_{\alpha}(x)=\begin{pmatrix}
	\ds{N_\alpha(x) } & L_{\alpha}(x)\\
	&\\
	L^\star_{\alpha}(x)  & \ds{M}
\end{pmatrix},\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matrix
\begin{equation}

M:=\int_0^{+\infty} e^{-A s}BB^\star e^{-As}\,ds,	
\end{equation}
and for every $\alpha \in\,(0,+\infty)$ and $x \in\,\mathbb{R}^d$, $L_\alpha(x)$ and $N_\alpha(x)$ are the matrices defined by 
\begin{equation}

L_{\alpha}(x):=\frac 1\alpha \int_0^\infty e^{-\frac 1\alpha \gamma(x)t}\sigma(x)Me^{-A^\star t}\,dt,	
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

N_\alpha(x):=\int _0^\infty e^{-\gamma(x) t}\,[L_{\alpha}(x)\, \sigma^\star(x)+\sigma(x) L^\star_{\alpha}(x)]\,e^{-\gamma^\star(x)t}\,dt.	
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.424288+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_674f9fb3680d9d71b19c76cacc1ed3b1cb5b103e536765cfcdcb7a33fc272d59","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:J-edge of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[lemma]{lem:GOE-mean-LDP}
Let \(\rho_N(x)\,\mathrm d x\) be the normalized expected empirical measure of the \(N\)-dimensional GOE in the normalization used in [ABAC].  These probability measures satisfy a large-deviation principle at speed \(N\) with good rate function
\begin{equation}
 J_{\rm edge}(x)=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&\left\lvertx\right\rvert\le\sqrt2,\\[1mm]
 \displaystyle\int_{\sqrt2}^{\left\lvertx\right\rvert}\sqrt{z^2-2}\,\mathrm d z,
 &\left\lvertx\right\rvert>\sqrt2.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
Consequently, if \(W\) is continuous and \(W(x)\le C-c_0x^2\) for some \(c_0>0\), then
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\log
 \int_\mathbb R e^{NW(x)}\rho_N(x)\,\mathrm d x
 =\sup_{x\in\mathbb R}\{W(x)-J_{\rm edge}(x)\}.
\end{equation}
For every compact interval \(K\) with nonempty interior, the same conclusion holds with both the integral and supremum restricted to \(K\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.912651+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6750e4dfd9496ec6fdfd720929cea8bce6e69f6046612278ae9075687a936e95","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2608.20613, 'Random walks on wreath products \\and spectral gaps for coloured interchange processes'] Let $\Lambda=(\lambda^\gamma)_{\gamma\in\Gamma}$ be a $G$-multipartition of $n$.  Then
\[
\dim V_\Lambda^{\Sn_n}
=
\prod_{\gamma\in\Gamma}
\dim \Schur_{\lambda^\gamma}(\C^{d_\gamma}).
\]
Moreover, $V_\Lambda$ occurs in $\mathcal P_n(G)$ if and only if $\len(\lambda^\gamma)\leqslant d_\gamma$ for every $\gamma\in\Gamma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.912592+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6750ff629535b1753310a09b9dcdb7f055b79894b24147546c85943f3b81f1b6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main theorem of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Suppose that $\{f_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of $\mathcal{F}$-measurable functions satisfying the following conditions:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item there exists $\lambda >0$ such that $-\lambda n\le f_n(\omega)\le \lambda n$ for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$, and $\omega\in\Omega$;
		\medskip
		\item for each $k,n\in\mathbb{N}$, $f_{n+k}\le f_n+f_k\circ T^n$, $V$-a.s.
	\end{longlist} 
	Then there exists $f^*\in B(\Omega,\mathcal{I})$ such that 
	\[\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}f_n(\omega)=f^*(\omega)\text{ for }V\text{-a.s. }\omega\in\Omega.\]

	If, in addition, $V$ is ergodic, then $f^*$ is a constant $V$-a.s.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.656281+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67513031da3bb8c20b466ee84de899bdea8e7a7d9a8dab8c6aa6316c96e76acc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 16 of arXiv:2609.03726, 'Inverse Scattering for Single Photons in Quantum Optics'] Let $k_\rho$ be the threshold from Lemma \ref{lem_LAP} with $l=[n/2]+1$ and $\delta=3/4$. Let $k>k_\rho$ and let $\alpha,\theta\in\mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ satisfy $\theta\neq\alpha$.
If $\mathcal{I}_{\rho_1}$ and $\mathcal{I}_{\rho_2}$ are equivalent in the sense of \eqref{eq:equivalence_relation}, then
\begin{equation}
    A_k^{\rho_1}(\theta,\alpha) = A_k^{\rho_2}(\theta,\alpha),
\end{equation}
where $A_k^{\rho_j}(\theta,\alpha)$ denotes the scattering amplitude associated with $\rho_j$, $j=1,2$.

