{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.320641+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67743848e40e1205a6645df6603cc53c337986635c6e61d6b7a8d6bfa5fab017","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 11 (Poisson equation) of arXiv:2506.21087, 'Quasi-Stationary Distributions of Interacting Dynamical Systems and their approximation'] Assume \ref{condpzero}, \ref{condpun} and \ref{condpdeux}. For any $\mu\in{\cal P}({\MM})$, for any measurable function  $f:{\MM}\rightarrow\ER$, the Poisson equation \eqref{Poissonequa} admits a unique solution denoted $Q_\mu f$ and
defined by
\begin{equation}

 Q_\mu f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{+\infty} \left(\kmu^n f(x) -\Pi_\mu (f)\right).
\end{equation}
Furthermore, there exists a constant $C$ such that,
\begin{itemize}

\item[(a)]  For every $\mu,\alpha \in {\cal P}(\MM)$,  
