{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.302451+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67b71ed13918c61b774c974ce42fa028b55fee1d5caba41a05b7bd6885a451b2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:overlap of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] The following holds on a single event of full probability. Let $(p_n;q_n) = (x_n,s_n;y_n,u_n) \in \mathbb{R}_{{\scaleobj{0.7}{\uparrow}}}^4 \to (p;q) = (x,s;y,u) \in \mathbb{R}_{{\scaleobj{0.7}{\uparrow}}}^4$, and let $g_n$ be any sequence of geodesics from $p_n$ to $q_n$. Suppose that either
\begin{enumerate} [label=\rm(\roman{*}), ref=\rm(\roman{*})]  \itemsep=3pt
    \item  For all $n$, $(x_n,s_n;y_n,u_n) \in \mathbb{R}_{{\scaleobj{0.7}{\uparrow}}}^4 \cap \mathbb{Q}^4$, or
    \item  There is a unique geodesic $g$ from $p$ to $q$.
