{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.331608+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67e20887a126672caa79d447f64ac3a59e2c06b5fb6fa43dbff9791f1c0ba453","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorema them:critere_id_intro of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Let $X = (X_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ be a Gaussian process with continuous covariance function $K : (s,t) \mapsto \mathbb{E}((X_s-\mathbb{E}(X_s))(X_t- \mathbb{E}(X_t)))$ and $\alpha^K : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}_+$ that satisfies, for every $s<t \in \mathbb{R}^2$,
			\begin{equation}
				\sup_{(v,h) \in [s,t] \times ]0,h^*]} \left|\alpha^K(v)- \frac{1}{h}\left(1 - \frac{K(v,v+h)}{\sqrt{K(v,v)}\sqrt{K(v+h,v+h)}} \right) \right| \xrightarrow[h^* \to 0^+]{} 0.
			\end{equation} 
			Then, any Gaussian process $(Y_t)_{t \in \mathbb{R}}$ that satisfies, for every $s<t \in \mathbb{R}^2$, 
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb{E}(Y_t) = \mathbb{E}(X_t) 
			\end{equation}
			and
			\begin{equation}
				\mathbb{E}((Y_s-\mathbb{E}(Y_s))(Y_t-\mathbb{E}(Y_t))) = \sqrt{K(s,s)}\sqrt{K(t,t)} \exp\left(-\int_s^t \alpha^K(u) du \right),
			\end{equation}is a strong local Markov transform of $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

