{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.552721+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67f0870d82054a7e2319647e32071516c29ab58f21f1a37809919ea0b7c3c0dd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 (Convergence in probability and pointwise in time bounds) of arXiv:2604.02129, 'Realisation of constraints in underdamped Langevin dynamics'] Let $(Q_t,P_t)$ and $(q_t,p_t)$ be as above. For any $t \geq 0$ we have  
    \begin{align*}

        &\mathbb{E} \biggl[  \left|\begin{pmatrix}
       q_t - Q^{2}_t \\ p_t-P^{2}_t  
        \end{pmatrix} \right|^2 \biggr] \leq 
        \begin{cases}
        C_3 e^{C_4 t} \Bigl(
          t |Q_0^{1}|^2 + \eps t |P_0^{1}|^2 + \eps t^2 \Bigr) \ \  &\text{if $\alpha=0$}\\
          C_5  \left( \eps t + |Q^1_0|^2 + \eps |P^1_0|^2 \right) &\text{if $\alpha>0$},
          \end{cases}
\end{align*}
Additionally if $\alpha>0$, we have the time independent $L^1$-estimate
\begin{equation*}
    \mathbb{E} \biggl[ \biggl|\begin{pmatrix}
       q_t - Q^{2}_t \\ p_t-P^{2}_t  
    \end{pmatrix} \biggr| \biggr] \leq
    C_6  \Bigl(\sqrt{\eps} + \sqrt{\eps}  \bigl|P_0^{1}\bigr|  +\bigl|Q_0^{1}\bigr|\Bigr)
\end{equation*}
where $C_i >0, i\in\left\{1,\ldots,6 \right\}$ are independent of $t$ and $\eps$.
In particular, if $Q^1_0=0$, then for any $t\geq 0$
\begin{align*}
     Q^1_t \xrightarrow{\eps\to 0} 0 \text{ in probability}, 
\end{align*}
and in $C([0,\infty],\R^{2(d-k)})$ we have 
\begin{align}
     (Q^2,P^2) \xrightarrow{\eps\to 0} (q,p) \text{ in probability}.  
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.316880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67f205023f8293080f2bde264ea20fcca8eb32962fc00560bea6297e563ee560","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Malianx of arXiv:2411.06131, 'Non-Markovian dynamics: the memory-dependent probability density evolution equations'] If $ A_t, B_t $ and $ C_t $ are measurable and essentially bounded deterministic functions in $ t $, then, Eq. ((mSDEline of the paper)) admits a unique solution. Moreover, let $X_t$ be the solution of ((mSDEline of the paper)), then
