{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731556+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6802c425b2037647e8eb0e385987da67188c27b2da6b2be5dd1eb3d80da1456d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Fix $T > 0$. Let $h_\eps$ denote the BPHZ solution of the KPZ equation with initial data $\psi \in C^\vartheta(\mathbb{T})$ for $\vartheta \in (0,1/2)$ driven by $\xi_\eps = \xi \ast \rho^\eps$ where $\xi$ is space-time white noise and $\rho$ is a non-negative symmetric standard mollifier. Then for any $\kappa > 0$
	\begin{align*}
		\limsup_{M \to \infty} \sup_{\eps > 0} \mathbb{P} ( \eps^{-1/2} \| h_\eps - h \|_{C^{-\kappa}([0,T] \times \mathbb{T})} > M) = 0.
	\end{align*}
	Furthermore, the rate $\eps^{1/2}$ implied by the above statement is sharp since, writing $w_\eps = \eps^{-1/2} (h_\eps - h)$, we have that $(h_\eps, h, w_\eps)$ converges in law in $C^{1/2-\kappa}((0,T] \times \mathbb{T}) \times C^{1/2-\kappa}((0,T] \times \mathbb{T}) \times C^{-\kappa}((0,T] \times \mathbb{T})$ to $(h, h, w)$ where $w$ is the BPHZ solution of
	\begin{align*}
		(\partial_t - \partial_x^2) w = 2 \partial_x w \partial_x h +  c_1 \tilde{\xi}_1+c_2\tilde{\xi}_2
	\end{align*}
	with initial data $0$ where $c_1$, $c_2$ are strictly positive constants and $\tilde{\xi}_1$, $\tilde{\xi}_2$ are partially correlated\footnote{the precise covariance matrix is provided in Proposition~\ref{prop:new_noise_conv} below} space-time white noises  independent from $\xi$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.330461+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6802e7ac0c6f56e804a3f3fdaaf1d2bf949b2ea5cb826fdb5cd29e249e1fbcd6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:levelwise-smash-before-loc of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The levelwise smash product
    \begin{equation}
        {N_\textup{top}}(\textbf{\textup{Top}}_*^\textup{cof,$\flat$})\times{N_\textup{top}}({\textbf{\textup{OrthSp}}}^\flat)\to{N_\textup{top}}({\textbf{\textup{OrthSp}}}^\flat)
    \end{equation}    
    descends to $-\otimes-\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_*\times{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}\simeq \ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_*^\textup{cof}\times{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$, and this is the unique bifunctor preserving ${\vphantom{\textup{t}}\smash{\textup{Orb}}}$-colimits in the first variable and such that $S^0\otimes-$ is the identity.
    \begin{proof}
        Lemma~(lemma:G-global-spectra-tensoring of the paper) shows that the levelwise smash product descends accordingly and that each individual ${{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_{G,*}^\text{cof}\times{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}\to{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}$ preserves colimits in the first variable. It remains to show that for any injective $\alpha\colon G\to G'$, any cofibrant $G$-space $X$, and any $G'$-global spectrum $Y$ the Beck--Chevalley map $\textbf{\textup{L}}\alpha_!(X\otimes \alpha^*Y)\to (\textbf{\textup{L}}\alpha_!X)\otimes Y$ is invertible. In light of Lemma~(lemma:gl-sp-(co)ind-homotopical of the paper), we may equivalently show that the pointset level map $\alpha_!(X\smashp \alpha^*Y)\to(\alpha_!X)\smashp Y$ is a weak equivalence. A direct computation shows that the latter is even an isomorphism.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.707824+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6806aa73fa27977981b64646d2e8a156b0b904664aae036d4cb96ef9862e4d6b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:SlSm of arXiv:2604.10214, 'Large deviations for maximum local time of simple random walk in dimensions \texorpdfstrin'] Uniformly for \(1\le i<j\le K\) with \(j-i\ge2\), \(k\in I_i\), and \(m\in I_j\), we have
