{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.501663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6887ba7b5ede6130464503c67233dd2d0e34fd98381bfcd7d7de7d8fe08e2928","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:rigid-simple-nsc of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] Let   $ k\in \mathbb{N}$.
    Assume that one of the following holds:\begin{itemize}
\item (i) $ k = 0$
\item (ii) $    \mathsf s$ is invariant under rotations
\item (iii) $    \mathsf s$  is  {\it separable}, i.e. $    \mathsf s(u)=   \mathsf s_{1}(u_{1})\dots    \mathsf s_{d}(u_{d}),u\in \mathbb{R}^{d}$, for some measurable symmetric $    \mathsf s_{i}\geqslant 0.$

\end{itemize}
Then for $ R>0,$ $ \mathsf{M}$ is linearly $ k$-rigid on $ B_{ R}$ if for all $ \varepsilon >0$
\begin{align}

 \int_{B(0,\varepsilon )}\frac{ \|u\|^{ 2k}}{   \mathsf s(u)}du=\infty .
