{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.674363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6895930a75dbfff8fe963e1da5c13a8410e280e0d8b75ec24aebd52af31254d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Existence-r of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] Let $r\!\in [2, +\infty)$. Assume that  the distribution $P$ of $X$ satisfies the  moment assumption~(equation eq:Integrabilite of the paper), that  

$F$ is $C^2$ on $U$ with $\nabla^2F(x)$ (symmetric) positive definite for  every $x\!\in U$.
Assume that at every $\bar x \!\in\hat\partial U =  \partial   U$, 

\noindent either
\begin{equation} 
\nabla^2F \mbox{ can  be continuously extended at } \bar x \mbox{ and } \nabla^2F(\bar x) \mbox{ is (symmetric) positive definite}
\end{equation}
or \\
\indent the l.s.c. extensions $\phi_{_F}(\xi, \cdot)$  on $ \bar U^{\widehat{\mathbb{R}^d}}$ satisfy at $\bar x$ 
\begin{equation}

 \forall\, \xi \!\in U, \qquad \phi_{_F}(\xi, \bar x) =\displaystyle  \sup_{x\in U}\phi_{_F}(\xi,x).
\end{equation}
Finally,  if $U$ is unbounded, assume that $\bar x = \infty$ satisfies the above condition~(equation eq:Cond-rb2 of the paper).

\medskip
\noindent $(a)$ Then for every $n\ge1$ there exists an $n$-tuple $x^{(r,n)}= \big(x^{(r,n)}_1, \ldots, x^{(r,n)}_n\big) \!\in U^n$ which minimizes $G_{r,n}$ over $U^n$. Moreover, if  the support (in $U$) of the distribution $P$ has at least $n$ points then $x^{(r,n)}$ has pairwise distinct components and $P\big(\mathring C_i(x^{(r,n)})\big)>0$ for every $i\!\in \{1,\ldots,n\}$.

\smallskip
\noindent $(b)$ The distribution  $P$ assigns no mass to the   boundary of  any $\phi_{_F}$-Bregman--Voronoi partitions of $x^{(n)}$ i.e.
\[
P\left (\bigcup_{i=1}^n\partial C_i\big( x^{(r,n)})\right) = 0
\]
and $x^{(r,n)}$ satisfies the $(r,\phi_{_F})$-stationary (or $(r,\phi_{_F})$-master) equation
\begin{equation}
 x^{(r,n)}_i = \frac{ \int_{C_i(x^{(r,n)})}\xi \phi_{_F}( \xi,x^{(r,n)}_i )^{\frac r2-1}P(\mathrm{d}\xi)}{\int_{C_i(x^{(r,n)})}\phi_{_F}( \xi,x^{(r,n)}_i )^{\frac r2-1}P(\mathrm{d}\xi) } , \quad  i=1,\ldots,n.
\end{equation}

\noindent $(c)$ The sequence $\displaystyle G_{r,n}\big( x^{(r,n)}\big) = \min_{U^n} G_{r,n}$ decreases as long as it is not $0$ and converges to $0$ as $n$ goes to infinity. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.309658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6895c3473fad8f2c40521f6d70047529a574cea6dbc216846cd42bbffcf96447","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2609.02392, 'Large values of cubic and quartic Dirichlet $L$-functions of prime moduli'] With the notation as above and assuming the truth of GRH, we have, for large $X$ and any positive integer $c \in \mz$, 
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{\substack{(p,3)=1}} \ \sumstar_{\substack{\chi \shortmod{p} \\ \chi^3 = \chi_0}} (\log p)\chi(c) \Phi\Big(\frac{p}{X}\Big)=
\displaystyle \delta_{c=\text{cubic}} {\widehat \Phi}(1) X  + O( X^{1/2+\varepsilon}\log (c+2) ),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
