{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.312082+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68b7e056253c0c655739f9a953d5c173c5d0429e2ac9eaee3c581f1863f7a141","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem bhp of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Let $U$ be a domain in $\mathbb{R}^3$ and $K$ a compact set such that $K\subseteq U$. For $\mathbb{P}_{\vec x}$-almost every $\mathcal{W}$, there exists $C(U,K,\mathcal{W})\in (0,\infty)$ such that for all closed subset $\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{W}$, if $u$ and $v$ are bounded positive harmonic functions in $U\setminus \mathcal{A}$ that vanish continuously on regular points of $\mathcal{A}\cap U$, then
    \begin{equation}
        u(x_1)\,v(x_2)\leq C\,u(x_2)\,v(x_1) \quad \text{for all $x_1,x_2\in K\setminus \mathcal{A}$}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.747906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68b9aca4c1f1ee5b6f5356d1a59483d340db110cf5e32e5ef1dea9af17c0ee6b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:allmoments of arXiv:2608.28169, 'characteristic polynomial of self-normalized random matrices'] Consider integers $k_1\geqslant\cdots \geqslant k_r\geqslant 1$ and let $\Gamma(\cdot)$ denote the gamma function. 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] For any distribution of $X_{11}$, it holds $\beta_2=1/n$ and
\begin{equation*}
n^r  \beta_{2k_1,\ldots, {2k_r}}=O(1)\,, \qquad n\to\infty\,.
\end{equation*}
If additionally $k_1\geqslant 2$, then $n^r  \beta_{2k_1,\ldots, {2k_r}}=O(n\beta_4)$.
\item[(b)] If $\alpha\in [0,2)$ and ${\mathbb P}(|X_{11}|>x)=x^{-\alpha} L(x)$ for $x>0$, where $L$ is a slowly varying function at infinity, then it holds
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty} n \beta_{2k} = 
\frac{\Gamma(k-\alpha/2)}{\Gamma(1-\alpha/2) \Gamma(k)} = c_k^{(\alpha)} \,, \qquad k\geqslant 2\,.
\end{equation}
\item[(c)] If $X_{11}\in {\mathrm{DAN}}$, then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty} n \beta_{2k} = 
0 = c_k^{(2)} \,, \qquad k\geqslant 2\,.
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.359250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68bcc324d84b1a50127621c6a4b7fd71e639ee0fdc613253fe334bbb2c5517d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop of arXiv:2507.09421, 'Stability of randomly switching stochastic reaction networks with asymptotically linear tr'] In Example (ex:switching ergodic non-monomolecular of the paper), for any $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, the mixed process is exponentially ergodic as long as $\kappa$ is large enough.
