{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.060546+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69c012d6d36cc1570ad7e696074831c8901f9d65486572d3b479c5d205781047","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm LLN of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] Fix $a,b>0$ and $\ell> \bar{\mathcal{L}}(a,b)$. 
Let $D=\ell^2 - 2(a+b)\ell + (a-b)^2$ 
as in (equation eq:Ddefn of the paper) and define the function 
\begin{equation} 
    h(x,y)=  \frac12 \left[ (\ell +a-b)x + (\ell -a+b)y -  |x-y| \sqrt{D} \right].
\end{equation}
Then, for every $\epsilon>0$, 
\begin{equation} 
    \lim_{N\to \infty} \mathbb{P}\left[ \left| \frac{\mathcal{L}(xaN,ybN)}{N}- h(x,y)\right|>\epsilon \,\, \bigg| \, \mathcal{L}(aN,bN)=\ell N \right] =0 
\end{equation}
for $(x,y)\in (0,1)^2$ satisfying 
\begin{equation}  
    \frac{1}{\mathrm m} < \frac{y}{x} < \mathrm m  \qquad \text{where} \quad \mathrm m := \frac{\ell-a-b+\sqrt{D}}{\ell-a-b-\sqrt{D}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.367749+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69c0d0b33e5ca5eb4935d4d8c072a54792b59b9d35bf58594e758f7e2ddafdbd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.03122, 'Propagation of support for super-Brownian motion with general branching mechanism'] Let $X$ be a super-Brownian motion with a spatially dependent branching mechanism $\Psi$ satisfying Condition \ref{con_localize} with a local lower bound family $\{\Phi_R\}$ of branching mechanisms satisfying Condition \ref{con1}. A sufficient condition for $X$ to possess the compact support property in finite time, i.e., for any $t>0$ and $\mu\in M_c$,  
\begin{align}
\mathbb{P}_\mu\left\{ \overline{\bigcup_{s \le t} S(X_s)} \text{ is compact} \right\} = 1,
\end{align}
is given by
\begin{align}
\liminf_{R\to\infty}\dfrac{\ln G_{\Phi_R}^{-1}(R/2)}{R^2}<\infty.
\end{align}
Equivalently, along some sequence $R_n\to\infty$, the quantity $G_{\Phi_{R_n}}^{-1}(R_n/2)$ grows at most like $\exp(C R_n^2)$ for some finite $C$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

