Then, for all $t> 0$, we have $x^1_t<x^2_t$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.064538+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69e516726fcadc92d2a744df18be5cc0d9eb7066fda6fa0be4ca38d6bd0d7970","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop cor:decomposition of arXiv:2508.04954, 'Conditional exponential directed last passage percolation under a one-point upper large de'] For every $\mathsf{q} \in \mathsf{R}_1\cup \cdots \cup \mathsf{R}_7$, there exist constants $C, c, L_0>0$ such that for every $\mathbf{n} \in {\mathbb N}^3$ with $\mathbf{n}\neq (1,1,1)$, and for ${\boldsymbol \sigma}, {\boldsymbol \tau} \in \mathcal S_{\mb{n}}$ of the forms 
\begin{equation} 
    {\boldsymbol \sigma} = 3^{n_{31}}2^{n_{21}}1^{n_{1}}2^{n_{22}}3^{n_{32}}, \qquad {\boldsymbol \tau} = 3^{n'_{31}}2^{n'_{21}}1^{n_{1}}2^{n'_{22}}3^{n'_{32}}
\end{equation} 
satisfying 
\begin{itemize}
