{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.314505+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6a704fe74e036a5c379de7870a0252b24249f2b0e90fbfa7d7287878743ce9e0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:5.17 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Let $a<b$. Let $A$ be a closed set in $\mathbb R^3$ such that $\partial\mathcal B_a\not\subseteq A$ and $\partial\mathcal B_b\not\subseteq A $. Let $\mathcal U$ be the class of all bounded positive harmonic functions $u$ on $\mathcal B_b^\circ\setminus A$ such that  $u$ vanish continuously on the regular points of $ A\cap\mathcal B_b^\circ$ and can be extended continuously to $\partial\mathcal B_b\setminus A$. We have
    \begin{equation}
    \sup_{u,v\in\mathcal U,x_1,x_2\in\partial\mathcal B_a\setminus A}\dfrac{u(x_1)v(x_2)}{u(x_2)v(x_1)}
    =K(p_{a,b}^ A).    
