{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.988112+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6a7af12fc3d56b8cc72a13e23d1b1f2d26acc9000bdccec0a4a4682f3abf3c8c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm her_Hironaka of arXiv:2412.05999, 'Non-Archimedean GUE corners and Hecke modules'] Define $\mathbb{Z}$-linear mappings
\begin{align}\begin{split}\psi_n:H(G,K)&\rightarrow\mathbb{C}[x_1^{\pm 1},\ldots,x_n^{\pm 1}]^{S_n}\\
c_\mu&\mapsto q^{2\langle \mu,\rho_n\rangle}P_\mu(x_1^2,\ldots,x_n^2;q^{-2})\end{split}\end{align}
and
\begin{align}\begin{split}
\psi^{\her}_n:H(G^{\her},K)&\rightarrow\mathbb{C}[x_1^{\pm 1},\ldots,x_n^{\pm 1}]^{S_n}\\
c_\nu^{\her}&\mapsto (-1)^{n(\nu)+|\nu|}q^{\langle \nu,\rho_n\rangle}P_\nu(x_1,\ldots,x_n;-q^{-1})
\end{split}\end{align}
where $\langle \cdot,\cdot\rangle$ is the canonical inner product, $\rho_n=\frac{1}{2}(n-1,n-3,\ldots,1-n)$. Then we have
\begin{equation}
\psi_n(f)\psi^{\her}_n(g)=\psi_n^{\her}(f*g),\quad\forall f\in H(G,K),g\in H(G^{\her},K).
\end{equation}
Thus the mappings $(\psi_n,\psi^{\her}_n)$ give a module homomorphism from the $H(G,K)$-module $H(G^{\her},K)$ to $\mathbb{C}[x_1^{\pm 1},\ldots,x_n^{\pm 1}]^{S_n}$, viewed as a module over itself.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.609301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6a80219e6501ef1476565bea92805cdc8f3b5fa534ac82089a8a4edd5d9ad7f3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mf_meas of arXiv:2412.05239, 'Conditions for uniform in time convergence: applications to averaging, numerical discretis'] Suppose (ass_meanfield of the paper) holds. Let $\nu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then, there exists a constant $\tilde{\mathbf{C}}>0$ such that for all $t\ge 0$ and $N\in \mathbb{N}$
