{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.858491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b279b19ddec8a908853cdb5bb8ff4776233a31cc3367f38bff1a5cb5b17174c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2511.21496, 'Optimal local central limit theorems on Wiener chaos'] Let $\mathbf{F} = \{F_n\}_{n \in \N}$ be a sequence of random variables living in the $m$th Wiener chaos with $m \geq 2$ such that $\E[F_n^2] = 1$ and $F_n \xrightarrow[n \to \infty]{d} \cN(0,1)$.
Then, for any $j \in \N_0$, there exists $N_{\mathbf{F}, j} \in \N$ such that $\rho_{F_n} \in C_{\mathrm{b}}^{j}(\R)$ and
\begin{equation}
    C_{\mathbf{F}, j,m} \mathbf{M}(F_n) \leq \left\lVert\rho_{F_n}^{(j)} - \rho_{\cN}^{(j)}\right\rVert_{\infty} \leq \wt{C}_{\mathbf{F}, j, m} \mathbf{M}(F_n)
\end{equation}
hold for every $n \geq N_{\mathbf{F}, j}$, where the constants $C_{\mathbf{F}, j, m}$ and $\wt{C}_{\mathbf{F}, j, m}$ depend on $\mathbf{F}$,  $j$, and $m$ but not on $n$.
As a consequence, $\{\mathbf{M}(F_n)\}_{n \in \N}$ provides an optimal convergence rate for $\norm{\rho_{F_n}^{} - \rho_{\cN}^{}}_{C_{\mathrm{b}}^{k}(\R)}$, for every $k \in \N_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.119518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b2b4faf724a88cf58cd5f66e874d5c22a1715b96b73849c0e2cd8a9d3813c16","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2605.27212, '2605.27212'] For every fixed \(M<\infty\) there is a constant
\(C_{p,\varepsilon,M}<\infty\) such that the following holds.  If
\(h\le\varepsilon r\) and
\begin{equation}
    t_*=C_{p,\varepsilon,M}r\log r\,(h+M\log r),
\end{equation}
then, uniformly in \(x\in\Omega\) and all \(t\ge t_*\),
\begin{equation}
    \Prob_x(X_t\notin\Good_{\mathrm H})\le r^{-M}.
