{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.795319+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b32ac9bc5869df0966e16bcc5ffb081937c045a6017aa50f46dcbba55440be6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:asymp_escape_crossing_probabilities of arXiv:2509.23496, 'Crossing probabilities in geometric inhomogeneous random graphs'] Let $2<\delta\leq\infty$ and $0\leq\gamma<\frac12$, excluding the case $(\gamma,\delta)=(0,\infty)$, and adopt the convention $\frac{1}{\gamma}=\infty$ when $\gamma=0$. Then, for arbitrary $\lambda>0$,
    the one-edge escape and crossing probabilities satisfy 
    \begin{align} 
        \pi_{(u), R} \asymp 
        \begin{cases}
            u^{-1}g(R) \wedge 1 & \mbox{ if } \delta\ge\frac1\gamma, \\
            u^{-\gamma\delta}g(R) \wedge 1 & \mbox{ if } \delta<\frac1\gamma,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    and
    \begin{align} 
        \pi_{r, R} \asymp 
        \begin{cases}
            r^d (\log R) g(R)  & \mbox{ if } \delta\ge\frac1\gamma, \\
            r^d g(R)  & \mbox{ if } \delta<\frac1\gamma,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    and the two-edge escape and crossing probabilities satisfy



    \begin{align} 
        \pi_{(u), R}^{(2)} \asymp 
        \begin{cases}
        \pi_{(u),R} + u^{-\gamma}h(R)\wedge 1 & \mbox{ if } \delta\ge \frac1\gamma-1,\\ \pi_{(u),R} & \mbox{ if } 
        \delta< \frac1\gamma-1,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    and
 \begin{align} 
        \pi^{(2)}_{r, R} \asymp 
        \begin{cases}
            \pi_{r,R} + 
            r^{d\gamma} h(R)   & \mbox{ if } \delta>\frac1\gamma,\vspace{0.5mm} \\
             \pi_{r,R} + r^{d\gamma} (\log r)^\gamma h(R)   & \mbox{ if } \delta=\frac1\gamma,\vspace{0.5mm} \\
            \pi_{r,R} + r^{\frac{d}{\delta}} h(R)  & \mbox{ if } \frac{1}{\gamma}-1\leq\delta<\frac1\gamma, \vspace{0.5mm}\\
             \pi_{r,R}    & \mbox{ if } \delta<{\frac1\gamma-1},
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}    
    where the {asymptotic order of} $g(R):=\pi_{(1),R}$ and $h(R):=\pi^{(2)}_{(1),R}$ are stated in Table~(tab:gandh of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:57.004275+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b3369ad5c2044cd5aed080dac1f1f5c5210bc2ec3547b7e5eacc1c2b2009366","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma E[multi_edges]_asymp of arXiv:2604.05921, 'Simplicity of random hypergraphs'] If $\forall e \in E: \delta_e = \delta\in O(1)$ and 
