 \end{equation}
 where $\lambda_\mu=\sup_{\theta\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}}\Var_\mu[\braket{\theta,X}]=\|\Cov(\mu)\|_\mrm{op}$ denotes the largest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix of $\mu$, given by $\Cov(\mu)\coloneqq\left(\int_{\R^d}x_ix_j\diff\mu(x)-\int_{\R^d}x_i\diff\mu(x)\int_{\R^d}x_j\diff\mu(x)\right)_{1\leq i,j\leq d}$. (Verbatim from the paper's TeX; symbols are the source paper's. Stated as open/conjectural; no resolution of it was found in the paper's own text by the rules of problem_index.py - a paper may still prove special cases of a conjecture it states.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.790662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd0601b6b41f4bb3462a237b500fe1180fa87053309692414e09b7f778dd0ea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 64 (Vacuum Feynman diagram) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] A \emph{vacuum diagram} is a multigraph $\Gamma = (\cV,\cE)$, meaning there can 
