{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.751659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd2bf6f48612581f5c91161cdef5a074b1344353e402ac490c51cecf844641a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2609.00448, 'Equivariant formality and representation theory'] (\cite[\S I.4]{Ho}, \cite[\S 3]{BZ}, \cite[Definitions 2.2 and 2.5]{F})
	Let $\delta: R(G)\to K^{-1}(G)$ be the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map which sends a complex representation $\rho$ with underlying vector space $V$ to the $K$-theory class represented by the complex of vector bundles
	\begin{align*}
		0\longrightarrow G\times\mathbb{R}\times V&\longrightarrow G\times\mathbb{R}\times V\longrightarrow 0\\
		(g, t, v)&\mapsto\begin{cases}(g, t, -t\rho(g)v),&\ \text{if }t>0\\ (g, t, tv),&\ \text{if }t\leq 0.\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	More generally, for an element $\rho\in\text{ker}(i^*: R(G)\to R(K))$ which is the formal difference $\rho_1-\rho_2\in R(G)$ of two complex $G$-representations restricting to the same $K$-representations, we define the $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map $\delta^{G/K}:\text{ker}(i^*)\to K^{-1}(G/K)$ which sends $\rho$ to the complex of vector bundles
	\begin{align*}
		0\longrightarrow G/K\times\mathbb{R}\times V&\longrightarrow G/K\times\mathbb{R}\times V\longrightarrow 0\\
		(gK, t, v)&\mapsto\begin{cases}(gK, t, t\rho_1(g)\rho_2(g)^{-1}v),&\ \text{if }t>0\\ (gK, t, tv),&\ \text{if }t\leq 0.\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	The map $\delta_K^{G/K}: \text{ker}(i^*)\to K_K^{-1}(G/K)$ is defined using the same complex of vector bundles and equipping it with the $K$-action on $G/K\times \mathbb{R}\times V$ given by $k\cdot(gK, t, v)=(kgK, t, \rho_1(k)v)$. The map $\delta_K^G: R(G)\to K_K^{-1}(G)$ can be defined similarly (here $K$ acts on $G$ by conjugation). We will also use the same notation to denote the rationalized maps from the rational representation ring to the rational $K$-theory $K^{-1}(G; \mathbb{Q})$, $K^{-1}(G/K; \mathbb{Q})$ etc.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:53.166367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd42c52662da0ce4a4c368100f0b20c54ed3a148f42788c330e3d05eacb1dd3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 35 of arXiv:2607.02777, 'Hypocoercivity for Hamiltonian Diffusions \\ with Singular Drift'] Let $h \in L^2(\mu)$ be a probability density with respect to $\mu$ and set $\mathbb{P}_{h\mu}\defeq \int_{\Omega} \mathbb{P}_{(x,y)}(\cdot) \, h(x,y) \,\textup{d}\mu(x,y)$.
        Then $\mathbb{P}_{h\mu}$ solves the martingale problem for $(\cL,D(\cL))$, i.e., for all $f\in D(\cL)$, the process $(M_t^{[f]})_{t\geq 0}$ on $(C([0,\infty);\Omega), \mathcal{B}, \mathbb{P}_{h\mu})$ defined by
		\begin{equation}

			M_t^{[f]} \defeq f(X_t, Y_t) - f(X_0, Y_0) - \int_{0}^{t} \cL f(X_s, Y_s) \,\textup{d}s, \quad t \ge 0,
		\end{equation}
		is an $(\mathcal{G}_t)_{t\geq 0}$-martingale. 
        Furthermore, if $f\in D(\cL)$ with $f^2\in D(\cL)$ and $\cL f \in L^4(\mu)$, then also the process $(N_t^{[f]})_{t\geq 0}$ given by
		\begin{equation}

			N_t^{[f]} \defeq (M_t^{[f]})^2 - \int_{0}^{t} \bigl(\cL(f^2)-2f \cL f\bigr)(X_s, Y_s) \,\textup{d}s, \quad t \ge 0,
		\end{equation}
		is a martingale with respect to $(\mathcal{G}_t)_{t\geq 0}$, identifying the quadratic variation of $(M_t^{[f]})_{t\geq 0}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.733531+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6bd59318e7671fa19ef90948ca45046726c9876286e69f03038302c459805ef6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop uniformBoundRepulsive of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] Assume $0\le\mathsf{s}<\mathsf{d}/2$, $\mu\in L^\infty\cap\mathcal{P}({\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d})$, and $\beta\ge0$ is fixed.  If $\mathsf{s}=0$, assume in addition (equation eq:logEnergyAssumption of the paper).  Then
\begin{align}
\sup_{N\ge1}{\mathsf{K}}_{N,\beta}(\mu)\le C_{\mathrm{ub}}(\beta,\mu)<\infty.
