for all $U(t)\le c_0/\delta$, where the second line holds for all $1\le j\le N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.700346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be625c1c1c07313be7ad77b61d84e09c747ce1a2958d506fb8db2608f053eb6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop coupling2 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] If $\gamma > 0$ is taken small enough then, for any $\eta \in (0,\tfrac{1}{2})$ sufficiently small (depending only on $\gamma$) we have that, for all $k$ sufficiently large and $T \in [k,{\rm e}^k]$,
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{P} \bigg( \bigg\{ \sqrt{\widehat{L}_{\widetilde{v}_k}(0)} \in {\rm d} u\bigg\} \setminus \mathcal{L}^{\eta,[1,T-1]}_{\widetilde{v}_k} \bigg)=\frac{4}{\sqrt{\pi}} \cdot \frac{1}{Tk^{1/2+\gamma}}{\rm e}^{-2Tk^\gamma}\left(\widehat{u}{\rm e}^{2\sqrt{2}\widehat{u}}\right)\left(\widehat{v}{\rm e}^{-2\sqrt{2}\widehat{v}-\frac{\widehat{v}^2}{Tk^\gamma}}\right)(1+o_k(1))\,
	\end{equation} holds uniformly over $u \in \mathcal{Q}_k^\eta(0)$ and $v \in \mathcal{Q}_k^\eta(T)$, where $\widehat{u}:=u-\sqrt{2}k$ and $\widehat{v}:=v -\sqrt{2}(k+Tk^\gamma)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.966645+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6be8bf92760d58c36bbfa091a610120e9b226b07de2ceb8d5784bfee108a6da5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.22416, 'A multi-player optimal stopping game based on a spider Brownian bridge'] We say that $(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*}) \in \sT \times \sT$ is a Nash equilibrium (for the game with expected payoffs $(\GG_i, \LL_i)_{i = 1,2}$) if 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{split}
            R_{1}(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*})=\sup_{\tau \in \sT} R_{1}(\tau,\sigma^{*}),  \\
            R_{2}(\tau^{*},\sigma^{*})=\sup_{\sigma \in \sT} R_{2}(\tau^{*},\sigma).
        \end{split}
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

