{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:44:12.641553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c7c2ba9b6f9759e8a866e54e1161ea2f42e5da6c26a5009bbefd0451b7745da","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:lip_samples of arXiv:2512.04371, 'Constructive Approximation under Carleman's Condition, \\ with Applications to Smoothed An'] Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and $\delta\in(0,1)$. 
Let $\sigma \in \mathsf{Lip}^{k,M}(\RR)$. 
Suppose that the $y$-marginal of $\mu$ has a finite fourth moment, i.e., $\EE[|y|^4] <\infty$. 
Let $\ell(y,y') = (y-y')^2$ be the quadratic loss function.
Then there exists an algorithm that agnostically learns $\cH_\sigma$ with accuracy $\varepsilon$ and confidence $1-\delta$ using i.i.d. samples of size 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $O\Big(\varepsilon^{-3} d^{ O\left((M/\varepsilon)^{r/k} \cdot \left(k\log k \right)^{r-1}\right)} \log(1/\delta)\Big)$ under \(r\)-strictly sub-exponential input $\mu_x$;
