{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.866034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c845e57958c17f874c392fc696187c5e97ad9be76814cd2c18072938edca7a9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 (Discrete-time first-passage identity) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] Let $(M_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a nonnegative martingale with $M_0=1$ and $M_t\to0$ a.s.  For $x\ge1$ let $\sigma_x:=\inf\{t: M_t\ge x\}$ and, on $\{\sigma_x<\infty\}$, the overshoot $J_x:=M_{\sigma_x}-x\ge0$.
Then the optional-stopping mass is conserved,
\begin{equation}
  \E\!\left[M_{\sigma_x}\,\ind\{\sigma_x<\infty\}\right]=1,
\end{equation}
and the first-passage probability satisfies the identity
\begin{equation}
  \PP\!\Bigl(\sup_{t\ge0}M_t\ge x\Bigr)
  =\PP(\sigma_x<\infty)
  =\frac1x\Bigl(1-\E\!\left[J_x\,\ind\{\sigma_x<\infty\}\right]\Bigr).
\end{equation}
The named slack is the deficit $\E[J_x\ind\{\sigma_x<\infty\}]/x$.
Equality with the Pareto$(1)$ law of Theorem~\ref{thm:ville-tight} holds iff $J_x=0$ a.s.\ (no overshoot).
The overshoot law depends on the increment distribution, so no universal closed form for $\E[J_x]$ exists beyond the overshoot-free extreme; the identity~\eqref{eq:fp-exact} accounts for the slack in every case. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.587659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c85c896cfe7c810c52295a9ed1e82dec20adbb6705ed3e2c36dada76a660d1f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2602.19773, 'Regularization of a stationary point process by a stationary increments perturbation'] If $0 < t < 2\pi$, then
