{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.845970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d363e475aef7c76613ba8e3d0f1a9391412e6cc0c178fc6021c5528fe008422","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:GeneralDiscrepancy of arXiv:2410.23915, 'Asymptotic Bounds and Online Algorithms for Average-Case Matrix Discrepancy'] Assume that $A_1,\ldots,A_n$ is a sequence of independent centered $m \times m$ random symmetric matrices, for some $m = m(n)$, that have $\|A_i\|_{\psi_1} \leq \psi$ for all $i \in [n]$ and satisfy Condition~(equation eq:NormConcentration of the paper). Then, there exist constants $C_1,C_2 > 0$ such that for $n \ll m^2$,
        \begin{equation*}
            \lim_{n \to \infty} \P{C_1\sqrt{nm} \leq \disc(A_1,\ldots,A_n) \leq C_2m^{3/2}} = 1.
        \end{equation*}
        In particular, when $n = \Theta(m^2)$, we have that $\disc(A_1,\ldots,A_n) = \Theta(m^{3/2})$ with high probability as $n \to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:44.240747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d36f2214e9f6363a0a6bfbf69b8d4062e18dd56b05d4adf1b1cb1da9b58326b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Propo 13 of arXiv:2604.14752, 'Strong and weak rates of convergence in the Smoluchowski--Kramers approximation for stocha'] Let Assumptions~\ref{ass:mapping-f} and~\ref{ass:noise} be satisfied. Let $\varphi:H\to\R$ be of class $\mathcal{C}^4$ with bounded derivatives of order $k\in\{1,2,3,4\}$.

For all $T\in(0,\infty)$, all $k\in\{1,2,3,4\}$ and all $\bigl(\alpha_\ell\bigr)_{1\le \ell\le k}$ such that $\sum_{\ell=1}^{k}\alpha_\ell<2$, there exists a real number $C_{\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_k}(T)\in(0,\infty)$ such that for all $N\in\N$, all $t\in(0,T]$ and all $u\in H_N$ and $\bigl(h_\ell\bigr)_{1\le \ell\le k}\in H_N^k$, one has
\begin{equation}
|D_u^k\Phi^{(0,N)}(t,u).(h_1,\ldots,h_k)|\le C_{\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_k}(T)\vvvert\varphi\vvvert_k t^{-\frac{\alpha_1+\ldots+\alpha_k}{2}}\|h_1\|_{H^{-\alpha_1}}\ldots\|h_k\|_{H^{-\alpha_k}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.538205+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d37b67e321ba2fe66abca94d6d500b3cf7765593eaf60d2fa307a262eef8383","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-Jakubowski_Skorokhod_Ondrejat of arXiv:2505.24043, 'Weak solutions of Navier-Stokes Equation with purely discontinuous L\'evy Noise'] \rm (Theorem A.1 in [Brz+Ondrejat_2011]) \it

Let $\mathscr{X} $ be a topological space such that there exists a sequence $\{ {f}_{m}\} $ of continuous functions ${f}_{m}\colon \mathscr{X}  \to \mathbb{R} $ that separates points of $\mathscr{X} $. Let us denote by $\mathcal{S} $ the $\sigma $-algebra generated by the maps $\{ {f}_{m}\} $.
Then
\begin{description}
\item[(j1) ] every compact subset of $\mathscr{X} $ is metrizable;
\item[(j2) ] if $({\mu }_{m})$ is a tight sequence of probability measures on $(\mathscr{X} ,\mathcal{S} )$, then there exists a subsequence $({m}_{k})$, a probability space $(\Omega , \mathscr{F} ,\mathbb{P})$ with $\mathscr{X} $-valued Borel measurable variables ${\xi }_{k}, \xi $ such that  ${\mu }_{{m}_{k}}$ is the law of ${\xi }_{k}$
and ${\xi }_{k}$ converges to $\xi $ on $\Omega $.
Moreover, the law of $\xi $ is a Radon measure.
\end{description} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.994216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d391d2d38ea12e91b322c74763869735831510cbc35c64fb8488d424f52c4c4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-explengthbrige of arXiv:2604.14318, '\large The free energy of the interacting Bose gas:\\\medskip a variational description \\'] For any $M\in(0,\infty)$, there is a constant $C_M\in(0,\infty)$ (depending on $d$, $\beta$ and $M$) such that, for any $R\in\mathbb{N}$,
