uniformly in $x_0\in \mathbb{T}_{L^2}$, where $\mathrm{dist}(x, 2L^2\mathbb{Z})$ denotes the distance from $x\in \mathbb{R}$ to the nearest point in $2L^2\mathbb{Z}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.831715+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d41d6af010fbef51f1b2b04160989569698c5d1937262e811d171b607804d48","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 65351 of arXiv:2606.29143, 'Comonotonic and moment matching approximations for sums of lognormal random variables'] Let $A = (a_1, ..., a_m)^{'}$ and $Q = (q_1, ..., q_m)^{'}$ be two numerical vectors with $\min(a_1, ..., a_m) \geq 0, \max(a_1, ..., a_m) > 0$ and $0 = q_0 < q_1 < q_2 < ... < q_m = 1$. Then for any continuous random variables $X_1, ..., X_n$ and for any $p \in (0, 1)$ the TVaR of the step-weighted comonotonic sum $(S^c)_{\omega}$ with $\omega = \omega_{A, Q}$ is given by 
\begin{align*} 
\text{TVaR}_p[(S^c)_{\omega}] = 
\frac{a_{l}(1 - p^{*})}{a(1 - p)}\sum^n_{i = 1}\text{TVaR}_{p^{*}}[X_i] + \sum^{n}_{i = 1}\sum^{m - 1}_{k = l}\frac{(a_{k + 1} - a_{k})(1 - q_k)}{a(1 - p)}\text{TVaR}_{q_k}[X_i]
\end{align*}
with $p^{*} = G^{-1}_{A, Q}(p) \in [q_{l - 1}, q_{l}]$ for some $l \in \{1, ..., m\}$ and $\sum_{k = m}^{m - 1} = 0$ by convention.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.755887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d437a73664b7c2ccde08739ce338576bd19512cfb50d2bef23aa43a201421bd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:abstract-positive-response-gram of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For a faithfully potential-realizable response, let
