In particular,  the filtration ${\overline{\mathbb{F}}_n}$ is the usual augmentation of filtration $\mathbb{H}_n$ and satisfies the usual hypothesis, see   [Jacod_Shiryaev].","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.349736+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6dfa0729437953d624a31555cceff83a14a8db06a188cc8bf2aa1e1460b69d3f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 of arXiv:2506.15341, '\bf Superpositions for General Conditional Mckean-Vlasov Stochastic Differential Equations'] Assume that Assumptions \ref{assump1} and \ref{assump2} are in force. Assume that there exists a measure-valued process $\n\in \hS^2_{\hF^Y}(\sM_1)$,   defined on the canonical space, such that the SPDE (\ref{dual})--(\ref{Zakai0}) hold. Then, there exists a probability measure $\hP^\n\in \sP_1(\O^0)$, and  $\hF^0$-progressively measurable processes $(X^\n, L^\n, B^{2, \n})\in\hL^2_{\hF^0}(\O;\hC([0,T];\hR^{d+2}))$, such that, 


 (i) $(X^\n, L^\n)$ satisfies SDE (\ref{SDE2}), $\hP^\n$-a.s.; 

 (ii)   $\frac{d\hP^\n}{d\hQ^0}\big|_{{\cF}_T}=L^\n_T$;

 (iii)  $(B^1, B^{2,\n})$ is a $(\hP^\n, \hF^0)$-Brownian motion;

 (iv) $(\O^0, \cF^0, \hP^\n, \hF^0, B^1, B^{2,\n}, X^\n, Y)$ is a weak solution to CMVSDE (\ref{SDE1}). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.022037+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6dfc988fa8537387c72b8b2899ff01daed7c9ba57264af7c472ba9cab1050161","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 67 of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $Y=(Y_n)_{n\in\mathbb Z}$ be a stationary, irreducible, and aperiodic Markov chain with countable state space $B$, transition matrix $P$, and unique stationary distribution $\pi$.  
Then the following properties are pairwise equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
\item $Y$ is geometrically ergodic;
\begin{comment}
that is, there exists $\rho\in(0,1)$ such that for every $a\in B$,
$\Vert P^n(a,\cdot)-\pi(\cdot)\Vert_{{\scriptscriptstyle \mathrm{TV}}}\le C_a\,\rho^n$.
\end{comment}
\item $Y$ satisfies Gaussian concentration;
\item $Y$ has exponential return times;
\item $Y$ is a finitary process with exponentially decaying coding radius;
\item $Y$ is a coding of an i.i.d. process;
\item $Y$ is finitarily isomorphic to an i.i.d.\ process.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.359261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6dfea27f0d513d65accacc9ce08cf317894127d7294cffd5e07894884ed5f956","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:continuity-delta-high of arXiv:2412.01645, 'Regularisation by Gaussian rough path lifts of fractional Brownian motions'] For any $p \in [1, \infty)$ and $m \in \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}$, there exists $n=n(p,m) \in \mathbb{N}$, $n\geq m$, and a constant $C=(p,m)$, such that for any separable Hilbert space $V$, any $u \in \mathbb{D}^{m,p}(V \otimes \mathcal{H})$, and any $s \in [0,1]$,  we have with probability one
    \begin{equ}
         \| \delta_{\mathcal{H}_s^\perp}(u) \|_{\mathbb{D}^{m,p}_s(V)} \leq C \| \Pi_{\mathcal{H}_s^\perp}u\|_{\mathbb{D}^{n,2p}_s(V\otimes \mathcal{H})}.
    \end{equ}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.254437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e00f31ce144767d2c77105c994d4fa3ae83a3a3a35d1f7b8c51dd33bab60a88","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 94 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] The following statements hold. 

	\begin{enumerate}
		\item If $\mathfrak{z}\ne \mathfrak{w}_0$, then $\mathfrak{p} < \bar{x}$ (that is, $\mathfrak{z}$ is to the left of $\bar{z}$). 
		\item If $\mathfrak{z} \ne \mathfrak{w}_0$ and $\mathfrak{p} \ge x^{SW}_q$ (that is, $\mathfrak{z}$ is to the right of the southwest tangency location), then $\mathtt{d}_{\mathfrak{z}} \le N^{-4R\delta} \mathtt{d}_{\bar{z}}$, 
		\item If $\mathtt{d}_{\bar{z}} < N^{2R\delta} \mathtt{e}_{\bar{z};q'}$, then $\mathtt{d}_{w} \ge N^{R\delta/4} \td_{\bar z}$ holds for all $w = (x,y) \in \mathcal{U}_{q'}^{k/N}$ with $x \in [x^-, \mathfrak{p}]$ (that is, to the left of $\mathfrak{z}$).	
