   where $G_0$ and $G_1$ denote $G_0:=\left({H-z_0}\right)^{-1}$ and $G_1:=\pa{H_{\Lambda}-z_1}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:56.812104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e3568142e333f5d01180d50100114297b3086a047372377f84085e78f5d7752","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Pisztora_event of arXiv:2603.28520, 'The Supercritical Loop O(1) and Random Current Models: Uniqueness and Mixing'] For a box $\Lambda_n$ (or a translate of it), Pisztora considered the event $\mathtt{Pis}_{\Lambda_n}(\varepsilon, \theta, L_0)$ defined as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[$i)$] There is a cluster $\mathtt{Giant}_{\Lambda_n} $ touching all faces of $\partial_v \Lambda_n$ and the box $\Lambda_{n/2}$. 
    \item[$ii)$] The cluster has a density: $\abs{\mathtt{Giant}_{\Lambda_n}}\geq (\theta- \varepsilon)|\Lambda_n|$.
    \item[$iii)$] Most other clusters have size bounded by $L_0:$ $\abs{ \left \{v \in \Lambda_n \setminus \mathtt{Giant}_{\Lambda_n} \mid \abs{\mathcal{C}_v} \geq L_0 \right \} } \leq \varepsilon |\Lambda_n|.$
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.793460+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e37a2877bb501a4368bd9c1d90dc7305cde275f0dd3823bc06e5c96590bdc8f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main-nontriviality of arXiv:2609.02829, 'Quantitative explosion and percolation of the divisible sandpile'] Let $d\geq2$, let $(\mu_\rho)_{\rho\in(0,1]}$ be a family of laws with mean $\mathbb{E}_{\mu_\rho}\sigma(0)=\rho$, and let the masses be i.i.d.\ with law $\mu_\rho$. Suppose there are $\rho_0\in(0,1)$, $\nu_0>0$, $\theta_0>0$, and $K_0<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \inf_{\rho_0\leq\rho<1}\Var_{\mu_\rho}(\sigma(0))\geq\nu_0^2
    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
    \sup_{\rho_0\leq\rho<1}\mathbb{E}_{\mu_\rho}e^{\theta_0|\sigma(0)-\rho|}\leq K_0\, .
\end{equation}
Then there is $\rho_+=\rho_+(d,(\mu_\rho))\in[\rho_0,1)$ such that $\mathcal T^{(\rho)}$ contains an infinite nearest-neighbor component almost surely for every $\rho\in(\rho_+,1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.307915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e38d282e40b7d9c39b4df7ec8527badda83c14f0c486feafa8263a21ca92ee6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2607.22892, 'Mirror Langevin diffusions: Convergence rates and Markov chain approximations'] Suppose that a weak solution of the MLD exists and
    \begin{enumerate}
    	\item[(a)] $V, F$ are in $C^1$. $u, u^*$ are strictly convex and in $C^2$. 
        \item[(b)] $g=\nabla^2 u$ is locally bounded and locally uniformly elliptic on $R^d$. That is, for any closed ball $B(0,r)$ of radius $r>0$, there exist positive constants $c_r, C_r$ such that $c_r I \leq g(x) \le C_r I$, for all $x\in B(0,r)$.  
        \item[(c)] For some $\alpha \in (0,1]$, 
    \begin{equation}
    \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty}\left[  x \cdot \frac{\partial V}{\partial x} - \alpha x^T \nabla^2u(x) x\right]=\infty.
