{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.765864+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e4929c5b2d75f9d29e27be9e0c96c216b8cbcea9f66cc964988894d9c6c6a9a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 (\cite{Che26}*{Theorem 1.13}) of arXiv:2608.26723, 'Extrinsic upper bounds for the harmonic mean of eigenvalues in curved manifolds'] Let $M$ be an $m$-dimensional closed submanifold in $\mathbb{R}^n(c)$. Then the first $m$ nonzero eigenvalues of the Laplacian satisfy
\begin{equation}
	\mathfrak{H}(\lambda_1,\cdots, \lambda_m) \le \frac{m}{\vol(M)}\int_M\left(c+|\mathbf{H}|^2\right),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
	\mathfrak{H}(\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_m)=\Big(\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^m\lambda_i^{-1}\Big)^{-1}
\end{equation*}
represents the harmonic mean of the first $m$ nonzero eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_m$ of the Laplacian on $M$.

Equality holds if and only if $M$ is either minimally immersed in $\mathbb{S}^n$ by a subspace of the first eigenspace, or minimally immersed in a geodesic sphere of $\mathbb{R}^{n}(c)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:15:46.791572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e49e09db9c6d13ca009e192ee2418a115fa44f01ea87280ec0005024f5d5574","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[OPEN PROBLEM - not a proved fact. Stated in arXiv:2506.09609, 'Schramm-Loewner evolution contains a topological Sierpiński carpet when $κ$ is close to 8' (conjecture environment labelled conj:exotic-1).] For each $\underline{\kappa} \in (0,4)$, there exists $\rho_c = \rho_c(\kappa) \in (-2-\underline{\kappa}/2,-2) \cap [\underline{\kappa}/2-4,-2) $ such that the following holds. Let $\eta$ be an SLE$_{\underline{\kappa}}(\rho)$ curve from $0$ to $\infty$ on $\mathbb{H}$ with force point always located to the right of the tip.
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item For $\rho \in (\rho_c, -2)$, almost surely, the range of $\eta$ is conformally removable and the adjacency graph of $\mathbb{H} \setminus \eta$ is connected.

