{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.667958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f54834d52c1b1c8707613fb515afe0f8e60c21527e2129164eed3085ff00e9e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.20838, 'Long-memory Markov chains with power-law intensities'] The latent processes $\mathbf{X}_n = \{(X_n, c_n)\}$ evolve according to 
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbf{X}_n = \Psi(\mathbf{X}_{n-1}, \tau_n) = (\Psi_x(x, c, t), \Psi_c(x, c, t)).

	\end{equation}
	where
	\begin{align}
		\Psi_x(x, c, t) &= x \left(1 + \frac{t}{c}\right)^{-p} + \xi,  \\
		\Psi_c(x, c, t) &= \frac{\Psi_x(x, c, t)}{\,\frac{x}{c} \left(1 + \tfrac{t}{c}\right)^{-p-1} + \tfrac{\xi}{\gamma}}, 
	\end{align}
	with parameters $\mu> 0, \gamma> 0, \xi > 0$ and $p>1$.
	Equivalently, the update equations can be written as
	\begin{align*}
		&X_n = X_{n-1} \left(1  + \frac{\tau_n}{c_{n-1}} \right)^{-p} + \xi \\
		&\frac{X_n}{c_n} =  \frac{X_{n-1}}{c_{n-1}} \left(1  + \frac{\tau_n}{c_{n-1}} \right)^{-p-1} + \frac{\xi}{\gamma}.
	\end{align*}
	Define $\Phi(t, x, c)$ as
	\begin{align}
		\Phi(t, x, c) & = \int_0^t  \lambda_{n-1}(s; x, c) \D s= \int_0^t \left( \mu + \frac{x c^p}{(c+s)^p} \right)\, \D s \\
		& = \mu t + \dfrac{x c}{p-1}\left( 1 - \bigg(1+\dfrac{t}{c}\bigg)^{-p+1}  \right). 
	\end{align}
	The inter-arrival time $\tau_n$ is generated via the inverse transform
	\[
	\tau_n = \Phi^{-1}(\varepsilon_n,  X_{n-1}, c_{n-1}),
	\]
	where the inverse is taken with respect to $t$ for fixed $x$ and $c$,
	and $\{\varepsilon_n\}$ is an i.i.d. sequence drawn from $F_\varepsilon$
	satisfying $F_\varepsilon(0) = 0$ (so that $\varepsilon_n \ge 0$ a.s.), $\mu_{\varepsilon} = \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon] < \infty$,
	and $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon^2] < \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.776848+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f549eb8f81829a27918dfe5c3bff4160b5cb38425a8b45e119eec4939ade5e7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 4 of arXiv:2603.25459, 'Cram\'{e}r-type moderate deviation for double index permutation statistics'] Let $\text{Des}(\pi)$ and $\text{Inv}(\pi)$ be the number of descents and inversions of a random permutation $\pi\in S_{n}$, $W$ and $T$ are normalized statistics defined as (\ref{normalized-descent-inversion}). Then we have 
\begin{align}
    \left|\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(W>z\right)}{1-\Phi(z)}-1\right| = O(1)(1+z^3)/\sqrt{n},
\end{align}
\begin{align}
    \left|\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(T>z\right)}{1-\Phi(z)}-1\right| = O(1)(1+z^3)/\sqrt{n},
\end{align}
for $0\leq z \leq n^{1/6}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.712348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f56aa442f31ec17cdd86a1e8ccaa17fa54c7a6bc46824bb34661b5f58bfc6a7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition stoch_domination of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] {\rm{(i)}} Let
	\[\xi=\left(\xi^{(N)}(u):N\in\mathbb N, u\in U^{(N)}\right),\hskip 10pt \zeta=\left(\zeta^{(N)}(u):N\in\mathbb N, u\in U^{(N)}\right),\]
	be two families of non-negative random variables, where $U^{(N)}$ is a possibly $N$-dependent parameter set. We say $\xi$ is stochastically dominated by $\zeta$, uniformly in $u$, if for any fixed (small) $\tau>0$ and (large) $D>0$, 
	\[\mathbb P\bigg(\bigcup_{u\in U^{(N)}}\left\{\xi^{(N)}(u)>N^\tau\zeta^{(N)}(u)\right\}\bigg)\le N^{-D}\]
	for large enough $N\ge N_0(\tau, D)$, and we will use the notation $\xi\prec\zeta$. 

	If for some complex family $\xi$ we have $|\xi|\prec\zeta$, then we will also write $\xi \prec \zeta$ or $\xi=\mathrm{O}_\prec(\zeta)$. 

	\vspace{5pt}
	\noindent {\rm{(ii)}} As a convention, for two deterministic quantities $\xi$ and $\zeta$, we will write $\xi\prec\zeta$ if and only if $|\xi|\le N^\tau |\zeta|$ for any constant $\tau>0$. 

 \vspace{5pt}
	\noindent {\rm{(iii)}} Let $A$ be a family of random matrices and $\zeta$ be a family of non-negative random variables. Then, we use $A=\mathrm{O}_\prec(\zeta)$ to mean that $\|A\|\prec \zeta$, where $\|\cdot\|$ denotes the operator norm. 

	\vspace{5pt}
	\noindent {\rm{(iv)}} We say an event $\Xi$ holds with high probability (w.h.p.) if for any constant $D>0$, $\mathbb P(\Xi)\ge 1- N^{-D}$ for large enough $N$. More generally, we say an event $\Omega$ holds $w.h.p.$ in $\Xi$ if for any constant $D>0$,
	$\mathbb{P}( \Xi\setminus \Omega)\le N^{-D}$ for large enough $N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.024211+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f59121dd8dccf858ec5181cb2d3e869e0322b0eacb16c37c5186a96c82df0c0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:symmetric of arXiv:2609.03858, 'High-Dimensional Learning Dynamics of \\ Attention-Indexed Models'] Under the Assumption (assum:attention-index of the paper) except that the condition $\lim_{d\to\infty}\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}{\rm Tr}(S^k_{ij})=\mu_{ijk}$ is dropped, we have
\begin{equation}
\lim_{d\to\infty} \mathbb{E}_x\left[\mathcal{L}\left(\left\{\frac{{\rm Tr}[S^k_{ij}(x_jx_i^T-\mathcal{C}_{ij}I_d)]}{\sqrt{d}}\right\}_{i,j,k=1}^{L,L,K}\right)\right] = \mathbb{E}\left[\mathcal{L}(\{G_{ijk}\}_{i,j,k=1}^{L,L,K})\right]
\end{equation}
where $\{G_{ijk}\}_{i,j,k=1}^{L,L,K}$ is a multivariate Gaussian vector whose mean and covariance are given by:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[G_{ijk}] = 0,\
\text{Cov}(G_{ijk}, G_{i'j'k'}) = \mathcal{C}_{ii'}\mathcal{C}_{jj'}\Omega_{ijk,i'j'k'} + \mathcal{C}_{ij'}\mathcal{C}_{ji'}\Psi_{ijk,i'j'k'}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.521259+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f5a7b402e3d05810f13fe26dd879bac2ca62930d63565909356f348b871c337","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:u_t_small_t of arXiv:2512.17808, 'Zeros of polynomial powers under the heat flow'] For any fixed $z\in\mathbb{C}\setminus B$ and $t\to 0$, there exists one saddle point that converges to $\lambda_j$ for each initial zero $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_d$ of $P(z)$, and one saddle point that converges to $z$. For fixed $\tilde z\in\mathbb{C}$ and $j\le d$, set $z=\lambda_j+\tilde z \sqrt{\frac{t\alpha_j}{\alpha}}$ and assume $z\in\mathbb{C}\setminus B$ for all sufficiently small $t$. Then there exist branches of the saddle points satisfying
	\begin{align}
		u_t^{d+1}(z)&= \lambda_j + \sqrt{\frac{t\alpha_j}{\alpha}}
		u^+(\tilde z)+o(\sqrt t), \\
		u_t^j(z)&= \lambda_j + \sqrt{\frac{t\alpha_j}{\alpha}}
		u^-(\tilde z)+o(\sqrt t) , \\
		u_t^k(z)&=\lambda_k+\frac{\alpha_k t}{\alpha (\lambda_j-\lambda_k)}+o(t)\quad \text{for }k\neq j, d+1,
	\end{align}
	where $u^{\pm}(\tilde z)=\frac 1 2(\tilde z \pm \sqrt{\tilde z^2 -4})$ are defined in (equation eq:u_pm of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.475129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f61ef1c469f219c17d0aa90d5b446981349bec440891756c70df0e73e5e88b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2609.03332, 'Singularity Models of Finite-Time K\"ahler-Ricci Flows'] Suppose $(M^0, g)$ closes smoothly at $s = 0$ and that $H, F_1, \dots, F_r$ extend continuously to $s = 0$. Let $J := \{\, j : F_j(0) = 0 \,\}$. Then necessarily $H(0) = 0$, and exactly one of the following holds:

	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $J = \varnothing$ (\emph{bolt of codimension two}): only the circle fibers collapse, and the sub-manifold $Q$ at $\{s=0\}$ is diffeomorphic to $N$.
		\item $J = \{ j_0 \}$ (\emph{nut-type collapse}): $(N_{j_0}, g_{j_0})$ is holomorphically isometric to $\mathbb{CP}^{n_{j_0}}$ with the Fubini--Study metric, $|q_{j_0}| = 1$, and $Q \cong \prod_{k \neq j_0} N_k$ is a bolt of codimension $2 n_{j_0} + 2$. When $r = 1$ this means $P \cong S^{2n+1}$ and the entire leaf collapses to a \emph{nut} $Q = \{ \mathrm{pt} \}$; for $r \geq 2$ the collapse is a nut \emph{fiberwise over} the bolt $Q$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.418294+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6f6490a5d315e61fe49d3b2d34b48caad5ddd410ef4fff34267d7cd12d917b6f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:MHn72 of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] Let $v>0$ and $(s_1, \ell_1), (s_2, \ell_2)$ be two points such that $s_1<s_2$. Suppose the ellipses in ((eq:v_ellipses of the paper)) intersects at $(t(v), h(v))$. Then the area of the region $E^{s_1,v}_{\ell_1}\cup E^{s_2,v}_{\ell_2}$ is given by

\begin{align}
