{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.457210+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6fa7214c780a569832f6ac77058469a463d7099eb6ff62616d4da1d5b87c860b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:rigidity of arXiv:2411.16572, 'Optimal decay of eigenvector overlap for non-Hermitian random matrices'] Fix a small constant $\tau>0$ and pick $z$ such that $ |z|\le 1+\tau $. Then 
	there exists a small constant $c>0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		|\lambda_i^z-\gamma_i^z|\prec \max\left\{\frac{1}{|i|^{1/4}n^{3/4}+\sqrt{(1-|z|^2)_+}n}, \frac{\Delta^{1/9}}{n^{2/3}|i|^{1/3}}\right\}, \qquad |i|\leq cn
	\end{equation}
	with $\Delta$ denoting the size of the gap around zero
	in the support of $\rho^z$ (in particular $\Delta=0$ if $|z|\leq 1$), where $\gamma_i^z$ is the $i$-th quantile of $\rho^z$ given by
	\begin{equation}
		\int_0^{\gamma_i^z}\rho^z(x)\,\mathrm{d} x=\frac{i}{2n}, \qquad \gamma_{-i}^z:=-\gamma_i^z, \qquad i\in [n].
	\end{equation}	
	 In addition, there exists a small constant $c'>0$ such that 
	\begin{equation}
		\Big| \#\{j: E_1 \leq \lambda^z_j \leq E_2\}-2n \int_{E_1}^{E_2} \rho^z(x) \mathrm{d} x \Big| \prec 1, \qquad |E_i|\leq c', \quad i=1,2.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:16:46.145087+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6fa907adc10706e49c2b2929ccf543275fc05e7a7aac6cecf41550b30a8bffef","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:deriv of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] {talagrand2010mean}}]
Let $F$ be bounded and depend on $k$ replicas $(\rho^1,\varepsilon^1),\dots,(\rho^k,\varepsilon^k)$. Then, for $s\in(0,1)$,
\begin{align}
