    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.389093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6ffefa0f8b0150edb5331b511ced5bf07c8fc4d3922a81061174ff248d833989","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:La2-conv of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] For $n\ge1$, let $\mu,\mu_n\in\Lambda_2(\mathbb R^d)$. The following are equivalent: 

	(i) $\mathcal{W}_\Lambda(\mu_n,\mu)\to 0$; 

	(ii) for all Lipschitz functions $\varphi\in C(\mathbb R^d)$ satisfying $|\nabla \varphi(x)|\le C|x|$ Lebesgue-a.e. for some $C\ge 0$, it holds
	\begin{align*}
		\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{\mathbb R^d} \varphi d\mu_n=\int_{\mathbb R^d} \varphi d\mu;
	\end{align*}

	(iii) $\mu_n\to \mu$ $\Lambda$-weakly, and $$\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{\mathbb R^d} |x|^2 d\mu_n= \int_{\mathbb R^d} |x|^2 d\mu.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.048124+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70045523cb7ebf20232b1f39a85b7217cfed6f346a96463c02db99b64eab5f4e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 5 of arXiv:2510.25151, '$L^{\alpha-1}$ distance between two one-dimensional stochastic differential equations with'] (\cite[Lemma 5.3]{Kurtz})
