{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:43.854958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7019af94060a138cc90340d5a66d1e068d6692c57563673f451b82b05c998def","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2412.04334, 'Fractional counting process at L\'{e}vy times and its applications'] Let the random variables $\{Y_i, i\in \mathbb{N}\}$  are absolutely continuous with  pdf $f_y(\cdot)$, then the probability law of $Z(t),\;t\geq 0$  has the following absolutely continuous and discrete component\\
    (i) the absolutely continuous component is given by
   \begin{equation*}
    h_{Z}(y,t) =  \mathit{\Gamma } ( \vartheta )\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{\lambda_{\theta}^m(\zeta )_m}{m!}\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\zeta  + m)_{l}(-\lambda_{\theta})^l}{l! \mathit{\Gamma } (\mu (l+m)+ \vartheta )}\mathbb{E}\left[(H(t))^{\theta(l+m) }\right]f_{Y}^{(m)}(y),
\end{equation*}
where $f_{Y}^{(m)}(\cdot)$ is the m-fold convolution of $f_{Y}(\cdot)$.\\
(ii) the discrete component of the probability law is
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[Z(t)=0\right] &= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\mathbb{P}\left[ \mathcal{X}_{\mu, \vartheta }^{\zeta ,\theta}(n)= 0\right]\mathbb{P}\left[ H(t) = n\right]\\
    &=  \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\mathit{\Gamma } ( \vartheta )E_{\mu, \vartheta }^{\zeta }(-\lambda_{\theta}n^{\theta})\mathbb{P}\left[H(t) = n\right],
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left[ \mathcal{X}_{\mu, \vartheta }^{\zeta ,\theta}(t)= 0\right] &=  W_{X}(0,t) -  W_{X}(y,t),\;\;\; y<0\\
    &= \mathit{\Gamma } ( \vartheta )E_{\mu, \vartheta }^{\zeta }(-\lambda_{\theta}t^{\theta}).
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:24:20.643730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_701a595d574a373612391553732ab2edaa824e9562bf2e713d71c74900ee1900","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] Let $A$ be a $C^*$-algebra, given with a positive trace $tr:A\to\mathbb C$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The elements $a\in A$ are called random variables.

\item The moments of such a variable are the numbers $M_k(a)=tr(a^k)$.

\item The law of such a variable is the functional $\mu_a:P\to tr(P(a))$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.275192+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_701a70c6273794f0db96c57739d379ce30693cc93e0cbfe3ba99e7ba45ff84ff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:matrix_azuma-gtp of arXiv:2512.21026, 'Minimax Duality in Game-Theoretic Probability\thanks{Work in progress: mistakes are likely'] Let $(\mathcal{Y},\hat\mathcal{Z},T)$ be a sequential gamble space, $T\in\mathbb{N}$, satisfying Condition~(cond:usa-minimax of the paper).
  Consider sequences $\{X_k:\mathcal{Y}^k\to \mathrm{SA}_d\}$ and $\{A_k\in\mathrm{SA}_d\}$ satisfying
  \begin{align*}
    \textsf{\textbf{E}}[ X_k \mid s ] = 0 \text{ for all } s\in\mathcal{Y}^{k-1}, \quad \text{and} \quad X_k^2 \preceq A_k^2 \quad \text{g.t.a.s.}
