{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.801891+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_703da528db02a516c612551ca07628592a378fa0b7c19bf2fa21e26e2a752fab","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm l:restricted-L-properties of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] Almost surely, the following properties hold for every $R = (s_0,t_0) \times (x_0,y_0) \in \mathcal{R}$.
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
        \item 
        \textup{(Metric composition).}
        For every $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}(R)$ and every $s<r<t$, we have 
        \begin{align*}
            \mathcal{L}_R(s,x;t,y) = \sup_{z\in(x_0,y_0)}\left(
                \mathcal{L}_R(s,x;r,z) + \mathcal{L}_R(r,z; t,y)
            \right).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Finiteness).}
        For every $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}(R)$, we have $|\mathcal{L}_R(s,x;t,y)|<\infty$.
        \item
        \textup{(Continuity).}
        $\mathcal{L}_R$ is a continuous function $\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}(R) \to \mathbb{R}$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

