\item[(2)] There exists a Hunt process $\widehat{X}$ on $\widehat{E}$ and an MF $\widehat{M}\in \text{MF}(\widehat{X})$ with $\widehat{m}(\widehat{E}\setminus E_{\widehat{M}})=0$ such that $\widehat{X}$ is properly associated with $j({\mathscr E},{\mathscr F})$, and the subprocess $(\widehat{X},\widehat{M})$ is properly associated with $j({\mathscr E}',{\mathscr F}')$.  
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.723850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_71565f834ac4fa945f32397793f2e7566b272aea2e5a2729d5837c6e3046a84a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 37 (Critical learning-rate scaling) of arXiv:2609.02728, 'Momentum in large-batch training: Polyak enlarges the critical batch size, Nesterov improv'] \normalfont
For sufficiently large \(B\) and sufficiently small \(1-\rho\), the critical learning rates satisfy
\begin{align}
\eta_{\sgd}^{\crit} &\eqsim 1,\\
