	\end{equation*}
	where the union is non-decreasing as $s\downarrow 0$. Moreover,
	\begin{equation*}
	\overline{\bigcup_{s\in(0,t) \cap \BQ} \left( \widetilde{\SCB}^\partial_s(\SCB_{t-s} (\alpha)) \setminus  \SCB_{t-s} (\alpha) \right)}  \supset \partial \SCB_t (\alpha).
	\end{equation*}
	{The same is true if we replace the segment $\alpha$ by a loop $\SCL \in \Gamma$.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:49.557709+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7239a53eca198b7b84855d6b7dfd8186baf0c51ab1217979c5b735318faad464","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defs 13 ({Extended metric measure space cf.~\cite[Dfn.~4.7) of arXiv:2509.06869, 'Infinite Interacting Brownian Motions and EVI Gradient Flows'] {AmbErbSav16}}]  We say that $(X, \tau, \mssd, \nu)$ is an {\it extended metric measure space} if 
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item  $(X, \tau)$ is a Polish space;
\item   $\mssd$ is a complete extended distance, and there exists a family of $\tau \times \tau$-continuous bounded pseudo-distances $\mssd_i: X^{\times 2} \to [0,\infty)$ with $i \in I$ such that 
$\mssd = \sup_{i \in I}\mssd_i \ ; $
\item   the topology~$\tau$ is generated by $\Lip_b(X, \mssd) \cap \mcC(X, \tau)$;
\item   $\nu$ is a finite measure on $(X, \msB(\tau))$.

\end{enumerate}
Note that the cardinality of the index set $I$ may be uncountable. 
We say that $(X, \tau, \mssd, \nu)$ is a {\it metric measure space} if $\tau$ is generated by $\mssd$ and $\mssd$ is a distance. In this case, we simply write $(X, \mssd, \nu)$. We say that $(X, \tau, \mssd)$ is an {\it extended metric topological space} if \ref{d:EMM1}--\ref{d:EMM3} hold.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:35:59.929002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723c503ec8da1a0ddb0357773bde8fae83152e5a1b49b1c6e3cef27ec9fbcab4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 64 of arXiv:2608.26084, 'Localic Esakia Duality\\via Conic Frames'] An \emph{Esakia frame} is a pair~$(E,\dc)$ such that:
	\begin{enumerate}[label=(E\arabic*),start=0]
		\item $(E,\dc)$ is a conic frame;
		\item $E$ is a Stone frame;
		\item $\dc$ is a closure operator;
		\item $(E,\dc)$ is closed;

		\item $\Kdc(E)$ Boolean generates~$K(E)$.
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.077355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723ecd6baa3beb45091cacbe32e9810fc6f6aee938b3689d1ad7a1623ea4bc8d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Pro hash of arXiv:2507.00737, 'Dispersion models on a circle: universal properties and asymptotic results'] We have the following convergences in distribution, [Flajolet \& Poblete \& Viola [flajolet1997Analysis], Janson [MR1871562],
	Chassaing \& Marckert [MR1814521]], 
	\[{\sf Cost}(n) / n^{3/2}\xrightarrow[n]{(d)} \int_0^1 e(t)\mathrm{d} t\]
	and [Chassaing \& Louchard\footnote{In [chassaing2002phase], \eref{eq:cl} is not stated, but it is a consequence of their Lemma 3.7, in which they prove the convergence in distribution of the {\L}ukasiewicz  path encoding the parking at time $n-\lambda\sqrt{n}$, and normalized by $n^{1/2}$ and suitably change of origin, to  $(e_\lambda(s)-\underline{e_\lambda}(s), s\in [0,1]$). The cost being an integral of the {\L}ukasiewicz  path, the convergence stated in our Proposition (pro:hash of the paper) follows (this tool is used in a lot of places in the literature, including  [MR1814521], [MR3547745], [MW2019]).}[chassaing2002phase]]
	\begin{equation}
	{\sf Cost}(t_n(\lambda)) / n^{3/2}\xrightarrow[n]{(d)} \int_{0}^1 {\sf e}_\lambda(s)-\underline{{\sf e}_\lambda}(s) \mathrm{d} s.\end{equation}
	where ${\sf e}$ is again, the Brownian excursion, and
	${\sf e}_\lambda(s)= {\sf e}(s)-\lambda s,~\textrm{and }~ \underline{{\sf e}_\lambda}(s)= \min_{t \leq s} ~{\sf e}_\lambda(t).$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.778150+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723f221d15d9035ffb8cc10d671af00953c660340f0c8d001093b265d45e37f9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem conv of arXiv:2411.09257, 'Iterated Generalized Counting Process and its Extensions'] Let $0=t_0\le t_1\le\dots\le t_n=t$ be a partition of $[0,t]$ and $h_X^{*(m)}(\cdot)$ denotes the $m$-fold convolution of the density function $h_{X_1}(\cdot)$ of $X_1$. Then, the finite dimensional distribution of compound IGCP has the following form:
{\small\begin{align}
\hat{H}_{Z(t_1), Z(t_2),\dots, Z(t_n)}(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n)&=\sum_{\substack{m_l\ge0\\ l=1,2,\dots,n}}\Big(\prod_{l=1}^{n}\hat{p}(m_l,\Delta t_l)\Big)\nonumber\\
&\ \  \cdot\int_{-\infty}^{u_1}\int_{-\infty}^{u_2}\dots \int_{-\infty}^{u_n}\Big(\prod_{r=1}^{n}h_{X_{1}}^{*(m_r)}(y_r)\Big)\mathrm{d}y_n\mathrm{d}y_{n-1}\dots\mathrm{d}y_1,
\end{align}}
where $\Delta t_l=t_l-t_{l-1}$ and $u_r=x_r-(y_1+y_2+\dots+y_{r-1})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.161095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_723ffe376fb9981d5d311ee2652032048bfc984d7287ed062b1abaf2c2978006","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:detailed_balance of arXiv:2510.20461, 'Long time behaviour of one facilitated kinetically constrained models: results and open pr'] We say that  $\mu$ satisfies detailed balance if   for any finite set $\Lambda\subset {\ensuremath{\mathbb Z}} ^d$ and any $ x$ such that $x+[-\operatorname{r},\operatorname{r}]^{d}\in\Lambda$
\begin{equation}
c_{x}(\eta_{\Lambda})\pi(\eta_{\Lambda}^{x})\mu(\eta_{\Lambda})=c_{x}(\eta^x_{\Lambda})\pi(\eta_{\Lambda})\mu(\eta_{\Lambda}^{x})=c_{x}(\eta_{\Lambda})\pi(\eta_{\Lambda})\mu(\eta_{\Lambda}^{x}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:37.357345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7240de24aefa6919d32b6808c0c6526841e1ed10664c1e2ff2b9e665d74303bf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2606.17859, 'Full $\Gamma-$expansion for the level-two large deviation rate functionals of non-reversib'] Consider a non empty Polish space $\mc X$ and a sequence of
functionals $U_n: \mc X \to [0,+\infty]$. A full $\Gamma-$expansion of
$U_n$ is given by the speeds $(\alpha^{(p)}_n, n\geq 1)$,
$-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$, and the functionals
$U^{(p)}: \mc X \to [0,+\infty]$, $-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$, if:

\begin{enumerate}

\item The speeds $\alpha^{(-\mf r)}_n, \dots, \alpha^{(\mf q)}_n$ are
positive sequences such that $\alpha^{(p)}_n\prec \alpha^{(p+1)}_n $,
for every $-\mf r \leq p < \mf q$;

\item $\alpha_{\epsilon}^{(p)} U_n$ $\Gamma-$converges to $U^{(p)}$,
$-\mf r \leq p \leq \mf q$;

\item $U^{(p+1)}(x)$ is finite if, and only if, $x$ belongs to the
$0-$level set of $U^{(p)}$, for every $-\mf r \leq p < \mf q$;

\item $\beta_n U_n$ $\Gamma-$converges to $0$, for every sequence
$\beta_n \prec \alpha_n^{(-\mf r)}$; and

\item the $0-$level set of $U^{(\mf q)}$ is a singleton.
\end{enumerate}

In this case, we express the expansion as 
\begin{equation*}
U_n = \sum_{p\in -\mf r}^{\mf q}\frac{1}{\alpha_{n}^{(p)}} \, U^{(p)}.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.480452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7240ec4a13155645aabfdb299d2b29fe457f3ba9a91e47a92fed2c7f6bb2b17f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 113 (Finite-grade reconstruction with deterministic branches) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Fix $2\le r<\infty$ and an integer $Q\ge0$.  For every
$0\le q\le Q$, let $E_q(I)$ be an order-continuous
$r$-reconstruction lattice for the fixed two-sided block support system,
with constant $C_{E_q,r}$.  Let
\[
 H_{\nu;i}^{(0)}\in
 \Sch_r(Q_{\mu(i)}\cC,P_{\lambda(i)}\cE)
\]
and, for $q\ge1$, let
\[
 H_{\nu;i}^{(q)}\in
 \mathfrak W_{q,r}
 (\cH_{\R};Q_{\mu(i)}\cC,P_{\lambda(i)}\cE)
\]
be symmetric block kernels, all realized over one isonormal Gaussian
process.  Assume, for every $q$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $H_{\nu;i}^{(q)}$ is Cauchy blockwise in $\Prof_{q,r}$ for every
      $i\in I$;
\item there is $M^{(q)}\in E_q(I)$ such that
\begin{equation}

 \Prof_{q,r}(H_{\nu;i}^{(q)})\le M_i^{(q)}
 \qquad(\nu\in\N,\ i\in I).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Then every branch
\[
 H_\nu^{(q)}:=\sum_{i\in I}H_{\nu;i}^{(q)}
\]
is well defined in its coefficient completion and is Cauchy there.  The
finite Gaussian polynomials
\begin{equation}

 T_\nu:=\sum_{q=0}^{Q}I_q(H_\nu^{(q)})
\end{equation}
are Cauchy in $L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$ for every $1\le p<\infty$.

More quantitatively, if
\[
 d_{\nu,\mu;i}^{(q)}
 :=\Prof_{q,r}(H_{\nu;i}^{(q)}-H_{\mu;i}^{(q)})
 \quad(0\le q\le Q),
\]
then
\begin{align}

 \norm{T_\nu-T_\mu}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 &\le C_{E_0,r}\norm{d_{\nu,\mu}^{(0)}}_{E_0}
 \notag\\
 &\quad+
 \sum_{q=1}^{Q}A_q(p+r)^{q/2}C_{E_q,r}
 \norm{d_{\nu,\mu}^{(q)}}_{E_q}.
\end{align}
The same conclusion holds for two cutoff schemes with common majorants and
the same blockwise limits.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.945492+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7241e6f9bcc1f4eb119e519e85ad3413f59c6854961c1c516cd6637d900ce595","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 56 (Habiro diagonal lift) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] Let $K$ be a zero-framed knot, let $n\ge2$, and let
