    for every $t\in[0,\life{X})$. Moreover, the singular integrals in \eqref{eq:thm:existence-sde} are finite on compact subintervals of $[0,\life{X})$, with the usual improper interpretation at the initial time if $x_0\in\Cb$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.871463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72a1922003c5e606cc15509dc2194f719ec92a67dcb232487076ad65ba426f5a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 (Divisor obstruction) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Let $1<r<\infty$.
For $N\ge2$, let
\begin{equation}

 D_N:=\operatorname{diag}(1,2^{-1/r},\ldots,N^{-1/r}).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}

 \norm{D_N}_{r,\infty}^{*}=1,
 \qquad
 \norm{D_N}_{\Sch_{r,\infty}}\le\frac r{r-1},
\end{equation}
whereas, for every $0\le\alpha<1/r$,
\begin{equation}

 \norm{D_N\otimes D_N}
  _{\Sch_{r,\infty}(\log\Sch)^{-\alpha}}
 \ge c_{r,\alpha}
       (\log(e+N))^{1/r-\alpha}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, neither
$\Sch_{r,\infty}\times\Sch_{r,\infty}\to\Sch_{r,\infty}$ nor a
logarithmic enlargement with exponent $\alpha<1/r$ can hold uniformly over
matrix dimensions for external tensor products.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.847456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72a4a5e616c4eb22464e658d428062c00efa5ab39113c36f38e358097b90b908","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Xfree of arXiv:2510.01021, '\Large Random Matrices, Intrinsic Freeness, and Sharp Non-Asymptotic Inequalities\relatedv'] Let $A_0,A_1,\dots,A_n$ be $d\times d$ matrices. Let $W_1^{(N)},\dots,W_n^{(N)}$ be independent standard Wigner matrices, and let $s_1,\dots,s_n$ be a free semicircular family.
\begin{equation}
    X = A_0 + \sum_{k=1}^n g_k A_k, \qquad
    X^{(N)} = A_0\otimes I + \sum_{k=1}^n A_k \otimes W_k^{(N)}, \qquad X_{\mathrm{free}} = 
 A_0\otimes \1 + \sum_{k=1}^n A_k \otimes s_k
