{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.690526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e4e3f52ba610c721d919413cea8239c36d1602a00b173e2a5ce633d20efcd3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:concentration3 of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] There exists  $0<C_\star<C_2$ such that if $t\in [0,1)$  and $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy \begin{equation}
1-t^2\leq C_\star^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{-C_\star} \text{ and } \|tx\|^2\leq 1-C_2^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1}+1)^{-K},
\end{equation}
then for all $l\in \{1,...,\lfloor \beta \rfloor\}$ we have
    $$\int \|f^l(y)-f^l(tx)\|^2dp^{t,x}(y)\leq C\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{C_3}(1-t^2)^{\beta-\lfloor \beta \rfloor }\log((1-t^2)^{-1})^{-2\theta}.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.315578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e515c361011ac3306d7f1cf4fbe176788500557735fff66860bd7115861e41","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] A \emph{sutured manifold} \((N,R_{+},R_{-},\gamma)\) is a compact oriented 3-manifold \(N\) with boundary together with a set \(\gamma\subset \partial N\) satisfying the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\gamma\) consists of pairwise disjoint annuli \(A(\gamma)\) and tori \(T(\gamma)\). A component of \(A(\gamma)\) is called a \emph{sutured annulus} and a component of \(T(\gamma)\) is called a \emph{sutured torus}.
\item Each sutured annulus contains a homologically nontrivial oriented simple closed curve, called a \emph{suture}.
\item \(R_{+}\cup R_{-}= \partial N\setminus \operatorname{int}(\gamma)\) is oriented such that the orientation of \(R_{\pm}\) is coherent with the orientation of sutures in \(\partial R_{\pm}\) by boundary orientation principle.
Denote by \(R_{+}(\gamma)\) (resp. \(R_{-}(\gamma)\)) the components of \(\partial N\setminus \operatorname{int}(\gamma)\) whose normal vectors point out of (resp. into) \(N\).
\end{enumerate}
In practice, we usually abbreviate the notation to \((N,\gamma)\), or more simply to \(N\), when there is no ambiguity.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.219759+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72e7a63190aabd8b078d15f566c529c0860e08254be105eecc5adbb79931ff26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: convergence of the polymer partition function of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Let $S=(S_{n})_{n\geq 0}$ be a random walk on~${\ensuremath{\mathbb Z}} ^{d}$ satisfying the assumptions of Section~(subsec: longrange RW of the paper), and assume that $\gamma < 1+\frac{\alpha}{d}$. 
	Note in particular that it guarantees that \( V_N (a_N)^{-d}\to \infty\) as \(N\to\infty\).
	Assume also the following scaling for the parameter~\(\beta_N\): 
	\begin{equation}

	 \lim_{N\to\infty} \beta_N V_N (a_N)^{-d}= \hat{\beta} \in [0,\infty) \,.
	\end{equation}
	Then, we have the following convergence in distribution:
	\begin{equation}
	Z_N^{\omega,\beta_N} \xrightarrow[\ N\to\infty\ ]{(d)}	
	{\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}} = 1 + \sum\limits_{k \, \geqslant\, 1} \frac{\hat{\beta}^k}{k!} \int_{(0,1)^k \times ({\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} ^{d})^k} \prod\limits_{j=1}^k  \boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\big((t_1,x_1),\dots,(t_k,x_k)\big) \prod\limits_{j=1}^k \boldsymbol\zeta(\mathrm{d} t_j \mathrm{d} x_j) \,,
	\end{equation}
	where $\boldsymbol\zeta$ is a $\gamma$-stable noise on $(0,1) \times {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} ^{d}$ and \(\boldsymbol\psi^{(k)}\) is defined in~(equation eq: continuum correlation functions of the polymer model of the paper).
	This convergence holds jointly with the convergence of the discrete noise to $\boldsymbol\zeta$.
	Furthermore, for every $p \in [1,\gamma)$, ${\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}}$ has a finite moment of order~$p$, and 
	$\lim_{N \to +\infty} \mathds{E}[(Z_{N}^{\omega,\beta_N})^p] = \mathds{E}[({\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}^{\boldsymbol\zeta,\hat{\beta}})^p]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:12.370262+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_72ecd209a86af12d9dde0e42e2eff4265f17536081077067cf3772c0180a285c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[OPEN PROBLEM - not a proved fact. Stated in arXiv:2608.25925 (problem environment labelled prob:recursive-alpha).] Construct a canonical family of monotone domain operators $\Theta_\alpha$ for recursive ordinals $\alpha$ such that:
