{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.604882+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7330848d7838da17ff18ef73448793e6d92e8d7a271352ed278207e7bcd8912c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop gbdl of arXiv:2606.30517, '\textsc{Convergence towards Ideal Poisson--Voronoi tessellations with a focus on Diestel--'] {\textsc{(Horoboundary of ${\rm DL}(p,q)$)}}

		The horoboundary of ${\rm DL}(p,q)$ consists of the following horofunctions:
		\begin{eqnarray*}
			\rho_{\omega}(y,y') &\coloneqq & d(y,\omega) \text{ given } w\in \partial T,\\
			\rho'_{\omega'}(y,y') &\coloneqq & d(y',\omega') \text{ given } w'\in \partial T',\\
			\rho_{x_0}(y,y') &\coloneqq & -2h(y\wedge x_0) - \left| h(y)-h(x_0) \right| = d(y,x_0)-2\max\{h(y),h(x_0)\} \text{ given } x_0\in T,\\
			\rho'_{x'_0}(y,y') &\coloneqq & -2h'(y'\wedge x'_0) - \left| h'(y')-h'(x'_0) \right| = d(y',x'_0)-2\max\{h'(y'),h'(x'_0)\} \text{ given } x'_0\in T',\\
			\rho_i(y,y') &\coloneqq & -\left| h(y)-i \right| = -\left| h'(y')+i \right| \text{ given } i\in\mathbb Z.
		\end{eqnarray*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.039233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73329981ae068c4e039807521a27a60ec6224e72cdec3f64e09c9436a71d0737","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-commutaror of arXiv:2512.17727, 'Stochastic transport equation with L\'evy noise'] Suppose that $u:\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}$ is locally Lebesgue essentially bounded and $b:\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}^d$ is locally Lebesgue integrable  such that $\operatorname{div} b :\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}$ is locally Lebesgue integrable. We define  an expression $\mathcal{R}_\ensuremath{\varepsilon}[b,u]$  by the following formula
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{R}_\ensuremath{\varepsilon}[b,u]:= \vartheta_\ensuremath{\varepsilon} \ast ( b\cdot  D u) -  b \cdot   D ( \vartheta_\ensuremath{\varepsilon} \ast u),
\end{equation}
where $b\cdot  D u$ is defined in Definition (def-weak derivative of the paper) and $\vartheta_\ensuremath{\varepsilon}$ is defined by (equation def-vartheta-eps of the paper),  and we call $ \mathcal{R}_\ensuremath{\varepsilon}[b,u]$ the  $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}$-commutator.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.805786+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73335bb05bf709b767ec0ac14d5a2ef7ed0f33f4e0984fb04083cd0905c83998","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop p:CDRP-scaling of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] There exists an event $\Xi_0\subset \Xi$ with $\mathbb{P}(\Xi_0)=1$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\roman*)}]
        \item\textup{(Translation).} For each $(u,z) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap\mathsf{T}(u,z)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s+u, x+z),(t+u, y+z)}\left(
                X(\cdot + u) - z
                \in S\right)
            \circ \mathcal{T}_{-u, -z}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}(X\in S).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Shearing).} For each $(r,\nu)\in\mathbb{R}^2$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap\mathsf{S}(r,\nu)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s, x+\nu(s-r)),(t, y+\nu(t-r))}\left(
                X(\cdot) - \nu(\cdot - r)
                \in S
            \right)
            \circ \mathcal{S}_{r, -\nu}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}(X\in S).
        \end{align*}
        \item\textup{(Diffusive scaling).}
        For each $\lambda>0$, on the event $\Xi_0\cap \mathsf{D}(\lambda)$, it holds for all $(s,x;t,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{4}_{\uparrow}$, all $\beta\geqslant 0$, and all Borel sets $S\in\mathcal{B}(C([s,t]))$ that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta\lambda^{-1/2}, (\lambda^2 s, \lambda x),(\lambda^2 t,\lambda y)}\left(
                \lambda^{-1}X(\lambda^2\cdot)
                \in S
            \right)
            \circ \mathcal{D}_{\lambda^{-2},\lambda^{-1}}
            =
            \mathbb{P}_{\beta,(s,x),(t,y)}\left(X\in S\right).
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
    The events $\mathsf{T}(u,z), \mathsf{S}(r,\nu), \mathsf{D}(\lambda)$ are defined in (t:AJRS-Z-scaling of the paper).
    Like in (t:AJRS-Z-scaling of the paper), the composition notation $\circ$ reflects that the above polymer probabilities are regarded as real-valued functions of the white noise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.282176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_733540549a5bb64b3cd71e5f721951833b09d5cbac94add88d9d4df3f88f9ce2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Under assumptions \textbf{H1}-\textbf{H3} and \(\bar h (x)= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{m}} h(x,y)\mu(\dd y)=0\). Define that 
\begin{equation}
u(x,y) =\int_{0}^{\infty}\mathcal{P}_t^x h(x, Y_t^x) \dd t.
\end{equation} 
Then \(u(x,y)\) is the solution to the Poisson equation \eqref{Poisson_eq}.
Moreover, \(u(x,y)\in C^2\). For all \((x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^n\times \mathbb{R}^m\), \(D_xu(x,y)\) and \(D^2_{xx}u(x,y)\) is linear growth, that is for all \((x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^m\times \mathbb{R}^n\), there exists a constant $L_u>0$ such that 
$$|D_xu(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{xx}u(x,y)|\le L_u(1+|x|+|y|).$$
And \(D_yu(x,y), D^2_{xy}u(x,y), D^2_{yy}u(x,y)\) is globally bounded, that is there exists a constant $L_u>0$ such that 
$$|D_yu(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{xy}u(x,y)|\vee|D^2_{yy}u(x,y)|\le L_u.$$
Moreover, when \(Y\) is independent of \(x\), \(\sup_{x\in \mathbb{R}^m}|u(x,y)|\le C(1+|y|)\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.316274+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7335d471e09cee598ca9a517e7affbfff2484c982ce054a909d5a5e52653de58","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:triple of arXiv:2601.10359, 'An Operator It\^o Formula for Volterra Gaussian Processes:\\ The Intrinsic Bracket via Cau'] A \emph{causal derivation--divergence triple} is $(D,\delta,\Pi)$ where:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item $D: \Dom(D) \subset L^2(\Omega) \to L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H})$ is a densely-defined closed linear operator (the \emph{derivation}) with $\mathbf{1}\in\Dom(D)$ and $D\mathbf{1}=0$.
\item $\delta: \Dom(\delta) \subset L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H}) \to L^2(\Omega)$ is the Hilbert space adjoint of $D$ (the \emph{divergence}):
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}[F\delta(u)] = \mathbb{E}[\inner{DF}{u}_\mathcal{H}], \qquad F \in \Dom(D),\; u \in \Dom(\delta).
\end{equation}
\item $\Pi: L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H}) \to L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{H})$ is a bounded projection (``causal'' or ``predictable'') with $\Pi^2 = \Pi$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.825297+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7336970761cf8a7bb364f09225fe4a08313267250fa58c23a21ad4be09fb129f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:optimal-four-mark of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every $h\geq2$, put
\[
 D_h^{\max}=\binom{h+2}{2}+\mathbf 1_{\{2\nmid h\}}.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\qquad \eta_0(h)=D_h^{\max}.\qquad}
\end{equation}
Writing
\[
 Q_h=1+\binom{h+1}{2},
\]
the minimum is attained by
\begin{equation}
 A_h^\star=\{0,1,Q_h,D_h^{\max}\}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\[
 R_h^{-1}(4)=\binom{h+2}{2}+1+\mathbf 1_{\{2\nmid h\}}
\]
in the positive-label interval convention.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.406071+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7337efdbd0482eea9dd0a90ed66bce2854f48f573f09cd62e1fafab486e3034f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2605.15781, 'Mean-Field Backward Stochastic Differential Equations with Nonlinear Resistance and Double'] Let  Assumption \ref{ass1ipterminalp} and \ref{assLR} hold. Given $C\in \mathcal{H}^p$, the BSDE with double mean reflections 
   \begin{equation}
   \begin{cases}
