{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.534001+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73c79a25c79d6b2d9d14d456f57ef7b4639af6fc80aad7efa7d5dcabbc9fb519","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] Consider \(W_{n}'\) a \(T_{n}\)-walk in \(\Z\), where \((T_{n})\)
satisfies \Cref{Assum:MaillardDimension} for some $D>1$. Assume that the jumps in \(W_{n}'\) are i.i.d. copies of
a centered variable \(J'\) such that for all \(\delta < 2D\), $\E(\lvert J' \rvert^{\delta}) < +\infty$.

