{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.272542+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_74385b682eabfd616b0c1f7c33ca60257c7c5c5fa8908e2a184ea803e8ee37a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop propim1 of arXiv:2507.07923, 'Optimal Sets and Quantization Errors under Geometric Constraints for Discrete Distribution'] Let $\beta_n$ be an unconstrained optimal set of $n$-means for $\mu$. Then, $U(\beta_n)$ is a constrained optimal set of $n$-points for $\mu$ with respect to the constraint $L$. The converse is also true, i.e., if $\beta_n$ is a constrained optimal set of $n$-points for $\mu$ with respect to the constraint $L$, then $U^{-1}(\beta_n)$ forms an unconstrained optimal set of $n$-means for $\mu$. Further,
\begin{equation}  Q_n^{(c)}=Q_n^{(u)}+\frac 1 {1+m^2}\sum_{j=1}^{n}(m Av[q_j^{(1)}, q_j^{(2)}] +c)^2(2^{1-q_j^{(1)}}-2^{-q_j^{(2)}}),
\end{equation}
if $\beta_n:=\set{Av[q_j^{(1)}, q_j^{(2)}] : 1\leq j\leq n}$, where $q_1^{(1)}=1$, $q_n^{(2)}=\infty$ and $q_j^{(2)}+1=q_{j+1}^{(1)}$ for $1\leq j\leq n-1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:16:49.558238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7438788e71b8bb504eeda69c19d3f108cbb51506d79e0e773d6da45c3f5c6a32","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defs 21 (Core) of arXiv:2509.06869, 'Infinite Interacting Brownian Motions and EVI Gradient Flows'] For $r>0$, $\mathcal C_r$ is defined as the space of functions $u$ such that
