{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.500435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_749496f15ba8eb0ea6dcda1e2261a32c3e77125f14a983fab75fbb3dfa6962d4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 25 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\alpha, \beta > 0$ be arbitrary. We denote by $\calc_2^{\alpha,\beta}([0,T],W)$ the space of all functions $\Xi : \Delta_T^2 \to W$ such that $\Xi_{t,t} = 0$ for all $t \in [0,T]$ and
\begin{align}
\| \Xi \|_{\alpha,\beta} := \| \Xi \|_{\alpha} + \| \delta \Xi \|_{\beta} < \infty.
\end{align}
Here $\| \Xi \|_{\alpha}$ denotes the H\"older norm
\begin{align}
\| \Xi \|_{\alpha} := \sup_{\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{(s,t) \in \Delta_T^2}{s < t}} \frac{|\Xi_{s,t}|}{|t-s|^{\alpha}}.
\end{align}
Furthermore, the function $\delta \Xi : \Delta_T^3 \to W$ is defined as
\begin{align}
\delta \Xi_{s,u,t} := \Xi_{s,t} - \Xi_{s,u} - \Xi_{u,t}, \quad (s,u,t) \in \Delta_T^3,
\end{align}
and its H\"older norm is defined as
\begin{align}
\| \delta \Xi \|_{\beta} := \sup_{\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{(s,u,t) \in \Delta_T^3}{s < u < t}} \frac{|\delta \Xi_{s,u,t}|}{|t-s|^{\beta}}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:18:54.371489+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_749518e9015bf0eded9b0c97a4438e21516797c323b0f5e1f9b89b855ab1a1d3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The following happen, in relation with separability:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The Hilbert space $H=L^2[-1,1]$ is separable, with orthonormal basis coming by applying Gram-Schmidt to the basis $\{x^k\}_{k\in\mathbb N}$, coming from Weierstrass.

\item In fact, any $H=L^2(\mathbb R,\mu)$, with $d\mu(x)=f(x)dx$, is separable, and the same happens in higher dimensions, for $H=L^2(\mathbb R^N,\mu)$, with $d\mu(x)=f(x)dx$.

\item More generally, given a separable abstract measured space $X$, the associated Hilbert space of square-summable functions $H=L^2(X)$ is separable.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224287+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7496322c24a9324013d72b001c4c17766e3624bf8d81f4fbad9d8cce69e8e631","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:fractionalbubbles of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $\gamma\in (0,n/2)$. Then for all $w \in \dot{H}^{\gamma} (\mathbb{R}^n )$ we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \|w \|_{L^{2^{*}}(\mathbb{R}^n )}^2 \leq S(n,\gamma) \|(-\Delta )^{\gamma/2}w \|_{L^{2} (\mathbb{R}^n )}^2 = S(n,\gamma) \int_{\mathbb{R}^n } w (-\Delta )^{\gamma}w  dx,
    \end{equation*}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        S(n,\gamma) = \frac{\Gamma ((n-2\gamma)/2)}{\Gamma ((n+2\gamma)/2)} |\operatorname{vol}(\mathbb{S}^n )|^{-\frac{2\gamma}{n}}.
    \end{align*}
    We have equality if and only if
    \begin{align}
        w(x) = c\left( \frac{\mu}{|x-x_0 |^2 + \mu^2 }  \right)^{\frac{n-2\gamma}{2}} \quad x\in \mathbb{R}^n,
    \end{align}
    for $c\in \mathbb{R}$, $\mu > 0$ and $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^n$. We denote the set of these functions as $\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n} $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:47:04.945057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_74990cb5c336cfbd7622006cc8a015ed86332d4b50c10ba1fe15eae8d4a9ac3d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:bicausaltptplanbetweenSDEs of arXiv:2412.02948, 'Causal transport on path space'] $\pi \in \Pi_{bc}\deln{\mu^{\sigma,b}_z, \mu^{\overline{\sigma}, \overline{b}}_{\tilde{z}}}$ if and only if  there exists a stochastic basis $\del{\Omega, \mathcal{G}, \del{\mathcal{G}_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}, \mathbb{P}}$ 
and two $d$-dimensional $\del{\mathcal{G}_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$-Brownian motions $B = \del{B_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$ and $\tilde{B} = \deln{\tilde{B}_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$
        and $\del{\mathcal{G}_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$-adapted processes $Z = \deln{Z_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$, $\tilde{Z} = \deln{\tilde{Z}_t}_{t \in \sbr{0,1}}$ such that 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\deln{Z,B}$ and  $\deln{\tilde{Z},\tilde{B}}$ satisfy  (equation eqn:firstsde of the paper) and (equation eqn:2ndsde of the paper) respectively, and
            \item $\pi$ is the joint law of  $(Z,\tilde{Z})$: $\pi = \deln{Z,\tilde{Z}}_{\#}\mathbb{P}$.
        \end{itemize}
        In particular, the joint law of $(B,\tilde{B})$ under $\mathbb{P}$ is a bicausal Wiener coupling. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.354203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_749b0863d366f6dea27073d86ac0faba7b4ce9c16181f4742fa3381b9a9a8d06","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop.dpi of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Fix $p > 4$ and $(t_0,m_0) \in [0,1) \times \mathcal{P}_p({\mathbb R})$. Suppose that $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{F}, \mathbb{P})$ is a filtered probability space hosting a Brownian motion $B$, and an $\mathcal{F}(t_0)$-measurable random variable $X_0$ with $X_0 \sim m_0$. Let $\alpha$ be an $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable process with
    \begin{align*}
        \|\alpha(t)\|_2 \leq c_0,\qquad \|\alpha(t)\|_{\infty} \leq C \qquad \text{ for a.e.\ $t\in[t_0,t_0+h]$},
    \end{align*}
    for $c_0=\sqrt{2/\pi}$ and some arbitrary constant $C<\infty$. Then, for any $h\in(0,1-t_0)$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{V}_p^+(t_0,m_0) \leq \mathcal{V}_p^+\Big( t_0 + h, \mathcal{L}\big( X(t_0 + h) \big)\Big),
    \end{align*}
    where $X$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        X(t) \coloneqq X_0 + \int_{t_0}^t \alpha(s)\, ds + B(t) - B(t_0), \quad t_0 \leq t \leq 1.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.520141+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_749d74440fc4e5787403c8cda35e2662523842a060bf7855eb9d8ba96e8c9c3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary Law of large numbers of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{thm:lln}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$, and suppose that $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ is a transitive kernel satisfying $J(x,y) = \Omega(d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha \in (0,2)$. 

