{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.539719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_749db02fc03d63ab0f7e50982f27e8cc4b8104cba069a017e274753ef311bf28","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L-Lip of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] \noindent  For each $n\geq 1$, the map $L_{n}:[0,1]^{2}
\rightarrow \lbrack 0,1]$ is uniformly Lipschitz on $[0,1]^{2}$.
Consequently, since under the standing density assumption $L_{0}\in
C([0,1]^{2})$, the full family $(L_{n})_{n\geq 0}$ is relatively compact in $
C([0,1]^{2})$ with the uniform topology.

In particular, every sequence $\{L_{n_{k}}\}_{k=0}^{\infty }$ with $
n_{k}\rightarrow \infty $ admits a subsequence, again denoted by $
\{L_{n_{k}}\}$, and a limit $L^{\ast }:[0,1]^{2}\rightarrow \lbrack 0,1]$
such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{(x_{1},x_{2})\in \lbrack 0,1]^{2}}\left\vert
L_{n_{k}}(x_{1},x_{2})-L^{\ast }(x_{1},x_{2})\right\vert \rightarrow 0\quad 
\text{as }k\rightarrow \infty .  
\end{equation}
Moreover, each such $L^{\ast }$ is a continuous distribution function on $
[0,1]^{2}$, and its marginals 
\begin{equation}
L_{1}^{\ast }(x)=L^{\ast }(x,1),\qquad L_{2}^{\ast }(x)=L^{\ast }(1,x)

\end{equation}
are continuous strictly increasing distribution functions on $[0,1]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.500957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_74a1dfab027fa716a24b0645bb7c3161a796e1c5e6163ef00d17ebcd8d5b67b0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem : FdTmon/UpperBound/Initialisation of arXiv:2412.03320, 'Large deviation principle at speed $n$ for the random metric in first-passage percolation'] The metric induced by $\mu$ on $X$ satisfies
