{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.011097+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772c52edd0f9577e31bfc531671795dc8bf65bccb3db27bb432a1471a42aa698","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2605.04168, 'Error analysis for learning fractional stochastic differential equations with applications'] Suppose that the Assumptions of Section \ref{sec:assumptions} are valid. Let $\tilde{\delta} < \HeM$, $s,s_2\in (0,1)$ be such that we can choose $\alpha \in (1-\HeM, \min(1/2,s\tilde{\delta},s_2))$. Set $\tilde\sigma = \sigma - \sigma_n$ and $\tilde b = b - b_n$. Then for any $\lambda \geq 0$ and any 
$q>\frac{d}{1-s}$, $q_2>\frac{1}{1-s_2}$, and $\rho > \frac{1}{1-\alpha}$ such that $q\ge\rho$, we have, for suitably large $\lambda$, that
 \begin{align*}
   \Vert X_\cdot - X_\cdot^n\Vert_{\alpha,\lambda} 
    &\lesssim \Vert\partial_t\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} +\Vert\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\partial_t\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)}  \\
    &+\Vert \nabla_z \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)}  + \Vert  \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)} . 
\end{align*}
    Consequently, we have
     \begin{align*}
   \Vert X_\cdot - X_\cdot^n\Vert_{\alpha,\infty} 
    &\lesssim \Vert\partial_t\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} +\Vert\nabla_z\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\partial_t\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)} + \Vert\tilde\sigma\Vert_{(q_2,q)}  \\
    &+\Vert \nabla_z \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)}  + \Vert  \tilde b\Vert_{(\rho,q)} . 
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.795594+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772d8b4e9027d3f8f0b196b64b3be1c2e3802fb5d21b238a69e1e00fcca7b4c5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop dimshe1sec5 of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] For each $\gamma\in(0,2\sqrt{2})$, the set of $\gamma$-thick points $T^{\gamma}$ of the SHEF $\Psi$ has Hausdorff dimension
\begin{equation}

    \dim_H(T^{\gamma}) = \min\left \{4-\frac{\gamma^2}{2},3-\frac{\gamma^2}{4}\right \}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.901983+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772dd2497cff77ed64cb2a0ac9ff9251103921d53495a39bbc079d569da38608","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition GWlengthera of arXiv:2608.09856, '\bf Mass erasure on measured $\bbR$-trees,\\ applications to L\'evy forests'] Let $\xi, c,\varrho, \texttt Q_{\xi,c}$ and $\texttt P_{\! \xi, c, \varrho}$ be as in Definition (meaGWregedef of the paper). We assume that $\xi(1)\! = \!  0$. Let $b\! \in \!  \mathbb{R}_+^*$. We recall from ((deflengthera of the paper)) (and Lemma (lengtheraprop of the paper)) the definition of $b$-length erasure $R_b \colon \mathbb{T}^{0}_{\! c} \! \to \!   \mathbb{T}^{0}_{\! c}$. We also recall that $\mathtt{Ht} \colon\mathbb{T}^{0}_{\! c} \! \to \!  \mathbb{R}_+$  stands for the height of compact $\mathbb{R}$-trees. To simplify, we set $\alpha \! :=\! \texttt Q_{\xi, c} (\mathtt{Ht} \! \leq \!  b) $, which belongs to $(0, 1)$ by ((heightGWexpli of the paper)). Then $R_b $ under $ \texttt Q_{\xi,c}(\, \cdot \, | \, \mathtt{Ht} \! > \!  b )$ (resp.~under 
$\texttt P_{\! \xi, c, \varrho}$) is a GW($\xi^*\! , c^*$)-tree (resp.~a GW($\xi^*\! , c^*\! , \varrho^*$)-forest) where for all $s\! \in \!  [0, 1]$, 
\begin{equation}

c^*\!\!  :=\! c (1\! -\! \varphi'_{\xi} (\alpha)), \; \varphi_{\xi^*} \! (s) \! = \!  s+ \frac{\varphi_\xi (\alpha + (1\! -\! \alpha) s) \! -\! \alpha \! -\! (1\! -\! \alpha) s }{(1\! -\! \alpha)(1\! -\! \varphi'_{\xi} (\alpha))}, \; \varphi_{\varrho^*}\!  (s) \! = \!  \varphi_{\varrho} ( \alpha + (1\! -\! \alpha) s). 
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.518414+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772dfa16d66db1a2a10a7c794dba14442fc445969c7c8c393d9fb3aaf3c25125","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:biskup_iterative_renormalisation of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{lem:second_iterative_renormalisation}
    Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$ and suppose that $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ is a transitive kernel satisfying $J(x,y) = \Omega (d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha \in (0,2)$.

    Let $\beta > \beta_c$, and let $((\Tile{i}{j})_{j \geq 0})_{i \geq 0}$ be the renormalisation scheme in \thref{def:renorm_scheme_2}.

    For $\delta \in (\max(0, 1-\alpha),2-\alpha)$ and $c > 0$, for  $r_0$ sufficiently large it holds for any tile $\Tile{i}{j}$ that 

    \begin{equation}

        \mathbf{P}_{\beta}
        (
            \Tile{i}{j} \text{ is bad}
        )
        \leq 
        \exp
        (
            -
            c
            (r_i/r_0)^{d(2 - \alpha - \delta)}
        ).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.066423+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772e4650d592565bc5fca344d598269feb25cbc79f58fb7fcf0e31f3054f2f33","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] For any vertices $u$ and $v$, the Green function $G^\kappa(u,v)$ is finite if and only if $\kappa \geq \kappa_c$. Furthermore, for $\kappa\geq \kappa_c$, 
\begin{equation} 
G^\kappa(u,v) = \frac{\tau_\kappa^{d(u,v)}}{1-\tau_\kappa (1+ \kappa)^{-1}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.780136+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772e53ad38bae8e1bdf990f235e6436cec1fbb252ff7ad807a7b40f37305aa93","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:chi2 of arXiv:2608.01253, 'The pseudo-quantum representation of \\finite reversible Markov chains'] Under the pseudo-wave map the semigroup acts by
$\psi_{\pi_t}=D^{-1}T_tD\,\psi_{\pi_0}=e^{\Lambda t(A-\mathrm{I})}\psi_{\pi_0}$ with
$A=A^{T}$, so $T_t$ is self-adjoint in $L^{2}(1/\nu)$. The stationary law maps to
the unit vector $\psi_\nu=\sqrt\nu$, and the $\chi^{2}$ divergence is the
corresponding squared distance to it,

\begin{equation}
\chi^{2}(p\,\|\,\nu)=\sum_i\frac{(p_i-\nu_i)^{2}}{\nu_i}=\|\psi_p-\psi_\nu\|^{2}.

\end{equation}

Consequently, with $\gamma$ the spectral gap of the chain,
$\chi^{2}(\pi_t\,\|\,\nu)\le e^{-2\gamma t}\,\chi^{2}(\pi_0\,\|\,\nu)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:13:21.317551+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772f27291117f3ed6c1146dcd30428947002c354c606a098c05426f3c71b34fc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 26 of arXiv:2609.01485, 'On the Ramification of Quadratic Fields where the Torsion of a Rational Elliptic Curve Gro'] Let $E/\mathbb{Q}$ be a rational elliptic curve such that there exists $P\in E(K)[7]\setminus E(\mathbb{Q})$, where $K/\mathbb{Q}$ is a quadratic field. Suppose that $E$ has additive reduction at $7$. Then the following are equivalent:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $7 \mid d_K$, where $d_K$ is the discriminant of $K$.
        \item The Kodaira symbol of $E$ at $7$ is not $II$.
    \end{itemize}
    Moreover, if $7 \mid d_K$, then $E$ has Kodaira symbol $IV^*$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.270352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_772f7a5c2d5af3b0d597afe5e96816bf6322d2c339bfb329c06d7e603ea45d44","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem setup1 of arXiv:2412.09535, 'Local limit theorem for joint subgraph counts'] Let $0 \leq \alpha,\beta \leq 1-\eps$ be real parameters and let $k$ be a nonnegative integer. Then $\abs{\sete[e^{if}]} = n^{-\omega_{\lambda,\ell,\eps}(1)}$, provided that
\begin{cond}
	\item  For all $H$ with $k+2$ vertices, $n^{k+2}\Delta_H^2 \leq n^{2+k\beta-\eps}$;
    \item  There is some connected $H$ with $k + 2$ vertices such that $n^{2\alpha+k\beta+\eps} \leq n^{k+2}\Delta_H^2$;
    \item  For every $H$ with greater than $k+2$ vertices 
    we have $n^{v(H)}\Delta_H^2 \leq n^{2\alpha+k\beta - \eps}$.
\end{cond}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.549763+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_773045f49c166a3f28f1d3ea3042b11331531e04fb36b766fa3c37f6cd960a7a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition lem:CLE-shrink of arXiv:2603.28161, 'Boundary four-point connectivities of conformal loop ensembles'] Suppose $x_1<x_2<x_3<x_4$, and $\Gamma$ is a $\CLE_\kappa$ configuration on $\mathbbm{H}$. We have
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $E$ be the event that there exists a loop $\ell\in\Gamma$ such that $\ell\cap I(x_1,r_1)\neq\emptyset$, $\ell\cap B(x_i,r_i)\neq\emptyset$ for $i=2,3$, and $\ell\cap I(x_4,r_4)\neq\emptyset$. Then $\prod_{i=1}^4 r_i^{-h}\mathbbm{P}[E]$ converges to $CG^{(1234)}(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)$ as taking first $r_1\to0$, then $r_2,r_3\to0$, finally $r_4\to0$.
    \item Let $F$ be the event that there exist two loops $\ell,\ell'\in\Gamma$ such that $\ell$ intersects $I(x_1,r_1)$ and $B(x_2,r_2)$, while $\ell'$ intersects $I(x_4,r_4)$ and $B(x_3,r_3)$. Then $\prod_{i=1}^4 r_i^{-h}\mathbbm{P}[F]$ converges to $C'G^{(12)(34)}(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)$ as taking first $r_1\to0$, then $r_2\to0$, then $r_3\to0$, finally $r_4\to0$.
\end{itemize}
Here $C$ and $C'$ are two constants in $(0,\infty)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.303743+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7731de030344d88709b0d897659d1669cc71e1e5d430e76a87acec02ccfa4a09","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2604.22130, 'Reflected Stochastic Differential Equations Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Nonlinear C'] Given two reflected $G$-BSDEs with parameters $(x^i,f^i,h^i,g^i,\alpha^i,\beta^i)$, $i=1,2$ satisfying  Assumption (ass2 of the paper), we additionally suppose in the following:
