{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.814020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77b312e7820e0b86073ed323a6334b6d5ab941e9d438f43f56499297e3a7d6c8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:virtual of arXiv:2609.01672, '\textbf{All-Minors Matrix-Tree Theory for Superport Networks:\\ Completed Quotient-Inciden'] Let \(P\) be an oriented forest of \(q\) virtual edges on vertices of \(G\) (the virtual edges need not be physical edges and may involve interior vertices). Let \(D_P\) be its reduced incidence matrix after grounding \(g\), and put
\begin{equation}
Z_P=D_P^TK^{-1}D_P,\qquad \mathcal L_P=Z_P^{-1}.
\end{equation}
For each physical spanning forest \(F\), contract its components in \(P\) to obtain a quotient virtual-port multigraph \(H_F^P\) with reduced incidence \(B_F^P=Q_FD_P\). Define \(\chi_F^P(S)=\det B_F^P[:,S]\). Then every inverse-Gram minor satisfies
\begin{equation}
\det Z_P[A,B]
=\frac{1}{\tau_c(G)}\sum_{F\in\mathcal{F}_{r+1}}
\chi_F^P(A)\chi_F^P(B)w(F).
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}
\Phi_P:=\sum_{\substack{F\in\mathcal{F}_{q+1}\\E(P)\text{ spans a tree of }H_F^P}}w(F),
\end{equation}
then for \(|I|=|J|=k\), \(r=q-k\), let \(E_I\) denote the selector matrix in the inherited virtual-edge order and define
\begin{equation}
\widehat\chi_F^P(I):=\det\begin{pmatrix}B_F^P\\E_I^T\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{align}
\det\mathcal L_P[I,J]
&=\frac{1}{\Phi_P}
  \sum_{F\in\mathcal{F}_{r+1}}
  \widehat\chi_F^P(I)\widehat\chi_F^P(J)w(F).

\end{align}
All coefficients remain \(0\) or \(\pm1\), and a completed determinant is nonzero exactly when its complementary virtual-edge set is a spanning tree of \(H_F^P\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.736420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77b39b5580fb11fe737a24f2f72a413a4fe2d45958d2efe099d4644599e2ae48","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma key_lemma_delocalized_eigenvector of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Take $z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta\in \mathbf D\left({\tau}\right)$ with $E=\gamma_k\in \qa{E^{-},E^+}$ and $\eta\sim N^{-2/3+\varepsilon_L}k^{-1/3}$ for a small constant $\varepsilon_L>0$ (recall that we assume $k\in[\![{1,DN/2}]\!]$). Under the assumptions of (mix of the paper), there exists a constant $c_L>0$ (depending on $\varepsilon_L,\delta_A,\varepsilon_A$) such that 
