c_n\sim Q n^{-a}
\quad\text{as }n\to\infty,
\end{equation}
where
$$
a=1-\frac{2q(\beta+1)}{\alpha+\beta+1}
\quad\text{and}\quad 
Q=\frac{\Gamma(\frac{1+\theta}{1+\alpha+\beta})}
{\Gamma(\frac{1}{1+\alpha+\beta})}.
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.611179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77e4a3f2dd6ee1eeb6051a9edf2f0975862d130b8f16b66760ee994872daa53f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2510.27451, 'Bi-martingale optimal transport and its applications'] Assume the prerequisites of Theorem \ref{thm:approx_MOT_intro}, except that $\mu,\nu$ may be any probability measures in $\mathcal{P}_2^\ba(\Rd)$ (sharing the barycentre, but not necessarily in convex order). Then, up to selecting a subsequence, $\gamma_n \rightharpoonup \gamma$, where $\gamma$ is a solution of the following problem,
