{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.065231+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77f93cc7dd0e64278c9bf6cd71d90a3bb4f1ace2539b4f82ec2da36c50c06ae3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 30 (Trajectorial completeness) of arXiv:2606.17452, 'Optional Stopping for Superhedging Supermartingales'] Suppose $(S^n)_{n\ge 0}$ is a sequence in $\mathcal{S}$ satisfying
	\begin{equation}
	S^n_i = S^{n+1}_i, \;\; 0 \leq i \leq n,
	\end{equation}
	for all $n\in \mathbb{N}_0$. Then, its \emph{limit} is defined as
	$$
	\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} S^n\equiv \overline{S}\equiv (\overline{S}_i)_{i \geq 0},~~\mbox{wherein}~~~\overline{S}_i \equiv S^i_i.
	$$
	Denote by $\overline{\Se}$ be the set of all such limits $\overline{S}$. Then, $\overline{\Se}$ is called the \emph{trajectorial completion} of $\Se$ and  the trajectory set $\Se$ is said to be  \emph{trajectorially complete}, (TC) for short, if $\Se=\overline{\Se}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.027619+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_77f9d178cbd81b4b59e8230e184930dc215a56c05e425245090442b21478db55","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2-l09 of arXiv:2412.04015, 'Linear fluctuation of interfaces in Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics'] There exists a finite constant ${\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N)$, depending
