{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.308698+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_788063aa56a71c0ccd825c28c675f10f57a6f6eea90db44ae6f37172e716af97","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:HWIPU of arXiv:2507.08692, 'Some notes on higher order concentration of measure'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on ${\mathbb R}^n$ which satisfies a Poincar\'{e}-type inequality as well as $\int x_i d\mu = 0$ for any $i=1, \ldots,n$, and define $f\colon {\mathbb R}^n \to {\mathbb R}$ by $f(x) := x^TAx$, where $A=(a_{ij})_{ij}$ is a symmetric $n \times n$ matrix. Then,
