{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:22:48.882312+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78d115072f7be2f0aae1a7e2677e5d05be10562501f5be1ae2ca8d4eb4968a75","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] For an increasing $f\colon X\to Y$, we define $\varphi_\alpha(f)\colon\varphi_\alpha(X)\to\varphi_\alpha(Y)$ recursively as follows:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\varphi_\alpha(f)(0)=0$.
        \item $\varphi_\alpha(f)(E_x)=E_{f(x)}$.
        \item $\varphi_\alpha(f)(\varphi_i(\xi))=\varphi_i(\varphi_\alpha(f)(\xi))$.
        \item For principal terms $p_0,\ldots,p_{m-1}$ in $\varphi_\alpha(X)$ with $m>1$, we define
        \begin{equation*}
        \varphi_\alpha(f)(p_0+\cdots+p_{m-1})
        =
        \varphi_\alpha(f)(p_0)+\cdots+\varphi_\alpha(f)(p_{m-1}).
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}
    We also define the support transformation $\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X\colon \varphi_\alpha(X)\to [X]^{<\omega}$ recursively as follows:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(0)=\varnothing$.
        \item $\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(E_x)=\{x\}$.
        \item $\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(\varphi_i(\xi))=\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(\xi)$.
        \item For principal terms $p_0,\ldots,p_{m-1}$ in $\varphi_\alpha(X)$ with $m>1$,
        \begin{equation*}
        \supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(p_0+\cdots+p_{m-1})
        =
        \bigcup_{i<m}\supp^{\varphi_\alpha}_X(p_i).
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.118717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78d18d271585a655283a814f870ce6dba1097a7a446da6ddcf04e80da238867f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Ergodic:WeakErgodic of arXiv:2608.17031, 'A shape theorem for periodic branching diffusion'] Let $\nu\in C^{2,\alpha}_p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be the density of the unique invariant measure of $(\dot{Z}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ on $
    \mathbb{T}^d$ and $f\in C^{0,\alpha}_p(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be such that $\nu(f)=0$. Fix $\epsilon>0$. There exists a constant $C=C(d,\|f\|_{0,\alpha})>0$ such that, for all $t$ sufficiently large (depending on $d$, $\|f\|_{0,\alpha}$, and $\epsilon$),
