{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.323803+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78f3237e095ceb0889f4c95fb1f2a61d46a2738ba957d3300207b4f1a3105c11","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 (Windowed Equivalence) of arXiv:2607.23526, 'Equivalence of Canonical and Microcanonical Ensembles for Euclidean Lattices'] Suppose $H(\Lambda)$ is nonlattice. Fix $\Delta>0$, let $E_N\in\R$ satisfy $E_N/N\to u>0$, and set $\beta=m^{-1}(u)$. Then $G_N(E_N,\Delta)>0$ for all sufficiently large $N$, and for every fixed $k\geq1$,
\begin{equation}
\left\|(\pi_k)_*\mu^{\mc}_{N,E_N,\Delta}-p_\beta^{\otimes k}\right\|_{\TV}\to0,\quad N\to+\infty.
\end{equation}
Moreover, let $u_N=E_N/N$ and $\beta_N=m^{-1}(u_N)$, then
\begin{equation}
G_N(E_N,\Delta)=\frac{Z(\beta_N)^Ne^{\beta_NE_N}}{\sqrt{2\pi N\sigma^2(\beta_N)}}\left(\int_0^{\Delta}e^{\beta_Nt}dt\right)(1+o(1)).
\end{equation}
Consequently, the thermodynamic limit
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to+\infty}\frac{1}{N}R_{N,E_N,\Delta}^{\mc}=\psi(\beta)+\beta u=s(u).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.890252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78f71ea44399e62304340d3b7b88745bc97f00322add42a392643249040745b6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:two-point of arXiv:2607.18824, 'Non-Abelian multiplicative chaos on the circle'] For all $z_1,z_2\in\mathbb D$ distinct,
    \begin{equation}
    \left|1-z_1\bar z_2\right|^\frac{2\lambda_\rho^-}{\kappa+\check h}\leq\mathcal{G}_{\kappa,\rho}(z_1,z_2)\leq\left|1-z_1\bar z_2\right|^\frac{2\lambda_\rho^+}{\kappa+\check h},
    \end{equation}
    where $\lambda_\rho^\pm$ are defined in (equation eq:lambda_rho of the paper). Moreover, if $\frac{2\lambda_\rho^+}{\kappa+\check h}>-1$, the family $(\mathcal{G}_{\kappa,\rho}(e^{-\epsilon}\,\cdot,e^{-\delta}\,\cdot)|_{\mathbb S^1\times\mathbb S^1})_{\epsilon,\delta>0}$ converges almost everywhere and in $L^1(\mathbb S^1\times\mathbb S^1)$ as $\epsilon,\delta\to0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.368485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78f80093fadf17d75bda7f36e33a54730820c498f67390f667fb0866b6ea9bf6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2606.03534, 'Construction and simulation of a path-valued model of dendrite development'] A solution to the \textit{martingale problem} for a generator $\mathcal{A}: \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A}) \rightarrow B(\W)$ is a measurable stochastic process $\omega_t$ with state space $\W$, defined on some probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F},P)$, such that for all $f \in \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A})$, 
    \begin{align*}
        f(\omega_t) - f(\omega_0)- \int_0^t \mathcal{A}f(\omega_s) \, ds \,,
    \end{align*}
    is a martingale with respect to its natural filtration. We say the martingale problem for $\mathcal{A}$ is \textit{well-posed} if there exists a unique solution to the martingale problem.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:51:11.961198+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78f830556d170b700d1019f74568dec2ce11bb0bd65415cc2cd5e10cb3687639","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 (Uniform first Malliavin derivative error) of arXiv:2608.08177, 'Uniform-in-Time Smoluchowski--Kramers Approximation\\ in Total Variation for Fractional SD'] For every $\gamma\in(1/2,H)$ and $p\ge2$,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{t\ge0}
 \norm{\D Z_t^\mu-\D Z_t}_{L^p(\Omega;\cH)}
 \le C_{p,\gamma}\mu^{2\gamma-1}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.713002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78f884e86ddb507d1bb8f91c5cb8eab93f4e67c4596fc9cec42fd17cd1af3e17","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Pro: tail of arXiv:2411.04778, 'Coupling between Brownian motion and random walks on the infinite percolation cluster'] There exists $C:=C(d)>0$, such that for every point $x,y \in \mathscr{C}_\infty$, we have
		\[
		p^\omega(t, x, y) \leqslant \begin{cases}C \exp \left(-\frac{\mathsf{d}(x,y)^{2}}{C t}\right) & \text { if }\mathsf{d}(x,y)\leqslant t,  \\ C \exp \left(-\frac{\mathsf{d}(x,y)}{C}\left(1+\ln \frac{\mathsf{d}(x,y)}{t}\right)\right) & \text { if }\mathsf{d}(x,y)>t.\end{cases}
		\]
		Here, $\mathsf{d}(x,y)$ denotes the chemical distance on $\mathscr{C}_\infty$, i.e., minimum length of the open paths connecting $x$ and $y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:19.153856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78fce7468d27e90e1884efaceb80a6e9cb15211e60c3ef9cf1dc9369b65634cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma3.4 of arXiv:2609.02607, 'Spatial inhomogeneity for a three-dimensional doubly degenerate nutrient system with indir'] Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^3$ be  a smoothly bounded domain, $k>-1$ and $\beta\in (k+2,k+\frac{8}{3})$. Then for all $m^*>0$, one can find $C_1=C_1(k, \beta)>0$ and $C_2=C_2(m^*,k,\beta)>0$ with the property that whenever $\varphi\in C^1(\overline \Omega)$ and $\psi \in C^1(\overline \Omega)$ are positive 
in $\overline \Omega$ fulfilling  $\int_{\Omega}\varphi\leq m^*$,
  \begin{align}
    \int_{\Omega}\varphi^{\beta}\psi\leq  2\int_{\Omega}\varphi^{k}\psi|\nabla \varphi|^2+C_1\int_\Omega\frac{|\nabla \psi|^{\frac{2\beta}{\beta-k-2}}}{\psi^{\frac{2\beta}{\beta-k-2}-1}}+C_2\int_{\Omega}\varphi \psi
\end{align} holds.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.755239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7901d4daefd62bc6a45bbbc93b0c17ba5e8d65311615410fea3199e2c5a01253","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2604.11691, 'Asymptotic behavior of spatio-temporal point processes of exceedances'] Let $\{\bm X_{t}, \, t \in \mathbb{Z}\}$ be a multivariate strictly stationary regularly varying time series with tail process $\{\bm Y_{t}, \, t \in \mathbb{Z}\}$ such that Condition \ref{con_1} and Condition \ref{con_2} hold. In addition, let $N_n(u)$ be the sequence of point processes given in \eqref{N_n} with $u>0$ satisfying the bound in Equation \eqref{eq:risk-bound}.
Then, $N_n(u)$ converges weakly to a point process $N(u)$ on 
$[0,1] \times \mathcal{X}_u$ which has the same distribution as the superposition of a random number of independent and identical copies of cluster processes, i.e.,
\begin{align} 
N(u)=_d{}\sum_{i=1}^{T} \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \delta_{(V_i,s,\bm Z_{ij})} ~\mathds{1}\{r^{(s)}(Z_{ij}) >u\},
\end{align}
where $T$ is a Poisson random variable with mean $\theta$
and $$\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \bigg(\sum_{j=-\infty}^{\infty} \delta_{(V_i,s, \bm Z_{ij})}\bigg)_{i=1}^{\infty}$$ are i.i.d.~copies of the process $C$ defined in \eqref{C} and independent of $T$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.573017+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_790321ec89b16a9a42bd12059eb55c48cae9acb90a42771dead9d4a2d9cbd7d2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2511.01058, 'Limit profiles and cutoff for the Burnside process on Sylow double cosets'] Let \(P\) be the transition kernel for the Sylow--Burnside process when \(k=1\). Let \(\pi\) be the stationary distribution for \(P\). Then for all \(t \ge 1\)
        \[
            \Vert P^t_\sigma - \pi \Vert_{\mathrm{TV}} =\begin{cases}
                \left(1-\frac{1}{n_1}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right)^t + \frac{n_2}{n_1(n_1+n_2)}\left(1-\frac{1}{p}-\frac{p-1}{p(p-2)!}\right)^t & \text{if } |H\sigma H| = p. \\
                \frac{n_1}{n_1+n_2}\left(1-\frac{1}{p}-\frac{p-1}{p(p-2)!}\right)^t                                                                 & \text{if }|H\sigma H|=p^2.\end{cases}
        \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.722509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_790498f4f1ca84b4653b3cfed2b30efd4eb791351c0c0e028c6e2ea4e9809a02","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm5.1' of arXiv:2606.01944, 'Propagation of Chaos for Mean-field Mean Reflected Backward Stochastic Differential Equati'] The reflected BSDE (equation eq7' of the paper) has a unique solution $(\{Y^i,Z^i\}_{1\leq i\leq N},K^{(N)})$ with $K^{(N)}\in \mathcal{A}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)})$, $Y^i\in \mathcal{S}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)})$ and $Z^i\in \mathcal{H}^2(\mathbb{F}^{(N)};\mathbb{R}^N)$ for $i=1,\cdots,N$. Moreover, there exists a constant $C$ independent of $N$, such that
\begin{align*}
    \mathsf{E}\left[|K^{(N)}_T|^2\right]\leq C.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.318759+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_79049cbc0678c01b4b44db03fd0bc7f62aa3790a4ded8f9b1ee37748d49c17af","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] A oriented embedded surface \(S\subset M\) is \emph{relatively carried by} \(\tau\) if
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(S \setminus \mathrm{int}(T_{\tau})\) is carried by the branched surface \(\tau^{(2)}\) in \(M\setminus \mathrm{int}(T_{\tau})\), and
\item each component of \(S\cap T_{\tau}\) is either a meridional disk or an annulus.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.446631+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7907a6fa2a802d4ea3e04b234a943b4464e768781034cac696a4e7f1ae3ec779","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.15843, 'Long-time behaviour of DLRA for SDEs'] Suppose Assumptions~(H1)--(H5) hold, and let $(U_t,Y_t)_{t\ge0}$ be the solution of Eqn.~\eqref{eq:DO} with initial condition $U_0 \in V_r(\bb R^d)$ and $Y_0 \in L^2(\Omega,\bb R^r)$. Assume further that there exists $\sigma_{Y_0} > 0$ such that $\bb E[Y_0 Y_0^\top] \succeq \sigma_{Y_0} I_r$.
For any $Q\in O(r)$, define $\al R_Q(u,y) := (Qu, Qy)$. Then, for any $t>0$ we have
\begin{equation}
    \scr L_{(U_t^{\al R_Q(U_0,Y_0)},Y_t^{\al R_Q(U_0,Y_0)})}=\scr L_{(QU_t^{(U_0,Y_0)},QY_t^{(U_0,Y_0)})},
\end{equation}
where $(U_0^{(U_0,Y_0)},Y_0^{(U_0,Y_0)})=(U_0,Y_0)$ and $(U_0^{\al R_Q(U_0,Y_0)},Y_0^{\al R_Q(U_0,Y_0)})=(QU_0,QY_0)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.544423+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7907ea470b0bd348599ddcb45a32e1e370e48a84e57c4cda11530e9101061ac5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: Bounds for M of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] Let $\beta$ be large enough. Then, the single-site marginal of $\mu_A$, which was defined in (equation def: single-site marg, diagonal elements of the paper), is bounded as follows
\begin{equation}
    \pi_A(A^c)\leq C_1 e^{-(d+1)\beta u}~~\text{and}~~\pi_A(A)\leq 1
\end{equation}
where $C_1$ is the positive constant given in Proposition (prop: Bounds A-loc. states of the paper). Moreover, the entries of the matrix $M$, introduced in (equation eq: Matrix M of the paper), can be upper bounded in the following way
\begin{gather}
    \nonumber M(1,1)\leq C_3 e^{-\beta u+t}+1,~~M(1,0)\leq C_3 e^{-(d+1)\beta u+t}\\
