\item[{\rm (2)}] for every $p\in (p_c(\lambda),p_u(\lambda))$, $\omega_p^{(\lambda)}$ has infinitely many unbounded clusters a.s.
\item[{\rm (3)}] for every $p\in (p_u(\lambda),1]$, $\omega_p^{(\lambda)}$ has a unique unbounded clusters a.s.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.263011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7afd324de633c4342dd631c6af11d8848ce333453bc36aba4cbbc4612421edfa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma comparisonstep3 of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp Fix any {\small$\mathrm{L}>0$}. There exists {\small$\gamma_{\mathrm{L},3}>0$} universal such that with high probability, we have 

