{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.374796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7b774ce15c3e91b17211790489a7c592e25e81a5f5d1483f19c34b074df7a6a5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 2 of arXiv:2606.04636, 'A Thomson-type variational principle for diffusion coefficients'] For a function $f:\Omega\to\R$, we define the gradient to be the
admissible flow given by
\begin{equation}
\grad f(\eta,\eta^{x,y})=c(x,y;\eta)\ \left(f(\eta^{x,y})-f(\eta)\right),\qquad\eta\in\Omega,\ x,y\in\Z^{d}.
\end{equation}
In this formula, and the ones to follow, it is implicit that $\grad f(\eta,\eta')=0$
elsewhere (i.e., if there are no $x,y\in\Z^{d}$ such that $\eta'=\eta^{x,y}$).

For a flow $\phi:\Omega^{2}\to\R$, the divergence is a function on
$\Omega$ given by
\begin{equation}
\div\phi(\eta)=\sum_{\eta'}\phi(\eta,\eta'),
\end{equation}
when the sum is well-defined.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.164227+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7b78e52156b0abbb17d2ce381c7d7efa1b2c1e2a5d7cf67ed81ba43d4a6d7635","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Steinslemma of arXiv:2411.16103, 'Non-commutative Stein's Method: Applications to Free Probability and Sums of Non-commutati'] If $\mu$ is a  probability measure with moments of arbitrary order, then $\mu$ is the standard semicircular distribution if and only if, for all $f\in \mathcal{C}^{1}(\mathbb{R};\mathbb{R})$, 
