	Moreover, for all $\varepsilon > 0$, the field~$\tilde h_{\ell}$, regarded as a random element of ${H}^{-\varepsilon}$, converges as~${\ell} \to \infty$ to a massive co-polyharmonic Gaussian field $\tilde h_0$ in $L^{2}(\mathbb{P})$ and $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.
	In particular, $\tilde h \in  H^{-\varepsilon}$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s..","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.458966+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bf4bed7d5604fe093eb19c2c9e22eb26a373ac53541fa34145e7ff017510cb3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2609.03418, 'Sampling and Density Theorems for the Fractional Opdam--Cherednik Transform'] For $j\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}$, set $D_j:=\mathcal I(\mu_j,\mu_j)$.
Then $D_j\neq0$, and
\begin{align}
\mathcal I(\mu_k,\mu_j)=
D_j\delta_{jk},
\quad j,k\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}.
\end{align}
Moreover,
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^{1}
G_{\mu_j}^{\alpha,\beta}(-x)
A_{\alpha,\beta}(x) dx
=0,\quad j\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}.
\end{align}
Consequently, if
$$ g(x) = c_0+\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z\setminus\{0\}} c_kG_{\mu_k}^{\alpha,\beta}(x) $$
is the Riesz-basis expansion of
$g\in L^2((-1,1),A_{\alpha,\beta})$, then
\begin{align}
c_j=\frac{1}{D_j}
\int_{-1}^{1}
g(x)G_{\mu_j}^{\alpha,\beta}(-x)
A_{\alpha,\beta}(x)\,dx,
\quad j\neq0.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.862976+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bf515a45cf503dabfebd84cd41de890a26106ce997825a71c6ccd663442c7b1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 (Local limit, macroscopic regime) of arXiv:2605.21459, 'Network evolution with self-reinforcement'] The sequence of random trees $(\cT_n)_{n\geq 1}$ with law $\cL(\delta)$ converges in probability in the local convergence sense (see Definition~\ref{def:local-weak} and \eqref{it:fringe-exp}) to the unique {\tt sin}-tree with distribution $\varpi_{\Sr,\delta}$ (see Definition~\ref{def:limit-bp-macro}). In particular, the degree distribution satisfies 
