{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.372123+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bfed3228c1cffe683955758edc2dbd3a4ce7b8076c927f6937f74b62c024052","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:walkl of arXiv:2604.15146, 'Renormalised two-point functions of CLE$_4$ gaskets'] Let $\mathbb{P}^W_{n}$ be the probability measure on simple random walks $(S_k)_{k\geq 0}$ starting from $n$. 
Then for any $m \geq 1$ there are real coefficients $(a_{m,n})_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}$ such that if we consider the infinite signed measure 
$\nu^{W}_m := \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} a_{m,n}\mathbb{P}^W_n$. 
Then $\nu^{W}_m(S_m = 0) = 1$ and 
$\nu^{W}_m(S_k = 0) = 0$ for all $k \neq m$.

Moreover, these coefficients are explicit and given for $m \geq 1$ as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item $a_{m,n} = 0$ for all $0 \leq n < m$ and all $n$ of different parity than $m$;
\item the coefficients are symmetric: $a_{m,n} = a_{m,-n}$;
\item we have that $a_{m,m} := 2^{m-1}$ and for $n > m$
$$a_{m,n} = 2^{m-1}(-1)^{\frac{n-m}{2}}\left( \binom{\frac{n+m}{2}}{\frac{n-m}{2}}+\binom{\frac{n+m}{2}-1}{\frac{n-m}{2}-1}\right).$$
\end{itemize}
In particular the growth of the coefficients $a_{m,n}$ satisfy $\vert a_{m,n}\vert \leq c_m (1+\vert n\vert^m)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.973065+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7bff2bcad279dece0b5b0f0de533af1b39ad268f59dd30b0fe752f589fe5238c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2606.13487, 'Branching-Selection Particle Systems and Inverse First Passage Problems'] Suppose that $f$ is continuous, and that $\rho\in C^2(\B{R})$ with $\rho$, $\rho'$, and $\rho''$ bounded. Suppose that either $\omega(x)=\is_{x\leq 0}$ or $\omega\in C^3(\B{R})$ is non-increasing and $\{x:\omega(x)\in (0,1)\}$ is bounded. If $(u,b)$ is the unique weak solution to \eqref{omegaNBMMHydro} and $\tau$ is the stopping time:
    \begin{align*}
        \tau:=\inf\left\{t:\Lambda\int_0^t \omega(W_s-b(s))ds\geq U\right\},
    \end{align*}
    where $U\sim Exp(1)$ and $W_0\sim \rho$, then $b$ is the unique barrier such that $\B{P}(\tau>y)=\exp(-\int_0^y f(s)ds)$. That is, $b$ solves the SKIFPP.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.921751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7c0246d23acec7fc1acfc1520f5637925354d09b9637e096e122fce2f241a520","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] [L\'opez-Rivera, Shenfeld \cite{RS}, Lemma 3.3]

