{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.052345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ce6e25e11115bcf2cf8b44ccac19df4244cdc709eb7658793211879cfcb2905","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 6 of arXiv:2607.00853, '\sf Energy integrals and asymmetric co-potentials for closed forms'] Assume $\beta_0=0$. For any $\mu\in {\sf S}_{0}^{(0)}$ and $f\in D(\form)_e$, we have
\begin{align*}
\int_Ef^2d\mu\leq K^2\|U\mu\|_{\infty}^{1/2}\|\widehat{U}\mu\|_{\infty}^{1/2}\form(f,f).
\end{align*}
If we further assume that $(\widehat{\form},D(\form))$ satisfies $(\form.4)$, then for any $\mu\in {\sf S}$ and $f\in D(\form)_e$, we have
\begin{align*}
\int_Ef^2d\mu\leq K^2\|R\mu\|_{\infty}^{1/2}\|\widehat{R}\mu\|_{\infty}^{1/2}\form(f,f),
\end{align*}
where $R\mu(x)=\mathbb{E}_x[{\sf A}_{\infty}^{\mu}]$ and $\widehat{R}\mu(x)=\widehat{\mathbb{E}}_x[\widehat{\sf A}_{\infty}^{\mu}]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.498529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7ce8a1e0779bca67fec07a60551d3707ef2a12b33f5d33f861b01c6ddf59bf3e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:RBJ-seed-linear of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] Let $H = \ell^2(\mathbb{Z})$.  The seed functions
$\psi_{t,r,n}$ and $\phi_{t,r,n}$ defined in
\S(sec:RBJ-kernel-reform of the paper) satisfy the linear problem
(equation eq:Linear-Psi-3 of the paper) and the adjoint linear problem
(equation eq:Linear-Phi-Prob of the paper) on $\mathcal{V}$ with edge
weights $(c_k, \lambda_k)$.  Explicitly, for each fixed
$r \in \mathbb{Z}$:
\begin{align}
\psi_{t,r,n+1}
&= (1{-}\theta)^{-1}\psi_{t,r+1,n}
   - (1{-}\theta)^{-1}\theta\psi_{t,r,n},
 \\
\psi_{t+1,r+1,n}
&= q\psi_{t,r+1,n}
   + p\theta\psi_{t,r,n},

\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\phi_{t,r+1,n}
&= (1{-}\theta)^{-1}\phi_{t,r,n+1}
   - (1{-}\theta)^{-1}\theta\phi_{t,r+1,n+1},
 \\
\phi_{t,r+1,n}
&= q\phi_{t+1,r+1,n}
   + p\theta\phi_{t+1,r+2,n}.

\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.858945+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cec891a2f9d73bfaff74a2c4ea6b25976d97b5dab30d9f9e02a55d6416332fb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 7 (\textit{cf.} {\cite[Lemma 1.5.3) of arXiv:2511.21496, 'Optimal local central limit theorems on Wiener chaos'] {Nualartbook}}]

Let $\alpha > 0$ and $p \in (1,\infty)$. 
Let $\fE$ be a real separable Hilbert space. 
If a sequence $\{F_n\}_{n \in \N} \subset \bW^{\alpha, p}(\fE)$ converges weakly to $F$ in $L^p(\Omega; \fE)$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \sup_{n \in \N} \norm{F_n}_{\bW^{\alpha, p}(\fE)} < \infty,
\end{equation}
then $F \in \bW^{\alpha, p}(\fE)$ and $F_n$ converges weakly to $F$ in $\bW^{\alpha,p}(\fE)$.
In particular, 
\begin{equation}
    \norm{F}_{\bW^{\alpha, p}(\fE)} \leq \liminf_{n \to \infty} \norm{F_{n}}_{\bW^{\alpha,p}(\fE)} \leq \sup_{n \in \N} \norm{F_n}_{\bW^{\alpha, p}(\fE)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.713416+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cef5e4a15d6d91eade45caf9464df320998641c04a237d5c04d6ab8e8f07904","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] Fix finite nonempty \(T\subset\mathbb D\), and choose \(N_T\) so that
\(T\subseteq E_N\) for every \(N\geq N_T\).  If
\((\nu_{E_N}^T)_{N\geq N_T}\) is tight, then, with
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal I_T
  :=\{F\subset\mathbb D:F\text{ finite and }T\subseteq F\}
\end{equation}
ordered by inclusion, the full net \((\nu_F^T)_{F\in\mathcal I_T}\) is tight
and has a unique weak accumulation point \(\nu^T\).  In particular,
if \((F_n)\) is any sequence of finite subsets of \(\mathbb D\) such that
every finite subset of \(\mathbb D\) is eventually contained in \(F_n\), then
\(T\subseteq F_n\) eventually and, after discarding the preceding terms,
\begin{equation}
  \nu_{F_n}^T\Longrightarrow\nu^T.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.769404+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cf02bf151deae3948cc46a8e4ac20d9816f804a47cce6156014d9dc7efe9f61","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma ratio of exponential of arXiv:2411.02898, '\vspace{-1.5cm}The connectivity and phase transition in inhomogeneous random graphs of fin'] Let $a \neq b$, and consider \[f_{a,b}(p):=\frac{p e^a + (1-p) e^b}{e^{pa+(1-p)b}}.\] Then, we have
\[
\max_{0 \leq p \leq 1} f_{a,b}(p) = f_{a,b}(p_0) = \frac{\frac{e^{b-a}-1}{b-a}}{\exp(b-a+1-\frac{b-a}{1-e^{-(b-a)}})}
\]
where $p_0 = \frac{1}{1-e^{-(b-a)}}-\frac{1}{b-a} \in [0,1]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:36.750170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cf1cdb6b1a201b7c375cb57d9238d80f85312deca0a56152474b192638c8a9b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 37 of arXiv:2511.20322, 'Dissertation'] Assume \assref{assum:H}. Then for all $\ka \in \N_0$ and $g\in \wC 5 \ka(\R^d)$ there exists a function $\rho^{g} : \lrates \to \wCZ{\ka + 13}(\R^d), h \mapsto \rho^{g,h}$ such that
\begin{align}
\E g(\chi_{T/h}^h) -  g(X_T^0)= \frac12 h \int_0^T (\innp{\nabla^2 v^{g}}{\Si} - \innp{\nabla v^{g}}{\nabla^\transp \bar f \bar f + \der_t \bar f})_t(X_t^0)\,dt + h^2 \rho^{g,h},
\end{align}
and
\[\nrm{\rho^{g,h}}{\wCZ {\ka + 13}} \lesssim \nrm{g}{\wC 5 \ka},\]
uniformly over $g\in \wC 5 \ka$ and $h\in \lrates$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.094658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cf67a8adc33a0ca166770f90b9c7d7c30233be29e219e10662025d02180b665","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi defi 2.8 of arXiv:2412.08497, 'Time discretization of Quadratic Forward-Backward SDEs with singular drift'] A stochastic homeomorphisms flow of class $C^{\beta}$ with $\beta\in (0,1)$ on $(\Omega,\mathfrak{F},\mathbb{P},(\mathfrak{F}_t)_{0\leq t \leq T})$ associated to (equation 2.1 of the paper) is a map $(s,t,x,\omega)\mapsto X_t^{s,x}(\omega)$, defined for $0\leq s\leq t \leq T$, $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$, $\omega \in \Omega$ with values in $\mathbb{R}^d$, such that 
				\begin{itemize}
					\item[(i)] The process $\{ X_t^{\cdot,x}\} = \{ X_t^{s,x} \}_{s\leq t\leq T}$ is a continuous $(\mathfrak{F}_{s,t})_{s\leq t \leq T}\text{-adapted}$ solution to (equation 2.1 of the paper), for every $s\in [0,T]$, $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$;
					\item[(ii)] The function $X_t^{s,x}$ and $(X_t^{s,x})^{-1}$ are continuous in $(s,t,x)$ and are of class $C^{\beta}$ in $x$ uniformly in $(s,t)$, $\mathbb{P}\text{-a.s.},$ for all $0\leq s\leq t \leq T$;
					\item[(iii)] $X_t^{s,x} = X_t^{s,X_r^{s,x}}$ for all $0\leq s \leq r \leq t \leq T$, $x\in \mathbb{R}^{d}$, $\mathbb{P}\text{-a.s.},$ and $X_s^{s,x}= x$. 
				\end{itemize}
				Moreover, if the map $(s,t,x,\omega)\mapsto X_t^{s,x}(\omega)$ satisfies ${\bf(iv)}$, it is called a stochastic flow of $C^k$ diffeomorphisms
				\begin{itemize}
					\item[(iv)] $(s,t,x,\omega)\mapsto X_t^{s,x}(\omega)$ is $k\text{-times}$ differentiable with respect to $x$ for all $s,t$ and the derivatives are continuous in $(s,t,x)$.
				\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.331575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cfeac159d4eca75d9dbdc455019c09f28989282cbea6e45479d01d803eda3dc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:LSMFinite of arXiv:2601.19630, 'Mass generation for the two dimensional O(N) Linear Sigma Model in the large N limit'] The  Linear Sigma Model on $\Lambda_{L}$ corresponding to a Wick renormalization of the classical potential (equation e115 of the paper), is the unique probability measure $\nu^{N}_{m,L}$ such that for any continuous bounded observable $F: \mathcal{D}'(\Lambda_{L})^{N} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$
\begin{align}
&\int_{\mathcal{D}'(\Lambda_{L})^{N} } F(\Phi)d \nu^{N}_{m,L}(\Phi) \nonumber \\
&\stackrel{\text{def}}{=} \lim_{\epsilon \to 0} \frac{1}{\mathbf{Z}_{m,L,\epsilon}^{N,\lambda} } \int_{\mathcal{D}'(\Lambda_{L})^{N} }F(Z_{\epsilon}) \text{exp} \bigg (-\frac{\lambda}{4N}\int_{\Lambda_{L}}:\|Z_{\epsilon}(x)\|_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}^{4}:dx\bigg )  d \mu_{m,L}^{\otimes N}(Z). \nonumber 
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:49.843133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7cff4c046869034db372e15caeb36c6030fd7f988e2c97794d59f225a6c0d308","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 17 of arXiv:2506.13913, '2506.13913'] {The strong Markov property for Itô diffusions}{}
Let \(\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}\) be an Itô diffusion in \(\mathbb{R}^d\), adapted to a filtration \(\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \geq 0}\), and let \(\tau\) be a \textit{stopping time} with respect to \(\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \geq 0}\), meaning that \(\{\tau \leq t\} \in \mathcal{F}_t\) for all \(t \geq 0\).  
Then, for every stopping time \(\tau\), every \(t \geq 0\), and any bounded measurable function \(f: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}\), we have
\[
\mathbb{E} [ f(X_{t+\tau}) \mid \mathcal{F}_\tau ] = \mathbb{E} [ f(X_{t+\tau}) \mid X_\tau ], \quad \mathbb{P}-\text{a.s.}
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:43:00.036222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d02cf1588041888a3465feb08fef49e0aad36fd6f23d9d38a39341af5727183","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 11 of arXiv:2609.00739, '2609.00739'] For almost every $0<r<1$,
\begin{equation}
\int_{B_r}(2a+x\cdot\nabla a)\frac{V(u)}{\eps}
=r\int_{\partial B_r}
\left\{\frac{\eps}{2}(\abs{\nabla_\tau u}^2-\abs{\partial_ru}^2)
+\frac{a}{\eps}V(u)\right\}.
\end{equation}
More generally, for almost every admissible radius of a ball $B_r(x_0)$ with
$\overline{B_r(x_0)}\subset B_1$,
\begin{equation}
\left(2-\frac{r\norm{\nabla a}_{L^\infty}}{a_-}\right)
\mathbb V_{\eps,a}(u;B_r(x_0))
\le r\int_{\partial B_r(x_0)}e_{\eps,a}(u).
\end{equation}
Thus, if $\frac{r\norm{\nabla a}_{L^\infty}}{a_-}\leq\frac{1}{2}$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb V_{\eps,a}(u;B_r(x_0))
\le \frac{2r}{3}\int_{\partial B_r(x_0)}e_{\eps,a}(u).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.905485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d04ed2ed3c158064c9c569b33fc98aff6f20b2e3d6f3e6b5e4b28c3aea50966","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma invariance_restriction of arXiv:2411.11425, 'Non-equilibrium steady states with a spatial Markov structure'] Let $\phi:\mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous symmetric function which satisfies the following scale and shift properties

\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
    \phi(\gamma \cdot x, \gamma \cdot y) = \widetilde{\lambda}(\gamma) \cdot \phi(x,y)\\
    \phi(x + \gamma, y + \gamma) = \widehat{\lambda}(\gamma) \cdot  \phi(x,y) 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

for some continuous 
 functions $\widetilde{\lambda}, \widehat{\lambda}: \mathbb{R}^+ \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^+$. Then $\phi$ is of the form

\begin{equation}
    \phi(x,y) = t \cdot |x-y|^\sigma
\end{equation}
with $\sigma,t \in \mathbb{R}$. As a consequence,  $\widetilde{\lambda}(\gamma)=\gamma^\sigma$ and $\widehat{\lambda} = 1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:55:27.166113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d080d3cd2514979533a43b2f097a3d4d31d17c21f18c9052a04feb4492f9a5e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma equivalence of monomials of arXiv:2609.04072, 'Oort's conjecture for split unitary Shimura varieties'] For two distinct tuples $(i,l,f),(i',l',f')\in \llbracket 1,g\rrbracket\times \llbracket0,N-1\rrbracket\times \mathfrak S_J$, we have $$P_{i,l,f}=P_{i',l',f'}$$ if and only if the following conditions are all satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item There exists a (necessarily unique) $k_0\in \llbracket 1,g'\rrbracket$ such that $r_{k_0}=0$,
	\item $l=l'=0$,
	\item $f'=f\circ \tau_{(0,k_0)}$ where $\tau_{(0,k_0)}$ denotes the transposition exchanging $0$ and $k_0$,
	\item $i=f(0)$ and $i'=f(k_0)$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, if $0\notin \{r_1,\dots,r_{g'}\}$, the monomials $P_{i,l,f}$ are pairwise distinct.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.431698+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d0b52c9dfcb9641af01f4abfe38774e69851079a2b3f21c6dc35c7135fe5d40","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (Binary polynomial positivity patterns) of arXiv:2608.31157, 'Sharp Approximation Rates for Neural Networks with Affine Latent Parameterizations'] Let $r,n,d_{\mathrm{pol}}\in\N^+$ with $n\ge r$.  Let
$p_1,\ldots,p_{n}$ be arbitrary real polynomials in $r$ variables,
each of total degree at most $d_{\mathrm{pol}}$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \Bigl\lvert
 \bigl\{
 \left(
  \one_{\{p_1(\bmmu)>0\}},\ldots,
  \one_{\{p_n(\bmmu)>0\}}
 \right):
 \bmmu\in\R^r
 \bigr\}
 \Bigr\rvert
 \le
 \left(\frac{4ed_{\mathrm{pol}}n}{r}\right)^r.
\end{equation}
The list may contain repeated polynomials or the zero polynomial.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.712782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d0e43f019a1b4c719d8e1fba7364855a9a3b0ac7ca0385b4d312e1e8f393578","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.29736, '2608.29736'] Let $(M^n,\omega)$ be a compact K\"ahler manifold of complex
 dimension $n\geq 2$. Let $\chi$ be another K\"ahler form. Suppose that the $J$-semistable condition ~\eqref{topintegralnonnegative}--~\eqref{$J$-semistablecondition} holds.
Then for any K\"ahler class $\beta$, we have $$\int_M F_{n-1}(\chi)\wedge\beta \geq c\int_M \omega^{n-1}\wedge \beta,$$ where \begin{equation}
  c= \frac{2n\displaystyle\int_M\Big(\chi^n-n\chi^{n-1}\wedge\omega\Big)+\int_M \omega^n}{8n^2\displaystyle\int_M\chi\wedge\omega^{n-1}}.\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:57:51.748603+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d120abd68b5edf48dea489e5992ab2797634163e4b69b28878c0411ba36103c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2608.30155, 'Gradient Estimates Near the Natural Exponent for Very Weak Solutions to $A_p$-Weighted Qua'] Let $1<p<\infty$ and let $\omega \in A_p$. Then the following properties hold:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(a)] (Doubling property) Let $E$ be a measurable subset of a ball $B$. Then
		\begin{eqnarray}
			\frac{1}{[\omega]_{A_{p}}}\left( \frac{|E|}{|B|} \right)^{p}  \leq \frac{\omega(E)}{\omega(B)}.
		\end{eqnarray} In particular, the weight \(\omega\) satisfies the doubling property
		\begin{equation}
		\omega(B_{\rho})
		\leq \omega(B_{2\rho})
		\leq 2^{np}[\omega]_{A_{p}}\omega(B_{\rho}),
		\end{equation}
		for every ball \(B_{\rho}\subset \mathbb{R}^{n}\).
		\item[(b)] (Self-improving property) 
		There exists a positive constant $p_{0} \in (1,p)$, depending only on $n, p$ and $[\omega]_{A_{p}}$, such that
		$$
		 \omega \in A_{p_{0}}
		$$
		with $[\omega]_{A_{p_{0}}} \leq C(n, p, [\omega]_{A_{p}}).$	

		\item[(c)] Assume $\delta \in \left(0,\frac{p-p_{0}}{2}\right]$. Let $f$ be a non-trivial
		locally integrable function on $B_{\rho} \subset \mr^n$, extended $f$ by zero to
		$\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Then the weight $[\mathcal{M}(|f|)]^{-\delta} \omega$ is in the Muckenhoupt class $A_{p}$ with
		\begin{align}
			\notag [ [\mathcal{M}(|f|)]^{-\delta} \omega ]_{A_{p}} \leq c,
		\end{align}
		where the constant $c$ depends on  $n, p$ and $[\omega]_{A_{p}}$. 	Consequently, if $|f|^{p-\delta}\omega \in L^1(B_{\rho})$, then we have
		\begin{align}
			\notag \int_{\mr^n} [\mathcal{M}(|f|)(x)]^{p-\delta}\omega(x)\,dx
			\leq
			c\int_{B_{\rho}} |f(x)|^{p-\delta}\omega(x)\,dx.
		\end{align}
		for some positive constant $c$ depending on $n, p$ and $[\omega]_{A_{p}}$. 
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

