{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.537775+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d5cbda8c0912c923dbda7c679b82d7eb3e73bb0783be8f3a6983ce76aa10825","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 25 of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] A measurable function \(f:(0,+\infty)\mapsto (0,+\infty)\) is said to be

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \emph{slowly varying} at \(+\infty\) when

  \begin{equation*}{
  \forall \lambda>0,\ \frac{f(\lambda x)}{f(x)} \xrightarrow[x \to \infty]{} 1;
  }\end{equation*}
\item
  \emph{regularly varying with index \(\beta \in \R\)} (or
  \emph{\(\beta\)-varying}) at \(+\infty\) when

  \begin{equation*}{
  \forall \lambda>0,\ \frac{f(\lambda x)}{f(x)} \xrightarrow[x \to \infty]{} \lambda^{\beta},
  }\end{equation*}

  or equivalently when \(x \mapsto x^{-\beta}f(x)\) is a slowly varying
  function at \(+\infty\).
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.470456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d5cec173f7d3dece8e8761865217df908a72bf18dfba6f924340862795637fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: presymplectic Hamiltonian action of arXiv:2608.28278, 'Reduction of symmetric time-dependent Hamiltonian systems I: presymplectic principal $\R$-'] A \textit{presymplectic Hamiltonian action}  on a presymplectic manifold $(M, $ $\omega)$ is an action $\Phi: G\times M \to M$ of a Lie group $G$ on $M$, satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] The action $\Phi$ is presymplectic, i.e., $\Phi_g^*\omega = \omega$, for all $g\in G$.
    \item[(ii)] For all $x\in M$,
    \begin{equation}
        \ker \omega (x) \,\cap\, T_x(G\cdot x) = \{ 0 \}.
    \end{equation}
    \item[(iii)] For every $\xi \in \mathfrak{g}$, the infinitesimal generator $\xi_{M}$ of $\Phi$ is a Hamiltonian vector field with respect to $\omega$, i.e., there exists a smooth map $J:M \to \mathfrak{g}^*$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        i_{\xi_M}\omega = dJ_\xi,
    \end{equation}
    where the function $J_\xi: M\to \mathbb{R}$ is given by $J_\xi(x) = J(x)(\xi)$ for all $x\in M$. The function $J$ is called the \textit{momentum map} for $\Phi$ on $(M,\omega)$.

\end{itemize}
We call $(M,\omega, \Phi, J)$ a symmetric presymplectic Hamiltonian system.","status":"unverified"}

