{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.651804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7dba675fa54c47e7333b6e0f4f9a4e731eedecb464e877838693d2f89c27ab51","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:holonomy-process of arXiv:2608.15923, 'Universality of two-dimensional Markovian holonomy fields'] A holonomy process on the pointed surface $(\Sigma,o)$ with structure group $G$ is a family $H=(H_\ell)_{\ell\in L_o(\Sigma)}$ of $G$-valued random variables on a common probability space such that, almost surely,
\[ \begin{cases}
 H_{1_o}=1_G \\
 \forall \ell_1,\ell_2\in L_o(\Sigma), H_{\ell_1\ell_2}=H_{\ell_1}H_{\ell_2} \\
 \forall \ell\in L_o(\Sigma), H_{\ell^{-1}}=H_\ell^{-1}
\end{cases}.
\]
Equivalently, it is a random element of $\mathrm{Hom}(L_o(\Sigma),G)$ equipped with the cylinder $\sigma$-field generated by the evaluation maps. If ${\mathbb{G}}$ is a topological map in $\Sigma$ containing $o$ as a vertex, a holonomy process on ${\mathbb{G}}$ is defined in the same way with $L_o({\mathbb{G}})$ in place of $L_o(\Sigma)$.","status":"unverified"}

