{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:21:28.715246+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7de944be9446a338aa905aa1c1b3375003686d6f3969b484e4f660abfef7a633","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition d:pw of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] {anari2024trickle}}]

    Let $\mu$ be a distribution on $\mathcal P([n])$. Define the polar homogenization kernel $\Pi$ from $\mathcal P([n])$ to $\binom{[n]\sqcup [n]}{n}$, by taking $S_1$ to $S_1\sqcup S_2$ where $S_2\in \binom{[n]}{n-|S_1|}$ is chosen uniformly at random. Note that its adjoint $\Pi^*$ takes $S_1\sqcup S_2$ (where $S_1,S_2$ are subsets of the disjoint copies of $[n]$) to $S_1$.

    Define the \emph{polarized walk} for $\mu$ as the Markov chain defined as the composition of the down and up operators $P_\nu^{\mathrm{pol}} = D^{\mathrm{pol}} U_\nu ^{\mathrm{pol}}$, defined below. These correspond to the down and up operators on the polar homogenized distribution.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The down operator $D^{\mathrm{pol}}$ is defined as $D^{\mathrm{pol}} = \Pi D_{n\to n-1} \Pi^*$. Explicitly, 
        \begin{align}
            D^{\mathrm{pol}}(S,T) =\begin{cases}
                \frac{1}{n} , & \text{if $T=S\setminus \{i\}$ for some $i\in S$,}\\
                \fc{n-|S|}{n}, & \text{if $T=S$,}\\
                0,&\text{otherwise.}
            \end{cases}
        \end{align}
        \item The up operator $U_\mu ^{\mathrm{pol}}$ is defined as $U^{\mathrm{pol}} = \Pi U_{n-1\to n,\mu}\Pi^*$. Explicitly,
        \begin{align}
            U_\nu^{\mathrm{pol}}(T,S) = \begin{cases}
                \frac{1}{n}\cdot \fc{\mu(S)}{\mu D^{\mathrm{pol}}(T)}, & \text{if $S=T\cup \{i\}$ for some $i\in [n]\setminus T$}\\
                \fc{n-|T|}{n} \cdot \fc{\mu(S)}{\mu D^{\mathrm{pol}}(T)}, & \text{if $S=T$,}\\
                0, &\text{otherwise.}
            \end{cases}
        \end{align}
    \end{enumerate}
    For a distribution $\mu$ on $\{\pm 1\}^n$, define the polarized walk centered at $x_0$ by the above with the bijection $\{\pm 1\}^n\to \mathcal P([n])$ defined by 
    $x\mapsto x_+:= \set{i}{x_i \ne (x_0)_i}$. 


























    Let $\rho_{\text{EC}}(\mu)$ denote the maximum $\rho$ for which $D^{\mathrm{pol}}$ satisfies $(1-\rho)$-entropy contraction with respect to $\mu$.\footnote{Note that the dependence on $x_0$ is implicit in the notation. When $\mu$ is supported on the wedge $B_r(x_0)$, we take $x_0$ as the center for the polarized walk.}","status":"unverified"}

