{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.439397+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e574bb4332b9992be9c691c6f5a9b7d7fcd629664f2bcefba37319581edc6c5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma1.5ext of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n$ be a finite set and let $\nu \in \mathcal{P} ( \left\{ 0 , 1 \right\}^{\Lambda})$ be probability measure satisfying the three following assumptions:
        \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(i)] For any $x \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}_n \setminus \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d},$ $\nu \left( r_x = 1 \right) = 1$;
        \item[(ii)] The marginal of $\nu$ on the set $\{0 , 1\}^{\Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}}$ is strictly positive;
        \item[(iii)] For any vertex $x \in \Lambda \cap \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$ and any configuration $r_1 \in \Lambda \setminus \{ x \}$ which is equal to $1$ on $\Lambda\setminus \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$, one has the inequality
        \begin{equation*}
            \nu \left( r_x = 1 \, | \, r = r_1 ~ \mbox{in} ~ \Lambda \setminus \{ x \} \right) \leq p.
        \end{equation*}
        \end{enumerate}
        Then $\mathbb{P}_{\Lambda,p}$ stochastically dominates $\nu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.327130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e577d65c0afc65318426a7d78458ff72fb45630f4817781edf0ab1a414bcf9b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop gl-flatness of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $X$ be any flat orthogonal $G$-spectrum. Then $X\smashp{-}$ preserves $G$-global weak equivalences. Moreover, for any $Y$, the functor ${-}\smashp Y$ preserves $G$-global weak equivalences between flat orthogonal $G$-spectra.
    \begin{proof}
        Let $f$ be a $G$-global weak equivalence. For the first statement, we have to show that $\varphi^*(X\smashp f)=\varphi^*(X)\smashp\varphi^*(f)$ is an $H$-equivariant weak equivalence for every $\varphi\colon H\to G$. As $\varphi^*(X)$ is again flat by [schwede2018global]*{Remark 3.5.8}, this follows at once from the first part of Theorem~(thm:equiv-flatness of the paper). The second part of the proposition follows in the same way from the second part of Theorem~(thm:equiv-flatness of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

