{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:13:21.260958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7efbe307ac9629e210aef6864bfd2cdbf270d67fa7dc2a58aa3ab07562e29958","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 27 of arXiv:2609.01473, 'Keller sequences of exact $\infty$-categories'] A map $p:\cal{C} \to \cal{B}$ in $\exact$ is a \emph{left-special projection} if the following hold:
	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
		\item $p$ is an exact localisation functor.
	\end{enumerate}
	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*.L)]
		\setcounter{enumi}{1}
		\item for every map $f\colon x \to y$ in $\cal{C}$ such that $p(f)$ is an equivalence there exists an $a \in \ker(p)$	 and a factorisation
		\[\begin{tikzcd}
		& x \oplus a \ar[rd, "q", twoheadrightarrow] \\
		x \ar[ru, rightarrowtail] \ar[rr, "f"] && y,
		\end{tikzcd}\]
		such that $q$ is a projection and the left diagonal map is the corresponding summand inclusion. 
	\end{enumerate}
	Dually, $p\colon\cal{C} \to \cal{B}$ is a \emph{right-special projection} if \ref{iLR} holds, and:
	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*.R)]
		\setcounter{enumi}{1}
		\item for each map $f\colon x \to y$ in $\cal{C}$ such that $p(f)$ is an equivalence there exists an $b \in \ker(p)$ and a factorisation
		\[\begin{tikzcd}
			& y \oplus b \ar[rd, twoheadrightarrow] \\
			x \ar[rr, "f"] \ar[ru, "j", rightarrowtail] && y,
		\end{tikzcd}\]
		such that $j$ is an inclusion and right diagonal map is the corresponding summand projection.  	\end{enumerate}
	Finally, $p\colon \cal{C} \to \cal{B}$ is a \emph{Keller projection} if it is both left and right-special.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.595211+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f017d108ef7ac9a3d6e8fe501903e09e66958132a12f41d6b92af7e2a08aa3e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2607.07022, 'Coordinate-wise Elephant Random Walk'] For every bounded $g:Q_k\to\R,$ every $n\geq0,$ and every integer $T\geq1,$
    \begin{equation}
        \left|\E[g(S_{n+T})|\F_n]-((K^{\star})^Tg)(S_n) \right| \leq 2\| g\|_{\infty}\E\left[ \sum_{r=0}^{T-1}\delta_{n+r} \mid \F_n\right] \text{ a.s.}
    \end{equation}
    Consequently, we have 
    \begin{equation}
        \E\left[ |\E\left[g(S_{n+T})\mid \F_n \right] - ((K^{\star})^Tg)(S_n) | \right] \longrightarrow 0, \quad \text{as } n\to\infty.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.268095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f05844cc5b91aad5c12c3f77ec8899a848b12e28ab9ef8d8860d166da5a1ff9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma discretization estimates for function powers of arXiv:2411.05924, 'Non-negative Martingale Solutions to the Stochastic Porous Medium Equation with Sticky Beh'] There exists a constant $C > 0$, independent of $n\in\mathbb N$, such that for any $\mathbf u_n \in \mathbb R^n_+$, $\theta\in[0,1]$, and $\mu \geq 1$ it holds
