{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.964850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7f9d15af2a2bf9699597ec8f27fd7c2a3ebe8caeecec08886aa2745054d5cf4f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:overlap of arXiv:2505.09604, 'Exceptional force, uncountably many solutions in the KPZ fixed point'] The following holds on a single event of full probability. Let $(p_n;q_n) = (x_n,s_n;y_n,u_n) \in \mathbb{R}_{{\scaleobj{0.7}{\uparrow}}}^4 \to (p;q) = (x,s;y,u) \in \mathbb{R}_{{\scaleobj{0.7}{\uparrow}}}^4$, and let $g_n$ be any sequence of geodesics from $p_n$ to $q_n$. Suppose that either
\begin{enumerate} [label=\rm(\roman{*}), ref=\rm(\roman{*})]  \itemsep=3pt
    \item  For all $n$, $g_n$ is the unique geodesic from $(x_n,s_n)$ to $(y_n,u_n)$ and $\mathcal G g_n \to \mathcal G g$ for some geodesic $g$ from $p$ to $q$, or
    \item  There is a unique geodesic $g$ from $p$ to $q$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, the \textbf{overlap}
    \[
    O(g_n,g) := \{t \in [s_n,u_n]\cap [s,u]: g_n(t) = g(t)\}    
    \]
    is an interval for all $n$ whose endpoints converge to $s$ and $u$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

