{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.894728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fa6e404124259ad8e71f4ecc6d253681a8345023b9dba327258be037dfa2ea5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 27 of arXiv:2508.04938, 'Perfect t-embeddings of doubly periodic Aztec diamonds'] The above proposition holds for any finite planar bipartite graph~$\G$ with outer boundary of degree four. More precisely, let~$w_0, b_0, w_1, b_1$ be the boundary vertices of~$\G$ listed counterclockwise. Similarly to~\eqref{eq:def_a} define
\begin{align} 
a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}=\sqrt{-\frac{K_{\G}^{-1}(w_0, b_0)\cdot K_{\G}^{-1}(w_{1}, b_{1})}
{K_{\G}^{-1}(w_0, b_{1})\cdot K_{\G}^{-1}(w_{1}, b_0)}}, 
\end{align}
where $K_{\G}$ is a Kasteleyn matrix of $\G$.
By applying (if needed) a gauge transformation described in Remark~\ref{rem:gauge_tilde_kast}, we can assume that 
\[
 K_{\G}^{-1}(w_0, b_0)
= K_{\G}^{-1}(w_{1}, b_{1})=a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}
\quad \text{ and } \quad
  K_{\G}^{-1}(w_0, b_{1})
 = - K_{\G}^{-1}(w_{1}, b_0)=1.
\] 
Then due to Proposition~\ref{prop:FG}, the Coulomb gauge functions 
\begin{align}
           \mathcal{F}^\bullet(b) =-\sqrt{-a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}-\i} \,  K_{\G}^{-1}(w_0, b) - 
    \sqrt{a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}+\i} \,   K_{\G}^{-1}(w_{1}, b),
        \end{align}
    and 
    \begin{align}
     \mathcal{F}^\circ(w) = -\sqrt{-a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}+\i} \,  K_{\G}^{-1}(w, b_{0}) + 
    \sqrt{a_{\scriptscriptstyle \G}-\i} \,  K_{\G}^{-1}(w, b_{1}),
    \end{align}
 define a perfect t-embedding~$\mathcal{T}$ of the augmented dual~$\G^*$, such that the boundary polygon of~$\mathcal{T}(\G^*)$ is a rhombus with boundary points at~$\{0,\, (a+\i)\sqrt{a^2+1},\, 2a\sqrt{a^2+1}, \, (a-\i)\sqrt{a^2+1} \}$. And the corresponding boundary points of the origami map $\cO(\G^*)$ at points~$\{0,\, -(a^2+1),\,0, \,-(a^2+1)\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:58:09.903267+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fa848826f21d006fb12650924b6f9a774b2ddf91a061050f66af3b2895da973","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def PSCR of arXiv:2607.19511, 'A Conditional Probability Hierarchy for Stochastic Choice\footnote{We thank Samson Abramsk'] Fix a stochastic choice rule $c: {\cal G} \to \Delta(\Omega)$. Suppose there is a selection $s$ such that, for each $G \in \cal G$ and $F \in \cal F$: 
\begin{equation}~
c_G(F)=\mathbf{1}_F(s(G)).
\end{equation}
That is, each $c_G(\cdot)$ is the Dirac measure concentrated on $s(G)$.  We call such a stochastic choice rule a \textbf{point stochastic choice rule}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.577870+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7facb228d04771e016619a2badcd24e39887a0f99e191349dd49825bef9cad68","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2606.14812, 'Joint convergence in Wiener chaos via transport hierarchy and Malliavin covariances'] Let $p\geq 2$ and let $(X_N=I_p(f_N), N\geq 1)$ 
		satisfy \eqref{c1}. Let $Y_N=I_q(g_N)$ with 
		$ap\leq q<(a+1)p$ for some integer $a\geq 1$, and 
		$\sup_N\|g_N\|_{H^{\otimes q}}<\infty$. Define 
		recursively $h_{0,N}:=g_N$ and, for $r=1,\ldots,a$:
		\begin{equation}
			h_{r,N} := \frac{f_N\otimes_p h_{r-1,N}}
			{\|f_N\|^2_{H^{\otimes p}}}\in H^{\odot(q-rp)},
		\end{equation}
		and
		\begin{equation*}
			g_{r,N}^{(2)} := h_{r-1,N} 
			- \frac{(q-(r-1)p)!}{p!(q-rp)!}
			f_N\widetilde\otimes h_{r,N},
			\qquad
			\varepsilon_{r,N} := I_{q-(r-1)p}(g_{r,N}^{(2)}),
		\end{equation*}
		so that $f_N\otimes_p g_{r,N}^{(2)}=0$ for each 
		$r=1,\ldots,a$. Then for every $N\geq 1$, in the 
		\textbf{non-critical case} $ap<q<(a+1)p$:
		\begin{equation}
			Y_N = \sum_{r=1}^a \frac{X_N^r}{p^r\sigma^{2r}}
			Y_{r,N} + \varepsilon_N + r_N,
		\end{equation}
		and in the \textbf{critical case} $q=ap$:
		\begin{equation}
			Y_N = \sum_{r=1}^{a-1}\frac{X_N^r}{p^r\sigma^{2r}}
			Y_{r,N} + \frac{\rho_a}{p^a\sigma^{2a}}X_N^a 
			+ \varepsilon_N + r_N,
		\end{equation}
		where in both cases $\varepsilon_N$ is asymptotically 
		independent of $X_N$ and $r_N\to 0$ in $L^2(\Omega)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.710139+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fb3d81e01b20e56cbbb65bf941e5acdbb3a642073b14257b8af8bbdbc940a8a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:CE-macro-uniformBC of arXiv:2606.06612, 'The limit shape and emergence of the \\ Discrete Gaussian level lines'] Fix $g\geq 0$, $n \geq 0$ and set $h = H+1-n$. Let $F\subseteq V \subset \mathbb Z^2$ where $V$ is connected, contains the origin and has $g$ holes. Suppose  $|\partial F|\vee|\partial V| \leq O(Le^{\sqrt{\log L}})$. Let $\gamma$ be a disagreement polymer where $D_0$ is the region containing the origin with $h_0=h$, and $D_1$ is the region containing the boundary vertices $\partial V$ with $h_1=h-1$. Moreover, assume that $|D_1 \cap F| \leq \big( \frac{3\beta}{{\widehat\pi}_\infty(\phi_o > h-1)}\big)^2$  and $|\gamma| \leq Le^{\sqrt{\log L}}$. Then, letting 
\[ \mathfrak{p}(\gamma) = \exp\bigg(-{\ensuremath{\mathscr E}}^*_\beta(\gamma) + \frac{\uprho_n}{N_n}|D_0 \cap F| + \mathfrak{I}_{V}(\gamma) + \upxi_{n+1}\sum_{i\geq 2}|D_i \cap F|\bigg)\,, \]
we have
    \begin{align}

    \pi_{V;F}^{h-1}(\mathfrak{S}) e^{O(L^{1/2+o(1)})} 
   \leq 
 \frac{\pi^{h-1}_{V; F}(\ensuremath{\mathcal C}_{\gamma, h})} {\mathfrak{p}(\gamma)} \leq 
 \frac{1}{\pi_{D_0^\circ\cap F}^h(\mathfrak{S})} e^{O(L^{1/2+o(1)})}\,.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.217754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fb55e6574c0fb5d50fe1d1c7928084b8e82e24ca7ddf7733034f43446edcf66","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:diff-coker of arXiv:2609.00233, 'A survey on mapping class groups of 3-manifolds'] There is an exact sequence
