where $\pi_1 \Diff^+(T_i) \cong \pi_1 \Diff^+(T^2) \cong \mathbb Z^2$ for every $i$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.794636+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fb729c30983a70a70bfc7db164e5bcaf89b5c6f0a57601e4af278dc67ec88a6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma coveq of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] Fix $\varepsilon,\delta>0 $ and $y\in D$ and define $u(x,t)=u_{\varepsilon,\delta}(x,t):=\mathbb E[\Psi_{\varepsilon}(x,t)\Psi_{\delta}(y,0)]$. We have that $u$ is equal to $\ipp{\mu_{\varepsilon,x}}{f(t)}$, where $f$ solves the following heat equation

\begin{equation*}

   \tag{H}\begin{cases}
        \partial_t h = \frac{1}{2} \Delta h, & \text{ in } D_T \\
    h = G^{D}_{\delta}(\cdot,y), & \text{ in } \partial_p D_T.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Here, $\partial_p D_T$ indicates the parabolic boundary that is its space-time boundary minus $D\times \{T\}$, and $G^{D}_{\delta}(\cdot,y) = G^{D}_{0,\delta}(\cdot,y)$. Here, one extends $h$ and $G^{D}$ to be zero outside $D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:32:34.763594+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7fb7e62a9dd132085cbb64fe5bfcc0b4334bf19cb0b7e4c317f83a3690c30c54","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2411.08220, 'Nonlocal operators with Neumann conditions'] For $t>0$ and
