{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:03:35.730488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8099936d3c7350a8a35a78832773c3c2cfc499eba03049df068a1d16dd2a2528","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm attractive-log-small-beta-partition of arXiv:2609.02743, 'Sharp mean-field estimates for diffusive log/Riesz gases in the Hilbert--Schmidt regime'] Let $\mu\in\mathcal{P}({\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d})$ be such that ${\mathsf{g}}_{\mathrm{att}}\in L^1(\mu^{\otimes2})$, and assume that there is a constant $C_{\mathrm{mom}}<\infty$ such that
\begin{align}
\sup_{y\in{\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d}}\int_{{\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d}}|\tilde{{\mathsf{g}}}_{\mathrm{att}}(x,y)|^p\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\mu(x)
\le C_{\mathrm{mom}}^p\,p!
\qquad\text{for every }p\ge1.
\end{align}
Then there is a universal constant $C_\ast<\infty$, the constant of the moment bound (equation eq:attMomentBound of the paper) in the proof, such that, setting
\begin{align}
\beta_0\coloneqq\frac{1}{C_\ast C_{\mathrm{mom}}},
\end{align}
for every fixed $0\le\beta<\beta_0$ with $e^{2\beta h_\mu^{\mathrm{att}}}\in L^1(\mu)$, there is a finite constant $C_{\mathrm{ub}}^{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu)$ satisfying
\begin{align}
\sup_{N\ge1}{\mathsf{K}}_{N,\beta}^{\mathrm{att}}(\mu)\le C_{\mathrm{ub}}^{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu).
\end{align}

Moreover, assume $\mu\in L^\infty({\mathbb{R}}^\mathsf{d})$, let $T_\mu^{\mathrm{att}}$ be the Hilbert--Schmidt operator with kernel $\tilde{\mathsf{g}}_{\mathrm{att}}$, and set ${\mathsf{K}}_{\infty,\beta}^{\mathrm{att}}(\mu)\coloneqq e^{\beta I_\mu^{\mathrm{att}}}\mathrm{det}_2\pa*{\mathrm{Id}+\beta T_\mu^{\mathrm{att}}}^{-1/2}$.  There is a threshold
\begin{align}
0<\beta_{\mathrm q}=\beta_{\mathrm q}(\mathsf{d},\|\mu\|_{L^\infty},C_{\mathrm{mom}})\le\frac{\beta_0}{2}
\end{align}
such that, for every fixed $0<\beta<\beta_{\mathrm q}$ with $e^{4\beta h_\mu^{\mathrm{att}}}\in L^1(\mu)$, there are constants $C_{\mathrm{rate}}^{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu)<\infty$ and $c_{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu)>0$ satisfying
\begin{align}
\left|
{\mathsf{K}}_{N,\beta}^{\mathrm{att}}(\mu)
-{\mathsf{K}}_{\infty,\beta}^{\mathrm{att}}(\mu)
\right|
\le C_{\mathrm{rate}}^{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu)(e+N)^{-c_{\mathrm{att}}(\beta,\mu)}
\end{align}
for every $N\ge1$.  The determinant defining ${\mathsf{K}}_{\infty,\beta}^{\mathrm{att}}(\mu)$ is positive. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.897829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_809a455f1d6687be3cbd3fcccc0c8f3b96630089ce684294f81dbf9ea72378dd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Teo 12 of arXiv:2608.02103, '$p$-Adic Diffusion and Random Walks on $[0,1]$'] Let \(X_t\in [0,1]\) be the strong Markov process attached to the infinitesimal generator \(D_f\) with probability transition \(p_t(x,\cdot)\). Let \(P_f\) be the operator defined on \(L^2(\mathbb{Z}_p,\mu)\) defined by equation (\ref{eq:padicoperator}). Let \(X^{*}_t:=\rho^{-1}(X_t)\) be the pull-back of \(X_t\). 
    Then the following holds
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The attached semigroups are unitarily equivalent:
        \begin{equation*}
            e^{tD_f}=\rho^{*-1} e^{tP_f}\rho^{*}
        \end{equation*}
        \item The pull-back \(X^{*}_t\in \mathbb{Z}_p\) is a strong Markov process on \(\mathbb{Z}_p\) with probability transition \(p_t^{*}(y,\cdot)\) satisfying
        \[p_t^{*}(x,B)=p_t(\rho(x),\rho(B)),\]for any Borel set \(B\subset \mathbb{Z}_p\).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.937346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_809adffd3623855e4325ec83598cb953855a782e1b6ff6b6815524408620646b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm|AbsoluteValueBound of arXiv:2504.08938, 'Fourier Levels and Almost Sure Bounds on Higher-Order Derivatives in First-Passage Percola'] Let $k\in W$ and let $S\subseteq W$ be a subset with at least two elements. The derivatives of the first-passage percolation time $f$ satisfy the following inequalities for every $\omega\in \Omega$.
