{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.874899+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_822d5a2d113ff7c7ad8d16e2769df2e86c52849a76c9fb8091f7bf0392afc021","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Minkowski integral of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $\set{K_s}{s\in S}$ be a measurable family of convex bodies in $V$ indexed by a measurable space $S$. Let $\mu$ be a non negative measure on $S$ such that $\int_S \|K_s\|\,\mathrm{d} \mu(s)<\infty $ for one (and thus any) Euclidean structure on $V$. Then, the \emph{Minkowski integral} is the convex body $\int_S K_s \,\mathrm{d}\mu(s)$ whose support function is given for all $u\in V^*$ by:
  \begin{equation}
    h_{\int_S K_s \mathrm{d}\mu(s)}(u):=\int_S h_{K_s}(u) \,\mathrm{d}\mu(s).
  \end{equation}
  In the particular case where $\mu$ is a probability measure, we adopt the probabilistic notation. That is, if $K$ is a random convex body of $V$ with $\mathbb{E}\|K\|<\infty$, we write $\mathbb{E} K$ for the convex body with support function given for all $u\in V^*$ by:
  \begin{equation}
    h_{\mathbb{E} K}(u):=\mathbb{E} h_{K}(u) .
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

