Moreover, $A_t\geq c\operatorname{Id}_E$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:58.436624+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_825d047b15abb443cad8536336d93829469a0cb8acf0a00a96bf68dda8dc2e2e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma1.5 of arXiv:2604.24743, 'The impact of disorder and non-convex interactions on delocalisation of height functions'] Let $\Lambda \subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}^d}$ be a finite set and let $\nu$ be a strictly positive probability measure on $\left\{ 0 , 1 \right\}^{\Lambda}$. We assume that for any vertex $x \in \Lambda$ and any configuration $r_1 \in \Lambda \setminus \{ x \}$, one has the inequality
        \begin{equation*}
            \nu \left( r_x = 1 \, | \, r = r_1 ~ \mbox{in} ~ \Lambda \setminus \{ x \} \right) \leq p.
        \end{equation*}
        Then $\mathbb{P}_{\Lambda,p}^{\mathrm{site}}$ stochastically dominates $\nu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.661930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_825dd6b3d37b8e140ded163f5ae7c2baf052ae2f7a5438686f369b46402cb6a3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LE1 of arXiv:2604.06012, 'Large fringe trees for random trees with given vertex degrees'] Let ${\mathbf{n}}$ be a degree statistic and let $T\in\mathbb T$ with $|T|\leqslant|{\mathbf{n}}|$.
Then
\begin{align}
  \operatorname{\mathbb E{}} N_T({\mathcal T}_{\mathbf{n}}) 
= \frac{|{\mathbf{n}}|}{(|{\mathbf{n}}|)_{|T|}} \prod_{i\geqslant0}(n(i))_{n_T(i)}
= \frac{|{\mathbf{n}}|}{(|{\mathbf{n}}|)_{|T|}} \prod_{i\in\mathcal D(T)}(n(i))_{n_T(i)}
.\end{align}
Hence,
\begin{align}
  \operatorname{\mathbb E{}} N_T({\mathcal T}_{\mathbf{n}}) 
\leqslant \frac{|{\mathbf{n}}|^{|T|+1}}{(|{\mathbf{n}}|)_{|T|}} \prod_{i\in\mathcal D(T)}p_i({\mathbf{n}})^{n_T(i)}
=\frac{|{\mathbf{n}}|^{|T|+1}}{(|{\mathbf{n}}|)_{|T|}} \pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}})}(T)
,\end{align}
and for every $C>0$ there is a $C'\geq 0$ such that if\/ $|T|^2\leqslant C|{\mathbf{n}}|$, then
\begin{align}
  \operatorname{\mathbb E{}} N_T({\mathcal T}_{\mathbf{n}}) 

\leqslant |{\mathbf{n}}|\pi_{\mathbf{p}({\mathbf{n}})}(T)\cdot\Bigpar{1+C'\frac{|T|^2}{|{\mathbf{n}}|}}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:24:20.976958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_825ede68d64ef49a18945fc5849bb0c886cf86c1ab62384d856a63ec6dbaf4f5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem exists an outer edge of arXiv:2508.13827, 'Surface sums in two-dimensional large-$N$ lattice Yang--Mills: Cancellations and explicit '] Let $\mathbb{T}^*=\mathbb{T}^*_{p,q,M}(\mathfrak{p})= \{T_1^*,T_2^*\}$ be a spanning pair of trees avoiding $p$ and $q$ generated from $\mathfrak{p} =\{p_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ of size $M\geq 1$ and $\ell$ be a loop. Then, for any loop $\Bar{\ell}$ with all its edges in $E(\mathbb{T}^*, \ell)$ such that $n^{\times}_{p}(\mathbb{T}^*, \Bar{\ell})\geq 1$ (resp.\ $n_{p,q}^{\times}(\mathbb{T}^*, \Bar{\ell})\geq 1$), there exists an edge $\Bar{e}$ of $\Bar{\ell}$ such that: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[1.] $\Bar{e}\in E^{\times}(\mathbb{T}^*)$;
        \item[2.] $\Bar{e}$ is the outermost edge of $\Bar{\ell}$ in $\mathfrak{p}^{\Bar{e}}$, for all $\mathfrak{p}^{\Bar{e}}\in \mathfrak{P}_{\mathbb{T}^*}(\Bar{e})$; 
        \item[3.] $\Bar{e}\notin \mathcal{E}(p)$ (resp.\ $\Bar{e}\notin \mathcal{E}(\{p,q\})$).
    \end{enumerate}
    Furthermore, for such an edge $\Bar{e}$, either $\Bar{e}\in \mathcal{I}(\mathfrak{p})$; in which case, taking $k\in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\mathfrak{e}(p_k,p_{k+1})$ corresponds to $\Bar{e}$,
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[{\crtcrossreflabel{4a}[item:3a]}.]  the path $\Bar{\mathfrak{p}} = \{\Bar{p}_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}:=\{p_{i+k-1}\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ connects $\Bar{e}$ to infinity;
    \end{enumerate}
    or $\Bar{e}\notin \mathcal{I}(\mathfrak{p})$; in which case, fixing any $\{p_i^{\Bar{e}}\}_{i=1}^{\infty} =\mathfrak{p}^{\Bar{e}}\in \mathfrak{P}_{\mathbb{T}^*}(\Bar{e})$ and taking $k\in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\mathfrak{e}(p_k^{\Bar{e}},p_{k+1}^{\Bar{e}})$ corresponds to $\Bar{e}$,
    \begin{enumerate}
         \item[{\crtcrossreflabel{4b}[item:3b]}.] 
         the path $\Bar{\mathfrak{p}} = \{\Bar{p}_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}:=\{p^{\Bar{e}}_{i+k-1}\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ connects $\Bar{e}$ to infinity. Moreover, in this case $\Bar{p}_1\notin\mathfrak{p}$.
