{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.743444+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_827d4b7fcc135ddc48b44a0b809f7acedddb63bb71ede44b8a54d47e7d370381","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 47 of arXiv:2605.02430, '\textbf{Scaling limit of the range of tree-valued branching random walks in random environ'] Let $\ccG\! \subset \! \ccF$ be a $\sigma$-field and let $(Z_s)_{s\in \bbR_+}$ be a $\bbR$-valued continuous process. Let $s_0, \ttb \ino (1, \infty)$ and $\ttc \ino (0, \infty)$. We assume that there is 
a $\ccG$-measurable and a.s.~finite r.v. $C$ such that for all $s, s'\ino [0,s_0]$,
\begin{equation}

\textrm{$\bP$-a.s.~} \quad \bE \Big[ \big| Z_s \! -\! Z_{s'}\big|^\ttb \Big| \, \ccG \Big] \leq C |s\! -\!s'|^{1+ \ttc}. 
\end{equation}
Then, for all $\gamma \ino (0, \frac{\ttc}{\ttb} )$, we get 
\begin{equation}

\textrm{$\bP$-a.s.~} \quad  \bE \big[  \big( \mathtt{Hol}_{\gamma, s_0} (Z) \big)^\ttb  \big| \ccG\big] \leq c_{\ttb, \ttc, \gamma, s_0} C, 
\end{equation}
where $c_{\ttb, \ttc, \gamma, s_0}\eqo 2^{2\ttb} (2s_0)^{1+c-\gamma b} \big(1 \! -\! 2^{-(\frac{\ttc}{\ttb}) +\gamma} \big)^{-\ttb} $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.088187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_827eef1327babed6145cb3adfb932cc42b33ca8fdd94e66607d3845a2f57caa1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-representation of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] A random signed measure $\xi$ on $S$ has independent increments iff a.s.
	\begin{equation}
		\xi (B)=\sum_{j=1}^p X_j\delta_{s_j}(B)+\alpha(B)+\int_{\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}}x\Psi(B\times dx),\quad B\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}
	\end{equation}
	where $p\in\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$, $X_1,...,X_p$ are independent real-valued random variables, $s_1,...s_p$ are fixed points in $S$, $\alpha$ is given by the difference of two deterministic atomless measures in $\mathcal{M}_S$ and $\Psi$ is a Poisson process on $S\times (\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\})$ independent of $X_1,...,X_p$. The intensity measure of $\Psi$, which we denote by $F$, satisfies 
	\begin{equation}
		\int_{\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}}1\wedge|x| F(B\times dx)<\infty,\quad\textnormal{for every $B\in\hat{\mathcal{S}}$.}
	\end{equation}
	and $F(\{s\}\times (\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}))=0$ for every $s\in S$. Moreover, the representation ((eq-representation of the paper)) is almost surely unique. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.623052+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8284449835c8e47439c9237f640f61da04fca1d2b4b57b4b5cf3319af5708811","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma EHI:l:poisson of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Fix $\lambda_1, \lambda_2 > 0$. If a random variable $N$ has Poisson($\lambda_1$) distribution, then
