{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.690273+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82876e163fc799b68b0331f6cee0bcf6c0ccb884cec583e690ac8fe800e6f3f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2605.17328, 'Transportation cost inequalities for mean reflection SPDEs with white noise'] {\rm(\cite[Lemma 3.1]{KS2019})}
{\rm There exists an adapted  random field $g=\left\{g(s,x)\right\}_{(s,x)\in[0,T]\times[0,1]}$ such that $\mathbb{Q}$-a.s. for all $t\in[0,T]$,}
\begin{align*}
\int^{t}_0\int_0^1 g^2(s,x)\mathrm{d}s\mathrm{d}x<\infty,
\end{align*}
{\rm and $\widetilde{W}:[0,T]\times[0,1]\rightarrow\RR$ defined by}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\widetilde{W}(t,x):=W(t,x)-\int^t_0\int_0^xg(s,y)\mathrm{d}s\mathrm{d}y,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
{\rm is a Brownian sheet under the measure $\mathbb{Q}$.
Moreover,}
\begin{align*}
M_t=\exp\left(\int^t_0\int^1_0g(s,x){W}(\mathrm{d}s,\mathrm{d}x)-\frac{1}{2}\int^{{t}}_0\int^1_0 g^2(s,x)\mathrm{d}s\mathrm{d}x\right),\,\,\mathbb{Q}\text{-a.s.},
\end{align*}
\begin{align}
H(\nu|\mu)=\frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{Q}}\left[\int^T_0\int^1_0 g^2(s,x)\mathrm{d}s\mathrm{d}x\right],
