{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:54.926350+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8296edec8f985db24ac03eb7bd00b7487169cbc0e61735174ea069c07bb4b2e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ik-geach-frame-condition of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] {plotkin_framework_1986}}] 
  Let $\textsf{G}(k, l, m, n) \coloneq \Diamond^k \Box^l \varphi \to \Box^m \Diamond^n \varphi$.
  An IK-frame $\mathfrak{F} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset)$ validates $\textsf{G}(k, l, m, n)$ iff
  \begin{equation*}
    w \sqsubset^k x \ \mathrel{\text{\&}}\  w \sqsubset^m y \implies \exists x' \, \exists z \, (x \leq x' \mathrel{\text{\&}} x' \sqsubset^l z \mathrel{\text{\&}} y \sqsubset^n z).
  \end{equation*}
  Here, $w_0 \sqsubset^n w_n$ is an abbreviation of $\exists w_1, \ldots, w_{n-1} \, (w_0 \sqsubset w_1 \sqsubset \ldots w_{n-1} \sqsubset w_n)$.
  In particular, $x \sqsubset^0 y \mathrel{\ratio\Leftrightarrow} x = y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.936482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82977eebc3584076185ee6e860de7ed75724cc52132d2df80a208f5c0518f9c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm|forComputer of arXiv:2504.08938, 'Fourier Levels and Almost Sure Bounds on Higher-Order Derivatives in First-Passage Percola'] The following two propositions hold for every $\omega\in\Omega$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $\sigma_j^a(\omega)\in E_j^C$, then $f(\sigma_j^b(\omega))=f(\sigma_j^a(\omega))$;
\item[(b)] If $\sigma_j^b(\omega)\in \hat E_j$, then $f(\sigma_j^b(\omega))=f(\sigma_j^a(\omega))+(b-a)$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.904433+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8298bd64b16324abb00590f7966c15dff32848c138794df238c787c361d155ea","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 61 of arXiv:2609.02065, '\textbf{From Bowditch's question to Goldman's conjecture for type-preserving representatio'] Let $\Sigma = \Sigma_{g,p}$ be a punctured surface with $g\geqslant 2,p\geqslant 1$. Let $s\in\{\pm 1\}^p$ and $n\in \mathbb{Z}$ satisfy:
