{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.681652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82c12a70255ec5e9b995b9145feb815b825158cdd57eedd93d4cd18f478fba07","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn d:connective-constant-pp of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] For a graph $\Psi = \Psi(\cdot)$ on the input process $\mathcal{P}$, the  
 {\em averaged connective constant} $\bar{\Delta} \in [0,\infty]$
is defined as 
\begin{equation}
\bar{\Delta} := \inf\Bigl\{\delta\ge 0: \forall\, p \in \mathbb{N} \;  \exists \, m_p \in \mathbb{N} \text{\ such that for all $m\ge m_p$}, \,
\mathfrak{n}_{p}(m)\le \delta^m\Bigr\},
\end{equation}
where \begin{equation}
\mathfrak{n}_p(m):=\sup_{B\in\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0)}\sup_{x_1,\ldots, x_p\in B}
  \mathbb {E} _{\bk x1p}[N(x_1, m; \Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B))],
\end{equation}
 with \(N(x_1, m;\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B))\) being the number of  self-avoiding paths of length \(m\) starting from \(x_1 \in \mathcal{P} \cap B\) in  $\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B)$, where $\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0)$, defined in~(equation e.particular-sets of the paper), are the sets of the form large windows
$W_n$, possibly intersected with finite unions of balls of sufficiently large
radius.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:44.028726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82c233ba16ef809971b75d941a0c8c833931a6162a36b8b99e9caad670f02e26","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary density3 of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] Lut us consider the eigenvalue point processes for our solvable instances of
${\tt{W}}_{n, n+a,\beta}(r, s)$. These have explicit joint densities proportional to
 \begin{align}

 |\Delta({\lambda})|^{\beta}
 \left( \sum_{(\mathcal{A}_1,\dots,\mathcal{A}_r)\in {\mathcal P}_{r,n}} \prod_{j=1}^r \Delta(\mathcal{A}_j)^2 \right) \prod_{i=1}^{rn} \lambda_i^{\frac{\beta}{2}( (r+s)a+1)-1} {\rm e}^{-\frac{\beta}{2} \lambda_i}
 {\bf{1}}_{{\mathbb R}_n^+}
 \end{align}
for $r \ge 2$ and $\beta s=2$, and to
 \begin{align}

 \Delta({\lambda})^{\beta+\frac{\beta s}{2}} \left|\operatorname{Pf} \left(\frac{{\bf{1}}_{i \neq j}}{\lambda_i -\lambda_j} \right)\right|^{\frac{\beta s}{2}}
 \prod_{i=1}^{rn} \lambda_i^{\frac{\beta}{2}( (r+s) a+1)-1} {\rm e}^{-\frac{\beta}{2} \lambda_i} {\bf{1}}_{{\mathbb R}_n^+}
 \end{align}
for $r=2$ and $\beta s =2$ or $4$. When $r=2$, $\beta s =2 $ and $\beta=1$, the scaling limit of the minimal points is given by ${\operatorname{Bessel}}_{2, a/2}$. When $r=2$, $\beta s = 4$, and $\beta = 2$, the scaling limit is $\operatorname{Bessel}_{4, a/2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.529332+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82c6bf2b49d98f1a6908b4f5a506760ce6f96043d1ac90cb66c33756d2d75310","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LV of arXiv:2510.22701, 'Gaussian fluctuations in Mean Field Stable matchings'] For $d>1$, $r\in\mathbbm{N}$ and $1\leqslant k\leqslant n$, we have that
