{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.238773+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82c8bcc656068d362eab288ebec4d107b213bf04aa26711c1c1e901e119ff743","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:cp-upper of arXiv:2607.00731, 'Sharp Asymptotics for the Largest Component in the Subcritical Regime of Preferential Atta'] Let $(d_n)_{n\ge 1}$ be deterministic degree sequences, and write
\(\ell_n = \ell(\mathbf d_n)\), \(D_n = D_{\mathbf d_n}\),
\(\Delta_n = \Delta(\mathbf d_n)\), and \(\phi_n = \phi_{\mathbf d_n}\).

Assume that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $D_n \Rightarrow D$ for some random variable \(D\) taking values in \(\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\);
\item $Q(d_n)\to 0$;
\item $\mathbb P(D=0)>0$ and $\mathbb P(D=1)>0$;
\item $\mathbb P(D_n=0)>0$ and $\mathbb P(D_n=1)>0$ for all large $n$;
\item $\mu_4(d_n)\le \Delta_n^{1/2}$ and $\Delta_n\le n^{1/6}$ for all large $n$.
\end{enumerate}
Assume further that, for all large $n$, the equation $\phi_n'(\theta)=0$ has a smallest positive solution $\theta_n\in(0,1)$, and define
\begin{equation}
T_n:=\frac{1}{-\log\phi_n(\theta_n)}
\left[
\log n+\frac{3}{2}\log\bigl(-\log\phi_n(\theta_n)\bigr)
-\frac12\log\phi_n''(\theta_n)
+\log\mathbb E\!\left(D_n e^{\theta_n D_n}\right)
\right].
\end{equation}
If $\theta_n\ell_n/T_n\to\infty$, then for every fixed $\delta>0$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P\bigl(L_1(\mathrm{CM}(d_n))\le (1+\delta)T_n\bigr)\to 1.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.683628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82c98e3ea47fc56b14bc5276144131a6c8fd7e9aae4ae6e86a8cb6be8516ec87","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ubs-count of arXiv:2609.00560, 'Distribution of Kaneko's val function'] {Ustinov2013}}]
	For fixed $\alpha,\beta\in[0,1]$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$, as $L\to\infty$,
	\begin{align*}
		\#\left\{
		w\in\mathcal R_{\mathrm{red}}:\varrho(w)\leq L,\ 0\leq w\leq\alpha,\ 0\leq-\frac{1}{\widetilde{w}}\leq\beta \right\} =
		\frac{e^L}{2\zeta(2)}
		\int_0^\alpha\int_0^\beta
		\frac{\mathrm{d} u\,\mathrm{d} v}{(1+uv)^2}
		+O_\varepsilon(e^{(3/4+\varepsilon)L}).
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.758678+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82cc6f35a45db636a760a7af1beebad14b43724a0bb1d6482684eb7bea1a3293","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2604.11921, 'Replica symmetry up to the de Almeida--Thouless line in the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model'] Define the finite measure
\[
\mu_2(A)=\E\bigl[S^2\mathbf 1_{\{S\in A\}}\bigr].
\]
Then $\mu_2\ll \nu$, and the density ratio is
\begin{equation}
r(s)=\frac{d\mu_2}{d\nu}(s)
=\frac{s}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}
\left(
 e^{-\frac{(y(s)-h)^2}{2\sigma^2}}+e^{-\frac{(y(s)+h)^2}{2\sigma^2}}
\right).
\end{equation}
If $h\le \sigma^2$, then $r$ is increasing on $(0,1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.315485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82cd36572eeb1ed20d002bbb5ae43734569005d218903baf1eea133d0423436a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: bulk equicoercivity of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] Let $(\Xi^\partial_T)_{T>0}$ converge to $\Xi^\partial_\infty$ in $\boldsymbol{\mathcal B}$. There exists a sequentially compact set $\mathcal K = \mathcal K(\sup_{0 < T \leq \infty}\|\Xi^\partial_T\|_{\boldsymbol{\mathcal B}})\subset \overline{\mathcal X}$ such that, for every $T \in (0,\infty]$,
