{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.641558+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83b8e03f84fca1984c1b62ccddad5a3a0e808a018f3a6e6ce2d49c61acb9b352","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 (\textsf{Graph closure of the fixed-chaos estimates}) of arXiv:2607.28742, '2607.28742'] Let $p, q\geq1$, let $F\in L^4(\Omega)\cap \bC_p$ and $G\in L^4(\Omega)\cap \bC_q$.
Let $F_n, G_n$ be the
 martingale approximants from
Theorem~\ref{thm_core}.  Then
 $ FG\in\bigoplus_{r=0}^{p+q}\bC_r.$
Let $\Gamma(F, G)$ denote the carr\'e-du-champ operator \eqref{Gamma_def}.
Then, we have
\begin{align}
  \Gamma(F_n, G_n)&\xrightarrow[\text{in }L^2(\Omega)]{n\to+\infty}\Gamma(F,G).

\end{align}
Moreover, the following  exact identity holds:
\begin{align}
  \frac1{2p}
  \int_\cZ\E[|D_zF|^4] \,\mu(dz)
  =\frac{3}{p}\E[F^2\Gamma(F,F)]-\E[F^4].

\end{align}
As a result,  the  estimates in
\cite[Lemma 3.2]{DP18} are valid under 
$F\in L^4(\Omega)\cap\bC_p$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.889630+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83b91d7a97b45140cec250841e86c2d22f3a86bd358e4e726e34cafb2adfb760","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:BKS_NS+NStable of arXiv:2606.29829, ''] Let $n\geq1$ be a positive integer, and $\mathfrak{f}_n:\{0,1\}^n\rightarrow\{0,1\}$ be a Boolean function indexed by $n$. Also, let $X_1,\dots,X_n$ be independent Bernoulli$(\frac{1}{2})$ random variables. Given $0<\varepsilon<1$, the sequence of Boolean functions $\{\mathfrak{f}_n\}_n$ is asymptotically 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \emph{noise sensitive} if \quad
		$\displaystyle\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\cov(\mathfrak{f}_n(X_1,\dots,X_n),\mathfrak{f}_n(X_1(\varepsilon),\dots,X_n(\varepsilon)))=0,\quad\text{ and }$
		\item \emph{noise stable} if \quad
		$\displaystyle\lim_{\varepsilon\rightarrow0}\sup_n\mathbb{P}(\mathfrak{f}_n(X_1,\dots,X_n)\neq \mathfrak{f}_n(X_1(\varepsilon),\dots,X_n(\varepsilon)))=0,$
	\end{itemize}
	where, 
	\[X_i(\varepsilon)=\begin{cases}
		X_i&\text{ with probability }1-\varepsilon,\\
		1-X_i&\text{ with probability }\varepsilon,
	\end{cases}\text{ for all }1\leq i\leq n.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.625131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83bdde47a955131154afb7645d6b817747b1c56f66c9787067e09ec9d63b9f82","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lem: Song3 of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] For $\phi\in C_{lip}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, let $v\in C_b^{1,1+\beta}(\mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}^d)$ with some $\beta\in (0,1]$ be the solution to the following PDE:
		\begin{equation}
			\begin{cases}
				\partial_tv(t,x)-p(\nabla_xv(t,x))=f(t,x), \ (t,x)\in \mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}^d,\\
				v(0,x)=\phi(x),
			\end{cases}
		\end{equation}
		where $p(a)=\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\langle a, X\rangle]$ for some $X\in \mathcal{H}^d$ with $\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X|^{1+\beta}]<\infty$. Then for any $0\leq t\leq \bar{t}\leq 1$, we have
		\begin{equation*}
			\begin{split}
				v(\bar{t},0)-\hat{\mathbb{E}}[v(t, (\bar{t}-t)X)]\geq -4(\bar{t}-t)^{\beta}\int_t^{\bar{t}}|\nabla_x v(s,\cdot)|_{\beta}ds\times \hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X|^{1+\beta}]-\int_t^{\bar{t}}\hat{\mathbb{E}}\big[-f(s,(\bar{t}-s)X)\big]ds,
			\end{split}
		\end{equation*}
		and
		\begin{equation*}
			\begin{split}
				v(\bar{t},0)-\hat{\mathbb{E}}[v(t,(\bar{t}-t)X)]\leq 4(\bar{t}-t)^{\beta}\int_t^{\bar{t}}|\nabla_x v(s,\cdot)|_{\beta}ds\times \hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X|^{1+\beta}]+\int_t^{\bar{t}}\hat{\mathbb{E}}\big[f(s,(\bar{t}-s)X)\big]ds.
			\end{split}
		\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:59:45.565316+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83be6a6e1a1f9e5802bc591e5521b64b7f57bb772b160ccc5c08f1878bcbb1a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: calculation of rj special of arXiv:2411.17109, 'On the maximal correlation of some stochastic processes'] Consider independent non-degenerate random variables $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n$ taking values in a general measurable space. Let $(U,S)$ be uniformly distributed with the constraints that $|U|=a$, $|S|=b$ and $U\subset S\subset [n]$. Suppose that $(U,S)$ is independent of $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_n$. Then we have that
