            \end{equation}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.637321+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83d92c2aeebb7b02ca36d4c712953a37ecdaa6a920bb7f2e864b3f470b7dc2a0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm DiffusionSBP of arXiv:2411.13765, 'Schr\"odinger Bridge Problem for Jump Diffusions'] Under the setting above, there exists a unique 
(up to scaling) pair of non-negative functions $(\varphi,\hat\varphi)$ on $[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^n$ such that $(\varphi(0,\cdot),\hat\varphi(0,\cdot),\varphi(T,\cdot),\hat\varphi(T,\cdot))$ solves the Schr\"odinger system (equation phiphihat of the paper), and the marginal distribution of $\hat\mathbf{P}$ at $t\in[0,T]$ admits a probability density function given by
\begin{equation*}
    \hat\mathbf{P}_t(x)=\varphi(t,x)\hat\varphi(t,x)\text{ for every }x\in\mathbb{R}^n.
\end{equation*}
If further $\varphi,\hat\varphi$ are of class $C^{1,2}$, then they satisfy the dynamic Schr\"odinger system (equation Equation:ItoSchrodingerSystem of the paper), and the canonical process under $\hat{\mathbf{P}}$ solves the modified SDE
\begin{equation*}
dX_t=\left(b+\sigma\sigma^T\nabla\log \varphi\right)(t,X_t)dt+\sigma(t,X_t)dB_t;\, t\in[0,T]. 
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.717011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_83d9d4babfa5a0b3702babf1b10a66110a4240d8dd6606fff744e50db0ddec16","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 (Degree order within a sector) of arXiv:2609.01143, 'The voter model on the hyperbolic graph'] Let $\mathcal I$ be a fixed angular arc of positive length, $0<\delta<1-\alpha$, and list the vertices of $\cG$ in $\mathcal I$ in decreasing degree order as $v_1,v_2,\ldots$. Uniformly for $(\log n)^2\leq j\leq n^\delta$, with high probability,
