{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.545434+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8406d50e690a559aaa1ee6bf3e70262a1ce07d716549f9464175aae36e079fe8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 (Local Lipschitz continuity) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] A function $f : \mc{A} \subset \mc{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ is said to be locally Lipschitz if for each $x \in \mc{A}$ there exists constant $\eta > 0$ and $\sigma > 0$ such that,
