{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.649881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_853840a139000c332ad63127baf3324c7428ee5d59c875b24a070495244ac4e0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Maintheorem thm:kpt of arXiv:2604.08462, 'Convergence of $k$-point functions in\\ high dimensional percolation'] Suppose $d>6$ and the limit (equation eqn: alphadef of the paper) exists. Letting $x_i^{(n)}=\lfloor ny_i\rfloor \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, where $y_0,\ldots,y_{k-1}\in \mathbb{R}^d$ are distinct points,
\begin{equation}
n^{-((4-d)(k-1)-2)}\tau_{k}(x_0^{(n)},\ldots, x_{k-1}^{(n)})\rightarrow\sum_{T\in \mathfrak{T}_{k}}\alpha^{2k-3}(2d \beta \rho)^{k-2}\mathcal{I}_{T}(y_0,\ldots, y_{k-1}),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \beta=\frac{p_c}{1-p_c},
\end{equation}
with $p_c$ the critical probability, $\mathfrak{T}_k$ is the set of binary branching trees on $k$ leaves (as defined in Section (sec:conntree of the paper)), and
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_T(y_0,\ldots,y_{k-1}):=\int_{(\mathbb{R}^d)^{k-2}}\prod_{\{a,b\}\in E(T)} \frac{1}{|u_a-u_b|^{d-2}}\,\prod_{v\in \mathrm{int}(T)}\mathrm{d}u_v,
\end{equation}
the integration being over the interior vertices of the tree. The quantity $\alpha$ (defined in (equation eqn: alphadef of the paper)) is defined in terms of the two-point scaling, whereas $\rho$ (appearing in (equation eq:rhodef of the paper)) is defined in terms of three copies of the IIC process. The powers of $\alpha$ and $\rho$ correspond to the number of edges and degree 3 points of the graphs in $\mathfrak{T}_{k}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.759642+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8539a80ee75916a33442d1c64e86e8409c39117847b918cbf3751ca3186b37a7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:tr of arXiv:2609.02682, 'LDP for tensor forms'] Given a hypergraphon $W\in \mathcal{W}_v $, and functions $\phi:[c]^v\to \mathbb{R}$ and ${\boldsymbol{f}}\in \mathcal{F}_c$, we define
 \begin{align}
\notag\mathcal T_r^{(m)}[W,\phi,{\boldsymbol f}](x)
:=&
\int_{[0,1]^{v-1}}
W(x_1,\ldots,x_{m-1},x,x_{m+1}, \ldots, x_v)\\
&\sum_{\substack{s_1,\ldots,s_{m-1},s_{m+1},\ldots,s_v\in[c]}}
\phi(s_1,\ldots,s_{m-1},r,s_{m+1},\ldots,s_v)
\prod_{\substack{a=1\\ a\neq m}}^v f_{s_a}(x_a)\prod_{\substack{a=1\\ a\neq m}}^vdx_a,
\end{align}
for $m\in[v], r\in [c]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.728976+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_853a790991f528185dba292ea63947225cc8592ef66bf41a652e995695d55bfa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:hitting-time of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Suppose that $\alpha>1$ and $\theta>\theta_{0,L}$, and let $C_E$ denote the lower bound obtained from Corollary (coro:m_easy of the paper) for the region $|m(x)| < \mathsf{m}_{3}$. Fix any initialization $X_0$ and let $\tau_{m,\varepsilon} $ denote the first time $|m_t| \geq \varepsilon$, then for any $r>0$ we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb P[ \tau_{m,\varepsilon} > T+r ] \leq  2e^{-r}\,,\quad
        \text{ where } \quad &T=\frac1{C_E}\Big( \log \sqrt{N}+\log(\sqrt{\frac{\beta C_E}2}\arcsin\varepsilon)\Big)\,.
    \end{align*}
    In particular, $|m_t|$ hits the threshold $\mathsf{m}_{3}$ of~(equation eq:def-of-m-1-2-3 of the paper)  with probability at least $1-2e^{-100}$ in time $T_{\text{hit}}$, where $T_{\text{hit} }$ is given by: 
    \begin{align} 
        T_{\text{hit}}=100 \frac{ \tfrac{1}{2} \log N+\log (\sqrt{\frac12\beta (\lambda_1-\lambda_2)(\mathsf{m}_{\textsc{e}}^2-\mathsf{m}_{3}^2)}\arcsin \mathsf{m}_{3})}{(\lambda_1-\lambda_2)(\mathsf{m}_{\textsc{e}}^2-\mathsf{m}_{3}^2)}\,.
    \end{align}
    Note that if $\lambda_1 - \lambda_2 = \Omega(1)$, then $T_{hit} = O(\log N)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:06.329992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_853d41ed05802628bc5d8c8b18c131759eb3957ea8d97d5a5b6cade1fb79d572","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2607.06428, 'Impact of spinning on the early-warning signs in non-Markovian stochastic systems'] Let $H\in\left(0,1\right)$ and $(\mathbf{x}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a solution to \eqref{eq:fast system simplified} in the setting \nameref{S2}. For any $\tau\ge0$, it holds that
\begin{align}
    \begin{split}
    &V_\infty(\tau)\left[ \mathbf{e}_{j_1}^*(\lambda), \mathbf{e}_{j_2}^*(\lambda) \right]\\
    =& \left(-\zeta_{j_1}(\lambda)-\overline{\zeta_{j_2}(\lambda)}\right)^{-1} H \left\langle  \mathbf{e}_{j_2}^*(\lambda), Q \mathbf{e}_{j_1}^*(\lambda)\right\rangle P(\zeta_{j_1}(\lambda),\zeta_{j_2}(\lambda),H,\tau) ,
    \end{split}
