{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.888857+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_85add8680c7afae79c7bf7a7acf0512c4738cf82a44f721c9b8f55134f75b7b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem pro:SurgaiProp3.1 of arXiv:2505.11389, '\bf The Poisson Multiplication Formula'] [\bf Proposition 3.1 in [surgailis84]]  Let the above notation prevail, and consider the setting of {\bf Problem A}. Assume that, for all $\sigma \in {\Pi}(k_1,\dots,k_m)$ and for all $A \subseteq \sigma_{\geq 2}$,
    \begin{equation} 
      {H(\sigma, A; |f_1|, \dots, |f_m|) \in L^2(\mu^{|A| + |\sigma_1|}),}
    \end{equation} 
    Then, 
    \begin{equation} 
        \Phi:=\prod_{i=1}^m I_{k_i}(f_i) \in L^2(\mathbb{P})
    \end{equation}
    and 
    \begin{equation} \Phi \in \bigoplus_{q=0}^{K} C_q \end{equation}
    where $C_q$ is the $q$th Wiener chaos of $\eta$. Moreover, the Wiener-It\^o chaos expansion of $\Phi$, written
