{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.496239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_86a0fa937e016ebad91a85f5266690843692ef4fbd889a668e4f58eebcdd57ff","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2605.28801, 'Microscopic Weak Selection Principle for the Logistic Branching Brownian Motion with selec'] The process $(\boldsymbol{Z}^c(t))_{t\geq 0}$ has an unique stationary distribution $\boldsymbol{\nu}^c$ on $\mathbb{E}$, such that 
	\[\boldsymbol{\nu}^c(dx_1,\cdots,dx_n,n) = \boldsymbol{\nu}^{c,n}(dx_1,\cdots,dx_n)\pi(n),\]
	such that $\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}  \boldsymbol{\nu}^{c,n}(dx_1,\cdots,dx_n)=1$ for each $n\in \mathbb{N}$. In addition, for any initial distribution $\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\pi}$,
