{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.226963+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_87df69eabffbac4b0aaa0531c7ebf156d96f003e60057559b4b748ce9df050c3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 26 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Defining system of equations for an integral molecule]
Given $\lambda \in INT(Mol) \cap MOL(M_0)$. Suppose
\begin{align*}
Supp(\lambda)&=\{h_1,\ h_2,\ \ldots, \ h_k\}, \\
INT(Supp(\lambda))&=\{\phi_{k+1},\ \phi_{k+2},\ \ldots,\ \phi_{k+n}\} \quad (\phi_{k+1}=\lambda),\\
Supp(\phi_{j})&=\{h_{j(1)},\ h_{j(2)},\ \ldots, \ h_{j(k_j)}\} \quad \subset Supp(\lambda)
\end{align*}
($j \in [k+1, k+n]$). Then,
\begin{align*}
\phi_{k+1}&=\lambda=h_1+ h_2+  \cdots+ h_k, \\ 
\phi_j&= h_{j(1)}+ h_{j(2)}+ \cdots + h_{j(k_j)}  \quad (j \in [k+2, k+n]).
\end{align*}
By replacing $h_i$ and $\phi_j$ with indeterminates $x_i$ and $y_j$, respectively, we obtain a system of equations:
\begin{equation}
y_j= x_{j(1)}+ x_{j(2)}+ \cdots + x_{j(k_j)}  \quad (j \in [k+1, k+n]).
\end{equation}
Equation (\ref{eq_defeq}) is called the \textit{defining system of equations} for $\lambda$. $\lambda$ is then obtained by
\begin{equation*}
\lambda=x_1+ x_2+  \ldots+ x_k.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.163339+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_87e063383c75e6da3d5c284589bbd18fba6a70d806a8a1f2f419ad1897e2c4f1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 (Exact nested-set reduction {\cite{FengProfiles}}) of arXiv:2606.29530, 'Cutoff profiles for colored top-\texorpdfstring{$m$}{m}-to-random shuffles with growing bl'] For every $n,p\ge1$, $1\le m\le n$, $k\ge0$, and $x\in G_{n,p}$,
\begin{equation}
        \frac{(Q_{n,p}^{(m)})^{*k}(x)}{U_{n,p}(x)}
        =\sum_{u=0}^{L_p(x)}P_k^{(m)}(u)u!p^u.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every continuous $\Phi:[0,\infty)\to\R$,
\begin{equation}
        \sum_{x\in G_{n,p}}U_{n,p}(x)\Phi\!\left(\frac{(Q_{n,p}^{(m)})^{*k}(x)}{U_{n,p}(x)}\right)
        =\sum_{\ell=0}^{n}U_{n,p}\{L_p=\ell\}\Phi\!\left(\sum_{u=0}^{\ell}P_k^{(m)}(u)u!p^u\right).
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
        \sepd\!\left((Q_{n,p}^{(m)})^{*k},U_{n,p}\right)
        =
        \begin{cases}
        1-P_k^{(m)}(0)-P_k^{(m)}(1),&p=1,\\
        1-P_k^{(m)}(0),&p\ge2.
        \end{cases}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.317477+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_87e0bd157bc0e0203ee58f25b55739711cdab5de030f5329cd89141251e8e98a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2608.30232, 'Strong Unique Continuation for Fractional Schr\"odinger Operators'] For $P,Q$ defined by \eqref{eq:PQ} we have,
