 Moreover, $\mathbf{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d,\beta}$ and $\mathbf{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d,\beta} \otimes \mathbf{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d,\beta}$  are exponentially ratio weak mixing. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.558279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_885b573f6c514cace2f88ae8c4f5ae08c0d8f2e041caf506e42ea83a2a28454f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:binary-characterization of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] Let $L$ be as in Lemma~(lem:cm-kernel of the paper), and let $\psi\in\cC$ be indecomposable in the sense of Definition~(definition:indecomposable of the paper). Suppose that $\eta_1,\eta_2\in\cC$, and set $f_j(\xi)\coloneqq L(\eta_j(\xi)),$ $j=1,2.$
