{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.310408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8863c39662828864eecab4ba953784e3cfea36dad59a3ba816dfd68a8467441d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:var_lb_stat_rand_meas of arXiv:2411.08848, 'Stationary random measures: Covariance asymptotics, variance bounds and central limit theo'] Let ${\mathcal X}$ be a stationary random measure on $\mathbb{R}^d, d \geq 2$ whose truncated correlation measure ${\bf K}$ has finite total variation and $\widehat{{\bf K}}$ vanishes at $\infty$. Let ${\mathcal X}_L$ be the scaled random measure defined by ${\mathcal X}_L(A) := {\mathcal X}(LA)$,  $L \geq 1$.  Then,  there exists a constant $c >0$ (depending only on $d$) such that for any bounded set $W$ and for all $L \geq 1$,  

