  with $A= \frac{-4}{g''(1)} = 2\left[\frac{p+1}{p} - \frac{\lambda}{p(p-1)} + 3 C^2 \right]^{-1} \in [\frac{p}{2p-1},2]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:53:39.137433+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8866b7be973ae4b807b965845aa1881132b6be13d77838602aa81f0a4b7e6bbe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 20 (Residual-augmented representation and response-aware resolvent) of arXiv:2608.26877, 'When Is the Sharp Covariance Envelope Tight? Feature-Only Geometry for Volume-Sampled Leas'] Suppose $\lossstar>0$ and $d<s<m$.  If $X$ is in row general position, then
the ordinary law and the auxiliary law $P_B$ are related by the exact
change of measure below; sampling and fitting remain under $P_X$.
\begin{equation}
  P_X(S)L_S=\beta\lossstar P_B(S),
  \qquad
  \frac{\overline M_s}{\lossstar}=I_d-\beta\E_B[K_S^{-1}],
  \qquad
  \E_BK_S=I_d-\gamma R.

\end{equation}
Classical operator Jensen for matrix inversion, with the minus sign in the
middle identity reversing the order, gives the interior resolvent bound
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{\overline M_s\preceq
  \lossstar\left[I_d-\beta(I_d-\gamma R)^{-1}\right]}

\end{equation}
and its linear consequence
\begin{equation}
  \overline M_s\preceq
  \alpha\lossstar I_d-\beta\gamma\lossstar R.

\end{equation}
For an arbitrary full-column-rank boundary design, as defined before
Theorem~\ref{thm:main-envelope}, only the congruent
one-sided inequality is asserted.  With
$Q=\sum_i(1-h_i)x_ix_i^\top$,
\begin{equation}
  M_s\preceq\lossstar\left[G^{-1}-\beta(G-\gamma Q)^{-1}\right].

\end{equation}
The exact inverse-moment covariance identity in~\eqref{eq:augmented-transform-main}
does not extend through a rank-changing boundary. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.654369+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8867582517bafea6b4a3f49a9911cf9d6be612984ebb21467eabe93b2cc62432","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 of arXiv:2605.07184, 'Convergence of Stochastic Gradient Descent with mini-batching and infinite variance'] Let
  \Cref{assumption:function_H,assumption:learning_rate_general,assumption:gradient_noise}
