{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.118342+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_886ae6fd534db06b85a92fbef7ab83ebb42e2bfa0c91758eb959fb0ca0ba2ebb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2604.04823, '2604.04823'] For all sufficiently small~$h$ and all~$x\in \Omega_h$, $\xi(x)$ is well-defined as in Lemma~\ref{l:facts-on-boundaries}, and
\begin{gather}
    \E\brak{\zeta_{t,i} \one_E} = O(h)\,, \quad 
    \E\brak{\zeta_n \zeta_{t,i}\one_E} = O(h)\,,\quad 
    \E\brak*{\zeta_{t,i}\zeta_{t,j} \one_E} = O(h)\,,\quad \forall i\neq j\,,
\end{gather}
where the implicit constants in the~$O(\cdot)$ notation depend only on the dimension.

Moreover, if we let~$a\geq 1$ and suppose~$a\sqrt{\epsilon}$ is sufficiently small and~$h\leq \epsilon^2$, then for any~$x\in \Omega_h$, $\xi(x)$ is well-defined. If, in addition,
