{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.619698+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_887bc567929dbca326c61b035bb7ceba33b855762dc34c285264b464acf3d115","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pr first stab of arXiv:2512.13200, 'Martingales on a Euclidean manifold with a boundary and reflected BSDEs in non-convex doma'] Let $(Y_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ and $(Y'_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ two martingales in $\bar{\mathcal{D}}$ with drifts $(f(t))_{t \in [0,T]}$ and $(f'(t))_{t \in [0,T]}$, and with prescribed terminal conditions $\xi$ and $\xi'$ valued in $\bar{\mathcal{D}}$. Set $\Gamma^{\lambda}_t = e^{\lambda \int_0^t |f'_s|\mathrm{d} s}$, $\lambda \ge 0$, $t\in[0,T]$ and assume that $f,f'$ 

    satisfies 
    \begin{align}
        \esp{e^{\lambda_0 \int_0^T |f'_s|\mathrm{d} s}} <+\infty
    \end{align}
    for all $\lambda_0$ (or for a $\lambda_0$ big enough depending on $\mathcal{D}$ essentially...) then the followings hold
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item `weak estimate':
        \begin{align}
            \sup_{t \in [0,T]} \esp{\Gamma^{\lambda_0}_t\Psi(Y_t,Y'_t)} &+\esp{\int_0^T\Gamma^{\lambda_0}_t|Z_t - R(\theta(Y_t,Y'_t))Z'_t|^2 \mathrm{d} t } \\
            &\le C\esp{\Gamma^{\lambda_0}_T \Psi(\xi,\xi')
            + \int_0^T \Gamma^{\lambda_0}_t |f_t-f'_t| \mathrm{d} t }\;,\nonumber
        \end{align}
        \item `strong estimate':
        \begin{align}
            \esp{\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\Gamma^{\lambda_0}_t \Psi(Y_t,Y'_t)} \le C \esp{\Gamma^{\lambda_0}_T \Psi(\xi,\xi')
            + \int_0^T \Gamma^{\lambda_0}_t |f_t-f'_t| \mathrm{d} t }^\frac12.
        \end{align}
    \end{enumerate}
Let us assume moreover that $f'$ is bounded, then "$Z'$ is BMO" (with BMO norm depending on $f'$) and we have 
\begin{align}
    \esp{\int_0^T|Z_t - Z'_t|^2 \mathrm{d} t }
    \le C\esp{ \Psi(\xi,\xi')
    + \int_0^T|f_t-f'_t| \mathrm{d} t }
    + C_{|Z'|_{BMO}} \esp{\sup_{t\in[0,T]} \Psi(Y_t,Y'_t)}^\frac12
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.954045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_887fefdd0bdc6515e36278b878c1ceda80cb191570e81b7829c248a84c8cd9d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Fix $(R,b)\subset \mathbb{R}^2$ flexible and let $h_b$ be the corresponding boundary height function. 
Let $\Lambda = \mathbb{Z}^2$ or the honeycomb lattice. The multinomial limit shape height function with boundary condition $h\mid_{\partial R}=h_b$ is a smooth function which is the envelope of $\kappa$-harmonically moving planes, where $z$ is a conformal coordinate in the Riemannian metric determined by $\sigma$ and $\kappa = \sqrt{\det \text{Hess}(\sigma)}$. Concretely,
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For $\mathbb{Z}^2$ embedded with edge vectors $(\pm 1/2, \pm 1/2)$, $\mathcal{N} = \{(s,t)\in [-1/2,1/2]^2\}$. A conformal coordinate $z = u+i v$ for $(u,v)\in[0,\pi]^2$ is given by $$(u,v) = (\arccos(2s),\arccos(2t))$$ and $\kappa(u,v) = \frac{4}{\sin(u)\sin(v)}$. 
        \item For the honeycomb lattice, $\mathcal{N} =\{(s,t): 0\leq s\leq 1, 0\leq t\leq s\}.$ A conformal coordinate $z = u+iv\in\mathbb{H}$ is given by 
        \begin{align}
            (u,v) = \bigg( \frac{s-t(1-s-t)}{(1-t)^2}, \frac{2\sqrt{st(1-s-t}}{(1-t)^2}\bigg)
        \end{align}
        and $\kappa(u,v) = \frac{(1+ \sqrt{u^2 + v^2} + \sqrt{(u-1)^2 + v^2})^2}{2 v}$.
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.335731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8880164da6f80767b9b26aa03ae2a3ede4386dbfdf92957151e5f720b05083e7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:smallt2 of arXiv:2602.11129, 'Information-Theoretic Thresholds for Bipartite Latent-Space Graphs Under Noisy Observation'] There is an absolute constant $C > 0$ such that for every $T \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$,
