{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.694250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_888ca9d45f3bed424347dda0e731751594d25e867eaaba17fd69f04e4f4930d8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop GR-1 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] There exists a constant $c>0$ such that, if $k$ is large enough, for any $i,j \in \mathbb N$ with $i<j$, $n \geq k$ and $x,\widetilde{x} \in {\rm D}_n$ such that $x \in {\rm B}^-[\widetilde{x};j-1]$ and $\overline{{\rm B}^+[\widetilde{x};j]}\subseteq {\rm D}_n$, we have that 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\Big( \sqrt{\widehat{N}_t[x;i]} \leq u \,\Big|\, \sqrt{\widehat{N}_t[\widetilde{x};j]} \geq v\,;\mathcal F[\widetilde{x};j]\Big) \leq 2 \exp\bigg\{ -\frac{(v-u)^2}{(j-i+1)k^\gamma}\bigg\}
\end{equation} uniformly over all $t > 0$ and $u,v \in \mathcal N_k$ satisfying that $u\leq v$,  $0 \leq \widetilde{u}_k \leq {\rm e}^{ck^\gamma}$ and $1 \leq \widetilde{v}_k \leq {\rm e}^{ck^\gamma}$, as well as  
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\Big( \sqrt{\widehat{N}_t[x;i]} \geq u \,\Big|\, \sqrt{\widehat{N}_t[\widetilde{x};j]} \leq v\,;\mathcal F[\widetilde{x};j]\Big) \leq 2 \exp\bigg\{ -\frac{(u-v)^2}{(j-i+1)k^\gamma}\bigg\}	
\end{equation} uniformly over all $t > 0$ and $u,v \in \mathcal N_k$ satisfying that $v \leq u$,  $0 \leq \widetilde{u}_k \leq {\rm e}^{ck^\gamma}$ and $1 \leq \widetilde{v}_k \leq {\rm e}^{ck^\gamma}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.473289+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_889099f079c3c63631ef0c1e39b7f51ff4731fa0eb58bc85f8d080f52ee12d3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2608.17562, '2608.17562'] Let $(X_{i})_{i \ge 1}$ be a sequence of continuous \iid random variables with common distribution function $F$. Assume $\widebar{F} \in \SRV{\alpha}{\rho}$, with $\alpha > 0$, $\rho < 0$ and auxiliary function~$A$. Then
