{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.888609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_889846405a5b365a17e93cf86253848eae3d8fa4a17e626688e6c8f3153ab49a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:commut otimes odot of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] Let $A,A'\subset \mathbb{R}^m$ and $B,B'\subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be finite sets. For every $\alpha\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^A$, $\alpha'\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^{A'}$, $\beta\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^{B}$ and $beta'\in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^{B'}$, we have
