{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.961361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_88de7d6d8ea4e41155b0dd3d15a4ea4a2a8ef05868fd33dc1c06204066c2e07d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2605.11279, 'Flocking with Multiple Types: Competition, Fluid Limits and Traveling Waves'] Suppose $v_+ > v_- = 0$ and $s = \alpha^+ + \alpha^->1$ is fixed.
    \begin{enumerate}[label = (\alph*)]
        \item We have
        \begin{align*}
            \lim_{\alpha^- \downarrow 0} \ \frac{\rho^+}{\alpha^-} = \frac{1}{s-1} & & \text{and} & & \lim_{\alpha^- \downarrow 0} \ \frac{\gamma}{\alpha^-} = \frac{v_+}{s-1};
        \end{align*}
        furthermore, the traveling wave tail exponents satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \lim_{\alpha^- \downarrow 0} \lambda_R = \frac{1}{v_+ + s} & & \text{and} & & \lim_{\alpha^- \downarrow 0} \lambda_L = \frac{1}{v_+ + s-1}.
        \end{align*}
        This is referred to as the ``small-$\alpha^-$'' regime.

        \item Suppose $\alpha^- \uparrow \infty$ and $\alpha^+ = \text{o}(\alpha^-)$. Then
        \begin{align*}
            \rho^- = \frac{\alpha^+}{\alpha^-}\bigparens{1 + \text{o}(1)} & & \text{and} & & \gamma = v_+ - \frac{v_+\alpha^+}{\alpha^-}\bigparens{1 + \text{o}(1)},
        \end{align*}
        and the traveling wave tail exponents satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \lambda_R = \frac{\alpha^+}{(\alpha\displaystyle^-)\displaystyle^2}\bigparens{1 + \text{o}(1)} & & \text{and} & & \lambda_L = \frac{\alpha^+}{(\alpha\displaystyle^-)\displaystyle^2}\bigparens{1 + \text{o}(1)}.
        \end{align*}
        This is referred to as the ``large-$\alpha^-$'' regime.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.988689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_88e6e2477a86e668abcdf8da54d988d04b61308f6fe0abed8a6a7cda0d04cb79","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:rec-geometry of arXiv:2604.13968, 'Divisible sandpiles via random walks in random scenery'] Let $C_R\coloneqq 2/(1-B^{-\alpha})$.  There exist constants $c_N, C_N, C_{\mathrm{ball}}>0$ depending only on $B$ and~$\alpha$ such that the following hold.
