{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.318815+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_88efb353500dbfb9a000548b1bd509c09992df326b266470b357ef9d2649590d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:two-dim-sys of arXiv:2607.13748, 'Load Balancing with Individually Heterogeneous Servers'] Consider the following integral equations
\begin{align}
x_1(t) &=h_1 +y_1(t) - \int_0^t(\mu_{\max} x_1(s) - \mu_{\min} x_2(s))ds -u_1(t), \\
x_2(t) &=h_2 +y_2(t) - \int_0^t\mu_{\min} x_2(s)ds +u_1(t)-u_2(t),\\
x_1(t)&\leq0, \ 0\leq x_2(t) \leq B, \ t\geq0, 
\end{align}
where $u_1$ and $u_2$ are non-decreasing and non-negative functions satisfying
\begin{align}
\int_0^t\mathbb{I}{x_1(s)<0}du_1(s)&=0,\\
\int_0^t\mathbb{I}{x_2(s)<B}du_2(s)&=0.
\end{align}
Then for given $B\in \bar{\mathbb{R}}_+$, $\mathbf{h}\in \mathbb{R}^2$, and $\mathbf{y}\in \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty)$~(equation eqn:ref_1 of the paper)-(equation eqn:ref_3 of the paper) has a unique solution $(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) \in \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty) \times \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty)$. Moreover, there exists a well-defined  function $(f,  g): \bar{\mathbb{R}}_+ \times \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty) \to \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty) \times \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty)$ which maps $(B,\mathbf{h},\mathbf{y})$ to $\mathbf{x} =f(B,\mathbf{h},\mathbf{y})$ and $\mathbf{u}=g(B,\mathbf{h},\mathbf{y})$. Furthermore, the function $(f,g)$ is continuous on $\bar{\mathbb{R}}_+ \times \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{D}_{\mathbb{R}^2}[0,\infty)$. Finally, $\mathbf{y}$ continuous implies that $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{u}$ are also continuous. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

