{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.973151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89d0144c0b03efdda84de62f5420859c9fe61b403a36aea7e3556dacd476628a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Propn 3 of arXiv:2606.13487, 'Branching-Selection Particle Systems and Inverse First Passage Problems'] Let $(\mu_t^N)_{t\geq 0}$ be the measure-valued process describing the $(\omega,f,\Lambda,N)$-BBM process, and define $b^N(t):=\inf\{x:\Lambda\langle \mu_t^N,\omega(\cdot-x)\rangle\geq f(t)\}$. Then for any bounded $\omega$ and any test function $\phi(x,t)\in \C{BC}^{2,1}(\B{R}\times [0,\infty),\B{R})$ we have
    \begin{align}  \langle \mu_t^N,\phi(\cdot,t)\rangle = \langle \rho^N, \phi(\cdot,0)\rangle &+ \int_0^t \langle \mu_s^N, \frac12 \phi_{xx}(\cdot,s) + \phi_t(\cdot,s)+f(s)\phi(\cdot,s)\rangle \\
    &- \frac{f(s)\langle \mu_s^N,\phi(\cdot,s)\omega(\cdot-b^N(s))}{\langle \mu_s^N,\omega(\cdot-b^N(s))\rangle} ds
    \nonumber + M_t^{N,W} + M_t^{N,b},
    \end{align}
    where $M^{N,W}_t$ is a continuous local martingale and $M^{N,b}_t$ is a local martingale, with $\B{E}[[M^{N,W}]_t],\B{E}[[M^{N,b}]_t]\to 0$ as $N\to\infty$ for fixed $t\geq 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.148535+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89d1660ea7232fff8efca1e54e9eae86c3bd1bf2f167e574162883acde0d5cf0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Jordan2_lower_tail_lower_bound of arXiv:2606.24465, 'History estimation in random recursive trees:\\Pointwise approach via iterated Jordan cent'] For the Jordan-$2$ ordering $\widehat{\sigma}_2$, there exist positive constants $C$, $\delta$ such that for any $n\geq 1$, any vertex $v \in[n]$ and any $S\in[1, \delta\sqrt{v}]$ 

    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}\Big(\widehat{\sigma}_2(v)\leq \frac{1}{S}v\Big) \geq  \frac{C}{S^2}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.436482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89d44a519ff0f1eaeb962a98b183034a0656ad72ee9c3fb1c74c60c164b0bd58","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2606.15178, 'The distribution of the de Moivre experiment'] Let $ X $ be a random variable that follows an $s$-Bernoulli distribution with parameter $ p $, $ X \sim \mathcal {B}^{(s)} (p) $. Then the probability generating function of $X$ is, for $ p\neq q $,
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t)=E(t^X) =  \sum_{k=0}^{s} t^k p^k q^{s-k} =\frac{(pt)^{s+1}-q^{s+1}}{pt-q} \qquad (pt\neq q,\ \text{removable singularity}),
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
		G_X(t)=\frac{1}{s+1}\left(\frac{1-t^{s+1}}{1-t}\right), \qquad\text{ for } p=q. 
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.775934+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89d6901806b748c751e6d5763dbd3cf0e383d87fea4e3cce6f06c4ebe93baf85","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 of arXiv:2603.25452, 'New approach to optimal control of delayed stochastic Volterra integral equations'] \textbf{(Generalized Duality Formula for the Brownian motion \( B \))}\\
	Fix \( s \in [0, T] \).  
	If \( t \mapsto \varphi(t,s,\omega) \in L^2(\varepsilon \times \mathbb{P}) \) is an \( \mathcal{F} \)-adapted process with  
\begin{eqnarray*}
		\E \left[ \int_t^T \varphi^2(t,s) \, dt \right] < \infty ,
\end{eqnarray*}
and $ F \in L^2(\mathcal{F}_T, \mathbb{P})$. Then we have
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\mathbb{E} \left[ F \int_0^T \varphi(t,s) \, dB(t) \right] 
		= \mathbb{E} \left[ \int_0^T \mathbb{E}[D_t F \mid \mathcal{F}_t] \, \varphi(t,s) \, dt \right].
	\end{eqnarray}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.660395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89dda1a03d83b137392b10c6a9d925bb832b2e3de2017f26833db35789a627b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2604.23462, 'Invariant measures for the open KPZ equation: the Gaussian case'] Let $w(t,x)$ be a bounded progressively measurable process. Given $\delta > 0$, there exist constants $C_0, C_1 > 0$ (independent of $\ep$) such that for all $\lambda > 0$ and $T > 0$,
            \begin{equation}
