{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:56:00.645312+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89de20954a6eab994c6bff9ba12a1556c94aaec3f0c93e76ca3c59247f0be25f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-adm of arXiv:2603.17861, 'Gaussian concentration, integral probability metrics, and coupling functionals\\ for infin'] A probability measure $\mu$ on $(\Omega,\mathfrak{F})$ is said to be asymptotically decoupled if there exist $g:\mathds N\to\mathds N$ and $c:\mathds N\to [0,\infty)$ such that
$\lim_{n\to\infty}g(n)/n=0$, $\lim_{n\to\infty} c(n)/|\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n|=0$, and $\forall j\in\mathds Z^d, \forall n\in\mathds N, \forall A\in\mathfrak{F}_{\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n+j}, \forall B\in \mathfrak{F}_{(\ensuremath{\Lambda}_n+g(n)+j)^{\mathrm{c}}}$,
\begin{equation}
\e^{-c(n)}\mu(A) \mu(B)\leq \mu(A\cap B)\leq \e^{c(n)}\mu(A) \mu(B).
\end{equation}
If only the upper bound holds, one says that $\mu$ is asymptotically decoupled from above.","status":"unverified"}

