{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.344799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a91ff273363c7e7516f556ada58d679c07e99f56101cc2e40f5d9066c168358","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 27 of arXiv:2506.15044, '2506.15044'] \normalfont
If there exists a $\mathbb{B}$-process $L\in(\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{loc}})^{i,\mathbb{B}}$ such that
\begin{equation}
[L,N]=H_{\bullet}[M,N]
\end{equation}
holds for every process $N\in(\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{loc}})^\mathbb{B}$ (this naturally implies $H\in\mathcal{L}_s^\mathbb{B}([M,N])$), then we say that $H$ is $\mathbb{B}$-integrable w.r.t. $M$. In this case, the $\mathbb{B}$-process $L$, denoted by $H_{\bullet}M$, is called the stochastic integral of $H$ w.r.t. $M$, and the collection of all $\mathbb{B}$-predictable processes which are $\mathbb{B}$-integrable w.r.t. $M$ is denoted by $\mathcal{L}_m^\mathbb{B}(M)$.","status":"unverified"}

