Moreover, we have $f_6 (0, y, z) \ne 0$ and either $s^2 y \in g_8$ or $s^2 z \in g_8$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.779343+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af0c068298cc328d661726d18513c405dad3547578b004e96d0ed2e7ca6b92b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Focal-radius law, at the Bayes limit) of arXiv:2608.23094, 'One Inverse Step is a Convex Program:\\ Bayes-Limit Calibration of Diffusion Inversion'] Let Assumption~\ref{ass:tube} hold, let $\epsb=\epss$, and displace inward from $\xo_{t+1}$ along a principal normal by $r$, writing $w_i:=r\kappa_i$ and $w:=r\kappa_{\max}$ for the scale-free depth ($1/\kappa_{\max}$ being the focal radius). The Picard multipliers are then exactly $+\rhog^{\star}$ ($\times\,D{-}d$) and $-\rhog^{\star}w_i/(1-w_i)$ ($\times\,d$), for every $\sigma_t$ and every data law. Hence \textup{(i)} the \emph{oscillation} boundary, where the dominant multiplier changes sign and equivalently where Assumption~\ref{ass:unimodal} fails, is $w_{\rm osc}=\tfrac12$, \emph{independent of the schedule}; \textup{(ii)} the \emph{divergence} boundary $\lambda_{\max}(\nabla^2\Psi_t)=2$ is $w_{\rm div}=1/(1+\rhog^{\star})$; and \textup{(iii)} at finite noise both carry a closed-form correction set by the full second fundamental form,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
w_{\rm div}&=\frac{1}{1+\rhog^{\star}}+\tfrac12\tilde\sigma_{t+1}^{2}\|\mathrm{II}\|^{2}+O(\tilde\sigma^{4}),\\
w_{\rm osc}&=\tfrac12-\tfrac14(\tilde\sigma\kappa)^{4}+O(\tilde\sigma^{6}).
\end{aligned}

\end{equation} \emph{(Proof: App.~\ref{app:proofs}.)} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.904338+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af2773e3feb48b3ccf6a4e35ba1c7307ac0eb91333b831d4ab4bc006240f41d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary c:wick of arXiv:2505.11389, '\bf The Poisson Multiplication Formula'] Let $k_1,\dots,k_m\geq 1$ be integers, and let the kernels $f_i \in L^2(\mu^{k_i})$, $i=1,\dots,m$, be such that the product $\Phi := \prod_{i=1}^m I_{k_i}(f_i)$ is square-integrable. Then, writing $K=k_1+\cdots +k_m$, one has that $\Phi \in \sum_{q=0}^K C_q$ and the projection of $\Phi$ on $C_K$ coincides with the multiple integral
\begin{equation}
I_K(f_1\otimes \cdots \otimes f_m),
\end{equation}
where we have used the notation (equation e:tensor of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.350501+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af28cbce2ebccfe08e4cceb0c1485ef48a1838941047fa2d5ef29d2f4df8bb3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2511.07350, 'Decoupling of clusters in independent sets in a percolated hypercube'] For a set $S \sse \cH$ and a dimer $\fd$, we write $\fd \sim S$ if $\fd$ shares a neighbor with a vertex in $S$.
Note that this includes the case where $S$ contains a vertex of $\fd$ itself.
Now for $S \sse \cH$, define
\begin{equation}
    \Adj(S) \coloneqq \sum_{\fd \sim S} \phi_\fd.
\end{equation}
For future reference, we also define $\tAdj(S)$ in the same way but with $\phi_\fd$ replaced by $\fphi_\fd$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.902627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af46e3cb487bbad2f02bac2e2e6af5f8f2155a13a1905f5b8cf279224b843a5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thmIP of arXiv:2608.09856, '\bf Mass erasure on measured $\bbR$-trees,\\ applications to L\'evy forests'] We keep the previous notation, we assume $\mathbf{A1}$--$\mathbf{A5}$ and we recall that $\mathbf m_p$ is viewed as a $\mathbb{T}_{\! c}$-valued r.v.~when it is convenient. Then the following holds true. 
\begin{compactenum}

\smallskip

\item[$(i)$] $\mathbf m_p \! \to \! \mathbf m$ in law in $(\mathbb{T}^*\! , {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{era}})$. 

\smallskip

\item[$(ii)$] $\mathbf m_p \! \to \! \mathbf m$ in law in $(\mathbb{T}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GP}})$ iff $\psi$ is of Type C. In particular, if $\psi$ is of Type B, $(\mathbf m_p)_{p\in \mathbb{N}}$ cannot converge in law in $(\mathbb{T}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GP}})$. 

\smallskip

\item[$(iii)$] The laws of $(\mathbf m_p)_{p\in \mathbb{N}}$ are tight on $(\mathbb{T}^1_{\! c}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GHP}})$ iff ((Greydiscr of the paper)) holds true. In this case ((Grey of the paper)) holds true, ${\bf P} (\mathbf m \! \in \!  \mathbb{T}_{\! c})\! = \!  1$ and $\mathbf m_p \! \to \! \Phi_1 (\mathbf m)$ in law in $(\mathbb{T}^1_{\! c}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GHP}})$. \smallskip
\end{compactenum}
Moreover, the convergence in law $\mathbf m_p \! \to \! \mathbf m$ holds jointly with the convergence in law in $\mathbb{R}_+$ of the total masses $\langle \mathbf m_p\rangle$ to $\langle \mathbf m \rangle$, and $\langle \mathbf m \rangle$ is furthermore distributed as the total population $\int_0^\infty \! Z^{x_0}_s ds$. If $\psi$ is of Type C in $(ii)$ (resp.~if ((Greydiscr of the paper)) is satisfied as in $(iii)$), the convergence $\mathbf m_p \! \to \! \mathbf m$ in law in $(\mathbb{T}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GP}})$ (resp.~in law in $(\mathbb{T}^{1}_{\! c}, {\boldsymbol{\delta}}_{\mathtt{GHP}})$) holds jointly with $({\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{\mathbf m_p} ([0, s]))_{s\in \mathbb{R}_+}  \!\!\!\!  \longrightarrow \! \!({\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{\mathbf m} ([0, s]))_{s\in \mathbb{R}_+}\!\! \! \overset{{\textrm{(law)}}}{=} \!\! (\int_0^s \! Z^{x_0}_r dr)_{s\in \mathbb{R}_+} $ in law in $\mathbf C (\mathbb{R}_+, \mathbb{R})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.326751+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af6d8a813ce447baa1ca7dd2d4427641dd57e7e00f0d3fb143c5bc90bfcafb2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 (Adapted linear realization of the arguments in the Hermite polynomials.) of arXiv:2607.14361, 'NeuralChaos: Optimal Adapted Approximation of Square Integrable Predictable Processes'] Let $T>0$ be a (fixed) time-horizon, $I_{\rm H}, J_{\rm H} \in \mathbb{N}$, and $i_{0} \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $J_{\rm H}\leq 2^{i_0}-1$. In addition, let $\Lambda_{I_{\rm H}, J_{\rm H}, i_0}\eqdef\{(i,k)\,:\,i_0 \leq i \leq i_0 + I_{\rm H},\,\,0 \leq k \leq J_{\rm H}\}$; in particular, $\# \Lambda_{I_{\rm H}, J_{\rm H}, i_0}=(I_{\rm H}+1)(J_{\rm H}+1)$ (cf.~Lemma \ref{lem:emulation}). Let $0<s_0<s_1<\ldots<s_{n_{\rm grid}} \leq T$ be the ordered dyadic sampling grid of Lemma \ref{lem:emulation}, where $n_{\rm grid}=(J_{\rm H}+1)(I_{\rm H}+2)$. Define the Brownian sample matrix  $\mathbf{W}^{I_{\rm H}, J_{\rm H}, i_0} \eqdef [W_{s_0}^{\top},\ldots,W_{n_{\rm grid}}^{\top}]^{\top} \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_{\rm grid}+1)\times D}$, and, for every $(i,k)\in\Lambda_{I_{\rm H}, J_{\rm H}, i_0}$ and $r \in [D]_{+}$
\begin{equation}
 Z_{i,k}^r= \tfrac{2^{i/2}}{\sqrt{T}} \,W^r_{\frac{Tk}{2^i}}
    -\tfrac{2^{i/2+1}}{\sqrt{T}}\,W^r_{\frac{T(1+2k)}{2^{i+1}}}
    +\tfrac{2^{i/2}}{\sqrt{T}}\,W^r_{\frac{T(k+1)}{2^i}}.
\end{equation}
Enumerate $\Lambda_{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0} =
\{(i_\ell,k_\ell):\ell\in [R_{\mathrm H}]_{+}\}$ so that the terminal information times $\theta_\ell\eqdef \frac{(k_\ell+1)T}{2^{i_\ell}}$ are non-decreasing, i.e., $\theta_1\le\theta_2\le\cdots\le\theta_{R_{\mathrm H}}$, with arbitrary ordering among ties. If  $\mathbf{Z}^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0}\eqdef [Z_{i_1,k_1}^{\top},Z_{i_2,k_2}^{\top},\ldots,Z_{i_{R_{\mathrm H}},k_{R_{\mathrm H}}}^{\top}]^{\top}$, then there exists a deterministic matrix $A^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0} \in \mathbb R^{R_{\mathrm H}\times(n_{\mathrm{grid}}+1)}$ such that $A^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0}
W^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0}=
Z^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0}$. Moreover, $\left\|A^{I_{\mathrm H},J_{\mathrm H},i_0}\right\|_0 = 3R_{\mathrm H} = 3(I_{\mathrm H}+1)(J_{\mathrm H}+1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.648847+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af740f3d6242abd47aaa366aaf5ee56f8188de39c00f53080cc4648f57f707c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:cut of arXiv:2606.05830, '{ Biased Random Walk on $\mathbb Z_+$ with Traps of Linearly Increasing Depth}\footnote{Su'] Let $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ be a Markov chain on a countable state space. For $n \ge 1$, if
  \begin{align*}
    \{X_0,\dots,X_{n-1}\}\cap \{X_n,X_{n+1},\dots\}=\emptyset,
  \end{align*}
  then $n$ is called a \emph{cut time} and $X_n$ is called a \emph{cutpoint} of the chain.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:31:49.847467+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8af7b3b3ce8b83f99d1226287600e88d2a416d9655cce604100af540b50bf241","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor: limit of hazard rate term of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] The following property holds with probability one:
