{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.766116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6aa9c1364d37db7ec350c2fe7863a50011ab913c16f7844a0bb8da8a555be5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 23 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Let $F_1,F_2$ be two sets such that $F_1 \cap F_2$ is a non-empty finite set. For $i=1,2$, let $(\CE_i,\CF_i)$ be a resistance form on $F_i$, and let $(\CE,\CF)$ be the resistance form on $F_1 \cup F_2$ defined by
 \begin{align*}\begin{split}
  \CF &= \{ f : f|_{F_1} \in \CF_1,\, f|_{F_2} \in \CF_2 \} ,\\
  \CE(f,f) &= \CE_1(f|_{F_1},f|_{F_1})+\CE_2(f|_{F_2},f|_{F_2}) .
 \end{split}\end{align*}
 Then the following holds. Let $A \subseteq F_1 \cup F_2$ be a finite set containing $F_1 \cap F_2$, let $A_i = A \cap F_i$ for $i=1,2$, and let $w_A, w_{A,i}$ be the weight functions associated with $\CE|_A, \CE_i|_{A_i}$, respectively.
 Then
 \[
  w_A(x,y) =
  \begin{cases}
   w_{A,1}(x,y)+w_{A,2}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   w_{A,1}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_1 ,\, \{x,y\} \nsubseteq F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   w_{A,2}(x,y) ,& x,y \in F_2 ,\, \{x,y\} \nsubseteq F_1 \cap F_2 ,\\
   0 ,& \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
 \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.742023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6b3b32bf5d1065a9d5020dea05928f323d1979fa9754252253191870ec3465","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 30 of arXiv:2605.02430, '\textbf{Scaling limit of the range of tree-valued branching random walks in random environ'] \textbf{(Brownian snakes)} 
A $q$-dimensional Brownian snake is a r.v. $(H, W)\! :\!  \Omega\!  \rightarrow \! \fSigma_q(\bbR_+)$ that is measurable with respect to $\mathscr F$ and to the Borel $\sigma$-field of $\fSigma_q(\bbR_+)$, such that for all $p\ino \bbN^*$, and all real numbers 
$0\leqo s_1\leko \ldots \leko s_p$, 
\begin{equation}

\big(W_0,W_{\! s_1}, \ldots , W_{\! s_p} \big) \; \overset{\textrm{(law)}}{=} \;  F_p \big( H ; (B^{(j)}) _{0\leq j\leq p}  ;  (s_j)_{1\leq j\leq p} \big)\; , 
\end{equation}
where $B^{(0)}\eqo W_0$, where $(B^{(j)})_{1\leq j\leq p}$ are independent standard $q$-dimensional Brownian motions that are moreover independent from $W_0$ and $H$. \cq","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:12:13.514784+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6cfd852b0ab0d53ed7996313fc1bc80750252be58b042b095c6177b0163b6f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 (Iteration inequality) of arXiv:2608.28528, 'Strong solutions of SDEs with critical discontinuities in diffusion coefficients'] Fix some $0 < \alpha < 1$ and 
$\varsigma$, $\lambda>1$ such that $$\varsigma<\frac{d}{d-2+2\alpha},$$ and $$\frac{1/(1-\alpha)}{\lambda}=\frac{d/(d-2+2\alpha)}{\varsigma}.$$
Then there exist $\theta>0$, $k>1$ and $m_0$ such that for all $m,n \geq m_0$,
for all $r \geq r_0>\frac{2}{2-\sqrt{\delta_a}}$, we have
\begin{multline}

\|u_m-u_n\|_{L^{\frac{r}{1-\alpha}}([s,s+\theta],L^{\frac{rd}{d-2+2\alpha}})}\\ \leq \left(C_0 \|\nabla u_m\|^2_{L^{2\lambda'}([s,s+\theta],L^{2\varsigma'})}\right)^{\frac{1}{r}}(r^{2k})^{\frac{1}{r}}\|u_m-u_n\|_{L^{(r-2)\lambda}([s,s+\theta],L^{(r-2)\varsigma})}^{1-\frac{2}{r}},
\end{multline}
for a constant $C_0$ that does not depend on $m$ or $s$. Here, as usual, $\frac{1}{\varsigma}+\frac{1}{\varsigma'}=1$, $\frac{1}{\lambda}+\frac{1}{\lambda'}=1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.778352+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8b6cff83f309b8faa5ea37517c736cd06d43b07f02d3476d8c91fc5cf177bae7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 (Uniform local and local-maximum bounds) of arXiv:2609.02257, 'Aperiodic random walks: uniform estimates, local limits and invariance principles'] There are constants \(C,c>0\), independent of \(N,L,z,R\), such that
for all sufficiently large \(N\), \(L,R\in\Z_{\ge0}\), and \(z\in\Z\),
\begin{align}
 \Pp(S_N(L)=z)
