{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.612612+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8cce5c04da20f4e0408ad950e97dd00c3149c54a471874a07669120f294a7c8a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition EHI:d:smallflat of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an isotropic unimodal L\'{e}vy jujmp process on $\mathbb{R}^d$ with jump kernel $j(r)$. For each $r_0>0$, we define the \emph{small/flat decomposition} of $X$ at $r_0$ as follows: For all $r>0$, let
    \begin{equation*}
        j^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}(r) := \left\{\begin{matrix}
            j(r) - \frac12 j(r_0) &:& \mbox{if $r \leq r_0$} \\
            0 &:& \mbox{if $r>r_0$}
        \end{matrix}
        \right.
        , \qquad
        \hat{j}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}(r) := \left\{
        \begin{matrix}
            \frac12 j(r_0) &:& \mbox{if $r \leq r_0$} \\
            j(r) &:& \mbox{if $r>r_0$}
        \end{matrix}
        \right..
    \end{equation*}
    Let $X = X^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle} + \hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ be a Meyer decomposition of $X$, such that $X^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}_0 = X_0$, $\hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}_0=0$, $X^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ has jump kernel $j^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}(r)$, $\hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ has jump kernel $\hat{j}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}(r)$, and $X^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ and $\hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ are independent.
    Let
    \begin{equation}
        m_2\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} j^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle} |x|^2 j(|x|) \; \mathrm{d} x
        \qquad\mbox{and}\qquad
        \lambda\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \hat{j}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}(|x|) \; \mathrm{d} x.
    \end{equation}
    By the L\'{e}vy-Khintchine condition, $\lambda\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle<\infty$.
    Let $T^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle} := \inf\{ t>0 : \hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}_t \neq 0 \}$ be the first time that the process $\hat{X}^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ takes a jump, and note that $T^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$ is Exponential($\lambda\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle$) and independent of the process $X^{\left\langle r_0 \right\rangle}$.

    Denote exit times for the process $X^{\left\langle r \right\rangle}$ by $\tau^{\left\langle r \right\rangle}_U := \inf \{t \geq 0 : X^{\left\langle r \right\rangle}_t \notin U \}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.381132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ccec1248ea773ac56b8b4b19cf437845443e65fde4371b94d44cfd8caaa8029","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem main1 of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] Let \( \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B} \in \mathcal{G}_2^\Lambda(\mathbb{R}^d) \) be two L\'evy generators with finite second moments, and let \( \theta_2(\cdot, \cdot; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) \) denote the Markovian transport derivative of $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}$ defined in Definition~(c-opt-gen of the paper). 

	(i) It holds that
	\[
	\theta_2(x, y; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) 
	= \theta_2(0, 0; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) + (m_\mathcal{A} - m_\mathcal{B})^\top (x - y),
	\]
	where \( m_\mathcal{A}, m_\mathcal{B} \in \mathbb{R}^d \) are the mean vectors associated with \( \mathcal{A} \) and \( \mathcal{B} \), respectively.

	(ii) A $2$-optimal Markovian coupling \( \mathcal{J}_* \in \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) \) exists. In particular, if the generators are in global form with L\'evy triplets $\mathcal{A}=\Lambda_{\text{glob}}(\kappa,\alpha,\mu)$ and $\mathcal{B}=\Lambda_{\text{glob}}(\zeta,\beta,\nu)$, then $\mathcal{J}_*=\Lambda_{\text{glob}}(\eta_*,\sigma_*,\gamma_*)\in \mathcal{G}_2^\Lambda(\mathbb R^{2d})$ is a $2$-optimal coupling, where:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \( \eta_* = \kappa\oplus\zeta \in \mathbb{R}^{2d} \);
		\item \( \sigma_* = \begin{bmatrix} \alpha & K_* \\ K_*^\top & \beta \end{bmatrix} \), where \( K_* \in M(\mathbb{R}^d) \) solves the semidefinite program (equation posdef-prog of the paper);
		\item \( \gamma_* \in \Lambda_2(\mathbb{R}^{2d}) \) is a $2$-optimal L\'evy coupling of \( \mu \) and \( \nu \), given by Definition (levy-opt-prob of the paper).
	\end{itemize}

	(iii) For any $2$-optimal coupling \( \mathcal{J}_* \) of $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}$, it holds that
	\begin{align}
		(e^{t\mathcal{J}_*}c_2)(x,y)
		= c_2(x,y)+t\theta_2(x,y;\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})+ \frac {t^2}{2}|m_\mathcal{A}-m_\mathcal{B}|^2. 
	\end{align}
	In particular, the semigroup $\{e^{t\mathcal{J}_*}\}_{t\ge 0}$ satisfies the following \emph{minimal growth condition}: for every Markovian coupling semigroup $\{T_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ of $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}$,  
	\begin{align}
		(e^{t\mathcal{J}_*}c_2)(x,y) \;\le\; (T_t c_2)(x,y), 
		\qquad \forall\,x,y\in\mathbb{R}^d,\; t\ge 0.
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.662313+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ccef1c64760c3cfe0bec8692a099fcc915cf736e5d9be845101c41a298c122b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm full-joint of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $D$ and $\mathbb P_\delta$ be as in Lemma (lem:conv-fields-fractal of the paper). As $\delta\to0$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \left( h_\delta,\ \tau_\delta,\ \tau_\delta^\dagger \right)  \overset{(d)}{\longrightarrow}\left(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}\lambda}\ h,\ \field{\cos(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}h)},\ \field{\sin(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}h)} \right).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:15:36.166107+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ccf331d79eacc6c7f7712ce41465d0a5d0bec8067b7484cfc2b4d6aad42a307","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 of arXiv:2609.01770, 'Support of Dyson Brownian Motion'] Let $m(z)=m_\mu(z)$ be the Stieltjes transform of a finite measure $\mu$ with $A=\mu(\bR)$. For any integer $p\geq 1$, we denote its $p$-th derivative by $m^{(p)}(z)$. Then, 
\begin{align}
|m(z)|\leq \frac{A}{\dist(z,\supp(\mu))},\quad
\quad |m'(z)|\leq \frac{\Im[m(z)]}{\Im[z]}, 
\quad |m^{(p)}(z)|\leq \frac{p!A}{\dist(z,\supp(\mu))^{p+1}}
