{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:11:44.346065+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d1113e57e07ec318ae8c367f523e9303d6617a7fe0ed6c3860b981cced0d41b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2609.03098, 'Standing waves for Schr\"odinger equations with Kato class potentials and $L^\infty$-bound'] Let $f$ satisfy the conditions $(f1)$\,--\,$(f3)$ with $f(x,0)=0$ for a.e.\
$x\in\R^N$, and let $\alpha, \omega\in L^{\infty}(\R^{N})$ be such that
$$\lim_{u\to 0} \frac{f(x,u)}{u} = \alpha(x) \quad \text{for a.e. }
x\in\R^N, \leqno{(L_0)}$$
and
$$\lim_{|u|\to \infty} \frac{f(x,u)}{u} = \omega(x) \quad \text{for a.e. }
x\in\R^N. \leqno{(L_\infty)}$$
Assume that $\lambda$ belongs to the resolvent set of both $-\Delta+V-\alpha$
and $-\Delta+V-\omega$, and that
\begin{equation}
\max\{\hat\varrho(|\alpha|), \hat\varrho(|\omega|)\} < \inf \sigma_{\mathrm{ess}}
(-\Delta+V)-\lambda \quad\text{and}\quad d^{-}(V-\alpha, \lambda)\neq
d^{-}(V-\omega, \lambda).
\end{equation}
Then there exist a nonzero solution $\bar u \in H^2(\R^N)$ of $(E)_\lambda$
and a bounded solution $u : \R \to H^1(\R^N)$ of $(P)_\lambda$ such that
either $\bar u$ belongs to the $\alpha$-limit set of $u$ and $u(t) \to 0$ in
$H^1(\R^N)$ as $t \to +\infty$, or $\bar u$ belongs to the $\omega$-limit set
of $u$ and $u(t) \to 0$ in $H^1(\R^N)$ as $t \to -\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.850874+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d142fb24fc444f9175396f1d4dab06cc7758a7db297d352d72f9c5b421371cc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 5 of arXiv:2605.18751, '\mytitle'] Let $X$ be a random variable with law $P_\nu$. For every \(x\in J\) with \(\bar F_\nu(x)>0\),
\begin{alignat}{2}
\partial_\nu\log f_\nu(x) &=s_\nu(x)=K_\nu(x)-\E[K_\nu(X)], \\
\partial_\nu\log\bar F_\nu(x) &=\E[s_\nu(X)\mid X\ge x]=\E[K_\nu(X)\mid X\ge x]-\E[K_\nu(X)], \\
\partial_\nu\log h_\nu(x) &=s_\nu(x)-\E[s_\nu(X)\mid X\ge x]=K_\nu(x)-\E[K_\nu(X)\mid X\ge x]. 
\end{alignat}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:23:37.839851+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d1584d09cc8ff2ccd7b19df3fc01cf9c0b2302d7eb1783edeadaa12551d1c1b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2609.03647, 'Extension Problems for the Vladimirov--Taibleson Operator and the Hierarchical Laplacian'] The functions
    \begin{align*}
        \Psi_{\gamma,\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{j}}(\boldsymbol{x})
        =p^{\frac{n(\gamma-1)}{2}}
        \chi_p\!\left(p^{-\gamma}\boldsymbol{j}\cdot
        (\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{a})\right)
        \boldsymbol{1}_{\boldsymbol{a}
        +p^{\gamma-1}\mathbb{Z}_p^n}(\boldsymbol{x}),
    \end{align*}
    where $\gamma\in\mathbb{Z}$, $\boldsymbol{a}$ ranges over a fixed set of representatives of $\mathbb{Q}_p^n/p^{\gamma-1}\mathbb{Z}_p^n$, and $\boldsymbol{j}\in\mathbb{F}_p^n\setminus\{\boldsymbol{0}\}$, form a complete orthonormal basis of $L^2(\mathbb{Q}_p^n)$ and satisfy
    \begin{align*}
        D^\alpha\Psi_{\gamma,\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{j}}
        =p^{\gamma\alpha}
        \Psi_{\gamma,\boldsymbol{a},\boldsymbol{j}}.
    \end{align*}
    Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \operatorname{Spec}(D^\alpha)
        =\{0\}\cup\{p^{\gamma\alpha}\mid\gamma\in\mathbb{Z}\}.
    \end{align*}
    Every positive eigenvalue has infinite multiplicity. The point $0$ belongs to the spectrum as an accumulation point, but it is not an $L^2$-eigenvalue.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.877463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d19e1bf715359a01258367652f1e3038cba413dadb05a4e134b11f95b2f65bb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop-fixation of arXiv:2504.04943, 'Emergence of \bl microbial \bl host dormancy \\ \bl during \bl a persistent virus epidemic'] Assume $r\kappa\mu_1 \neq v \sigma$.
