{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.326398+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d2b623df6bc1c802b2eeec24289b82bd30fb96b5ca92e09412b07e2ea2721a9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:Wasserstein of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Let~$(M,g,\nu)$ be a weighted Riemannian manifold as in Assumption~(ass:WRM of the paper).
Assume further that~$\nu\in \msP_2$ and fix~$\pi\in\msP(\mbfT_\circ)$.
Then,
\begin{enumerate}[$(i)$]
\item $\mcQ_\pi$ is concentrated on~$\msP_2$, i.e.~$\mcQ_\pi(\msP\setminus\msP_2)=0$;
\item $\mcQ_\pi$ is partially quasi-invariant under the action~(equation eq:DiffeoAction of the paper) w.r.t.\ the filtration~$\tseq{\sigma(\hCylP{}{\epsilon})}_{\epsilon\in\mbbI}$, with Radon--Nikod\'ym derivative
\begin{equation}
R^\psi_\epsilon(\eta)= \prod_{x\in \mu : \eta\left\{x\right\}>\epsilon} \frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\psi_\sharp\nu}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d} \nu}(x) \,\mathrm{,}\;\, \qquad \epsilon\in\mbbI\,\mathrm{,}\;\, \quad \eta\in\msP_2\,\mathrm{,}\;\, \quad \psi\in \Diff^\infty_c(M)\,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Furthermore,
\begin{enumerate}[$(i)$, resume]
\item the form~$\tparen{\widehat\mcE_\pi,\FC{\msA}{}}$ in Proposition~(p:StandardCyl of the paper) satisfies assertions~(i:p:StandardCyl:1 of the paper)-(i:p:StandardCyl:3 of the paper) in Proposition~(p:StandardCyl of the paper);

\item its closure~$\big({\mcE_\pi,\msD(\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ coincides with the Cheeger energy~$\Ch_{W_2,\mcQ_\pi}$ of the metric measure space~$(\msP_2,W_2,\mcQ_\pi)$;
\item $\big({\mcE_\pi,\msD(\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ is quasi-regular for the $W_2$-topology;
\item $\big({\mcE_\pi,\msD(\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ satisfies the Rademacher Theorem on~$(\msP_2,W_2)$. That is, every Lipschitz function~$u\colon \msP_2\to{\mathbb R}$ belongs to~$\msD(\mcE_\mcQ)$, is Fr\'echet differentiable $\mcQ$-a.e.\ and its Otto gradient~$\boldsymbol\nabla u\colon \msP_2\to T\msP_2$ satisfies
\[
\left\lVert\boldsymbol\nabla u\right\rVert_{T_\eta\msP_2}\leq \Lip_{W_2}[u] \,\mathrm{.}
\]
\item the semigroup~$\mcH_\bullet$ associated with~$\big({\mcE_\pi,\msD(\mcE_\pi)}\big)$ satisfies the following one-sided integral Varadhan short-time asymptotic estimate: for every pair of Borel subsets~$A_1,A_2\subset\msP_2$ with~$\mcQ_\pi A_1, \mcQ_\pi A_2>0$,
\begin{equation}
-2\, \lim_{t\downarrow 0} t \log \scalar{\car_{A_1}}{\mcH_t \car_{A_2}}_{L^2(\mcQ_\pi)} \ \geq\ \mcQ_\pi\text{-}\essinf_{\eta_i\in A_i} W_2(\eta_1,\eta_2)^2 \,\mathrm{,}\;\,
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\begin{proof}
(i:t:Wasserstein:1 of the paper) is a simple consequence of choosing~$f=\mssd_g(o,{\,\cdot\,})$ in Lemma~(l:SimpleTridLp of the paper).

By assumption, the density~$\nu=\rho\vol_g$ is continuous and everywhere positive.
Thus, since each~$\psi\in \Diff^\infty_c(M)$ is compactly non-identical, the Radon--Nikod\'ym derivative~$\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\psi_\sharp\nu}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\nu}$ is continuous, and uniformly bounded away from~$0$ and infinity on~$M$.
This shows that the product in~(equation eq:t:Wasserstein:0 of the paper) is well-defined, finite, and non-zero for every~$\epsilon>0$, every~$\eta\in\msP_2$, and every~$\psi\in\Diff^\infty_c(M)$.
As a consequence,~$\psi_\sharp\nu$ too is an element of~$\msP_2$.
Finally, for each~$\mbfx\eqdef\left(x_1,\dotsc, x_N\right)\in M^{\scriptscriptstyle\times N}$ (including in the case~$N=\infty$) and every~$\psi\in\Diff^\infty_c(M)$, set~$\psi^\diamond(\mbfx)\eqdef \left(\psi(x_1),\dotsc, \psi(x_N)\right)$.
Note that the action of~$\Diff^\infty_c(M)$ on~$\msP_2$ commutes with the measure representation~$\mathrm{em}$ in~(equation eq:TransferMap of the paper), and we have
\[
\psi_\sharp \mathrm{em}(\mbfs,\mbfx)= \mathrm{em}(\mbfs, \psi^\diamond(\mbfx)) \qquad \text{and}\qquad {\psi_\sharp}_\sharp\mcQ_{\pi,\nu}= \mcQ_{\psi_\sharp\nu,\pi}\,\mathrm{.}
\]
Then,~(i:t:Wasserstein:1.5 of the paper) holds similarly to the proof of the same assertion for the Dirichlet--Ferguson measure in~[LzDS17+].

(i:t:Wasserstein:2 of the paper) is shown in Proposition~(p:StandardCyl of the paper). 

(i:t:Wasserstein:3 of the paper) is a consequence of~[ForSavSod22].

(i:t:Wasserstein:4 of the paper) is a consequence of the identification with~$\Ch_{W_2,\mcQ_\pi}$ and either: the quasi (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:06:55.258401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d2b903c2e19fa2947e7bfb16b5f9ebb80fd0d1374fd5f4c872f7a6d018282f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:sion of arXiv:2512.21026, 'Minimax Duality in Game-Theoretic Probability\thanks{Work in progress: mistakes are likely'] {sion1958general}}]
  Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a convex subset of a linear topological space and $\mathcal{Y}$ a convex subset of a linear topological space, where at least one of $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$ is compact.  If $f:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfies
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item $\forall x\in \mathcal{X}$, $f(x,\cdot)$ is upper semicontinuous and quasi-concave on $\mathcal{Y}$, and
  \item $\forall y\in \mathcal{Y}$, $f(\cdot,y)$ is lower semicontinuous and quasi-convex on $\mathcal{X}$,
  \end{enumerate}
  then $\min_{x\in \mathcal{X}}\sup_{y\in \mathcal{Y}} f(x,y)=\sup_{y\in \mathcal{Y}}\min_{x\in \mathcal{X}}f(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

