{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.302988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d330fb8f770068b9f12712e7943d18096fce5225382e977963b4d6549fb6ea5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop5.2 of arXiv:2604.22130, 'Reflected Stochastic Differential Equations Driven by $G$-Brownian Motion with Nonlinear C'] Let $(x^i,f^i,h^i,g^i,l^i,r^i)$, $i=1,2$ be two sets of parameters satisfying Assumption (ass1 of the paper) and Assumption (ass2 of the paper). Suppose that $(X^i,A^i)$ is the solution to reflected $G$-SDE with parameters $(x^i,f^i,h^i,g^i,l^i,r^i)$, $i=1,2$. Set 
\begin{align*}
\hat{x}:=x^1-x^2, \hat{f}:=f^1-f^2, \hat{h}:=h^1-h^2, \hat{g}:=g^1-g^2, \hat{X}:=X^1-X^2.
\end{align*}
Then, there exists a positive constant $C$ depending on $T,G,L,p$, such that
\begin{align*}
\hat{\mathbb{E}}\left[\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|\hat{X}_t|^p\right]\leq C&\Bigg\{|\hat{x}|^p+\hat{\mathbb{E}}\left[\int_0^T|\hat{f}(t,X^1_t)|^pdt\right]+\hat{\mathbb{E}}\left[\int_0^T |\hat{h}(t,X^1_t)|^pdt\right]\\
&+\hat{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(\int_0^T|\hat{g}(t,X^1_t)|^2 dt\right)^{\frac{p}{2}}\right]+\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|\bar{l}_T|^p]+\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|\bar{r}_T|^p]\Bigg\},
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
\bar{l}_T=\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|(l^1)^{-1}(t,0)-(l^2)^{-1}(t,0)|, \  \bar{r}_T=\sup_{t\in[0,T]}|(r^1)^{-1}(t,0)-(r^2)^{-1}(t,0)|.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.143138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d343519005e883d63674602fdd24b170114cf8385c82b8a84ef90c2dae2d06b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2604.05999, 'Branching Process in a Varying Environment: How to Grow Like the Product of Means'] Let $\{Z_n\}$ be a BPVE, ${\bf E}X_{i,1}$ be positive and $\Var X_{i,1}$ be finite for every $i$. Let 
    $\xi_i = \log {\bf E}X_{i,1}$, $\zeta_i = \Var X_{i,1} e^{-2\xi_i}$, $i>0$. Let $S_n=\xi_1+\dotsb+\xi_n$. Assume that 
    \begin{eqnarray}  
     {\bf P}\left(Z_n \rightarrow \infty \right) + {\bf P}\left(\exists n:  Z_n = 0 \right) = 1,\\
     \liminf_{l \to \infty} a_l<+\infty,\ a_l:=\sum_{i = 0}^{\infty} \zeta_{i+l} e^{-(S_{i+l} - S_l)}. 
    \end{eqnarray}
    Then the martingale $W_n=Z_n/\exp(S_n)$, $n\ge 1$, converges in $L^2$ to some random variable $W$ and 
    \begin{equation}

    {\bf P}(W>0)={\bf P}(Z_n>0,\ n>0)>0.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.019220+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d344695d6c899f0fff79128e44200af5e5d90e3e913e7610932040b6146d50c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 21 (Laguerre approximation on a logarithmic negative window) of arXiv:2608.15137, '2608.15137'] Fix constants \(0<c_0<C_0<\infty\) and \(D<\infty\). For positive integers \(n\leq m\), let
\(\phi_{n,m}\), \(\mu_{n,m}\), \(\sigma_{n,m}\), and \(\delta_{n,m}\) be as in Proposition~\ref{prop:positive-airy-laguerre}. There are constants \(C<\infty\) and \(N_0\) such that, whenever
\[
N\geq N_0,\qquad
c_0N\leq n\leq m\leq C_0N,\qquad
0\leq R\leq D(\log N)^{1/3},
\]
and \(-R\leq s\leq0\),
\[
\abs{
\frac{
(-1)^n\sqrt{\sigma_{n,m}}\,
\phi_{n,m}(\mu_{n,m}+\sigma_{n,m}s)
}{
\sqrt{\delta_{n,m}}
}
-\Ai(s)
}
\leq
CN^{-2/3}(1+R)^3\exp\bigl(C(1+R)^2\bigr).
\]
In particular, the right side is \(N^{-2/3+o(1)}\) uniformly over the stated family.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.370997+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d3459d747a1be24cc2b4ad04b38d175291052fdae3f0ff7a9f326c5be9cee0b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2606.03661, '2606.03661'] Let $\mathscr{L}_t[f(t);z]$ be the Laplace transform of the function $f(t)$ with conjugate variable $z$. Here $t$ denotes the time variable and $z$ is the Laplace dual variable. Then 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split} 
& \mathscr{L}_t[t^\mu \Psi_\alpha^\nu(-\kappa t);z] \\[1ex]
& \quad = 
\frac{1}{z^{1+\mu}} \frac{G(1+\nu)}{G(2+\nu-\alpha)} \frac{1}{2\pi i} 
