{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.737286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8df0d6c976e23f68f40dfb41683c50db35b11999affade7fb2ff98a5fd15fed9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi combs of arXiv:2609.02346, 'Regularised double shuffle relations for planar \AZVs{}'] With these notations, we notice that all trees $t$ admit respectively a \emph{right comb representation} and a \emph{left comb representation}:
	\begin{align}
		t&=\decombright{F_1}{F_2}{F_k}{\omega_1}{\omega_2}{\omega_{k}}{\omega_{k+1}}
		& \text{ and } &&  t=\decombleft{F_{k+2}}{F_{k+3}}{F_{k+l+1}}{\omega_{k+2}}{\omega_{k+3}}{\omega_{k+l+1}}{\omega_{k+l+2}},
	\end{align}

	where $F_1,\dots,F_k$ and $F_{k+2},\dots, F_{k+l+1}$ are forests, possibly empty . In the following, we will consider that there exist two further forests $F_{k+1}$ and $F_{k+l+2}$, both empty and respectively grafted to the nodes decorated by $\omega_{k+1}$ and $\omega_{k+l+2}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.360551+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8df260fcafcad32c1605d409faa02fddde26dc65373871678ce7532fc7967c15","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition c-opt-gen of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] Let $\Pi$ be a state space, $c:\Pi^2\to[0,\infty)$ be a cost function, 
and let \( \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B} \in \mathcal{G}(\Pi) \) be two Feller generators on \( \Pi \). The \emph{Markovian transport derivative} is the function \( \theta_c(\cdot, \cdot; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) : \Pi^2 \to [-\infty, \infty] \) defined by
\begin{align}
    \theta_c(x, y; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) := \inf_{\mathcal{J} \in \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B})} (\mathcal{J}^- c)(x, y),
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{J}^-$ is the lower generator given in Definition (def:lower-gen of the paper). We say that \( \mathcal{J}_* \in \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}) \) is a \emph{\( c \)-optimal coupling} of \( \mathcal{A} \) and \( \mathcal{B} \) if $c\in \bar D(\mathcal{J}_*)$ and it holds for all $(x,y)\in\mathbb R^{2d}$:
\begin{align}
    (\mathcal{J}_* c)(x, y) = \theta_c(x, y; \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B}).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.217355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8df385a7379543450fa968e7fd6a91a73744d13c6aefd0e66f1c2e80de591342","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:projection-geometry of arXiv:2608.19358, 'An optimal Poincaré inequality\\for the complex Ginibre log-gas'] The following properties hold.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    $\mathcal{H}_n^{\mathrm{div},0}$ and
    $\overline{\mathcal{H}_n^{\mathrm{div},0}}$ are orthogonal.
  \item For every symmetric $f\in L^2(\mu_n)$,
    \begin{equation}
      \|Pf\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2+\|Qf\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2
      \leq
      \|f\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2+\|P_0f\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2.
    \end{equation}
  \item If $f\in L^2(\mu_n)$ is real-valued, symmetric, and centered, then
    $h=Pf$, its pointwise complex conjugate $\overline h$, and
    $r=f-h-\overline h$, are three pairwise orthogonal functions in $L^2(\mu_n)$, and
    \begin{align}
      \|f\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2
      &=2\|h\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2+\|r\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2,
      \\
      \operatorname{dist}_{L^2(\mu_n)}
      \bigl(f,\mathcal{H}_n^{\mathrm{div}}\bigr)^2
      &=\frac{1}{2}\|f\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2
        +\frac{1}{2}\|r\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2.

    \end{align}
    In particular, we have the half-distance estimate 
    \begin{equation}
      \operatorname{dist}_{L^2(\mu_n)}
      \bigl(f,\mathcal{H}_n^{\mathrm{div}}\bigr)^2
      \geq\frac{1}{2}\|f\|_{L^2(\mu_n)}^2.
    \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.711747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8df3d2e68dc5241ec76f223a60f9508d80f663a030f9c3482c0900dcd9c45b08","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma NONet of arXiv:2604.11761, 'The smallest singular value of signed random combinatorial matrices'] For any $0<\varepsilon<1$ there exists an $\varepsilon$-net $\mathcal{N} \subset {\mathbb{S}}^{n-1}$ such that
