        s_z(k,i)\coloneqq \theta^{\frac{i(i+1)}{2}-ki}\left(\frac{za}{a+1}\right)^{i+1}\frac{1}{(i+1)!}\left(\frac{(a+1)(i+1)}{za}-\theta^{i+1-k}\right).
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.466936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e04654b4b8352294f6b84bbb8b60c64e895bd20462167561dd8313fe75c5272","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 24 (Mass retracts) of arXiv:2604.04267, 'On Sharpest Tail Bounds for Functions \\ of Tail Bounded Random Variables'] A mass retract \index{Mass retract} is a measurable function $\gamma$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that 
   \begin{align}
      0 \leq' \gamma(x) \leq' x, \ &\forall x \in [0,\infty)^n  \\
      \gamma(x) = x, \  \ &\forall x \notin [0,\infty)^n. 
   \end{align} 
   A mass retract $\gamma$ is said to be of $U$, for $U \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ measurable, iff $\gamma(U) \subset U$ and $\forall x \notin U, \ \gamma(x) = x$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.913025+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e05bcf9bbba462359d4278f6b006585562b0fb29a290d136c99ef336acb1b17","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:sub for noninvariant of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},P)$ be a probability space (not necessarily standard), and $T:\Omega\to \Omega$ be an invertible measurable transformation. Suppose that the limit $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P\circ T^{i}$ exists, denoted by $Q$. 	Suppose that $\{f_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a sequence of $\mathcal{F}$-measurable functions satisfying the following conditions:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item there exists $\lambda >0$ such that $-\lambda n\le f_n(\omega)\le \lambda n$ for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$, and $\omega\in\Omega$;
		\medskip
		\item for each $k,n\in\mathbb{N}$, $f_{n+k}\le f_n+f_k\circ T^n$.
	\end{longlist} 
	If $Q$ is ergodic, then there exists a constant $c\in\mathbb{R}$ such that 
	\[\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}f_n=c,\text{ }P\text{-a.s.}\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.242280+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e068b322525e5e6ef1809c46e246a286fe8c22dbc93a9f957ba994bc863169d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2605.25719, 'A note on convergence rate for reflected BSDEs with quadratic generators by penalization m'] Under the assumptions of Theorem \ref{th2}, define
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&p_{t}^{n,\lambda}=E^{\mathcal{F}_{t}}[Y_{\phi(n,t)}^{n,\lambda}]-Y^{n,\lambda}_{t}, \\
&q_{t}^{n,\lambda}=\lambda (E^{\mathcal{F}_{t}}[Y_{\phi(n,t)}^{n,\lambda}]-S_{t})^{-}-\lambda(Y^{\lambda}_{t}-S_{t})^{-}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Then $\vert p_{t}^{n,\lambda} \vert \leq C(1+\lambda)\frac{1}{n}+C\lambda^{max\{4-2\varepsilon,\frac{4}{\epsilon}-2\}}\left(\frac{1}{n} \right)^{\frac{\varepsilon}{2}}:=P^{n,\lambda}$, $\vert q_{t}^{n,\lambda} \vert \leq C\lambda :=Q^{n,\lambda}$.
\begin{proof}
From \eqref{e3} we have
\begin{equation*}
p_{t}^{n,\lambda}=-E^{\mathcal{F}_{t}} \left[ \int_{t}^{\phi(n,t)}  f^{\lambda} \left( s,E^{\mathcal{F}_{s}}\left[ Y_{\phi(n,s)}^{n,\lambda} \right],Z_{s}^{n,\lambda} \right)  \right],t\in[0,T].
\end{equation*}
Then 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\vert p_{t}^{n,\lambda} \vert &\leq E^{\mathcal{F}_{t}} \left[ \int_{t}^{\phi(n,t)} \vert f^{\lambda} \left( s,E^{\mathcal{F}_{s}}\left[ Y_{\phi(n,s)}^{n,\lambda} \right],Z_{s}^{n,\lambda} \right) \vert \right]\\
&\leq C(1+M_{\lambda}+\lambda)\frac{T}{n}+CE^{\mathcal{F}_{t}} \left[ \int_{t}^{\phi(n,t)}\vert Z_{s}^{n,\lambda} \vert^{2-\varepsilon}ds \right]\\
&\leq C(1+M_{\lambda}+\lambda)\frac{T}{n}+CE^{\mathcal{F}_{t}} \left[ \left( \int_{t}^{\phi(n,t)}\vert Z_{s}^{n,\lambda} \vert^{2}ds \right)^{1-\frac{\varepsilon}{2}} \left( \phi(n,t)-t \right)^{\frac{\varepsilon}{2}} \right]\\
&\leq C(1+M_{\lambda}+\lambda)\frac{T}{n}+CN_{\lambda}^{2-\varepsilon}\left(\frac{T}{n} \right)^{\frac{\varepsilon}{2}}\\
&=C(1+\lambda)\frac{T}{n}+C\lambda^{max\{4-2\varepsilon,\frac{4}{\epsilon}-2\}}\left(\frac{T}{n} \right)^{\frac{\varepsilon}{2}}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
On the other hand, 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\vert q_{t}^{n,\lambda} \vert &\leq C\lambda \left[ (E^{\mathcal{F}_{t}}[Y_{\phi(n,t)}^{n,\lambda}])^{-}+\Vert S^{+} \Vert_{\mathcal{S}^{\infty}(R)}+\Vert Y^{\lambda} \Vert_{\mathcal{S}^{\infty}(R)} \right]\\
&\leq C\lambda 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
from Lemma \ref{l2} and \ref{boundary_lemma}.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.830405+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e0dfda2a926f531cf3d4d8847ceafecbc4153c5f12b345d66dbf9d0fe842123","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2608.13358, 'Extreme points of sets of probability measures and $\phi$-divergences'] For an element $p \in \cM$, the following statements are equivalent.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $p$ is an extreme point of $\cM$.
        \item  There exists no $p' \in \cM\setminus \{p\}$ with $p'\ll p$ and $p$-a.s.\ bounded density $\frac{\mathrm{d}p'}{\mathrm{d}p}$.
        \item  For all $q\in \cM$ with $p\ll q$ and all $\eps\in (0,1)$, there exists no $p' \in \cM\setminus\{p\}$ with $p'\ll q$ and $q$-a.s.\ bounded density $\frac{\mathrm{d}p'}{\mathrm{d}q}$ and
        \begin{equation}
           (1+\eps) \frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}q} -\eps\frac{\mathrm{d}p'}{\mathrm{d}q}\geq 0 \quad q\text{-a.s.}
        \end{equation}
        \item 
        For every $p'\in\cM\setminus\{p\}$, every $p'$-admissible dominating measure $q$ of $p$, every $\eps_0 \in (0,1]$ satisfying condition \ref{it:admissible:eps} in Definition~\ref{def:admissible}, and every continuous convex function $\varphi \colon [0,\infty)\to [0,\infty)$ with $\varphi(0)>0$ and $\varphi=0$ on $[a,\infty)$ for some $a\in(0,\eps_0)$, the point $\lambda = 1$ is a strict local maximizer of the map
        \begin{equation}
        [0,1] \to [0,\infty),\quad \lambda \mapsto \Div{\varphi}{\lambda p + (1-\lambda)p'}{q}.
        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:51:33.322380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e0e44e1322cd22a39345278ebe44c5816d1d794a6f2e677571b3b04e4616aac","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma smash-module-internal-hom of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $({\mathcal{C}},X)$ be an idempotent in $\CAT_T^\textup{$S$-cc}$. Then ${\mathcal{D}}\in\CAT_T^\textup{$S$-cc}$ is a ${\mathcal{C}}$-module if and only if evaluation at $X$ defines an equivalence
    \begin{equation}
        \ev_X\colon\ul{\textup{Fun}}^\textup{$S$-cc}_T({\mathcal{C}},{\mathcal{D}})\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}{\mathcal{D}}.
    \end{equation}
    \begin{proof}
        By Remark~(rk:S-cc-closed of the paper), the symmetric monoidal structure on $\CAT_T^\textup{$S$-cc}$ afforded by the parametrized Lurie tensor product is closed, with internal homs given by $\ul{\textup{Fun}}^\textup{$S$-cc}$.
        Thus, the fully faithful functor $\textup{fgt}\colon\text{LMod}_{\mathcal{C}}(\CAT_T^\textup{$S$-cc})\to \CAT_T^\textup{$S$-cc}$ admits a right adjoint such that the counit is given for any ${\mathcal{D}}$ by the map $((eq:cofree-counit of the paper))$.
        The claim now follows since the essential image of any fully faithful left adjoint consists precisely of those objects for which the counit is invertible. 
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.318330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e101a5170e0e1692ca8fa9c7ccedfca5bd1cb48377a95fcb8c2c4496a073789","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:windowproduct of arXiv:2608.30973, 'Pseudo-Anosov flow and dynamics on guts'] Let \((N,N_{+},N_{-},\gamma_{N})\) be a product sutured manifold component of \(M-(F_{\varphi}(z)\cup \mathcal{A}_{\varphi})\).
