{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:27:11.332854+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e304d66e718744fb6c5449dfa586a06eaa4c50d204b6d521a15aabad79b26e3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition e: correlation moment integrability of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] In the setting of Proposition~(p:mixing condition uniformity of the paper), if $p\in[0,\infty)\setminus2\N_0$, then one can guarantee that $S_\Phi$ exists and has an expansion of the form~(equation e: sf asymptotics of the paper) with $a=0$ if 
    \begin{equation}
        \int (\|y\|+1)^p\, |\beta_\Phi|(dy) < \infty,
    \end{equation}
    and if 
    \begin{equation}
        \int y^{\otimes q}\, \beta_\Phi(dy) = 0
    \end{equation}
    for $q\in2\N_0$ with $q < p$. If $p\in2\N_0$ and if $\Phi$ is isotropic or $p=0$, then under the same conditions one obtains~(equation e: sf asymptotics of the paper) with
    \begin{equation}
        a = \frac{(-1)^{\frac{p}{2}}}{p!}\int y_1^p\, \beta_\Phi(dy).
    \end{equation}
    If $p\in2\N$ and $\Phi$ is not isotropic, then the coefficient, and even the leading order in the asymptotics of $S_\Phi$ can be direction-dependent. Still, $\Phi$ is beyond $p$-uniform iff~(equation e: correlation zero integral of the paper) holds also for $q=p$. If $p\in[-d, 0)$,~(equation e: correlation moment integrability of the paper) cannot guarantee that $\hat\beta_\Phi$ has a density, but it still implies that $\Phi$ is beyond $p$-uniform.
    \begin{proof}
         If~(equation e: correlation moment integrability of the paper) holds for $p\geq 0$, then $\hat\beta_\Phi$ has a continuous density. Further, since 
        \begin{equation*}
            e^{-i\langle k, y\rangle} = \sum_{q=0}^{\lfloor p \rfloor} \frac{(-i)^q}{q!} \langle k,y\rangle^q + o(|\langle k, y\rangle|^p), \quad k, y\in\R^d,
        \end{equation*}
        as $\langle k,y\rangle\to 0$, one can calculate that
        \begin{equation}
            S_\Phi(k) = \sum_{q=0}^{\lfloor p \rfloor} \frac{(-i)^q}{q!} \int y^{\otimes q}\, \beta_\Phi(dy)\,  k^{\otimes q} + o(\| k\|^p)
        \end{equation}
        as $k\to 0$. Hence, the assertion follows from the assumption of~(equation e: correlation zero integral of the paper). Note that isotropy of $\Phi$ implies isotropy of $\beta_\Phi$, whereby $\int \langle k, x\rangle^p\, \beta_\Phi(dy)$ is constant for $k\in\partial B_\varepsilon$ for every $\varepsilon>0$.

        If~(equation e: correlation moment integrability of the paper) holds for $p\in[-d,0)$, then 
        \begin{equation*}
            \frac{\BV[\Phi(B_r)]}{r^{d-p}} = \int r^p\frac{\I_{B_r}\star \I_{B_r}(y)}{r^d} \, \beta_\Phi(dy)
            \xrightarrow{r\to \infty} 0
        \end{equation*}
        by the theorem of dominated convergence. Hence, $\Phi$ is beyond $p$-uniform by Theorem~(t:asymptotic variance test function of the paper).
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:29:08.557165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e30527e2b846a24d7b8d04fdc73995ee492a6ff510ff214f104541f035ad0f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:disk+QT of arXiv:2411.19810, 'Radial conformal welding in Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $W,W_1,W_2,W_3>0$ such that $W_2+W_3=W_1+2$ or $W_1+W_3=W_2+\gamma^2-2$. There exists some constant $c:=c_{W,W_1,W_2,W_3}\in (0,
	\infty)$ such that
	\begin{equation}
	    \mathrm{QT}(W+W_1,W+W_2,W_3)\otimes \mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(W-2;W_2-2,W_1-W_2) = c\int_0^\infty {\mathcal{M}}^\mathrm{disk}_{0,2}(W;\ell)\times\mathrm{QT}(W_1,W_2,W_3;\ell)d\ell.
	\end{equation}
	That is, if we draw an independent $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(W-2;W_1-2,W_2-W_1)$ curve $\eta$ on a sample from $\mathrm{QT}(W+W_1,W+W_2,W_3)$ embedded as $(D,\phi,a_1,a_2,a_3)$, then the quantum surfaces to the left and right of $\eta$ are corresponding independent quantum disks and triangles conditioned on having the same interface quantum length. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.447877+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e306fae4ae9b48411de6592b84ed8e3003c8c5a97cff2e0f9bacbe921d708a3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Deff fxn-vanish of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] For an arbitrary set $A$ and functions $f: A \to [-\infty,\infty]$ and $g:A \to [0,\infty]$, we say $f$ vanishes over $g$ if one of the following equivalent conditions holds: \begin{enumerate}

        \item $\forall \epsilon > 0, \exists M > 0$ such that $|f|\mathbbm{1}_{|f|>M} \leq \epsilon g$
        \item $\forall \epsilon > 0, \exists N > 0$ such that $|f| \leq \epsilon g + N$
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.207233+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e312596964efc72b9ed2dcca30a4f7ab2f7d3bbed38b74d394a4852d178eab2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2604.27242, 'Statistical Inference for Homogenization Limits Driven by Wiener or Hermite Processes'] Suppose that \(m>2\) and \(H\in(3/4,1-1/(2m))\). Then:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If \(H^*(m)>1/4\), then \(r_I\geq r_S\).
    \item If \(H^*(m)<1/4\), then \(r_I\leq r_S\).
    \item If \(H^*(m)=1/4\) then \(r_I=r_S\).
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.028767+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e367dfaa2a59d8aa7dd49d882b6612e9474cc67bbecfd0f4d354118e017537c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:1* of arXiv:2512.09219, 'Strong confluence of geodesics in Liouville quantum gravity'] For distinct points $u_+,v_+,u_-,v_-\in \mathbb{C}$, we say that $\mathscr{X}^{u_+,v_+}_{u_-,v_-}$ occurs if the following hold.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item  The $D_h$-geodesics $P_+,P_-$ from $u_+$ to $v_+$ and $u_-$ to $v_-$ are unique. Further, $P_+\cap P_-$ is non-empty and is a non-trivial simple path, whose (distinct) starting and ending points we call $u$ and $v$ respectively.
  \item  There is a unique $D_h$-geodesic $\Gamma_{u_+,v_-}$ (resp.\ $\Gamma_{u_-,v_+}$) and this is equal to the concatenation of $\Gamma_{u_+,u}, \Gamma_{u,v}, \Gamma_{v,v_-}$ (resp.\ $\Gamma_{u_-,u}, \Gamma_{u,v}, \Gamma_{v,v_+}$).
  \item  The geodesics $\Gamma_{u_-,u}, \Gamma_{v,v_-}$ lie to the right of $P_+$ and the geodesics $\Gamma_{u_+,u},\Gamma_{v,v_+}$ lie to the left of $P_-$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.447972+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e467f898842e617f267edc054b21635050a8c6817625fcb86dcac71bf7d3b56","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Deff rv-vanish of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] For collections of random variables $\{X_t\},\{Y_t\}$ defined on the same probability space and $Y_t > 0$ a.s., we say $\{X_t\}$ vanishes over $\{Y_t\}$ if one of the following equivalent conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\forall \epsilon > 0, \exists M > 0$ such that almost surely $|X_t|\mathbbm{1}_{|X_t|>M} \leq \epsilon Y_t$ for all $t$.
        \item $\forall \epsilon > 0, \exists N > 0$ such that almost surely $|X_t| \leq N + \epsilon Y_t$ for all $t$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.662906+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8e47d4473471d131d6a3ec4c9605fd615c3b09300cda92fbace6795bc79680d1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] Let the multi-variable Fourier multiplier $\sigma(\xi,\eta)\in C^{\infty}(\R^{d}\times\R^{d}\setminus (0,0))$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&|\p_{\xi}^{\alpha}\p_{\eta}^{\beta}\sigma(\xi,\eta)|\lesssim_{\alpha,\beta}(|\xi|+|\eta|)^{-|\alpha|+|\beta|},\ \forall (\alpha,\beta),\ \forall (\xi,\eta)\neq (0,0);\\
&\sigma(\xi,0)\equiv 0.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Define the bilinear Fourier integral operator
\begin{equation}
B_{\sigma}(f,g)(x):=\int_{\R^{2d}}e^{ix\cdot(\xi+\eta)}\sigma(\xi,\eta)\hat{f}(\xi)\hat{g}(\eta)d\xi d\eta,\ x\in\R^{d}.
\end{equation}
Then for all $1<p<\infty$, we have
\begin{equation}
\|B_{\sigma}(f,g)\|_{L^{p}(\R^{d})}\lesssim_{p,\sigma}\|f\|_{L^{p}(\R^{d})}\cdot [g]_{BMO(\R^{d})}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

