{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.049752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fa9c43b58eddd7f25dbc45da5da649356912707f36be54216405264dadf7732","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 (\citet{ejp2021,sambale2023some}) of arXiv:2608.26618, 'A Unified Descriptive-Complexity Framework for Model Selection under Correlated Designs'] Let $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon_1,\ldots,\varepsilon_n)^\top$ be independent, mean-zero
sub-Weibull $(\alpha,\zeta)$ random variables for all $i$ and some $\alpha\in(0,2]$.
Let $A=(a_{ij})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ be a symmetric matrix. Then, for every $t\ge 0$,
\begin{equation*}
\Pr\!\Big(
\big|\varepsilon^\top A\varepsilon-\mathbb{E}(\varepsilon^\top A\varepsilon)\big|
\ge t
\Big)
\ \le\
2\exp\!\left\{
-c_{\alpha,\zeta}
