{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.784482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fe75e377679478b49eccafc8bca70639f8d053f9279ce3a96f4dd31ae604cee","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2604.00474, 'Trap behaviors for Brownian motions'] We will say that a domain $D$ belongs to class $\mathcal{D}$ if there exists an increasing
sequence of domains $D_n \subset D$ with the following properties.\begin{itemize}
\item (i) Each $D_n$ is a Lipschitz domain with characteristics $(\lambda, r_n)$ and $\cup_{n=1}^\infty D_n = D.$
\item (ii) For every $n\geq 1,$ the set $\partial D_n \cap  \partial D$ is a subset of the relative interior of $\partial D_{n+1} \cap  \partial D.$
\item (iii) $\sup_{n\geqslant 1} |\partial D_n| < \infty$  and $\lim_{n\to\infty} |\partial D_n \setminus  \partial D| = 0.$
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.226997+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fe7a710752e5831b7331406b403d2e386cb75b86b6812903f012daa0d500feb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def_DOA of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] For a point process $\Pi$, we say a point process $\theta$ is in the domain of attraction of $\Pi$, written as  $ \theta \in \textrm{Dom}_{\lambda}(\Pi)$, if $\theta_t^\lambda$ given by (equation theta_t-def of the paper) started from $\theta_0^\lambda = \theta$ converges to $\Pi$ in the sense that for all $f \in C_c^+(\mathbb R),$ 
