{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.324250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_901c3c916963f4016ada5e79c0bec8c481b8031f14397cddd36b4d6bf54f3485","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:SimpleTridLp of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Fix~$p\in [1,\infty)$ and~$\pi\in\msP(\mbfT)$.
Let~$f\in L^p(\nu)$. Further let~$\tilde{f}$ be a any $\nu$-representative of~$f$ and let~$\tilde{f}^\star\colon \msP \to {\mathbb R}\cup\left\{\pm\infty\right\}$ be defined as in~(equation eq:SimpleTrid of the paper).
Then,
\begin{enumerate}[$(i)$]
\item $\tilde{f}^\star$ is well-defined and independent of the choice of the representative;
\item its class~$f^\star\eqdef \tclass[\mcQ_\pi]{\tilde{f}^\star}$ satisfies~$f^\star\in L^p(\mcQ_\pi)$;
\item the map~$\square^\star\colon L^p(\nu)\to L^p(\mcQ_\pi)$ is Lipschitz non-expansive.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{proof}
Assume first that~$f\geq 0$ and take~$\tilde{f}\geq 0$ (everywhere on~$M$). Then $\tilde{f}^\star\colon\msP\to [0,\infty]$ is well-defined (possibly infinite), and, by Jensen's inequality,
\begin{equation}
\int (\tilde{f}^\star)^p \mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\mcQ_\pi \leq \int (\tilde{f}^p)^\star \mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\mcQ_\pi = \int \sum_i^Ns_i \int \tilde{f}(x_i)^p\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\nu(x_i) \mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\pi(\mbfs) = \left\lVertf\right\rVert_{L^p(\nu)}^p\,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
This proves all the assertions for~$f\in L^p(\nu)^+$. The conclusion follows by applying~(equation eq:l:SimpleTridLp:1 of the paper) to the positive and negative part of~$\tilde{f}^\star$.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.883624+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_901cb1db19f63d5ea81e12759f88c4cfd94055fdc6da3bb8c73e82be440b8171","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2506.11918, 'Central limit theorems for the Euler characteristic in the Random Connection Model for hig'] For $k\leq l$ and pairwise different $x_1,\dots,x_l\in\X$, it holds $\P$-almost surely that
