{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.094644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_905e510ad9ad4d9705a24b4db75e6a65679954615887b464e8e6002c3abf095c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 39 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $\mathfrak{u}\colon [0,1]\times U\to A$ be a $C^{k,\infty}$-time-dependent local section, $k\geq 1$, and assume that the evolution $\mathfrak{F}_\mathfrak{u}^{t,s}$ of the time-dependent complete lifts $\mathcal{X}_{\mathfrak{u}_t}$ is defined on the relevant domains for all $s,t\in[0,1]$.

Then the following assertions hold for the push-forward of sections. 
\begin{enumerate} 
\item For every local section $w$, the time-dependent section $Y_t:=(\mathfrak{F}_\mathfrak{u}^{t,s})_*w$ is for every $x$ for which the flows are defined the unique solution of 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial Y_t}{\partial t}(x) = \LB[Y_t,\mathfrak{u}_t]_A(x), \qquad Y_s(x)=w(x).
\end{align}
\item Let $f\colon [0,1]\times U\to A$ be a $C^{k,\infty}$-time-dependent local section. Define pointwise 
$v_t(y) := \int_0^t \mathfrak{F}^{t,r}_{\mathfrak{u},\varphi_{r,t}(y)} \left( f_r(\varphi_{r,t}(y)) \right)\,dr$ whenever all the terms are defined. For fixed $t,y$, the integral is the Bochner integral\footnote{cf. \cite[Sections 23-25]{Sche97} for the definition and basic properties used in the following.} in the Banach fibre $A_y$. Then $v$ is the unique $C^{k,\infty}$-solution of 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial v_t}{\partial t} - f_t = \LB[v_t,\mathfrak{u}_t]_A, \qquad v_0=0.
\end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.476245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_905f6e9f230fc35ab65fcdcdd75db074a2845ad7da6c50f60a4bfbc66c24b040","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma proj-formula-underived of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\alpha\colon G\to G'$ be a homomorphism of compact Lie groups, and let $X\in\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$, $Y\in\cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$. Then the map
    \begin{equation}
        (\alpha_!\textup{pr}_1, \varepsilon\circ \alpha_!\textup{pr}_2)\colon \alpha_!(X\times \alpha^*Y)\to \alpha_!X\times Y
    \end{equation}
    is an isomorphism in $\cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.325415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9060fc5e54e9f34b06030eefb72d58ae07dc4c1d40fdb03d100f3ddbb0c7c5cd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def-log-gf of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] For $\lambda = (\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_d ) \in (-\infty,\infty]^d$, define 
\begin{equation*}
g_k(\lambda) := \log f_k(e^{\lambda_1} , \dots, e^{\lambda_d} ) \quad \text{and} \quad 
g(\lambda) := (g_1(\lambda),\dots,g_d(\lambda)) .
\end{equation*}
We also let $g^{(n)}$ be the composition of $g$ with itself $n$ times. 
Then, using~(equation eqn-gen-func-n of the paper), we see that the $k$th component of $g^{(n)}$ is given by
\begin{equation}  
g_k^{(n)}(\lambda) 
= \log f_k^{(n)}(e^{\lambda_1},\dots,e^{\lambda_d})
= \log \mathbbm E_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ \prod_{j=1}^d e^{\lambda_j X_j(n)} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:49:15.476396+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9061c459c4a0aaca8301ca3482b054525292ca11c49eb60b1a2cb7b399cc3506","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm G-global-as-slice of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\alpha\colon G\to G'$ be a homomorphism of compact Lie groups and let $B\in\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}$ cofibrant. Then 
    \begin{equation}
        \alpha_!\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_{/B}\to \cat{$\bm{G'}$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}_{/\alpha_!B}
    \end{equation}
    is the left adjoint in a Quillen equivalence (for the usual slice model structures). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.524282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_90669353f598bd1164ee7fbb436b737dd658772726e7d190dac7cff5fc1672dc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop terminal string decomposition of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] We have the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For all triples $(s,K,Q) \in \partial_0 \Omega$, $\phi(s,K)=0$.
\item For all triples $(s,K,Q) \in \partial_1 \Omega$, $|\mathrm{supp}(B-K)|+ 8dM(P)\geq \underbar{area}(\ell)$. 
\end{enumerate}
\begin{remark}
Following Remark (remark:ptl-0-omega of the paper), this proposition says that when we are done exploring, either we realize that there cannot be a surface spanning the loop, and this case contributes zero, or we have seen $\gtrsim \underbar{area}(\ell)$ good or bad plaquettes. Put differently, to reach a terminal string in $\ptl_1 \Omega$ starting from $(\ell, B, \varnothing)$, we must either have taken many deformation steps, or gotten stuck in large bad plaquette clusters. We will pay an exponential cost in the combined total number, which by point (2), is at least given by $\underbar{area}(\ell)$. Thus, the cost will at least be exponential in $\underbar{area}(\ell)$. This is ultimately the reason why we can stop exploring once we have reached the boundary $\ptl \Omega$.
\end{remark}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.099426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_90672790ccdbeaeb68f8a492c8dd7bb48fcf9c41fec810f12b7193d1035f28dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Zconstruction of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Let $\mathbb{T}$ be a unimodular decorated BGW tree with reproduction law $\pi$ and edge weight law $\omega$. Let $\mathcal{H}$ be a solution to the system given by System~(equation eq:systemzoomh0singular of the paper) with input $\pi$ and $\omega$.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\roman*)]
        \item There exists a random decorated tree $(\mathbb{T}',((i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v))_{(u,v) \in \overset{\rightarrow}{E}})$ such that the marginal law of each $(i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v)$ is $\zeta'_{\mathcal{H}}$, the marginal law of $\mathbb{T}'$ is the law of $\mathbb{T}$ and such that for any $(u,v) \in \overset{\rightarrow}{E}$:
        \begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        (i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v)=\maxlex\left( (0,0) , \maxlex_{\substack{ u' \sim v \\ u' \neq u}}\left( (k,w(v,u'))-(i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(v,u') \right) \right).
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
        \item The rule
        \begin{align}
            (u,v) \in \mathbb{M}_{\mathcal{H}} \Leftrightarrow (i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v)+(i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(v,u)\overset{\lex}{<} (k,w(u,v)) 
        \end{align}
        defines a matching on $\mathbb{T}$. This rule is equivalent to the following vertex rule:
        $u$ is matched if and only if there exists $v\sim u$ such that
\begin{equation}
        \vphantom{\underset{v' \sim u}{\overset{\lex}{\argmax}}}
        (k,w(u,v))-(i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v) \overset{\lex}{>} (0,0),
\end{equation}
in which case $u$ is matched to the vertex $v$ defined by
\begin{equation}
        v=\underset{v' \sim u}{\overset{\lex}{\argmax}} \left( (k,w(u,v'))-(i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})(u,v') \right).
\end{equation}

        \item The random decorated tree $(\mathbb{T}', (i_{\mathcal{H}},Z_{\mathcal{H}})_{(u,v) \in \overset{\rightarrow}{E}})$ viewed as $\mathbb{T}'$ with an additional decoration is unimodular.
        \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.313615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9067f47d03c9603fbf2bbf0e686eb4ed86e318d4d7084499f8bcccf8fe373b7e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:1 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] There exist constants $0<C_2(k)<1$ and $R_0(k)>0$ such that for all $R>R_0$ and $x,x_i\in\mathbb{R}^3$,
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P}_{\vec{x}}\times\mathbf{P}_{x}\Big(W[0,\tau_{\partial B(x,R)}]\subseteq \sausage{\mathcal{W}}{2}\Big)\leq C_2^{\sqrt R}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.612529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_906ab44b1dd2b041cc265e100c78aad7cefa3189fe421a149a40f6f283b38820","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[1b 5 of arXiv:2506.09264, 'The bunkbed conjecture still holds for cactus graphs\\ and for graphs with certain biconne'] Let \(G\) and \(H\) be two graphs such that \(\vert V(G) \cap V(H)\vert = 1\). Consider two fixed percolation vectors \(\vec{p} \in [0,1]^{E(G)}\) and \(\vec{q} \in [0,1]^{E(H)}\) on \(G\) and \(H\), respectively.\\
Then, the statement that
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(G\cup H)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(G\cup H)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(G\cup H, T, (\vec{p},\vec{q}), v, w)\) holds\end{center}
is true if and only if both
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(G, T, \vec{p}, v, w)\) holds\end{center}
and
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(H, T, \vec{q}, v, w)\) holds\end{center}
are true. In particular, \(G\cup H\) satisfies the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture if and only if both \(G\) and \(H\) satisfy the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture.\vspace{6pt} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.103288+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_906c3905e36729f8aefac2abc173becf27266b684c844220f294be2d99fad3b2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 39 of arXiv:2609.03821, 'Condensation and Metastability in the Supercritical Two-Species Zero-Range Process via Res'] For fixed $k\ge1$ and $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}$, as
$N\to\infty$,
\[
\sum_{\substack{p_{1},\dots,p_{k}\ge1:\\
p_{1}+\cdots+p_{k}=N
}
}\frac{1}{p^{\alpha}_{1}p^{\alpha}_{2}\cdots p^{\alpha}_{k}}\simeq\begin{cases}
k\bm{\zeta}(\alpha)^{k-1}N^{-\alpha} & \text{if}\quad\alpha>1,\\
\frac{k(\log N)^{k-1}}{N} & \text{if}\quad\alpha=1,\\
\frac{\bm{\Gamma}(1-\alpha)^{k}}{\bm{\Gamma}(k(1-\alpha))}N^{k(1-\alpha)-1} & \text{if}\quad\alpha<1.
\end{cases}
\]
Above, $\bm{\Gamma}(\cdot)$ and $\bm{\zeta}(\cdot)$ denote the usual
gamma and zeta functions, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.473456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_906dcbf77b3b5c9cfeed5821c4309f56f07822b6b2afd000c2814793085ddb39","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:FKG of arXiv:2505.02685, 'On the Spectral Expansion of Monotone Subsets of the Hypercube'] Let $(X,Y)$ be a pair of real-valued random variables with bounded second moment. Suppose there is a constant $c\in[0,1)$ such that for all $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$,
\begin{equation}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}} \left[ X\geq a, Y\geq b \right]\geq c\cdot\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}} \left[ X\geq a \right]\cdot\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}} \left[ Y\geq b \right],
\end{equation}
then we must have
\begin{equation}
\Cov{X}{Y}\geq -\sqrt{(1-c)\cdot\mathrm{Var} \left[ X \right]\cdot\mathrm{Var} \left[ Y \right]}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.043026+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9074cf8717963f5edcdc7769d7aba8eba0b51a74b4a0ed83223d20a6196ec33d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2606.31936, 'Coupling and Maximal Inequalities for Graph-Dependent Empirical Processes'] Let \(G_n=(N_n,V_n)\) be a uniform doubling with polynomial growth graph of dimension $d$. Then, for every \(q\ge 1\), there exists
 	a partition \(\mathcal P_n(q)=\{B_1,\ldots,B_M\}\) of \(N_n\) such that
 	\begin{align}
 		\max_{1\le m\le M}|B_m|
 		\lesssim q,
 	\end{align}
 	and, for every \(r\ge 1\),
 	\begin{align}
 		\chi\big(H_{n,r}(\mathcal P_n(q))\big)
 		\lesssim
 		1+\frac{r^d}{q}.
 	\end{align}
 Also,
 	\begin{align}
 	\bar N_{B_m}(r)
 	: =
 	\frac{|S_{B_m}(r)|}{|B_m|}
 	\lesssim
 	\min\{r^{d-1},q\}.
 \end{align}
Moreover,
\begin{align}
	\operatorname{diam}(B_m)
	:=
	\max_{i,j\in B_m} d_n(i,j)
	\le 2 q^{1/d},
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.767317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9078e31ec731a4262ee0ffc26989c92395afb94e9dc92feea937c339e8d4abcc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \weyl_k$, $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and two continuous functions $f: [a,b] \rightarrow (-\infty, \infty]$ and $g: [a,b] \rightarrow [-\infty,\infty)$. This means that either $f \in C([a,b])$ or $f \equiv \infty$, and similarly for $g$. In addition, we assume that $f(t) > g(t)$ for $t \in[a,b]$, $f(a) > x_1$, $f(b) > y_1$, $g(a) < x_k$, $g(b) < y_k$. With this data we let $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ denote the law of $k$ independent Brownian bridges $\{B_i\}_{i =1}^k$ with law $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{free}}^{a,b,\vec{x}, \vec{y}}$, conditioned on the event 
\begin{equation}
E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}} = \{f(t) > B_1(t) > B_2(t) > \cdots > B_k(t) > g(t) \mbox{ for all } t\in [a,b]\}.
\end{equation}
As explained in \cite[Definition 2.7]{DEA21}, we have that $E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}}$ is measurable and $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{free}}^{a,b,\vec{x}, \vec{y}}(E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}}) > 0$, so that the law $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ on $C(\llbracket 1, k \rrbracket \times [a,b])$ is well-defined. The expectation with respect to $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ is denoted by $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$. When $f = \infty$ and $g = -\infty$, we drop them from the notation and simply write $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}}$ and $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.413557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_907a831c22ca32d7293b07628c3aaf51b56599b8b789a4a6e309b153ef8ed3c1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] For $p\in (1,\infty)$, the immersed curve $\gamma \in W^{2,p}(0,1;\R^n)$ is called a \textit{$p$-elastica} if there exists $\lambda \in \R$ such that 
  \begin{equation*}
    \frac{d}{d\varepsilon} \left( \mathcal{B}_p[\gamma+\varepsilon \eta] + \lambda \mathcal{L}[\gamma + \varepsilon \eta] \right) \bigg|_{\varepsilon=0} = 0 \qquad \forall \eta \in C_c^\infty(0,1;\R^n).
  \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.818257+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_907d1c388e18d6843fc707a4eea156c37e293f710e8b3ac8efd1a08d29094832","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 26 of arXiv:2608.30570, '2608.30570'] Assume $U\subset \Sigma$ is a smooth domain in the surface $\Sigma$ with compact closure and $f$ is a smooth function on $\Sigma$.
  We call $s$ a {\bf relative regular value of $f$ with respect to $U$} if $s$ is a regular value of  $f|_{\overline{U}}$. We call $s$ a {\bf relative singular value} if $s$ is not a relative regular value.
 For any subset $A\subset U$ and  $x\in A$, we define
 \begin{align*}
 \Gamma_{f,A,U}(x):=\text{ the connected component of } f^{-1}(f(x))\cap U \text{ passing through } x.
 \end{align*}
 Sometimes, we simply write $\Gamma_{f,U}(x):=\Gamma_{f,A,U}(x)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.245332+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_907daf0fa25ebf5da9239b3999e29c1898b3a6b7157bc33b5d9df1154ef007c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo eq:coupling-intro-mass of arXiv:2607.01877, 'Coupling some conditioned L\'evy trees with the Kesten tree'] We assume the infinite variation setting and the Grey condition, as well as the existence of continuous density on $(0, +\infty)$, say $f_\sigma$, of the total mass under the excursion measure ${\mathbb N}^psi$ such that $r\mapsto r f_\sigma(r)$ is non-increasing. 
The critical Lévy tree ${\mathcal T}$ conditioned under the excursion measure to have total mass $r$, say ${\mathcal T}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r$, 
is distributed as ${\mathcal K}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r$:
\begin{equation}

{\mathcal T}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r
   \, \overset{\text{(d)}}{=} \,
   {\mathcal K}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r, 
\end{equation}
and 
 the sequence $({\mathcal T}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r)_{r> 0}$  converges  to the Kesten tree ${\mathcal K}$ in $({\mathbb T}, {d_\mathrm {LGH}})$, and we have the following  local   convergence in distribution:
 \begin{equation}

      {\mathcal T}^{\mathbf{\mathrm{mass}}}_r  \xrightarrow[r \rightarrow
   +\infty]{\text{(d)}}
      {\mathcal K}
     \quad\text{in}\quad
  ({\mathbb T}, {d_\mathrm {LGH}}).
