{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.331764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91a55a1d043f39ef58b450cebf14f7314413da5deeeed419196c4ddcfe173972","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Notdef def:compo of arXiv:2412.10001, 'On the Markov transformation of Gaussian processes'] Let $E_1, \dots, E_d$ be Polish spaces, $(\mu_i)_{i \in \ent{1}{d}} \in \prod_{i=1}^d \mathcal{P}(E_i)$, and $(P_i)_{i \in \ent{1}{d-1}} \in \prod_{i = 1}^{d-1} \textnormal{Marg}(\mu_i,\mu_{i+1})$. The concatenation of $P_1, \dots, P_{d-1}$ is the probability measure $P_1 \circ \dots \circ P_{d-1} \in \mathcal{P}(E_1 \times \cdots \times E_d)$ defined by \[ (P_1 \circ \dots \circ P_{d-1})(A_1 \times \dots \times A_d) = \int_{A_1 \times \dots \times A_d} \mu_1(dx_1)k^{1,2}(x_1,dx_2)\dots k^{d-1,d}(x_{d-1},dx_d), \]
		\begin{comment}
			 (P_1 \circ \dots \circ P_{d-1})(A_1 \times \dots \times A_d) = \int_{A_1} \left( \int_{A_2} \dots \int_{A_d} k^{d-1,d}(x_{d-1},x_d)\cdots k^{1,2}(x_1,dx_2) \right) \mu_1(dx_1), $$
		\end{comment}
		where $k^{i,i+1}$ is the probability kernel defined by the disintegration $P_i(dx_i,dx_{i+1}) = \mu_i(dx_i)k^{i,i+1}(x_i,dx_{i+1})$. Defining $k^{2,1}$ as the kernel given by the disintegration   $P_{1}(dx_1,dx_2) = \mu_2(dx_2)k^{2,1}(x_2,dx_1)$, we leave it to the reader to verify that $(P_1\circ P_{2})(dx_1,dx_2,dx_3) = \mu_2 (dx_2) [k^{2,1}(x_2, \cdot) \otimes k^{2,3}(x_2, \cdot)](dx_1,dx_3)$. This means that, conditionally to the present, the future is independent of the past. For $T \subset \mathbb{R}$ and $(E_t)_{t \in T}$ a family of Polish spaces, we say that a probability $P \in \mathcal{P}( \prod_{t \in T} E_{t})$  is a Markov measure if for all subset  $\{t_1< \dots <t_m\} \subset T$, $P^{t_1,\dots,t_m} = P^{t_1,t_2} \circ \cdots \circ P^{t_{m-1},t_m}.$ Of course the notion of Markov  measure is related to the more usual notion of Markov process: we leave it to the reader to verify that a process is Markov (relatively to its canonical filtration) if and only if its law is a Markov measure. The composition $P_1  \cdot \ \dots \ \cdot  P_d \in \mathcal{P}(E_1 \times  E_d)$ of $P_1, \dots, P_d$ is now defined by $ P_1 \cdot \ \dots \ \cdot P_d  := \textnormal{proj}^{1,d}_\# (P_1 \circ \dots \circ P_d).$
		For $s<t \in \mathbb{R}^2$, we write $\mathcal{S}_{[s,t]}$ the set of partitions of $[s,t]$, \textnormal{i.e.} , \ the sequences $(t_1 < \dots < t_m) \in \mathbb{R}^m$ with $t_1 = s$ and $t_m = t.$ If $R = (t_1 < \dots < t_m) \in \mathcal{S}_{[s,t]}$, we set $P_{\{R\}}^{s,t} := P^{t_1,t_2} \cdot \ \dots \ \cdot P^{t_{m-1},t_m}$ and denote by $\sigma_R := \sup_{i \in \ent{1}{d-1}} |t_{i+1} - t_i|$ the mesh of $R$.","status":"unverified"}

