{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.999654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91cb0aa9209878cd2b23bbfc36bfcd5381048f057afda36dda6732a76f08c930","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 20 of arXiv:2512.02661, 'Worst-case mixing estimates for Brownian motion with semipermeable barriers'] There exist absolute constants $c_1,c_2>0$ such that the following holds.
	Denote $R \de c_1\min\{1/\kappa, 1/\lambda_{\max}, \rho \}$ and $C\de (R \lambda_{\min})^{\Delta/R}/\Area(D)$.
	Then, for every $T\geq c_2 \Delta^2$, initial condition $x_0\in D$, and measurable $E\subseteq D$,
	\begin{align}
		\bbP(X_{T}\in E \mid X_0 = x_0) \geq C \Area(E).
