{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.115437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_92cd26ecaaee20ed9d7ef881f4274e532b428464e4b844c2bb5239662405ff2d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 5.8 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Let $X$ be a Borel right process on $(E,d)$ that satisfies (AC) and the following short-time heat kernel lower bound at a fixed point $x\in E$: For some $T>0, \alpha>0$ and $\beta>1$, there exist constants $C_1,C_2>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
p(t,x,y)\ge \frac{C_1}{t^{\alpha/\beta}}\exp\left\{-C_2\left(\frac{d(x,y)}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta -1)}\right\}, \quad \forall t\in (0, T], \, y\in E.
\end{equation}
Let $B\in \mathscr{B}^n(E)$ be a nearly Borel set.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(1)] If $X$ satsifies (H3) and $p^{(\lambda)}_B\in {\mathcal{F}}$ for some $\lambda>0$, then for any $\delta,\rho>0$,
 \[
        \mathbb{P}^x[\sigma_B\leq t]\geq \frac{\nu^{(\lambda)}_{B_{x,\delta}}(\overline{B}_{x,\delta})}{\mathrm{e}^{\lambda T}}\cdot \frac{C_1 C_3}{ t^{(\alpha-\beta)/\beta}}\exp\left\{-(1+\rho)C_2\left( \frac{\widetilde{d}(x,B)+\delta}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}\right\},\quad 0<t\leq T,
    \]
    where  $B_{x,\delta}:=\{y\in B:d(y,x)\leq \widetilde{d}(x,B)+\delta\}$, $\nu^{(\lambda)}_{B_{x,\delta}}$ is the measure obtained in Corollary~(COR55 of the paper), $C_1$ and $C_2$ are as in (equation eq:511-2 of the paper), and $C_3=(\beta-1)\rho\cdot \min\{(1+\rho)^{\alpha-\beta-\alpha/\beta}, 1\}>0$ is a constant depending on $\alpha, \beta,\rho$.
\item[(2)] If $X$ is transient and satisfies (H3'), and $p_B\in {\mathcal{F}}_\mathrm{e}$, then the same conclusion as in the first statement holds with $\lambda=0$ and $\nu^{(0)}_{B_{x,\delta}}=\nu_{B_{x,\delta}}$, where $\nu_{B_{x,\delta}}$ is the measure obtained in Lemma~(LM53 of the paper). 
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.029420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_92cd58056a17a40c3026b0e84be0b6beecbb7e8512c23d130187972aeb2f02fa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:nondeg-additive of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Let $X$ be the variational solution of~(equation eq:SPDE of the paper) under the standing hypotheses. Suppose there exists a bounded linear operator $R: H \to H$ (deterministic, possibly with non-closed range) and a positive random variable $c_t: \Omega \to (0, \infty]$ such that the \emph{intrinsic observability inequality}
\begin{equation}
  \langle \gamma_t(\omega)\phi,\phi\rangle_H \;\geq\; c_t(\omega)\,\lVert R\phi \rVert_H^2 \qquad \text{for every } \phi \in H, \;\; \mathbb{P}\text{-almost surely},
\end{equation}
holds, with $c_t^{-1} \in L^q(\Omega)$ for some $q \in [2, \infty]$. Two conclusions follow, according to whether a further hypothesis is imposed on $R$.

\medskip\noindent The first gives a moment bound in the whole of $H$. Suppose, in addition to~(equation eq:intrinsic-observability of the paper), that $R$ is bounded below on $\overline{\operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t)}$ a.s., in the sense that there exists a deterministic constant $C_R \in [1, \infty)$ such that
\begin{equation}
  \lVert \psi \rVert_H \;\leq\; C_R\,\lVert R\psi \rVert_H \qquad \text{for every } \psi \in \overline{\operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t)}, \;\; \mathbb{P}\text{-almost surely}.
\end{equation}
(Equivalently, $R$ restricted to $\overline{\operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t)}$ has closed range with bounded inverse on its range.) Then for every $h \in R^{*}(H)$ (with $h = R^{*}\tilde h$, $\tilde h \in H$):
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(A\arabic*)}, leftmargin=3em]
  \item \textup{(Range condition)} $h \in \operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t)$ a.s.\ (clause~(i) of Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper));
  \item \textup{(Full moment bound)} $\norm{\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H \leq C_R\,c_t^{-1}\,\lVert \tilde h \rVert_H$ a.s., hence
    \[
      \mathbb{E}[\norm{\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H^q] \leq C_R^q\,\lVert \tilde h \rVert_H^q\,\mathbb{E}[c_t^{-q}] < \infty
    \]
    (clause~(ii) of Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper) at exponent $q$).
\end{enumerate}

\medskip\noindent The second requires no hypothesis on $R$, but bounds only the projection. Without the bounded-below hypothesis~(equation eq:R-bounded-below of the paper), and assuming now in addition that $h = R^{*}\tilde h \in R^{*}(H)$ satisfies the range condition $h \in \operatorname{Ran}(\gamma_t)$ almost surely (so that $\gamma_t^{\dagger}h \in H$ is well-defined; without~(equation eq:R-bounded-below of the paper), the range condition is no longer delivered by the observability alone, cf.\ the Tier~A derivation of~(A1)), the observability~(equation eq:intrinsic-observability of the paper) yields only the projected bound
\begin{equation}
  \norm{R\,\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H \;\leq\; c_t^{-1}\,\lVert \tilde h \rVert_H \quad \text{a.s.,} \qquad \mathbb{E}\bigl[\norm{R\,\gamma_t^{\dagger}h}_H^q\bigr] \leq \lVert \tilde h \rVert_H^q\,\mathbb{E}[c_t^{-q}] < \infty,
\end{equation}
which controls the projected pseudoinverse $R\,\gamma_t^{\dagger}h$ rather than $\gamma_t^{\dagger}h$ itself. Tier~B does not yield clause~(ii) of Assumption~(ass:nondeg of the paper) at the full Hilbert-space level, and consequently does not license the application of Theorem~(thm:main of the paper) for the direction $h$; nor does it identify the pseudoinverse of a projected covariance, since in general $R\gamma_t^{\dagger}R^{*} \neq (R\gamma_t R^{*})^{\dagger}$. A projected Bismut formula is instead obtained by applying Theorem~(thm:abstract-bismut-fomin of the paper) directly to the pushforward variable $RX(t)$, whose Malliavin covariance is $R\gamma_t R^{*}$, provided the corresponding range and inverse-moment hypotheses hold for that covariance; this is the route used for Navier--Stokes below (cf.\ Remark~(rem:observability-additive of the paper) for the connection to Mattingly--Pardoux/Hairer--Mattingly).

\medskip\noindent The admissible-direction subspace identified by Tier~A is $\mathcal{N}_R := R^{*}(H)$, intrinsically determined by the observability operator $R$. The Cameron--Martin compatibility clause~\textup{(iii)} of Assumption~(a (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

