{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.284283+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_92db01b051dddd4b99d7b6e7004f68fc9f5e3e9ad5509b6752e9010c612bb552","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Retrieving concepts) of arXiv:2604.25470, 'A mathematical analysis of hierarchical Hopfield models'] In the above setting, assume that there exists an integer $t\in\{0,\dots,L-1\}$ such that the \emph{margin condition}
	\begin{equation}

		t+\rho < (1-\delta)L
	\end{equation}
	holds. 
	Then, for every fixed $\alpha$,
	\begin{equation}

		\mathbb P\bigl(\widehat\alpha(x)=\alpha\bigr)
		\ \ge\
		1
		-\mathbb P\bigl(t_*^{(\alpha)}\ge t+1\bigr)
		-\mathbb P\bigl(\mathcal G_\alpha(\delta,\rho)^c\bigr).
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, in the i.i.d.\ concept model one has the explicit bound
	\begin{equation}

		\mathbb P\bigl(t_*^{(\alpha)}\ge t+1\bigr)
		\ \le\
		P\,\binom{L}{t+1}\Bigl(\frac{L}{M}\Bigr)^{t+1}.
	\end{equation}
	In particular, if $P=P(N_f)$ and $M=M(N_f)$ are such that
	\begin{equation}

		P\,\binom{L}{t+1}\Bigl(\frac{L}{M}\Bigr)^{t+1} \xrightarrow[N_f\to\infty]{} 0
