{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615692+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_937fb4044b4d7d371cf7efdc80964c77b611f295b648e8d1fb3d923f8154f64b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] There exists $c_\star\in(0,\infty)$ such that the quantity $\epsilon_{n,t,u}$ defined for integer $n\ge1$ and real $t>0$ and~$u>0$ by
\begin{equation}

P^\varrho \Bigl(\,\max_{x\in\BbbL_n} \sqrt{L_t(x)} - \sqrt{t} - a_n(t) > u \Bigr)
= c_\star \bigl(1+\epsilon_{n,t,u}\bigr) u\texte^{-2u\slb} 
\end{equation}
obeys
\begin{equation}

\lim_{m\to\infty}\sup_{t,u\ge m} \limsup_{n\to\infty}\,\bigl|\,\epsilon_{n,t,u}\bigr|=0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

