{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.776317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a0f09677e9dd684a6407327e21c122e7e5e58e89d0ec8415dc518a9fb08228","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2603.25452, 'New approach to optimal control of delayed stochastic Volterra integral equations'] Let $\hat{u} \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{G}}$ and denote by $\hat{X}(t)$ the corresponding state process. Let $\big(\hat{p}(t), \hat{q}(t,s)\big)$ be the associated adjoint processes, which are solutions of equations \eqref{p}.
Assume that the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] The function $x \mapsto g(x)$ and the Hamiltonian $(x, u) \mapsto \mathcal{H}(t, x, u, \hat{p}(t), \hat{q}(.,t))$ are concave for each $t \in [0,T]$, almost surely.
\item[(ii)] Assumption $({\bf A3})$ holds for all $u \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{G}}$.
\item[(iii)] (Maximum condition) For all $t \in [0,T]$, it holds that
\begin{eqnarray}
	\mathbb{E} \left[ \mathcal{H}(t, \hat{X}(t), \hat{u}(t), \hat{p}(t), \hat{q}(\cdot,t))|\mathcal{G}_t\right]
= \max_{v \in U} \mathbb{E} \left[\mathcal{H}(t, \hat{X}(t), v(t), \hat{p}(t), \hat{q}(\cdot,t)) \,\big|\mathcal{G}_t\right].
\end{eqnarray}
\end{itemize}
Then, the control $\hat{u}$ is optimal for the stochastic control problem \eqref{CP}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.634966+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a38978899b1f4a0b9f876768af3304705c396d32f5a5336914e9bc44c453b3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 95 (Exterior degree is not primitive ancestry) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] Let $V$ be a complex vector space with $\dim V\ge j$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Hom}_{GL(V)}(\Lambda^jV,L_j(V))=0,
 \qquad j\ge3.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, in stable dimension there is no nonzero homogeneous linear
natural transformation from the degree-$j$ fermionic sector to the
degree-$j$ free-Lie sector.  The coincidence $L_2(V)=\Lambda^2V$ is therefore
exceptional: exterior particle number is a covariance-reader degree, not a
second model of the Rees ancestry filtration.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.906617+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a6924db3876591eb4a37df9858d67ec6fa620691693c9f512d74cbfb9d5537","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ergodic via independece of core of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},V,T)$ be a capacity preserving system, where $V$ is an upper probability. Then the following statements are equivalent:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item $V$ is ergodic;
		\medskip
		\item there exists $Q\in\operatorname{core}(V)\cap \mathcal{ M}^e(T)$ such that for any  $P\in\operatorname{core}(V)$, 
		\[\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P(B)Q(C)\text{ for any }B,C\in\mathcal{F};\]
		\medskip
		\item there exists $Q\in\operatorname{core}(V)\cap \mathcal{ M}(T)$ such that for any  $P\in\operatorname{core}(V)$, 
		\begin{equation}
			\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P(B)Q(C)\text{ for any }B,C\in\mathcal{F}.
		\end{equation}
	\end{longlist}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:32.000229+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a771f820c42de003fa0219a3871140500865ba9b5e855a054e398accc897d4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 6 of arXiv:2512.02766, 'Fine-Graining and Continuous Space Scaling Limit of the $H^{2|2}$ Model on the Hierarchica'] Consider the $H^{2|2}$-model on a finite graph $\mathcal{G}=(V,E,W)$, and let $U\subset V$ be indistinguishable from outside. Define the random $\beta'$-field on the reduced graph $V'=(V\setminus U)\cup\{u\}$ with the coarse-graining procedure defined in Section~\ref{subse:coarse_graining_procedure}, and let $\mathcal{F}'$ be the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $(\beta'_k)_{k\in V'}$. Let $\eta\in\mathbb{R}^{V}_{+}$ be a vector such that $\eta_i=\eta_j$ for all $i,j\in U$. Then
\begin{equation*}
    \forall \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^{V}_+,\quad \mathbb{E}\left[e^{-\langle\lambda,G\eta\rangle-\frac{1}{2}\langle\lambda,G\lambda\rangle}~|~\mathcal{F}'\right]=e^{-\langle\widehat{\lambda},G'\widehat{\eta}\rangle-\frac{1}{2}\langle\widehat{\lambda},G'\widehat{\lambda}\rangle}
\end{equation*}
where we set $\widehat{\lambda}_{i}=\lambda_i, \widehat{\eta}_i=\eta_i$ if $i\ne u$, and $\widehat{\lambda}_u=\frac{1}{\sqrt{|U|}}\sum\limits_{k\in U}\lambda_{k}$, $\widehat{\eta}_u=\frac{1}{\sqrt{|U|}}\sum\limits_{k\in U}\eta_{k}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.757956+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a9313c3002b262462685813f6df60b2a00e5967022a4331ac69389d212b7f8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defn:mild-soln of arXiv:2411.02459, 'Existence and higher regularity of statistically steady states for the stochastic Coleman-'] Given initial condition $(u_0,\eta_0)\in \mathcal{H}^0_\mu$, a process $U(\cdot)=\big(u(\cdot),\eta(\cdot)\big)$ is called a weak solution of (equation eqn:react-diff:mu of the paper) if $u(\cdotp)$ is $\mathcal{F}_t$-adapted and there exists $q\geq1$ such that $\mathbb{P}$--a.s. one has
\begin{align*}
u\in C_{w}([0,\infty);H)\cap L^2_{\emph{loc}}([0,\infty);H^1), \quad \eta\in C({[0,\infty)};M^0_\mu),
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\varphi(u)\in L^q_{\emph{loc}}([0,\infty);L^q(\mathcal{O})),
\end{align*}
{where $C_{w}([0,\infty);H)$ denotes the space of functions that are weakly continuous in $H$ with respect to $t$. Moreover,} for $\mathbb{P}$--a.s.
\begin{align*}
\langle u(t),v\rangle_H&=\langle u_0,v\rangle_H  -\kappa\int_0^t\langle u(r),v\rangle_{H^1} \emph{d} r-(1-\kappa)\int_0^t\langle \eta(r),v\rangle_{M^0_\mu}\\
&\quad+\int_0^t\langle \varphi(u(r)),v\rangle_H \emph{d} r+\int_0^t\langle v(r),Q\emph{d} w(r)\rangle_H,\\
\langle \eta(t),\tilde{\eta}\rangle_{M^0_\mu}&=\langle\eta_0,\tilde{\eta}\rangle_{M^0_\mu}+  \int_0^t\langle \mathcal{T}_\mu\eta(r),\tilde{\eta}\rangle_{M^0_\mu} \emph{d}r+\int_0^t\langle u(r),\tilde{\eta}\rangle_{M^0_\mu}\emph{d} r,
\end{align*}
{holds for all $v\in H^1\cap L^{q'}(\mathcal{O})$, where $q'\geq1$ is the H\"older conjugate of $q$, and for all $\tilde{\eta}\in M^0_\mu$.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.256985+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93a9c39d55316f58c4668bd2d328d62b046459dba488522e37f28350a9605bc5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma avldptx of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp Fix any {\small$\mathrm{D}=\mathrm{O}(1)$} and {\small$\delta>0$}, both independent of {\small$N$}. With high probability, we have the following for any admissible {\small$\mathfrak{q}_{\n}$} in the sense of Definition (definition:admissible of the paper):

