{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.898493+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93b76df8c97d1816ab8ffa403d25205133d47bbd857481ceb10117b369e2389c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 (Central limit theorem for fixed filling fractions) of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] For the ensemble \eqref{eq_Hahn_cut_2} with fixed filling fractions and under the off-criticality assumption, there exist holomorphic functions $W_{1}^{[1]}(z)$ and $W_1^{[2]}(z)$ of $z \in \amsmathbb{C} \setminus \amsmathbb{A}$ such that, as $\N\rightarrow\infty$ uniformly over $z$ away from $\amsmathbb{A}$
\begin{equation}
   \E\big[G(z)\big] = \N \mathcal{G}(z)  + W_1^{[1]}(z) + \frac{W_1^{[2]}(z)}{\N} + o\bigg(\frac{1}{\N}\bigg).
\end{equation}
Moreover, there exists a holomorphic function $\mathcal{F}(z_1,z_2)$ of $(z_1,z_2) \in (\amsmathbb{C} \setminus \amsmathbb{A})^2$ such that for any $n \geq 1$, as $\N \rightarrow \infty$ uniformly for $z_1,\ldots,z_n$ away from $\amsmathbb{A}$
\begin{equation}
\E\bigg[ \prod_{i=1}^n \big(G(z_i)-\E[G(z_i)]\big)\bigg]= \E\bigg[ \prod_{i = 1}^{n} \textnormal{\textsf{Gau\ss{}}}(z_i) \bigg] +o(1),
\end{equation}
 where $\big(\textnormal{\textsf{Gau\ss{}}}(z_i)\big)_{i = 1}^{n}$ is a centered random Gaussian vector with covariance matrix $(\mathcal{F}(z_i,z_j))_{i,j = 1}^{n}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.297404+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93b8ff22b202a0421662679fb75d2ff470b2907c67a8dcf768284f48f16e6a11","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] For any $ t_{0} \in (0,T) $,
    there exists a positive constant $ \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{C}(t_{0})$ such that for  $ \gamma = \gamma(t_{0},T) \in (0,1)$ as stated in (thmDou2022 of the paper), for  all $ \delta \in (0,1) $ and $ \tau = \delta^{2} $,
    putting $ \bar{y}_{0}^{\delta} $  as the minimizer of functional  $\mathcal{J}_\tau(\cdot) $, then
    we have
    \begin{align*}
        &|y(t_{0}; \bar{y}_{0}^{\delta})  -  y(t_{0}; y^{*}_{0})|^{2}_{L_{\mathcal{F}_{t_{0}}}^2\left(\Omega ;   H ^1(G)\right)}
        \leq
        \mathcal{C}  M  \operatorname{exp}  [-  3^{-\gamma} \ln^{\gamma} (M^{-1/2} \delta^{-1}) ]
        ,
    \end{align*}
    where $ M = 1 + |y_{0}^{*}|_{\mathcal{H}_{0}}^{2}  $ and  $y(\cdot; \bar{y}_{0}^{\delta})$ (resp. $  y(\cdot; y^{*}_{0})$) is the solution to (eqDou1 of the paper) with respect to the initial datum $\bar{y}_{0}^{\delta}$  (resp. $   y^{*}_{0}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.970948+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93bb84e4ff8770d1f82e94edbf7bf423056165f19d515036926495bb73b98001","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 32 of arXiv:2606.13459, 'Symmetric Cooperative Motion in Higher Dimensions'] For $T > 0$, we say that a measurable function $u: Q_{T}\to \R$ is a \emph{nonnegative entropy solution} to the equation $u_{t} - \frac{1}{2d}\Delta(u^{m+1}) = 0$ in $Q_{T}$ if the following holds:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $u \in  L^{1}(Q_{T}) \cap L^{\infty}(Q_{T}) \cap C((0,T);L^{1}(\R^{d}))$.
		\item $u \geq 0$ a.e.\@ in $Q_{T}$.
		\item For all $c \in \R$ and all non-negative $\vp \in C^{\infty}_{c}(\R^{d} \times [0,T])$,
		\begin{equation} 
			\int_{\R^{d}}\int_{0}^{T}\left(|u-c|\vp_{t} +\left|u^{m+1}-c^{m+1}\right|\frac{1}{2d}\Delta \vp\right)\,dt\,dx \geq 0.
		\end{equation}
		\item $u^{m+1} \in L^{2}((0,T) ;H^{1}(\R^{d})),$ where $H^{1}(\R^{d})$ is the Sobolev space $W^{1,2}(\R^{d})$.
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:43:45.397935+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93bbb5ac87604a9b8b92fcfeb44c998de19561d413179534d60635c043431897","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2609.04143, 'The Geometry of Real Anisotropic Bohnenblust--Hille Constants'] For every $1\le p\le2$,
\begin{equation}
 A_p^{-1}\le \exp\left(\frac{1}p-\frac{1}{2}\right).
\end{equation}
In particular, for $p_k$ as in \eqref{eq:intro-pk-definition},
\begin{equation}
 A_{p_k}^{-1}\le e^{1/(2k)}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $k\ge2$,
\begin{equation}
 A_{p_k}^{-1}\le 2^{1/(k+2)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.957566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93c250e7229cadbe8685473e4dc889b560e9fc9b89da15809b84d3302fa596fa","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 (Weak cumulant generating function and weak cumulants) of arXiv:2604.20634, 'Distributional Statistical Models:\\ \large Weak Moments, Cumulants, and a Central Limit T'] Assume ${}^{(\varphi)}\phi(t)\neq 0$ in a neighbourhood of $t=0$.  The \emph{weak cumulant generating function} is ${}^{(\varphi)}K(t):=\log {}^{(\varphi)}\phi(t)$, and the $n$-th \emph{weak cumulant} is
\[
{}^{(\varphi)}\kappa_n
\;:=\;
\left.\frac{d^n}{dt^n}{}^{(\varphi)}K(t)\right|_{t=0}.
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.559720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93c354a8bab3ec5deb33a1c0a1ae0b6cbab01c44aa3457a193f92ed3f439df46","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] For every $n\in\mathbb N$, $\lambda>0$, and $\theta\in(0,1/2)$, the functions
    \begin{align}
        F_1(z)\coloneqq \left(\frac12+\frac{1}{2\bigl(1+\frac{\theta\lambda}{n}(1-z)\bigr)}\right)^n,
        \qquad
        F_2(z)\coloneqq \left(
        \frac{2+\frac{\theta\lambda}{n}(1-z)}
        {2+\frac{2\lambda}{n}(1-z)+\frac{\theta\lambda^2}{n^2}(1-z)^2}
        \right)^n
    \end{align}
    are pgfs satisfying
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi_n(F_1(z),F_2(z))
        =
        \left(1+\frac{\lambda}{n}(1-z)\right)^{-n},
        \qquad
        \Phi_n(x,y)\coloneqq \bigl(x^{-1/n}+y^{-1/n}-1\bigr)^{-n},
    \end{align*}
    but neither factor belongs to the negative binomial family $\left\{\left(1+\frac{\mu}{n}(1-z)\right)^{-n}:\mu>0\right\}.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.576576+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93c474681ce6ecaec42c1aafb47e8122bfb5968087a810543e22cf6536f15541","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem the1ch5 of arXiv:2512.09308, 'On asymptotic behavior of solutions to random fractional Riesz-Bessel equations with cycli'] Consider the random field $u(t,x),$ $t>0,$ $x\in\mathbb R,$ defined by ~{\rm ((modch5 of the paper))} with  $\alpha>1/2$, and the random initial condition {\rm((modCch5 of the paper))}. Let $\xi (x),$ $x\in \mathbb R,$ have a covariance function satisfying Assumption~{\rm(Asumptionmain of the paper)} with $A_{0}=0$. If $\varepsilon\to 0,$ then the finite-dimensional distributions of the random fields
\begin{equation*}
    U_{\varepsilon}(t,x)={\varepsilon^{-\beta/2\alpha}}u\left(\frac{t}{\varepsilon},\frac{x}{\varepsilon^{\beta/\alpha}}\right)
\end{equation*}
converge weakly to the finite-dimensional distributions of the zero-mean Gaussian random field defined by
\begin{equation}ch5}
    U_{0}(t,x)=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_1(\kappa_j)A_{j}K_{\frac{\kappa_j-1}{2}}(|w_j|)|w_j|^{\frac{\kappa_j-1}{2}}}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{i\lambda x} E_{\beta}\left(-\mu t^{\beta}|\lambda|^{\alpha}\right)W(d\lambda),
