Let $\boldsymbol q_{  {\boldsymbol v}}^{(2)}((x_1,x_2),(\dr y_1,\dr y_2))$ denote the Markov kernel defined by a Radon-Nikodym derivative with respect to $\boldsymbol p^{(2)}((x_1,x_2),(\dr y_1,\dr y_2) )$ that is proportional to $e^{\sum_{j=1,2}   {\boldsymbol v} \bullet (x_j-y_j) }.$ 
 Let $\mathbf E_{y}^{(  {\boldsymbol v},\mathrm{diff})}$ denote the expectation with respect to the Markov chain $(X_r)_{r\ge 0}$ with state space $I$, given by the difference of the two coordinates of the Markov chain associated to the kernel $\boldsymbol q_{  {\boldsymbol v}}^{(2)}((x_1,x_2),(\dr y_1,\dr y_2))$. Define $\pi_{  {\boldsymbol v}}^{\mathrm{inv}}(\dr y )$ to be the unique invariant measure for that Markov chain $(X_r)_r$. Then we have that 
\begin{equation}\nu_{\mathrm{eff}}^2(  {\boldsymbol v}):= \frac1{|  {\boldsymbol v}|^{2p}} \int_I \left(e^{\boldsymbol u_{  {\boldsymbol v}}(   y)} -1\right)\mathbf E_y^{(  {\boldsymbol v},\mathrm{diff})} \bigg[e^{\sum_{s=1}^{\infty} \boldsymbol u_{  {\boldsymbol v}}(X_s)} \bigg] \pi_{  {\boldsymbol v}}^{\mathrm{inv}} (\dr y). 



