{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:53.199221+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_951f5fbcb756b05c5c8a1b6397f25f0906f00c63c30426853484a734865d5e7f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 8 of arXiv:2606.11774, 'Martingale Solutions to a Stochastic Keller-Segel System with nonlocal Source and Super-li'] Let $d\ge3,\,\alpha,\,\beta >1, \,\gamma\ge 2$ and $\alpha+1>\beta$. 	Under Assumption \ref{ass}, for $n\in\mathbb N$ and $N>0$, let  $(u_n,{\tau_n^N})$ be a pathwise unique local strong solution to (\ref{equ2}). If \begin{align}
     \max\{3,\gamma,\alpha+1\}<\frac{2}{d+2}(\alpha-1+\beta)+2,
 \end{align}
    then for all $T>0$, there exists a positive  constant $C(T)$ independent of $n$ and $N$  such that
    \begin{align}
\sup_{n\ge1}\mathbb{E}\Big(\sup_{t\in[0,T\wedge{\tau_n^N}]}\|u_n(t)\|_{L^2(\mathcal{O})}^2\Big)\leq C(T),
   \\
\sup_{n\ge1}\mathbb{E}\Big(\int_{0}^{T\wedge{\tau_n^N}}\|\nabla u_n(t)\|_{L^2(\mathcal{O})}^2dt\Big)\leq C(T),
  \\
\sup_{n\ge1}\mathbb{E}\Big(\int_{0}^{T\wedge{\tau_n^N}}\|u_n(t)\|_{L^{\alpha+1}(\mathcal{O})}^{\alpha+1}\|u_n(t)\|_{L^{\beta}(\mathcal{O})}^{\beta}dt\Big)\leq C(T).
    \end{align}
    Moreover,  for any $k\in[1,\frac{2}{d+2}(\alpha-1+\beta)+2)$, 
   there exists a positive  constant $C(T,k)$  independent of $n$ and $N$  such that
 \begin{align}
\sup_{n\ge1}\mathbb{E}\Big(\int_{0}^{T\wedge{\tau_n^N}}\|u_n(t)\|_{L^{k}(\mathcal{O})}^{k}dt\Big)\leq C(T,k).
 \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.851813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_95215d2ca8b3756ffe30e586eb24f8c7a3db68c21fa73657ea52dcb7ab9245d7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.aux2 of arXiv:2607.10105, 'The four-dimensional Anderson model: a case study for critical SPDEs'] For any $\theta>0$ and positive integers $m\leq n$, we have
\begin{equation} \sum_{1\leq z_1,\cdots,z_m\leq n}\prod_{j=1}^{m-1}\min(1,2^{-\theta(z_j-z_{j+1})})\leq C^n,
\end{equation} where $C=C(\theta)>1$ is an absolute constant depending on $\theta$. Similarly, if $(P_1,\cdots,P_n)$ are distinct elements of $2^\mathbb N$, then
\begin{equation}\sum_{1\leq x(1),\cdots x(n)\leq n}\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}\min\bigg(1,\bigg(\frac{P_{x(j+1)}}{P_{x(j)}}\bigg)^{\theta}\bigg)\leq C^n.
\end{equation} 
The same result is true if the product in ((eq.aux6 of the paper)) is taken over $j\in\{1,\cdots,n-1\}\backslash E$ for any set $E$ with at most $100$ elements.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.614395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9522bc3790555b79a743388bbea2dcb5505da1997d94a44c2c6641bc1205c254","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2506.09462, 'Transition Path Theory For L\'{e}vy-Type Processes: SDE Representation and Statistics'] The \textbf{\(AB\)-transition starting point distribution} on \(A^c \cap (\partial A + 1)\) and the \textbf{\(BA\)-transition starting point distribution} on \(B^c \cap (\partial B + 1)\) are defined as follows:
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            \eta_A^{*}(x) &= v_A^{*-1} \int_{\bar{A} \cap (\partial A + 1)} \int_{|r| < 1} \mathbf{1}_{\{\mathrm{dist}(y, A^c) \leq F(y, r)\}} \nu(\d r) \eta_A^-(y)\d y, \quad x \in A^c \cap (\partial A + 1), \\
            \eta_B^{*}(x) &= v_B^{*-1} \int_{\bar{B} \cap (\partial B + 1)} \int_{|r| < 1} \mathbf{1}_{\{\mathrm{dist}(y, B^c) \leq F(y, r)\}} \nu(\d r) \eta_B^-(y)\d y, \quad x \in B^c \cap (\partial B + 1),
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    where \(v_A^*\) and \(v_B^*\) are normalization constants, ensuring that \(\eta_A^*\) and \(\eta_B^*\) define probability measures on \(A^c \cap (\partial A + 1)\) and \(B^c \cap (\partial B + 1)\), respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.584704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9524240a41b47fb228be0f8d7b55caec58f4cd57c221437b11ade03e8bdaeee8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop eq:FE_h_right of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] For $(x,y)\in\mathbb C^2$ such that $\Re x>0$ and $\Re y>0$, we have
\begin{equation}

K^*(x,y)\,\widehat h^\rightarrow(x,y)
=
k_1^*(x,y)\,\widehat h^\rightarrow_1(y)
+
k_2^*(x,y)\,\widehat h^\rightarrow_2(x)
+
c\,h^\rightarrow(0,0),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}

    \left\{
    \begin{array}{rcl}
    K^*(x,y) & = & \frac{1}{2}(x^2+y^2+2\rho xy)-\mu_1x-\mu_2y,\\
    k_1^*(x,y) & = & \frac{1}{2} (x-r_1 y)+\rho y +\mu_1,\\
    k_2^*(x,y) & = & \frac{1}{2} (y-r_2 x)+\rho x +\mu_2,\\
    c & = & \frac{1}{2}(r_1+r_2)-\rho.
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:29:35.082073+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9524a9becd61cf3c67b71123a2a4035d87d48708186efbd8e0c278f1f43c3e1e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2609.03803, 'List-distance consistent vertices in trees are confined to a path'] Let $G=SP(w;1,\ell_2,\dots,\ell_k)$ with $k\ge4$ and $\ell_k\ge2$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textup{(i)}] $\bigl\lceil \ell_k/2\bigr\rceil+1\ \le\ \ldc(G)\ \le\
\bigl\lceil \ell_k/2\bigr\rceil+2$;
