{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:18:19.226601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9570eb52e20771c863b7cae7d0b4300feedd3eae2c0c5020c18d74213bf33148","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [Integral molecules $INT(Mol)$]
Given $m \in Obj(Mol)$. $m$ is called an \textit{integral molecule} if 
\begin{equation*} 
A \rightarrow_M m. 
\end{equation*} 
$INT(Mol)$ denotes the collection of all integral molecules, i.e.,
\begin{equation*} 
INT(Mol):=\{m \in Obj(Mol) \ |\  A \rightarrow_M m\}.
\end{equation*} 
The collection INT(Loop) of all \textit{integral loops} and the collection INT(Reg) of all \textit{integral regions} are defined by
\begin{align*}
INT(Loop):&=\{ l \in Obj(Loop)\  |\  A \rightarrow_L l\},\\
INT(Reg):&=\{ |m| \in Obj(Reg)\ |\  m \in INT(Mol) \}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

