{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:28.068496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_957aa20d87a05b353c7582f188d7b5681b9c83d9c193d0438871d6fc63fd32c2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma recursive_Gbiadapted of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \\ and Wasserstein Distances'] Suppose $n > 1$ and that $G$ is transitively closed. A function $T : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ given by $T(x) = (T_{1:n-1}(x_{1:n-1}), T_n(x_n, x_{\mathrm{pa}_{n}}))$ is $G$-biadapted if and only if 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $T_{1:n-1} : \mathcal{X}_{1:n-1} \to \mathcal{Y}_{1:n-1}$ is $G_{1:n-1}$-biadapted;
        \item $T_n^{x_{\mathrm{pa}_{n}}}: \mathcal{X}_n \to \mathcal{Y}_n$ is bijective for all $x_{\mathrm{pa}_{n}} \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{pa}_{n}}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    In this case, the inverse of $T$ is given by $S(y) = (S_{1:n-1}(y_{1:n-1}), S_n^{y_{1:n-1}}(y_n))$, where $S_{1:n-1} = T_{1:n-1}^{-1}$ and $S_n^{y_{1:n-1}} = \brackets{T^{x_{\mathrm{pa}_{n}}}_n}^{-1}$ for $x_{1:n-1} = S_{1:n-1}(y_{1:n-1})$.","status":"unverified"}

