{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.884895+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_95dd1f6c862b0fff39f35da911f057205a438a2e23b0e99e87ce38696bca861d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem bordif of arXiv:2608.09856, '\bf Mass erasure on measured $\bbR$-trees,\\ applications to L\'evy forests'] Let $(T,d,\rho)$ be a rooted complete $\mathbb{R}$-tree. We set $T^*\! = \!  T\cup \partial T$. If $\mathbf s\! \in \!  \partial T$, we 
recall from Definition (boundarydef of the paper) $(d)$ that $d(\rho, \mathbf s) \! = \!  \infty$ and we use the convention $e^{-\infty}\! = \!  0$ in the following.
\begin{equation}

\forall {\bf s}_1, {\bf s}_2\! \in \!  T^*\! , \quad d^* (\mathbf s_1, \mathbf s_2)\! :=\!  2e^{-d(\rho, \mathbf s_1 \wedge \mathbf s_2)}\!  -e^{-d(\rho, \mathbf s_1)} \! -e^{-d(\rho, \mathbf s_2)} .
\end{equation} 
Then, $(T^*\!, d^*\!, \rho)$ is isometric to $(T_{\! g}, d_g, \rho)$, where $g(x)\! = \!  1\! -\! e^{-x}$, $x\! \in \!  [0, 1)$. 
We call $(T^*\!, d^*\!, \rho)$ the \emph{bordification} of $(T,d, \rho)$: it a boundaryless rooted complete $\mathbb{R}$-tree, $T$ is open and dense in $T^*\!$, $\partial T$ is closed, totally disconnected and $\partial T \! \subseteq \! \mathtt{Lf} (T^*)$. Moreover if 
 $(T,d)$ is separable (resp.~compact), then so is $(T^*\! ,d^*)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

