{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.485280+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_95fe669a13c8311aa3616aeddc11a2fbc4b33e3804c64df4bec0c6c32b866827","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 67 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] The sequences $(a_n)_{n\geq 1}\in\R^{\N\setminus\{0\}}$ satisfying the formal identity
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} a_{n}
\dfrac{w^{n}}{(x-y)^{n}}
=
\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} a_{n}
\Lambda_{n}(w,y)
\dfrac{1}{x^{n}},
\end{equation}
with the convention that
\begin{displaymath}
\dfrac{1}{(1-y/x)^{n}} =
\sum_{m=0}^{+\infty}
\dfrac{(m+n-1)!}{m!(n-1)!}
\,\dfrac{y^{m}}{x^{m}}
\end{displaymath}
are exactly the sequences of form
\begin{equation}

a_{n} = (-1)^{n-1}
\dfrac{1}{n!} a_{1},
\end{equation}
with $a_1\in\R$ being arbitrary.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:38:17.788112+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9600a84117c93eb8639864982924281199858319a5494ed1e4bf3459cafdd81c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem tecn_estm2 of arXiv:2509.07729, 'Permuton and local limits for the Luce model'] Fix $k\geq 1$ and $\pi\in\mathcal S_k$. Consider the case of exponential Sukhatme weights $\theta_i=\alpha^{n-i+1}$ for some $\alpha>0$. Then \[\Lambda(\pi)=\lim_{n\to +\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-k}p\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\theta_{i+\pi^{-1}(1)},\dots, \theta_{i+\pi^{-1}(k)}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)=p\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(1)},\dots, \alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(k)}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright).\]
    Moreover, setting for all $k\geq 1$ and $1\leq h\leq k-1$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\eta_{\pi}(h,k):=\mathbb P\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(E_{\pi^{-1}(1)}<\dots<E_{\pi^{-1}(k)}, \,\, E_{h+\pi^{-1}(1)}<\dots<E_{h+\pi^{-1}(k)}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright), 
	\end{equation*}
	where $(E_{i})_i$ are independent exponential random variables of parameter $\alpha^{-i}$, and
	\begin{equation}
		\nu_\infty(\pi):=p\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(1)},\dots, \alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(k)}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)+(1-2k)p\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(1)},\dots, \alpha^{-\pi^{-1}(k)}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)^2+2 \sum_{h=1}^{k-1}\eta_{\pi}(h,k),
	\end{equation}
	then, as $n\to\infty$,
	\begin{equation*}
		(\nu_{n}(\pi))^2\sim n\cdot \nu_\infty(\pi).
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.225969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96017bcca2880c39ca727459b6505a5417d55035b34e7e6aca6b2614f751ab13","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq: def of bfL and bfM of arXiv:2608.23156, 'Disc counting statistics of the real Ginibre ensemble'] {Structured matrices}
Let $\mathbf{L}_N(R)$ and $\mathbf{M}_N(R)$ be the $n\times n$ matrices with
entries
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
[\mathbf{L}_N(R)]_{j,k} &:= \frac{\gamma(2j-1,NR^2)}{\Gamma(2j-1)} \delta_{j,k},
\\
[\mathbf{M}_N(R)]_{j,k} &:= \frac{ \gamma(j+k-\tfrac{3}{2},N R^2)}{\sqrt{2\pi\Gamma(2j-1)\Gamma(2k-1)}}, 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\delta_{j,k}$ denotes the Kronecker delta.

We further define the $n\times n$ matrices $\mathbf{E}_{N,1}(R)$ and
$\mathbf{E}_{N,2}(R)$ by
 \begin{equation}
 \begin{split} 
        [\mathbf{E}_{N,1}(R)]_{j,k} &:= \frac{ 2^{j-\frac{1}{2}} N^{k-1} R^{2k-2} e^{-\frac{NR^2}{2}} \gamma(j-\tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{NR^2}{2})}{\sqrt{2\pi\Gamma(2j-1)\Gamma(2k-1)}},
        \\ 
        [\mathbf{E}_{N,2}(R)]_{j,k} &:= \frac{ E_N(j,k;R) }{\sqrt{2\pi\Gamma(2j-1)\Gamma(2k-1)}}.   
 \end{split}
    \end{equation}
Here, $\delta_{j,k}$ denotes the Kronecker delta, and
    \begin{equation}
        E_N(j,k;R):= -(NR^2)^{j+k-\frac{3}{2}} \int_0^\pi   \sin((2j-2k +1)\theta) e^{-NR^2 \cos 2\theta} \operatorname{erfc}(\sqrt{2N}R \sin\theta)\,d\theta .
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

