{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.567829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_969559d4a4971ab7bbc5c14bc926223ff77735aeaa014317ca2bd9bbf3ae6de8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 38 (Metric-Santal\'o from Differential Isoperimetry) of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Let $(X,d,\mu)$ be a Polish probability space which admits a continuous isoperimetric function $I: (0,1) \to (0,\infty)$. Let $(R_s^I)_{s\geq  0}$ be the family of enlargement functions induced by $I$ via Lemma~\ref{l:isop_to_enl}, and let 
\begin{equation}

\Theta^I(s) = \sup_{0 \le v \le 1} \sqrt{v\bigl(1 - R_s^I(v)\bigr)}.\end{equation}
Suppose $\Theta$ is continuous and decaying to zero at infinity.
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The space $(X,\mu)$ satisfies the metric-Santal\'o inequality induced by $2\cdot \Theta^I$ on the domain $(\mathcal{B}(X), \mathcal{B}(X))$.
    \item For any sub-collections $\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{B}(X)$ of essentially disjoint sets ($\mu(A \cap D) = 0$ for all $A \in \mathcal{A}$ and $D \in \mathcal{D}$), $(X,\mu)$ satisfies the sharper metric-Santal\'o inequality induced by $\Theta^I$ on the domain $(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{D})$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.669995+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9697c94a4a7c2a4bf7a7ddd40eff02579c6d2b7ae80434f2fbfd2b4e1aa97cbf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:g(k)-a-ell of arXiv:2602.14998, 'Random geometric graphs with smooth kernels: sharp detection threshold and a spectral conj'] For $g(k)$ defined in \prettyref{eq:def-g(k)}, we have
\begin{equation*}
g(k) =\mathbb{E}_{A}
\qth{
\pth{ \sum_{\boldsymbol \ell } a_{\boldsymbol \ell} 
\cdot \mathbb{E}_{x_1,\ldots,x_k|A}
\qth{ \prod_{1 \le i<j \le k} 
\iprod{x_i}{x_j}^{\ell_{ij}}}
}^2} ,
\end{equation*}
where 
\begin{equation}
a_{\boldsymbol \ell}
\triangleq \frac{1}{ \prod_{i<j} \ell_{ij}!}
\sum_{\mathbf{m}} 
\frac{\prod_{i=1}^k b_{\ell_i} \ell_i! (2m_i-1)!!/(2m_i)!  }{d(d+2) \cdots (d+\sum_{i=1}^k \ell_i -2)} .

\end{equation}
In the above, $\boldsymbol{\ell} = (\ell_{ij})$ is a multi-index whose entries take values in ${0,\ldots,L}$, and $\mathbf{m} = (m_i)$ is a multi-index whose entries are nonnegative integers bounded by $L/2$.

In the special case of a linear kernel $\kappa(t) = b_1 t$, 

if $k$ is odd, then $g(k) = 0$; if $k$ is even, then
\begin{equation*}
g(k) = \left(\frac{b_{1}^k}{d(d+2) \cdots (d+k-2)}\right)^2  \mathbb{E}_{A}
\qth{
\pth{ \sum_{\boldsymbol \ell } \mathbb{E}_{x_1,\ldots,x_k|A}
\qth{ \prod_{1 \le i<j \le k} 
\iprod{x_i}{x_j}^{\ell_{ij}}}
}^2} ,
\end{equation*}
where $\boldsymbol \ell = (\ell_{ij})$  is a multi-index 

such that $\ell_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ and $\sum_{j \neq i} \ell_{ij} = 1$ for each $i$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.776330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96a0e839bcbb0c894c80648e0803f4745f625d7ea5db22230b369930c03b12d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:lipschitz_witness of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For all $\varepsilon > 0$, there is some $s > 0$ and a Lipschitz continuous function $h : \mathbb{A}_+^n \to \mathbb{R}$
