uniformly in $t\in [0,T]$ and $z=z_T\in\mathcal{D}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.159765+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96d6e0823467ed7ab2161f4ee230c04135841f3052180678f4210eee1b55c9a6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop MOP-as-colim of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The cocone $\sigma$ expresses $\textbf{\textup{MOP}}$ as the colimit of the global functor ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak J}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}\colon\ul\textup{VRep}\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$, i.e.\ as a left adjoint object with respect to $\textup{const}\colon{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\textup{gl}\to{\textup{Fun}}(\ul\textup{VRep},{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl})$.
    \begin{proof}
        As ${\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak T\kern-1pt\mathfrak h}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}_\textup{gl}$ is a global left adjoint, it suffices to prove that the terminal object of $\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{gl}[\textbf{\textup{BOP}}]$ is equivalent to the global colimit of $\bar y_{\textup{top}}$ (in a necessarily unique way). This on the other hand may be checked after postcomposing with the equivalence $\Psi^{-1}\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{gl}[\textbf{\textup{BOP}}]\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}\ul\Spc_{\textup{Glo}/\ul\textup{VRep}^{\oplus}}$. We claim that the resulting composite $\ul\textup{VRep}^{\oplus}\to\ul\Spc_{\textup{Glo}/\ul\textup{VRep}^{\oplus}}^\otimes$ is equivalent, even as a \emph{symmetric monoidal} global functor, to the parametrized Yoneda embedding; the proposition will then follow since the colimit of the latter is indeed terminal by [martiniwolf2021limits]*{Proposition 6.1.3}.
        To prove the claim, we may again restrict to $\ul\textup{Rep}^\oplus$, i.e.~it will suffice to identify $(\psi^\otimes)^{-1}\circ y_{\textup{top}}^\otimes$ with the Yoneda embedding of $\ul\textup{Rep}^\oplus$. This however holds by construction of $\psi^\otimes$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.474913+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96dd06e560a95359655fe39b09ff19af01f130eb0f92ce74100245631278122d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 66 (Deterministic matching lower bound and non-Cauchy criterion) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $1\le p,r<\infty$ and $q\ge0$.  For $q\ge1$, let
$(H_i)_{i\in I_0}$ be a finite family of symmetric algebraic order-$q$
kernels realized over one fixed isonormal process; at $q=0$, let them be
deterministic Schatten blocks.  Assume their
external input and output supports form a matching.  Then
\begin{equation}

 \left(\sum_{i\in I_0}
  \underline{\Prof}_{q,r}(H_i)^r\right)^{1/r}
 \le
 \left(\sum_{i\in I_0}
  \norm{I_q(H_i)}_{L^2(\Omega;\Sch_r)}^r\right)^{1/r}
 \le C_{q,p,r}
 \norm{\sum_{i\in I_0}I_q(H_i)}_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)},
\end{equation}
where $C_{0,p,r}=1$.  More generally, if $X$ is a global $\Sch_r$-valued
random operator in the closed $q$th chaos and $(P_i,Q_i)_{i\in I_0}$ is a
finite matching compression, then
\begin{equation}

 \left(\sum_{i\in I_0}
  \norm{P_iXQ_i}_{L^2(\Omega;\Sch_r)}^r\right)^{1/r}
 \le C_{q,p,r}\norm X_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}.
\end{equation}
If the compressed blocks admit algebraic kernels $H_i$, the corresponding
lower-profile term from \eqref{eq:matching-lower-obstruction} may also be
included.  Applied to cutoff differences, the estimate gives a non-Cauchy
criterion in $L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:38.636720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96dfdc262d866b7a4e387b4228a304ea27258206126353db28b4a59a9ff87e83","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2506.17470, 'The coalescent of a sample from a linear-fractional branching process'] Let $m\ge k$ and $\textbf{i}=(i_0,\dots, i_{m-1})\in \{0,1\}^m$ where ~$i_0+\dots +i_{m-1}=k$. Then
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
        & \mathbb{P}(N_T=m,\,   (I_0,\dots, I_{m-1})= \textbf{i} \mid N_T\ge k)  = \mathbb{Q}(\tilde{N}_T  = m, \, (J_0(Y),\dots, J_{m-1}(Y))= \textbf{i}\mid K=k).
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \mu_k(\mathrm{d}y):=\mathbb{Q}(Y\in \mathrm{d}y \mid K=k) = \frac{k\delta_T y^{k-1}}{(1-(1-\delta_T)(1-y))^{k+1}}\mathrm{d}y, \qquad y\in (0,1),  
\end{equation}
where 
$\delta_T:=\mathbb{P}(H> T)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.658212+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96e3c28f000cd97351b23c4bb8817bd5a3bdb46a26ee37405e1a4d888c370908","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 1 of arXiv:2608.30796, 'Tumor boundary instability induced by pressure feedback'] For fixed \(\lambda>0\) and \(\gamma>0\), the translation
mode is neutral:
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{F}_1(R;\lambda,\gamma)=0
\qquad\text{for every }R>0.
\end{equation}
For each integer \(l\geq2\),
\begin{subequations}

\begin{align}
\lim_{R\to0^+}\mathcal{F}_l(R;\lambda,\gamma)
&=\frac{1-l}{2}<0,
\\
\mathcal{F}_l(R;\lambda,\gamma)
&=
\frac{l^2-1}
{2(\sqrt{\lambda}+1)(\sqrt{\lambda}+\sqrt{\gamma})R^2}
+O(R^{-3})
\qquad\text{as }R\to+\infty.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
For fixed \(R>0\), the high-frequency behavior is
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{F}_l(R;\lambda,\gamma)
=
\frac{\partial_rp_0(R)}{R}\,l+O(1)
\qquad\text{as }l\to+\infty.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every \(l\geq2\), the function
\(\mathcal{F}_l(\,\cdot\,;\lambda,\gamma)\) has at least one zero on
\((0,+\infty)\), while for each fixed \(R>0\), all sufficiently large
modes are stable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.759213+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96e42a944797530d12c2f34bfc2358b33285dc9a4ac1ebeb9f4d0b1530958fbf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def sequence-prior of arXiv:2411.18155, 'Besov regularity of random wavelet series'] Let $d\in\mathds{N}$, $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\theta\in\mathds{R}$, $\mu,\nu\in (-\infty,0]$, and let $X$ be a (real-valued) random variable.
\begin{enumerate}[label={(\alph*)},leftmargin=*,align=left,noitemsep]
\item{We say that $a=(a_{j,t,m})_{(j,t,m)\in J}\in\mathds{R}^J$ \emph{follows a Besov sequence prior with parameters $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\theta)$ and template random variable $X$} if there are i.i.d.\ random variables $\xi_{j,t,m}\sim X$ for $(j,t,m)\in \mathds{N}_0\times \left\{F,M\right\}^d\times \mathds{Z}^d$ such that 
\begin{equation}
a_{j,t,m}=2^{j\alpha}(j+1)^\theta \lr{1+\frac{\left\lVertm\right\rVert_\infty}{2^j}}^{\beta + (\gamma-\beta)\mathds{1}_{j=0}}\xi_{j,t,m}.
\end{equation}}
\item{We say that $a=(a_{j,t,m})_{(j,t,m)\in J}\in\mathds{R}^J$ \emph{follows a Besov--Bernoulli{} sequence prior with parameters $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\mu,\nu)$ and template random variable $X$} if there are i.i.d.\ random variables $\xi_{j,t,m}\sim X$ for $(j,t,m)\in \mathds{N}_0\times \left\{F,M\right\}^d\times \mathds{Z}^d$ and
Bernoulli$(\varrho_{j,m})$-distributed random variables $\lambda_{j,t,m}$ for $(j,t,m)\in \mathds{N}_0\times \left\{F,M\right\}^d\times \mathds{Z}^d$, independent among each other and from all the $\xi_{j,t,m}$, with
\begin{equation*}
\varrho_{j,m}=2^{j\mu}\lr{1+\frac{\left\lVertm\right\rVert_\infty}{2^j }}^\nu
\end{equation*}
such that
\begin{equation}
a_{j,t,m}=2^{j\alpha} \lr{1+\frac{\left\lVertm\right\rVert_\infty}{2^j}}^{\beta + (\gamma-\beta)\mathds{1}_{j=0}}\lambda_{j,t,m}\xi_{j,t,m}.
\end{equation}}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:11:57.300170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_96e6a255f99a02e31f56ad7beabfff86cb0dcc4fa70a7a6a4032a73050f84ee0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2607.22878, 'Clustering of large deviations in heavy-tailed moving averages: the catastrophe principle '] Suppose Assumption
