{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:33:12.002349+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_97a7afc47b3e1791570b9f7ee40444848e259fe1b8b0736648591ea97bbc236e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:resolvent of arXiv:2609.02820, 'Sharpness and critical scaling of parking'] There are $c,C>0$ such that, for $0<a\leq1$ and $t\geq1$,
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{E}_0e^{-a|R_t|}\leq C
		\begin{cases}
			\exp\{-ca^{2/3}t^{1/3}\}&d=1\,,\\
			\exp\{-c\sqrt{at/\log(t+2)}\}&d=2,\ t\geq e/a\,,\\
			\exp\{-c\sqrt{at}\}&d\geq3\,.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, with $\Lambda=\log(e/a)$ and
	\begin{equation}
		T=C
		\begin{cases}
			a^{-2}\Lambda^3&d=1\,,\\
			a^{-1}\Lambda^3&d=2\,,\\
			a^{-1}\Lambda^2&d\geq3\,,
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
	the sum $\sum_{t>T}\mathbb{E}_0e^{-a|R_t|}$ is at most one.","status":"unverified"}

