{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.158297+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_97b8133a23fc33ab5f5d2faf595a47b57ed5e9a064e7339e36ce2a1a4d3adb31","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma conjugate-to-On of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $G$ be a compact Lie group, let $n\ge0$, and let $f_1,f_2\colon G\rightrightarrows {\textup{O}}(n)$ be homomorphisms. Then $f_1$ and $f_2$ are conjugate (i.e.\ homotopic in $\textup{Glo}$) if and only if for every $g\in G$ the two matrices $f_1(g)$ and $f_2(g)$ are conjugate in ${\textup{GL}}(n,{\mathbb{C}})$.
    \begin{proof}
        The condition is clearly necessary. To see that it is also sufficient, we first observe that $f_1$ and $f_2$ are conjugate if and only if they are conjugate as homomorphisms to ${\textup{GL}}(n,{\mathbb{R}})$ [broecker-tom-dieck]*{Exercise II.6.10.5}, i.e.\ if and only if the two representations $f_1^*{\mathbb{R}}^n$ and $f_2^*{\mathbb{R}}^n$ are isomorphic \emph{without} regarding the inner product. We will show that these representations become isomorphic after scalar extension to ${\mathbb{C}}$; forgetting the complex structure will then in particular give an isomorphism $f_1^*{\mathbb{R}}^n\oplus f_1^*{\mathbb{R}}^n\cong f_2^*{\mathbb{R}}^n\oplus f_2^*{\mathbb{R}}^n$ and counting the multiplicities of irreducible representations on each side yields the claim.

        To prove that $f_1^*{\mathbb{C}}^n$ and $f_2^*{\mathbb{C}}^n$ are isomorphic, it will suffice to show that their characters $\chi_i\colon g\mapsto\text{Tr}(f_i(g))$ agree [broecker-tom-dieck]*{Theorem II.4.12}. This follows at once from cyclic invariance of the trace and the assumption that $f_1(g)$ be conjugate to $f_2(g)$ for each fixed $g\in G$.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:54:27.993619+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_97baa57ae3e1094b1b5a21f607589a84454bc1612404e681a61435109813056e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem k1k2Ithm of arXiv:2608.04730, 'A new look at some aspects of one-dimensional random sequential adsorption and its continu'] Let $M^{(n)}_{k_1,k_2;I}$ denote the expected number of bonded molecules under Model I in (equation models of the paper).
