{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.325890+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_982ab740771b8fce809ae4e024529e832ab8899ab637a6664a7ace1be6b3e85c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn bound-edges of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Given an oriented planar map $\mathfrak m$ with missing edges, make the following definitions (see the map on the left-hand side of Figure~(fig-bipolar-boundaries of the paper)). 
\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textbf{upper-left} (resp.\ \textbf{lower-right}) \textbf{boundary} of $\mathfrak m$ is defined to be the set of edges $e\in\mathcal E(\mathfrak m)$ lying on the boundary of the external face of $\mathfrak m$  which are oriented so that the external face of $\mathfrak m$ is  immediately to the left (resp.\ right) of $e$. For all of the maps we consider, the upper-left and lower-right boundaries of $\mathfrak m$ will be (possibly empty) simple directed paths.
    \item The \textbf{upper-right} (resp.\ \textbf{lower-left}) \textbf{boundary} of $\mathfrak m$ is defined to be the set of consecutive \emph{missing} edges $e\notin\mathcal E(\mathfrak m)$ between the terminal (resp.\ initial) vertex of the upper-left boundary and the terminal (resp.\ initial) vertex of the lower-right boundary.  
\end{itemize}
When the orientation of  $\mathfrak m$ is bipolar and there are no missing edges, we simply call the upper-left boundary \textbf{left} boundary and the lower-right boundary \textbf{right} boundary.","status":"unverified"}

