{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.162552+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_98c9bb5a0763e09a30c66a0a843fe79cc5419708a346a1434ba8b9d563c23885","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Def_Universal_1 of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] Here is the first stage of the construction:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item

    The \defined{universal bundle of admissible singular spinors} is the \emph{set}
    \begin{align*}
      \mathbf{E}
      &\coloneq
        \coprod_{({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}} H_a^\infty\Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),S\otimes{\mathfrak l};{\mathbf{p}}}
    \end{align*}    
    together with the projection map
    $\mathbf{E} \to \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$
    and the tame Fréchet space structures on the fibres,
    defined in (Sec_ConormalAndAdaptedSobolevSpaces of the paper) and [BeraWalpuski2025].
    Here and throughout the remainder of this article,
    additional decorations with ${\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\mathfrak l}$ are used to indicate the dependence on these parameters.
  \item

    The \defined{universal bundle of singular spinors} is the \emph{set}
    \begin{align*}
      \mathbf{F}
      &\coloneq
        \coprod_{({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}} H_b^\infty\Gamma\paren{X{\backslash} \mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),S\otimes{\mathfrak l};{\mathbf{p}}}
    \end{align*}    
    together with the projection map
    $\mathbf{F} \to \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$
    and the tame Fréchet space structures on the fibres,
    defined in (Sec_ConormalAndAdaptedSobolevSpaces of the paper) and [BeraWalpuski2025].
  \item

    The \defined{universal bundle of residues} is the \emph{set}
    \begin{equation*}
      \mathbf{G} \coloneq \coprod_{({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}]) \in \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}} \Gamma\paren{\mathrm{Br}({\mathfrak l}),\check S_{\mathbf{p}}}
    \end{equation*}
    together with the projection map $\mathbf{G} \to \mathbf{Dir} \times \mathbf{Ram}$ and the usual tame Fréchet space structures on the fibres.
  \item

    The \defined{universal Dirac operator} ${\mathbf{D}} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E} \to \mathbf{F}$ is defined by
    \begin{equation*}
      {\mathbf{D}}({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];\phi)
      \coloneq
      ({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];D_{\mathbf{p}}^{\mathfrak l} \phi).
    \end{equation*}
  \item

    The \defined{universal residue map} $\mathbf{res} \mskip0.5mu\colon\thinspace \mathbf{E} \to \mathbf{G}$ is defined by
    \begin{equation*}
      \mathbf{res}({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];\phi)
      \coloneq
      ({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];\res \phi).
    \end{equation*}
  \end{enumerate}
  It remains to endow
  $\mathbf{E}$,
  $\mathbf{F}$, and
  $\mathbf{G}$
  with the structure of tame Fréchet space bundles such that ${\mathbf{D}}$ and $\mathbf{res}$ become maps of tame Fréchet space bundles.","status":"unverified"}

