The right-hand side extends continuously to $x=y$, where it is zero.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.227296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9929768850825e9ba7add20825757643a34c1b78803814cb4ec094ee013c83db","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Feller's lemma of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] Let $X_t,X$ be a family of $[0,\infty]$-valued random variables such that $\mathbb{P}\left(X <\infty\right) = 1$. Assume that for all $\rho>0$,
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}[e^{-\rho X_t}] \longrightarrow \mathbb{E}[e^{-\rho X}] \quad \text{as} \quad t\to\infty.
    \end{equation}
    Then, 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}\left(X_{t} = +\infty\right) \to 0 \quad \text{as} \quad t\to\infty,
    \end{equation} 
    and conditioned on the event $\{X_{t}<\infty \}$, $X_{t}$ converges weakly to $X$. In particular, for all $x \in [0,\infty)$ which is a point of continuity for  $\mathbb{P}(X\leq x)$, $\mathbb{P}\left(X_{t}\le x\right) \to \mathbb{P}\left(X\le x\right)$ as $t\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:06:18.027030+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9929cd731807fea64b5a9a5cc584f4a71dd9dbf939b0902aaf4944e6a7a8e8c7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.15616, '\textbf{Compact Support Property of Super-Brownian Motion with Irregular Drift}'] Let $\mu$ and $\eta$ be two finite measures. Then the following holds:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)]
		For all $t > 0$ it holds that
		\begin{equation*}
			V_t(\mu+ \eta)(x) \leq V_t(\mu)(x) + V_t (\eta)(x), \quad x\in\R.
		\end{equation*}
		\item[(ii)] If $\mu \leq \eta$, then 
		\begin{equation*}
			V_t(\mu)(x) \leq V_t(\eta)(x),\quad x\in \R,
		\end{equation*}
		for all $t > 0$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:15:55.137736+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_992ef8b182e3f19d9d4730d6027958ae4f7e3a32dc9df0bc4d66e34b35a0d966","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] For a CK-model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, W_\bot, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, we define a \emph{constructive satisfaction relation} $\Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M}$ on $W \times \mathscr{L}_{\Box\Diamond}$ by:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \bot \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} x \in W_\bot$
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \to \psi_2 \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, (x' \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_1 \mathrel{\Rightarrow} x' \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \psi_2)$
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \Box \varphi     \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, \forall y' \sqsupset x'\, (y' \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item $x \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \Diamond \varphi \mathrel{\;\ratio\Longleftrightarrow\;} \forall x' \geq x\, \exists y' \sqsupset x'\, (y' \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$
    \item The other cases are identical to the classical $\Vdash_\mathfrak{M}$.
  \end{enumerate}
  We write $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^{\text{c}} \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(c-)valid in} $\mathfrak{M}$'') to mean $\forall x \in W\,(x \Vdash^{\text{c}}_\mathfrak{M} \varphi)$,
  and $\mathfrak{F} \vDash^{\text{c}} \varphi$ (``$\varphi$ is \emph{(c-)valid on} $\mathfrak{F}$'') to mean that $\mathfrak{M} \vDash^{\text{c}} \varphi$ for every $\mathfrak{M}$ based on $\mathfrak{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.878953+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99305b2809e1eff65ff9a11514fb752164bf8838fc7bfcd285e13ab457759c60","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2608.29501, 'Disorder Thresholds and Free Energy of Brownian Directed Polymers with\\ Product and Radia'] Let \(0<\alpha<1\) and let \(Z\sim N(0,1)\).  There is
\(C_\alpha<\infty\) such that, for every \(a\in\R\) and \(s\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
  \E\big[(1+|a+\sqrt sZ|)^{-\alpha}\big]
  \le C_\alpha(1+s)^{-\alpha/2}.

\end{equation}
For every \(L<\infty\), there is \(c_{\alpha,L}>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
  |a|\le L\sqrt s
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \E\big[(1+|a+\sqrt sZ|)^{-\alpha}\big]
  \ge c_{\alpha,L}(1+s)^{-\alpha/2}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.952023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9937530813a02c9ef202fcd1817ed9305f6efd683136322773ac5885377cdee1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 44 (Bulk stability) of arXiv:2606.24887, '2606.24887'] For every $\delta >0$, there exist constants 
		$C$, $\mathfrak{c} >0$ depending only on $\delta$ such that for all $q \in \C$ and $r \in \C$ satisfying 
		\begin{equation} 
			q^3 + 2\ii\eta q^2 + \left(1-\eta^2 - |w|^2 \right) q +\ii \eta =r~\text{and}~ \left|q - \ii v(w,\eta) \right| \le \mathfrak{c}
		\end{equation}
		for some $(w,\eta) \in (\mathrm{D}_{1-\delta} \times (0,\infty))\cup (\C \times [1,\infty))$ we have 
		\begin{equation*}
			\left|q - \ii v(w,\eta) \right| \le \frac{C|r|}{1 + \eta^2}.
		\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:56:14.307482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_99393b9f57f795c386dff14d8af0c7d47fb3551b41a31563c4bff234506b25ee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma LEM:ort of arXiv:2412.09790, 'Critical threshold for weakly interacting log-correlated focusing Gibbs measures'] Let $f$ and $g$ be jointly Gaussian random variables with mean zero 
and variances $\sigma_f$
and $\sigma_g$.
Then, we have 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\big[ H_k(f; \sigma_f) H_\ell(g; \sigma_g)\big] = \delta_{k\ell} k! \big\{\mathbb{E}[ f g] \big\}^k, 
\end{align*} 

\noindent
where
 $H_k (x,\sigma)$ denotes the Hermite polynomial of degree $k$ with a variance parameter $\sigma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.116563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_993a1c7a0090cb49bd86e96f05179e3655ef5a73d5e5fd07d6f1cc4077cad238","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 ((Lazy) Metropolis random walk) of arXiv:2604.04823, '2604.04823'] Let~$h > 0$, and let~$\pi$ be a probability density on~$\T^d$. The Metropolis random walk with step size~$h$ and stationary density~$\pi$ is the Markov kernel~$P_{h,\pi}$ on~$\mathcal X = \supp(\pi)$ defined by
\begin{equation}
   P_{h,\pi}(x,dy) = \alpha(x,y)\, r_h(x,y)\,dy 
   + \delta_x(dy)\int_{\mathcal X} (1-\alpha(x,z))\, r_h(x,z)\,dz\,,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \alpha(x,y) = \min\set*{1, \frac{\pi(y)}{\pi(x)}}, 
    \quad 
    r_h(x,y) = \frac{\one_{B(x,h)}(y)}{|B(x,h)|}\,.
\end{equation}
The lazy Metropolis random walk with step size~$h$ and stationary density~$\pi$ is defined by
\begin{equation}
    T_{h,\pi} = \frac{1}{2}\paren*{I + P_{h,\pi}}\,.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.932526+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_993acdfe43c6e57c11fafeb48193981a31055b794dae9d8bfaa842fa9efaecce","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_final of arXiv:2412.05706, 'Quasi-multiplicativity and regularity for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $3\le d\le 6$, there exists $
	\refstepcounter{newcnstcnt}
	\ensuremath{C_{\thenewcnstcnt}}(d)>0$ such that 
	\begin{equation}
		 \mathbb{P}^{D}\big( A\xleftrightarrow{\ge 0} x,y \big)\lesssim r^{-\frac{d}{2}+1+3\varsigma_d(r)}\mathbb{P}^{D}\big( A\xleftrightarrow{\ge 0} x \big)
	\end{equation}
	if either of the following two conditions (where $N\ge 1 $ and $r:=|x-y|$) holds:	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(a)] $A \subset \widetilde{B}(N)$, $M\ge \ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_twopoint_set}}}N^{1+\varsigma_d(N)}$, $x,y \in \partial B(M)$ and $D\subset \widetilde{B}(N)\cup [\widetilde{B}(2M)]^c$; 


		\item[(b)] $A \subset [\widetilde{B}(N)]^c$, $M\le \ensuremath{C_{\ref*{const_twopoint_set}}}^{-1}N^{1-\varsigma_d(N)}$, $x,y\in B(M)$ and $D\subset [\widetilde{B}(N)]^c \cup \widetilde{B}(\frac{M}{2}) $.  

	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:16.157682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_993e4ee9ac086e27e27e574f313b23d5a4bea5bec235dd69034518f0817848a5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.13845, 'Minkowski content construction of\\ the CLE gasket measure'] For each $\ell \in \N$ there exists a constant $c > 0$ so that the following is true.  Suppose that $\Gamma$ is a $\CLE_{\kappa}$ on a simply connected domain $D \subseteq \C$.  Then for every $z \in D$ and $r > 0$ so that $B(z,2r) \subseteq D$ we have that
\begin{equation}
 \E[ \meas{B(z,r)}{\Upsilon_{\le\ell}} ] \leq c r^2 (\dist(z,\partial D))^{\dcle-2}.
