{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.090981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a1c33ce58c82cff2736c06c6850b6dc4ec72875a12a8f6178c4a66019d454d9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def1 definition:snaketrajectory of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Fix ${\mathrm{w}} \in \mathcal{W}_E$. A continuous function  $\omega:\mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathcal{W}_E$   started at  $\omega_0 = {\mathrm{w}}$
 and  satisfying for any $s,t \geq 0$,  that 
  \begin{equation}
    \omega_{s}(r) = \omega_{t}(r),  \quad \quad  \text{ for every } \, \,  0 \leq r \leq m_{\zeta(\omega)}(s ,t),
\end{equation}
will be referred to as a snake trajectory started from ${\mathrm{w}}$.  We denote  the collection of snake trajectories started from  ${\mathrm{w}}$ by $\mathcal{S}^\circ_{{\mathrm{w}}}$, and we write  
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{S}^\circ := \bigcup_{{\mathrm{w}} \in \mathcal{W}_E} \mathcal{S}^\circ_{\mathrm{w}} ,
\end{equation*}
for the set of all snake trajectories.\footnote{Note that [ALG15] defines a snake trajectory under the additional conditions that $\omega$ starts from  some $y \in E$  (instead of a general path ${\mathrm{w}} \in \mathcal{W}_E$)  and that $\sigma(\omega)$ is  finite.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.592698+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a1d6a74f03a38fd419556fb72a9e7f0d27d282b4b286ce4d6fe30d57e5235cb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm-1 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] For each~$b\ge2$ there exists a non-increasing continuous function $\sigma^2\colon\mathbb R_+\to\mathbb R_+$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

\sigma^2(\beta)\,\begin{cases}
=1/\beta,\qquad&\text{if }\beta\le\beta_\text{\rm c},
\\
<1/\beta,\qquad&\text{if }\beta>\beta_\text{\rm c},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
such that the following holds for all models satisfying Assumptions~(ass-1 of the paper)--(ass-2 of the paper) with~$\{\fraktura d_k\}_{k\ge0}$ decaying exponentially when~$\beta>\beta_\text{\rm c}$  and obeying $\sum_{j\ge1}\fraktura d_j\log(j)<\infty$ when~$\beta=\beta_\text{\rm c}$: 
There exists $\epsilon>0$ and, for all~$\beta>0$ with $1/\beta>1/\beta_\text{\rm c}-\epsilon$,
all~$n\ge1$ and all~$x,y\in\Lambda_n$,
\begin{equation}

\langle\phi_x\phi_y\rangle_{n,\beta}=\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \sigma^2(\beta)\log_{b^{1/2}}\Bigl(\frac{\operatorname{diam}(\Lambda_n)}{1+d(x,y)}\Bigr)+O(1),&\text{if }\beta\ne\beta_\text{\rm c},
\\*[4mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{\beta_\text{\rm c}}\log_{b^{1/2}}\Bigl(\frac{\operatorname{diam}(\Lambda_n)}{1+d(x,y)}\Bigr)-\bar c\log\Bigl(\frac{\log\operatorname{diam}(\Lambda_n)}{\log[2+d(x,y)]}\Bigr)+ O(1),&\text{if }\beta=\beta_\text{\rm c},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}

\bar c:=\frac{8\pi^2}{\beta_\text{\rm c}^2} \frac{b(b^3-1)}{(b-1)^3(b+1)^2}
\end{equation}
and~$O(1)$ are quantities bounded uniformly in~$n\ge1$ and~$x,y\in\Lambda_n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.517776+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a213d5a6b7f421d0b9ee36c4f8de50471eca31dba9b46bb9a013bc9371cc962","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{lem:biskup_size}
