{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.249668+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a80582e93645b91831c584fb9aea2484790ed82c00c479d5123173828e00808","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_spine_MAP of arXiv:2512.21159, '\textsc{From multitype branching Brownian motions\\ to branching Markov additive processes'] For any $x\in \mathbb{R}$ and $i\in \mathcal{I}$, 
    let $(\chi,\Theta)$ be a MAP with triplet $((\phi_i)_{i\in\mathcal{I}}, Q, G)$ under law $\mathbb{P}_{x,i}$. For $\theta \in (0,\bar{\theta})$, define 
    \begin{equation}
        \Xi_{\theta}(t) := e^{-\theta \chi_t - \lambda(\theta)t + \int_0^t(\beta(m-1))(\Theta_s)\mathrm{d} s} V_{\Theta_t}(\theta), \qquad t\ge 0.
    \end{equation}
    It is a $\mathbb{P}_{x,i}$-martingale. Define a new probability measure $\mathbb{P}_{x,i}^{\theta}$ by
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\mathrm{d} \mathbb{P}_{x,i}^{\theta}}{\mathrm{d} \mathbb{P}_{x,i}} \bigg{|}_{\mathcal{F}_t^{(\chi,\Theta)}} := \frac{\Xi_{\theta}(t)}{\Xi_{\theta}(0)}, \qquad t\ge 0, 
    \end{equation}
    where $(\mathcal{F}_t^{(\chi,\Theta)}, t\geq 0)$ is the natural filtration of the MAP $(\chi, \Theta)$. Then under $\mathbb{P}_{x,i}^{\theta}$, $((\chi, \Theta)_{t\geq 0})$ is a MAP with the following characteristics: for $k,j \in \mathcal{I}$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
        &\widetilde{q}_{kj} = \frac{q_{kj} V_j(\theta) G_{kj}(\theta)}{V_k(\theta)}, \; \quad \mathbb{P} (\widetilde{U}_{kj}\in \mathrm{d} x) = \frac{e^{-\theta x}}{G_{kj}(\theta)} \mathbb{P} (U_{kj}\in \mathrm{d} x), \quad \forall j\neq k, \quad \widetilde{q}_{kk} = -\sum_{j\neq k}\widetilde{q}_{kj}, \\
        &\widetilde{\sigma}_k = \sigma_k, \; \widetilde{\mathtt{a}}_k = \mathtt{a}_k - \theta \sigma_k^2 - \int x \mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ [0,1] \right\} } (|x|) (1-e^{-\theta x}) \Lambda_k(\mathrm{d} x), \; \widetilde{\Lambda}_k(\mathrm{d} x) = e^{-\theta x} \Lambda_k(\mathrm{d} x).
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}


    Therefore the corresponding MAP triplet is given by  
    \begin{equation}
        \left((\widetilde{\phi}_k)_{k\in\mathcal{I}}, \widetilde{Q}:=(\widetilde{q}_{kj})_{k,j\in\mathcal{I}}, \widetilde{G}:=(\widetilde{G}_{kj})_{k,j\in\mathcal{I}}\right),
    \end{equation}
    where $\widetilde{\phi}_k(\alpha)=\phi_k(\alpha+\theta)-\phi_k(\theta)$  for $k\in \mathcal{I}$, and $\widetilde{G}_{kj}(\alpha)=\frac{G_{kj}(\alpha+\theta)}{G_{kj}(\theta)}$ for $ k,j\in \mathcal{I}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:32.859565+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9a82acbf9c6439d21b72ea37f8b3c569978324be23db03b0c470b41a5c8a267d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2609.02423, '2609.02423'] Let $U_{\lambda,\mu}(t)$ be the block propagator with kernel \eqref{eq:kernel}.  If $A_x$ and $A_y$ denote the left-hand sides of \eqref{eq:kernel-x} and \eqref{eq:kernel-y}, then
