{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.469011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9aa17195ef0a953c5a1c5ced3d074c783410f9b545815828efae5beb150c8c07","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 (Schatten bound for decoupled Gaussian chaoses) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] For every $m\ge1$, there is a constant $C_m<\infty$ with the following
property.  Let $I_1,\ldots,I_m$ be finite index sets, set
$\cA_\nu=\ell_2(I_\nu)$, let $\cC,\cE$ be finite-dimensional Hilbert
spaces, and let
\[
  (K_{i_1\cdots i_m})_{(i_1,\ldots,i_m)\in I_1\times\cdots\times I_m},
  \qquad K_{i_1\cdots i_m}\in\cL(\cC,\cE),
\]
be a finite operator-valued coefficient array.  Let
$(a_i^{(\nu)})_{i\in I_\nu}$ denote the standard basis of $\cA_\nu$ and set
\begin{equation*}
  K:=\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_m}
  a_{i_1}^{(1)}\otimes\cdots\otimes a_{i_m}^{(m)}
  \otimes\Theta_{\cC,\cE}^{-1}(K_{i_1\cdots i_m}).
\end{equation*}
Let
\[
  \{g_i^{(\nu)}:\nu\in[m],\ i\in I_\nu\}
\]
be jointly independent standard real Gaussian variables, or jointly
independent standard circular complex Gaussian variables, and define
\begin{equation*}
  \cT_K^{(m)}
  :=\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_m}
  g^{(1)}_{i_1}\cdots g^{(m)}_{i_m}K_{i_1\cdots i_m}
  \in\cL(\cC,\cE).
\end{equation*}
Then, for all \(p\ge2\) and \(2\le r<\infty\),
\begin{equation}

  \|\cT_K^{(m)}\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r(\cC,\cE))}
  \le C_m(p+r)^{m/2}
  \max_{S\subset[m]}\|\Flat_S(K)\|_{\Sch_r}.
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation*}
  \|\cT_K^{(m)}\|_{L^p(\Omega;\cL(\cC,\cE))}
  \le C_m(p+r)^{m/2}\Prof_{m,r}(K),
\end{equation*}
and hence
\begin{equation*}
  \|\cT_K^{(m)}\|_{L^p(\Omega;\cL(\cC,\cE))}
  \le C_m\inf_{2\le s<\infty}(p+s)^{m/2}\Prof_{m,s}(K).
\end{equation*}
Moreover, one may take $C_m\le C_0^m$ for an absolute constant $C_0\ge1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.281343+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9aa72b164ef6a6536d44de70b112778110567c1b3adab82afcc2dfacac86d871","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 of arXiv:2606.22611, 'Upper and Lower Bounds on Expected Soft Maxima\\of Gaussian Processes'] For any $\mu \in \eP(T)$,
