In the case $f=0$,  $F^{0,0}_T=F^0_T$ and we write $\mathbb F^{0,0}_T=\mathbb F^0_T$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:25:44.970093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b23a8f23be3d2ad8d0b15385cc349834c9a3498a6743eec7662c3501763aa45","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 21 (Threshold form) of arXiv:2605.11451, '2605.11451'] Define
\begin{equation}
 R_{p,n}:=\frac{m_{4,p,n}}{v_{p,n}^2}
 =
 \frac{\Gamma(5/p)\Gamma(1/p)}{\Gamma(3/p)^2}
 \frac{\Gamma(1+(n+2)/p)^2}
 {\Gamma(1+n/p)\Gamma(1+(n+4)/p)}.
\end{equation}
Then $\Phi_{p,n}$ is nonmonotone if $R_{p,n}<3$ and strictly decreasing if $R_{p,n}\ge3$.
For every fixed $1\le p<2$, the sequence $n\mapsto R_{p,n}$ is strictly increasing and has limit
\begin{equation}
 R_{p,\infty}=\frac{\Gamma(5/p)\Gamma(1/p)}{\Gamma(3/p)^2}>3.
\end{equation}
Consequently there is a finite threshold
\[
 N(p):=\min\{n\ge2:R_{p,n}\ge3\}
\]
such that $\Phi_{p,n}$ is nonmonotone for $n<N(p)$ and strictly decreasing for $n\ge N(p)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.437926+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b2555e9b6d79de5a08452f2f17799c963d2849fffcebd98cce5185a900bb6b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $p\in \mathscr{C}^{0,\varrho}_{\mathrm{pw}}S_d$ and $\mathscr{U}_p$ be given by \eqref{Ensemble des singularités}. Assume that $p$ satisfies Assumptions \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - volume singularités} and \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - volume préimage}. Let $Q_N$ be an $N^d\times N^d$ random matrix satisfying Assumption \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - matrice aléatoire} for some $\kappa_3>0$. Then, for every $\delta_0>0$, setting 
    \begin{equation}
        \delta:=N^{-(\kappa_3+\delta_0)},

    \end{equation}
     the empirical spectral measure 
     \begin{equation}
