\end{align}
where $x>0$, $y>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.771674+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b627cff22ef32ebb7f2f44b7ee3c541ade48233e61ce644ff0355df90e62fe5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin Modified admissibility of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] For $d\geq1$ and $s\in\{0,1,\dots,d-1\}$, we call $(d,s)$-\textit{admissible} the $(d,s)$-non-degenerate weight pairs $({\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}})$ for which the random compositions $(C_{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$  with distributions $(\PComp{{\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}}}{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$ can be coupled in such a way that
	\begin{align*}
	C_s\covered[d] C_{d+s}\covered[d] C_{2d+s}\covered[d] \dots\covered[d] C_{nd+s}\covered[d] C_{(n+1)d+s}\covered[d]\dots.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.424050+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b645a0071aa9f8576f2a82337bb4187070fe6518293d6b689b6cdbc0266026a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def2 13 of arXiv:2508.02416, 'On the surjectivity of the conditional expectation\\ given a real random variable'] \begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) is called an accumulation point of \( {\mathcal X} \) if for every \(\varepsilon > 0\), the interval \((x-\varepsilon, x+\varepsilon)\)  contains infinitely many points of \( {\mathcal X}\).

\item[(ii)] \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) is called a right accumulation point of \( {\mathcal X} \) if for every \(\varepsilon > 0\), the interval \([x, x+\varepsilon)\) contains infinitely many points of \( {\mathcal X} \).

\item[(iii)] \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) is called a left accumulation point of \( {\mathcal X} \) if for every \(\varepsilon > 0\), the interval \((x-\varepsilon, x]\) contains infinitely many points of \( {\mathcal X} \).

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:01:56.345155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b68cd0e84ee8ede8fb753b1e59920a60070f4ce76bd46e674240c4af7822115","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor dominant-height-exists of arXiv:2609.01499, 'On the rigidity of sloped height functions in $d\ge 3$ \\ and non-crossing surfaces'] For any $C$ large, if $\mu_\infty^\nabla$ satisfies~(equation eq:exp-suppressed-gradients-condition of the paper) and $\min_{j=1,2} V_j(1)= \beta \ge \beta_0$ is sufficiently large, we have that for all large $L$ and all $\vec{k}\in \mathbb Z^{d-2}$, that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mu_\infty^\nabla \Big(|\{x\in [-L,L]^2 \times \{\vec{k}\}: \varphi_x \ne h(\mathcal L_{\vec{k}}) \}| \ge \frac{1}{C} 4L^2\Big) \le L^{- C}\,.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.852635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b69b17d56d34eaeba3272a61e57245bbeb67c147601f81f1d46fe0eaab356e4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Fix \(E\in(-2,2)\) and, for each \(N\), let \(i_N(E):=\min\{k:\,\lambda_k^{(N)}\ge E\sqrt{N}\}\).
For fixed offsets \(u\in\mathbb{Z}\), set \(i:=i_N(E)+u\).
Assume that the random family \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(N)},\,g_{j}^{(N)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) converges almost surely, as \(N\to\infty\), to \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(0)},\,g_{j}^{(0)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\).
Then, almost surely,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\left(\frac{\Delta_{i,i}^{(N)}}{\upsilon_{i}^{(N)}}\right)^{2}
=
\bigl(\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_i^{(0)}\bigr)^{2}
\sum_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}
\left(\frac{|g_j^{(0)}|}{\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_j^{(0)}}\right)^{2},
\end{equation}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:as-convergence}, the variables \(\{g_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) are independent Gaussian random variables (independent of \(\{\mu_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\)). \(\mu_i^{(1)}\) denotes the \(i\)-th solution \(z\) of the secular equation
\(
S_{\mu^{(0)},\,(g^{(0)})^{2}}(z)\;=\;-\frac{E}{2\sqrt{4-E^{2}}}\,.
\) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.530203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b6cbab4bc77aa46e4b171410ac33d4608a7c44961403f86d3beb89a7359fbd0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def.Meyers of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given three constants ${\varepsilon \in (0,1)}$, ${\mathsf{r} > \frac{4}{3}}$, ${C \in (0, \infty)}$, we denote by $\mathcal{M}(\mathbf{a}, \mathsf{r}, \varepsilon, C) \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ the collection of cubes ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$ satisfying the following property under $\mathbf{a}$
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}, \frac{4}{3}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}, \mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$ are very-well connected;
			\item For every cube ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \supseteq {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}$ and function $v: \mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying 
			\begin{equation}
				\left\{
				\begin{array}{cc}
					-\nabla \cdot (\mathbf{a} \nabla v) = -\nabla\cdot \mathbf{w}  &\text{ in }  {\mathrm{int} \mathscr{C}_{**}(\mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}')}, \\
					v = 0 & \text{ on } \mathsf{r} \cap \partial {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'.
				\end{array}
				\right.
			\end{equation}
			with  $\mathbf{w}$ an anti-symmetric vector field supported on $\overrightarrow{E_d^{\mathbf{a}}}$, one has the estimate
			\begin{multline}

				\left(\frac{1}{\left|\frac{4}{3}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}\right|} \sum_{e\in \mathcal{E}(\mathscr{C}_{**}(\frac{4}{3} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'))}|\nabla v(e)|^{2+\varepsilon}\right)^{\frac{1}{2+\varepsilon}} \\
				\leqslant C\left(\frac{1}{\left|\mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}\right|} \sum_{e\in \mathcal{E}(\mathscr{C}_{**}(\mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}'))}|\nabla v(e)|^2\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+C\left(\frac{1}{\left|\mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}\right|} \sum_{e\in \mathcal{E}(\mathscr{C}_{**}(\mathsf{r} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}} \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}')}|\mathbf{w}(e)|^{2+\varepsilon}\right)^{\frac{1}{2+\varepsilon}}.
			\end{multline}
		\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.225005+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b6e38ab282cc6d01ca6a10bd06d29e5ca4412f4636a8ad683ab791a08ca03d7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2512.06640, 'Existence and sharpness of the phase transition for the frog model on transitive graphs'] A graph $G=(V,E)$ satisfies the \textbf{volume doubling} condition if there exists $C > 0$ such that for all $x\in V$ and $n\geq1:$
