{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.866750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b713203d5d7a9a1997de79ed7ed3ff68f330325bbabf723dc9488868a998c9a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.26834, 'Small-time annealed large deviations principle for one-dimensional diffusions in a random '] Under assumption $H_1$, for each $\theta \in (0,1)$, there exist deterministic constants
		\[
		C=C(\theta)>0,\qquad N=N(\theta)>0,
		\]
		such that for $\Pbb$-a.e. $W$, one has for any $0<t<1, \ r>0$ and $ x \in \R$
		\begin{equation}
			P_x^W(\tau_{D_W(x,r)}\le t)
			\le C t^{-N}\exp\left(C M(D_W(x,r)) \right)
			\exp\left\{-a_\theta\frac{r^2}{t}\right\},
		\end{equation}
		where the constant $M(D_W(x,r))$ was defined in (\ref{01}) and 
		\begin{equation*}
			a_\theta=\frac{(1-\theta)^2}{2(1+\theta)}.
		\end{equation*}
		In particular, $a_\theta\uparrow 1/2$ as $\theta\downarrow0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.534413+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b73aec6ead0cb1ab0d9d7fe01641114388f6929fd3ac4f4c82450bb63073e23","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.center_cube of arXiv:2506.07158, 'The diffusivity of supercritical Bernoulli percolation is infinitely differentiable'] Given $\mathsf{r} \in (\frac{50}{27}, 2)$, then every ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m \in \mathcal{G}^{(N)}_k$ is $N$-$\mathsf{stable}$ for the following property: 



		for every ${\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \in \mathcal{P}^{(N,k)}_{h}({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m)$ with $0 \leqslant h \leqslant k-1$, we have
		\begin{align}
			\forall x \in \frac{16}{15} {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \cap \left({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m \setminus \mathscr{C}_{\operatorname{bc}}^\#({\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m)\right),  \qquad  [x]^\# \in \mathscr{C}_{**}\left(\mathsf{r}{\scaleobj{1}{\square}}' \cap {\scaleobj{1}{\square}}_m\right).
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.547576+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b73e8403c4dd0aee2189fcfffd9935c7395c67ca38c5a102bf3c48470beb98e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 15 ($(\beta-\slackmeasure,\sigma)$-local consistency of a retail set) of arXiv:2609.01397, 'Measuring consistency via ensemble margin and local prediction variability: Auditing decis'] Given a Rashomon \smallset{} set $\retailset = \{f_k :  k \in [\Nretail]\} \subseteq \emprashomonset$ of size $\Nretail$, a set of perturbed samples $\localtestmat = \{\localtest_{j}: j \in [\nogaussianpts]\} \subseteq \ltwohsphere(x,\sigma)$, the ensemble prediction $\ensmretset(\retailset,\localtestmat)$ in Definition \ref{def: local ensemble}, and a constant $\slackmeasure >0$, let  $ \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat,x) $ be defined as
\begin{align}
    \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat, x) = \babs{\ensmretset(\retailset,\localtestmat)-\frac{1}{2}} - \frac{1}{\nogaussianpts}\sum_{j=1}^{\nogaussianpts}\frac{1}{\Nretail}\sum_{j=1}^{\Nretail}\babs{f_k(x) - f_k(\localtest_j)}.
\end{align} 
for a fixed test instance $x$. Then, $\retailset$ is said to be $(\beta-\slackmeasure,\sigma)$-locally consistent if,
\begin{align}
    \localmarginconsistency(\retailset,\localtestmat,x) \ge \beta-\slackmeasure
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:28.094415+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b77158b2675ee930cabb0270297113a15fad1ea905b56a593ccf7b410dc33fa","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 33 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] Let $(X_t,\eta_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the unique solution to \eqref{eq:4.28} in the sense of  Definition \ref{Defi 317}. Then, $\mathbb{P}\mathrm{-a.s.}$, for almost every $t > 0$, the law ${\mu_t}$ of ${\bf x} \in {\mathbb S} \mapsto X_t({\bf x})$ (with ${\mathbb S}$ equipped with $\textrm{\rm Leb}_{\mathbb S}$) has a square integrable density $p_t(\cdot)$. {For all $T >0$,} it satisfies
\begin{align}

\mathbb{E}\int_0^T\int_{\mathbb{R}}{|p_{t}(z)|^2}\mathrm{d}z\mathrm{d}t < + \infty. 

\end{align}
Moreover, 
{${\mathbb P}$-almost surely and for almost every $t > 0$},
the density $p_t$ is supported by 
$[X_t(0),X_t(1/2)]$ and is almost everywhere positive on 
$(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$.
{For $\textrm{\rm Leb}_{\mathbb R}$-almost every $z \in {\mathbb R}$, $X_t$ is differentiable at $\tilde{F}_{\mu_t}(z)$, where $\tilde{F}_{\mu_t}$ is the cumulative distribution function of $\mu_t$, and for almost every 
$z \in (X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$,
\begin{equation}

D_{\mathbf x} X_t \bigl(\tfrac12 \tilde F_{\mu_t}(z) \bigr) = \frac1{2 p_t(z)}.
\end{equation}
In particular, 
for all $T>0$, 
\begin{equation}
{\mathbb E}
\int_0^T \int_{\mathbb R}
{\mathbbm 1}_{(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))}(z)
\frac1{p_t(z)}
\ud z  \ud t =
\frac14 {\mathbb E}
\int_0^T \int_{\mathbb S}
\vert D_{\mathbf{x}}
X_t({\mathbf x}) \vert^2
\ud {\mathbf x}  \ud t
< + \infty.

\end{equation}
Lastly, 
${\mathbb P}$-almost surely and for almost every $t>0$, 
$X_t(\cdot /2)$ is a continuous one-to-one mapping from $(0,1)$ onto $(X_t(0),X_t(1/2))$, with $\tilde F_{\mu_t}$ as inverse. 
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.014060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b799d0bfd101f8632a984fd52775e210c32c97a388d16d0d2968764daaec3c8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Z-wellposed of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] Let Assumptions (ass:LR of the paper), (ass:diff2 of the paper) and~(ass:SC of the paper) hold, let $0 \leq s \leq T$ and $r \in [0,T]$, and let $\mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v$ denote the solution of (equation eq:second-var of the paper)--(equation eq:Z-initial-cond of the paper) in the direction $a \in U$. Then for every $v \in H$ and every $a \in U$ this solution exists, is unique in $L^2(\Omega; C([\max(s,r),T]; H))$, and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\sup_{\max(s,r) \leq \tau \leq t} \norm{\mathcal{Z}^{a}(\tau, s; r)v}_H^2\Bigr] \;\leq\; C\,\lVert v \rVert_H^2\,\lVert a \rVert_U^2 ,
\end{equation}
the constant depending on $T, r, s$ and on the constants of \textup{(SC1)}--\textup{(SC3)}. The directions assemble, $\mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)v = (a \mapsto \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v)$ lying in $L^2(\Omega; C([\max(s,r),T]; L_2(U,H)))$ with
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\Bigl[\sup_{\max(s,r) \leq \tau \leq t} \norm{\mathcal{Z}(\tau,s;r)v}_{L_2(U,H)}^2\Bigr] \;\leq\; C\,\lVert v \rVert_H^2 ,
\end{equation}
independently of the basis of $U$, and $(v,a) \mapsto \mathcal{Z}^{a}(t,s;r)v$ is bilinear. In the $r < s$ branch of (equation eq:Z-initial-cond of the paper) this holds for every $r$; in the $s \leq r$ branch, for every $r$ in Regime~A, where $\mathcal{B}'_u$ extends to $H$, and for almost every $r > s$ in Regime~B, where it acts only on $V$ (Remark~(rem:SC-raised-two-regimes of the paper)).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.711915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b7d83b90570a68d48bd5abccb1c4d271ae06901b2bb2511f8a3d4d6adc04d46","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.expdecay of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let $Q_1, Q_2$ be two marking functions on finite marked point processes. Let $\tilde{\mathcal{P}}$ be a marked point process such that the ground process $\mathcal{P}$ satisfies the Palm correlation bound~(equation e:bd_palmcorr of the paper) for $m =1$.
 Suppose for $p\in\mathbb{N}$, $i=1,2$ that there are   fast decreasing functions $\varphi_{i,p}$ as in (equation varphibd of the paper) such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p \in W_n} \mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}
\bigl(Q_i(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)>r\bigr) \le \varphi_{i,p}(r),\quad r > 0.
\end{equation}
 Then 
for all $p \in \mathbb{N}$ there are fast decreasing functions ~$\tilde\varphi_p$ such that
$$
\sup_{1 \leq n < \infty} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p\in W_n}\mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p}\left( \max_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n :  |x - x_1| \leq Q_1(\tilde{x}_1, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n)} Q_2(\tilde{x}, \tilde{\mathcal{P}}_n) > r \right) \leq   \tilde{\varphi}_p(r), \quad r > 0.
$$
If the assumption (equation e:Q12tails of the paper) holds with the supremum taken up to $n = \infty$, i.e., $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$, then the conclusion  remains valid with  $\sup_{1 \le n \le \infty}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.805831+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b7dffbbd184e9e19c2ad5c8b89755094b5456b4b055e06fb97c1c9284edbdf4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma L:Int-16 of arXiv:2606.23865, 'Uniform-in-time Gaussian fluctuations for multiscale nonlinear stochastic systems via Mall'] For $0 \le u \le s \le w \le r \le t$, let the term $\mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta)$ be defined as follows:
\begin{multline*}
\mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta)  \triangleq K \int_{u=0}^t \int_{s=u}^t \int_{w=s}^t \int_{r=w}^t  e^{-2 \alpha (t-r)} e^{- \alpha(t-s)} e^{- \alpha(t-w)}   e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (r-u)} e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (s-u)} e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (r-w)} \\
\times e^{-\frac{\lambda}{\eta} (w-s)}     dr \thinspace dw \thinspace ds \thinspace du.
\end{multline*}
Then, there exists a time-independent positive constant $K$ such that
$$ \mathcal{J}_{16}(t;\varepsilon,\eta) \le K \eta^3.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.732393+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b814bf5fc3fe7d94815fa74e266ecc3e4162a86b7af1a30bd1ca951d3c0d7de","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma starconvltnbnd of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] If $\beta > 0$,  $c\ge 0$ and $a \ge 0$,  then for $t_1 + t_2 = t$,  
\begin{align}
	\Big(\frac{t_1^{2\beta-1}}{1 + a t_1^{2\beta}} e^{-c(\lambda t_1^2)^{1/4}} \Big)
	\Big(\frac{t_2^{2\beta-1}}{1 + a t_2^{2\beta}} e^{-c(\lambda t_2^2)^{1/4}} \Big) \le 
		\frac{1}{2^{2\beta -2}} \Big(\frac{t^{2\beta-1}}{1 + a t^{2\beta}} e^{-c(\lambda t^2)^{1/4}} \Big) .
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.011675+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b8213c83c2f996be7059358a21572821aaeb1dfd9bf9840dc17f1868bcbb6ef","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 (Gaussian concentration) of arXiv:2602.14618, 'Finitary coding and Gaussian concentration\\ for random fields'] Let $d\geq 1$ and $Y=(Y_i)_{i\in\Zd}$ be a $B$-valued random field where $B$ is a standard Borel space. Then $Y$ satisfies a Gaussian concentration bound if there exists a constant $C>0$ such
that, for any local function $f:B^{\Zd}\to\R$ with the bounded-difference property, and for any $\lambda>0$,  one has
\begin{equation}
\log\E\big(\e^{\lambda (f(Y)-\E[f(Y)])}\big)\leq \frac{\sC}{2} \lambda^2 \|\delta f\|_2^2\,.    
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.643041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b82923914f6b0132eaf56ca0c926ffd85317caf8d0c18b7146b01e8bdcd5688","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 11 (Oscillation-only work) of arXiv:2608.30715, 'Endpoint Energy Atoms Force Local Pressure Concentration in Three-Dimensional Navier--Stok'] The relative pressure-work identity can be written as
\begin{equation}
 \frac12\norm{r_\tau(t)}_2^2
 +\nu\int_\tau^t\norm{\nabla r_\tau}_2^2\dd\rho
 =\int_\tau^t
 \ip{u}{\mathfrak h_\tau}_{\BMO,\Hh^1}\dd\rho.

\end{equation}
If \(\Aa_a[w]\ge m\), then
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{t\uparrow T_*}
 \int_\tau^t
 \ip{u}{\mathfrak h_\tau}_{\BMO,\Hh^1}\dd\rho
 \ge\frac m2.

\end{equation}
The right-hand pairing is unchanged when its BMO representative is altered
by a spatially constant velocity.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.999011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b83791738f9c1bd5c6bb085644d84c9ac66b1bf489c86b0dd2f548c7350807f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:polynomial_decay of arXiv:2604.17253, 'Large Deviations for the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation with Randomized Quasi-Periodic Ini'] Assume that the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{(Related to initial data in Fourier space)} 
Let $\rho$ and $\kappa$ be as in (equation eq:decay of the paper) and (thm:poly of the paper), respectively. Assume that
\[
\rho_{j,j'} - \kappa_{j,j'} > 2, \quad j=1,\dots,d,\; j'=1,\dots,\nu_j.
\]

\item \textbf{(Size of initial data with respect to $\varepsilon$)} 
Let $\omega \notin \Omega_{\delta}$ with $\delta\sim\varepsilon^{1+\eta}$, where
\begin{equation}
0 \le \eta < \min\left\{ 2\Bigl(\frac{1}{\nu}-\mu_1\Bigr)\eta_2-\mu_2,\; \eta_3\right\}. 
\end{equation}
Here $0<\mu_1 \ll 1/\nu, 0<\mu_2 \ll 1$, $\eta_2$ and $\eta_3$ are given by
\begin{align}
\eta_2 &= \sum_{j=1}^{d} \sum_{j'=1}^{\nu_j}
\bigl(\rho_{j,j'} - \kappa_{j,j'} - 1\bigr), \\
\eta_3 &= \frac{2}{3}(\alpha-1)-\frac{\mu_3}{3}, 
\quad 0<\mu_3 \ll 1.

\end{align}
\end{itemize}
 Then the randomized Fourier coefficients satisfy the following almost sure polynomial decay estimates:
\begin{align}
\bigl|c(\boldsymbol{n}) g_{\boldsymbol{n}}(\omega)\bigr|
\le \varepsilon^{-\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\eta}{2}} \,\tnorm{\boldsymbol{n}}_{-(\rho-\kappa)},\quad\omega\notin\Omega_\delta,
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
\rho - \kappa 
= \bigl( \rho_{1,1}-\kappa_{1,1}, \dots, \rho_{1,\nu_1}-\kappa_{1,\nu_1}; \dots; \rho_{d,1}-\kappa_{d,1}, \dots, \rho_{d,\nu_d}-\kappa_{d,\nu_d} \bigr).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.955792+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b8586954c0d9626a153204c711d07728a7268624f1653fded880e8fedf8db70","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:prim of arXiv:2608.23135, 'Graph distance and effective resistance of the random walk trace in four and five dimensio'] For every $q>1$ there is $C=C_{\varepsilon,q}<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}

\mathbb{P}\Bigl(\exists (j,k)\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{RW}}:
S(J_j^{\rm R}(q))\cap S(J_k)\ne\emptyset\Bigr)
\le C\delta^{-1}|\log\delta|(\log n)^{-(q-1)}.
\end{equation}
The same bound holds with $J_j^{\rm R}(q)$ replaced by any of
$J_j^{\rm L}(q)$, $J_k^{\rm L}(q)$, or $J_k^{\rm R}(q)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.725215+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b8637e62d9b3761fd13e6ce3d1a8a2f1777bc1278089008f7a06fe376650afe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2609.02732, 'Conservation of mass for solutions of Leibenson's equation on Riemannian Manifolds'] \normalfont
		We say that a non-negative function $u=u(x, t)$ is a \textit{weak
			subsolution} of (\ref{dtv}) in $\Omega\times I$, if 
		\begin{equation}  
			u\in C\left(I; L^{q+1}(\Omega)\right)\quad \textnormal{and}\quad 
			u^{q}\in L_{loc}^{ p}\left(I; W^{1, p}(\Omega)\right),
		\end{equation} where (\ref{dtv}) holds weakly in $\Omega\times I$, which means that for all $t_{1}, t_{2}\in I$ with $t_{1}<t_{2}$, and all non-negative \textit{test functions} 
		\begin{equation}  
			\psi\in W_{loc}^{1, 1+\frac{1}{q}}\left(I;
			L^{1+\frac{1}{q}}(\Omega)\right)\cap L_{loc}^{p}\left(I; W_{0}^{1,
				p}(\Omega)\right),
		\end{equation}
		we have 
		\begin{equation}  
			\left[\int_{\Omega}{u\psi }\right]_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}+\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}{
				\int_{\Omega}{-u\partial_{t}\psi+|\nabla u^{q}|^{p-2}\langle\nabla u^{q},
					\nabla \psi\rangle}}\leq 0.
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.585603+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b86cae33ed47c9f700cd746f7f89d6fbb3276b00acb922e6e11bf2c232f24a8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Th25 of arXiv:2411.09949, '$W_{\bf d}$-convergence rate of EM schemes for invariant measures of supercritical stable '] Let $\alpha\in(\frac12,2)$. Under $(\mathbf{H}^\gamma)$ and (equation 926-2 of the paper),
