{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.491921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b926a5112c9bf67fb1ccfc77c946222bf8acddc6f84b2910cce70b6beed26aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mainthm 1 of arXiv:2609.02576, 'Sharp estimates for the Jacobi and trigonometric Dunkl heat kernels'] Let $k_1,k_2\geq 0$ be such that $k_1+k_2>0$. The $W$-invariant Opdam--Cherednik heat kernel satisfies the bound
	\begin{equation*}
		\hOC_t(x,y)\simeq t^{-1/2} e^{-\frac{(x-y)^2}{4t}} e^{-\rho^2 t} e^{-\rho(x+y)} (1+x)(1+y) \frac{(t+1+x+y+xy)^{k_1+k_2-1}}{(t+xy)^{k_1+k_2}},
	\end{equation*}
	uniformly in $t>0$ and $x,y>0$, where $\rho=k_1/2+k_2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:26.998378+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b9cc4224bad39a805b369901946923ceff5b1aa12b3392638116121997fc489","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem-explengthbrige of arXiv:2604.14318, '\large The free energy of the interacting Bose gas:\\\medskip a variational description \\'] For any $M\in(0,\infty)$, there is a constant $C\in(0,\infty)$ (depending on $d$, $\beta$ and $M$) such that, for any $R\in\mathbb{N}$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{n\in\mathbb{N}}n p_{y,x}^{{{\scriptscriptstyle{({W'}})}}}(n)\leq C |x-y|,\qquad y\in W',x\in (W')^{\rm c}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.730770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b9e0afecee58c769d2c5c7f10a3ca1f178b536fd5c5eeb46ca39d9c3e97ddd2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thmintro rationalfilling of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Suppose $\bm{s}\in \mathbb{Q}^n$. The multislope $\bm{s}$ is realized by a $\Phi$-horizontal foliation with linear boundary type if and only if one of the following holds:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\bm{s}\in \interior \mathcal{Z}(\Phi)$, or
        \item $\Phi$ is a suspension flow of a fibration with compact planar fibers with multislope $\bm{s}$.
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.238311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b9f70728881327c1ad9641751a630fe80a1b18b47e1eba378bf4431540d0ada","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 41 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] The function $(y,t) \mapsto \hfixp(\hfixp_0;y,t)$ in Definition~\ref{def:PeriodicKPZfixed} is (up to scaling the spatial coordinate) a periodic KPZ fixed point from Theorem ~\ref{thm:BaikPKPZ}  with initial data $\hfixp_0 \in \UCp$, i.e. 
\begin{equation}
      \hfixp\big(\hfixp_0;y,\tau\big) \overset{(d)}{=} \mathcal{H}^{\per}_2(\hfixpt_0;2y,\tau)
\end{equation}
jointly over $y\in \mathbb{T}$ and $\tau\geq 0$.
Here, we set $\hfixpt_0(x) := \hfixp_0(2x)$ for all $x\in \mathbb{T}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.497602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ba8216d58115f7009e0ba33cfa1678e091ee159cb9774f758410e291859c3c2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 30 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Let $\upsilon_1,\dots,\upsilon_N\in\mathcal A_2$ and
$|\varepsilon_j|\le\varepsilon_0$ where $\varepsilon_0$ is from \Cref{lem:rev-one-pulse-blocks}, and set
$G_j=I_{E_1}+\varepsilon_jR_{\upsilon_j}$.
Let $x\in H$ satisfy $\Pi_1x\ne0$, and suppose that
\begin{align}
 d_{\PP(E_1)}
 \bigl([G_N\cdots G_1\Pi_1x],[\phi]\bigr)<\eta/4.

\end{align}
If $\sum_{j=1}^N\varepsilon_j^2$ is sufficiently small, then there are
finite relaxation times $L_0,\dots,L_N$ such that, with
\begin{align*}
 x_0=D_{L_0}x,\qquad
 x_{j+1}
 =D_{L_{j+1}}V_{\varepsilon_{j+1},\upsilon_{j+1}}x_j,
 \qquad 0\le j<N,
\end{align*}
where $V_{\varepsilon_{j+1},\upsilon_{j+1}}$ is given as in \eqref{eq:veup}, one has
\begin{align*}
 \Pi_1x_j\ne0,\qquad 0\le j\le N,
 \qquad
 d_{\PP(H)}([x_N],[\phi])<\eta.
\end{align*}
The corresponding base pieces form a smooth zero to zero loop of finite
Cameron--Martin energy.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.686284+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ba962ef9b519b509e8bc99edaeade830053a7670374e533bd7e7f6b7122552f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Phasethm t:2b of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] Uniformly in $s \in \mathbb{R}$ on compacts, $(r_n, n-r_n)$-clustered sets ${\rm A} \subset {\rm D}^\circ_n$ and subsets ${\rm W} \subseteq {\rm D}$,
\begin{equation}

\bigg| \mathbb{P} \big( \widecheck{T}_{\rm A} \leq t_n^B + sn\,,\,\, \widecheck{X}_{\rm A} \in {\rm e}^{n} {\rm W} \big) -
	\exp \Big(-{\rm e}^{-(s-\log \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \|{\bf \Xi}_{{\rm A}, r_ n}\|)}\Big){\bf \Xi}^\circ_{{\rm A}, r_n}({\rm W}) \bigg| \underset{n \to \infty} \longrightarrow 0 \,.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for any fixed $s \in \mathbb{R}$, if $n$ is large enough, then, uniformly in all ${\rm A} \subset {\rm D}_n$ (not necessarily clustered),
\begin{equation}

\mathbb{P} \big( \widecheck{T}_{{\rm A}} > t_n^{B} + sn \big) \leq 2 {\rm e}^{-s}\frac{|{\rm A}|}{\sqrt{n}} \,.
\end{equation}
Lastly, for any fixed $s \in \mathbb{R}$,
\begin{equation}
	\frac{{\mathbf T}_{n,t_n^B+sn}/|{\rm D}_n| - (t_n^B+s n)}{n} \overset{\mathbb{P}}{\underset{n \to \infty}\longrightarrow} 0 \,.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:58:24.512406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9baadb8cbad82aa1215cd9a7783deaa74ceca7348bda2ade94abe9545fc96483","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lmlboundekk of arXiv:2601.09537, 'Gene genealogies in haploid populations evolving according to sweepstakes reproduction'] Suppose a population evolves according to Def.\ (hapltwo of the paper).  Then there is a constant $B > 0$ with
\begin{esplit}{eglboundekk}
 \frac{(N)_2}{c_N} \EE{ \frac{(X_1)_{k_1}(X_2)_{k_2} }{ (S_N)_{k_1+k_2} }\ensuremath{\mathds{1}_{\left\{ S_N \ge N \right\}}} | \ensuremath{{\alpha_1}}, \ensuremath{{\kappa}} }\frac{ \varepsilon_N}{(N)_2} \le  \frac{B}{N^{\ensuremath{{\alpha_1}}+\ensuremath{{\kappa}}-1 }} \varepsilon_N
\end{esplit}
for all $k_1, k_2 \ge 2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.438512+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bab704edcb60fef601e0acb03f4ad7d4378f60a2ea4cdce6699d7519fcf2972","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 9 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] We define $BS_\xi$ as the set of functions $p:\R^{2d}\to \C$ such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item for all $x\in \R^d$, $p(x,\cdot)\in \mathscr{C}^\infty(\R^d)$ satisfies: for all $\gamma\in \N^d$, there exists $C_\gamma>0$ (independent of $h$ and $x$) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \bb{\partial^\gamma_\xi p(x,\xi)}\leq C_\gamma,
        \end{equation*} 
        \item for all $x\in \R^d$, $p(x,\cdot)$ is $\Z^d$ periodic in the sense of \eqref{Def Z^d-périodicité en xi}.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.331358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bad475a85e77df5c3fda4ee630f22777e47d42230512cefd8326bcff9919973","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] Let $(\rho,\Theta)$ be the underlying MAP of $\mathcal{R}$ with respect to the norm $\|\cdot\|_{\text{L}^1}$ in dimension $d=2$. Then, $(\rho,\Theta)$ is the unique weak solution to the following SDE:
	\begin{equation*}
		d\begin{pmatrix}
			\rho_t \\ \Theta_t
		\end{pmatrix}=\boldsymbol{a}(\Theta_t) dt + \boldsymbol{b}(\Theta_t) d\boldsymbol{W}_t + \boldsymbol{\gamma}(\Theta_t) dl_t,
	\end{equation*}
	where $\boldsymbol{W}$ is $2$-dimensional Brownian motion, $l$ is local time of $\Theta$ at the boundary, $\{(0,1),(1,0)\}$, of $\mathcal{S}^{2,+}_{1}$, and for $\boldsymbol{\theta}=(\theta_1,\theta_2)\in\mathcal{S}^{2,+}_{1}$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\boldsymbol{a}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq \begin{pmatrix}
			-1 \\ \theta_1-\theta_2 \\ \theta_2-\theta_1
		\end{pmatrix},\qquad \boldsymbol{\gamma}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}
			1 \\ \mathbbm{1}_{\{(0,1)\}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})-\mathbbm{1}_{\{(1,0)\}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) \\ \mathbbm{1}_{\{(1,0)\}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})-\mathbbm{1}_{\{(0,1)\}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})
		\end{pmatrix},
	\end{equation*}
	and
	\begin{equation*}
		\boldsymbol{b}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}
			\frac{2\theta_1\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}}+\frac{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}}{2} & -\frac{\theta_1-\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}} \\
			-\frac{\theta_1-\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}} & \frac{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}}{4}-\frac{\theta_1\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}} \\
			\frac{\theta_1-\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}} & \frac{\theta_1\theta_2}{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}} - \frac{\sqrt{\lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})}+\sqrt{\lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})}}{4}
		\end{pmatrix},
	\end{equation*}
	where
	\begin{equation*}
		  \lambda_2(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq1+\theta_1^2+\theta_2^2+\sqrt{(1+\theta_1^2+\theta_2^2)^2-2},\qquad \lambda_3(\boldsymbol{\theta})\coloneqq1+\theta_1^2+\theta_2^2-\sqrt{(1+\theta_1^2+\theta_2^2)^2-2}.
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:23:37.859190+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9baf59d632c4663385bfb5702efa021d4805d4bc51748096475fcd76caa8e394","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 24 of arXiv:2609.03647, 'Extension Problems for the Vladimirov--Taibleson Operator and the Hierarchical Laplacian'] For every real-valued $f\in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{Q}_p^n)$, problem \eqref{eq:mass_square_term_extension} has a unique solution in this class. For $\boldsymbol{\xi}\neq\boldsymbol{0}$, its Fourier transform is
\begin{align*}
    \hat{\varphi}_{k}(\boldsymbol{\xi})=\begin{cases}
        \hat{f}(\boldsymbol{\xi})\left(p^{(\alpha-n)k}-p^{n-2\alpha}\|\boldsymbol{\xi}\|_p^{2\alpha-n}p^{-\alpha k}\right),&k\geq-v_p(\boldsymbol{\xi});\\
        0,&k<-v_p(\boldsymbol{\xi}).
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
The value at the zero frequency $\boldsymbol{\xi}=\boldsymbol{0}$ may be
taken from the same multiplier formula and does not affect the $L^2$
solution. Moreover, the renormalized boundary limit holds uniformly for
the canonical representatives of the layer functions.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.314133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bb10f188fa1c01b7917df3ff6ee07a15c5af8d68765b6c264e81a8d144d41ff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Tauberian of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let $X$ be a non-negative integer-valued random variable. Define the characteristic function $\varphi(t) = \mathbbm E[e^{itX}]$. Consider the following two sets of assumptions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For some $c \in \mathbbm C \setminus i \mathbbm R$ and $\nu \in (0,1)$,  
\begin{equation} 
\varphi(t) = 1 + (c \mathbbm 1_{(t > 0)} + \overline c \mathbbm 1_{(t < 0)} ) |t|^\nu  + o(t^\nu)  ,\quad \text{as $t \to 0$}.
\end{equation}
    \item For some $c_1 > 0$, $c_2 \in \mathbbm C \setminus  \mathbbm R$, and $\nu \in (0,1)$,  
\begin{equation} 
\varphi(t) = 1 + i c_1 t +     (c_2 \mathbbm 1_{(t > 0)} + \overline c_2 \mathbbm 1_{(t < 0)} ) |t|^{1+\nu}       + o(t^{1+\nu})  ,\quad \text{as $t \to 0$}. 
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item In the case of Assumption~(ass:1 of the paper), $\operatorname{Re}(c)<0$ and $\operatorname{Im}(c)>0$, and setting $a=-\frac{\operatorname{Re}(c)}{\Gamma(1-\nu)}\sec\Big(\tfrac{\pi\nu}{2}\Big)>0$,
    \begin{equation} 
\mathbbm P[X > R] = a R^{-\nu}  +  o(R^{-\nu}) ,\quad \text{as $R\to\infty$}. 
\end{equation} 
    \item In the case of Assumption~(ass:2 of the paper), $\operatorname{Im}(c_2)<0$ and setting $a=-\frac{\nu\operatorname{Im}(c_2)}{\Gamma(1-\nu)}\sec\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft(\tfrac{\pi\nu}{2}\aftergroup\egroup\originalright)>0$,
    \begin{equation} 
\mathbbm P[X > R] = a R^{-\nu-1}  +  o(R^{-\nu-1}) ,\quad \text{as $R\to\infty$}. 
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.777832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bb33141cef67d2cff88e49e6c83b7d5c129949af6bb46932686b6037740f9b5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm 5 (Switching lemma) of arXiv:2603.25536, 'The $H^{2|2}$ monotonicity theorem revisited'] Consider a smooth bounded function $F:\mathbb{R}^{N}\to\mathbb{R}$ and a $Q$-invariant action function $A$. For any $i,j,k\in\{1,\dots,N\}$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \int d\mu\,F(\bm{x}+\bm{z}) x_k\xi_i\eta_j A= -\int d\mu F(\bm{x}+\bm{z}) x_ky_iy_j A.
\end{equation*}
A further $Q$-integration by parts shows that this quantity also equals
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\int d\mu\,\partial_\ell F(\bm{x}+\bm{z}) \xi_\ell\eta_ky_iy_j A.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.437716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bb583e715f8512ecec08b34b2e7d3bce37e413787a00d3815431f3106e0c6f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm transition of arXiv:2509.26254, '$K$-Branching Random Walk with Noisy Selection: \\ \Large Large Population Limits and Phas'] Let $\beta>0$, $\gamma\in(0,1)$ and assume that $\rho=1$ in~(equation eq:defh of the paper). Then, the dynamical system~(equation eq:dynamics of the paper) admits a unique traveling wave solution $G$ (explicitly given by Proposition~(prop:unique of the paper)) with speed $\nu$. Furthermore, let
\begin{equation}
k := k(\beta) = \left\{\begin{array}{cc}
\lfloor\frac{1}{\beta}\rfloor & \mbox{if $\frac{1}{\beta}\notin\mathbb{N}$} \\ \frac{1}{\beta}-1 & \mbox{otherwise} 
\end{array}\right. \quad \text{and} \quad
 \gamma_c(\beta) :=  \frac{k(2-(k+1)\beta)}{(k+1)(2 - k\beta)}.
\end{equation}
Then the traveling wave solution exhibits the following phase transition. For a fixed value of $\beta$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] {\bf Selection of the luckiest}: If $\gamma<\gamma_{c}(\beta)$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a.1)] the speed of the wave is given by 
$$
\nu(\gamma,\beta)=\frac{2\gamma}{k(2-(k+1)\beta)}. 
$$
In particular, it is increasing in $\gamma$.
\item[(a.2)] If $g^0=G$, then  $\forall x\in \mbox{Supp}(g^1), \ \ r(g^0)(x)> g^1(x).$
\end{enumerate}
\item[(b)] {\bf Selection of the fittest}: If $\gamma \geq \gamma_{c}(\beta)$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(b.1)]  the speed of the wave is given by 
$$
\nu(\gamma,\beta)=1-\gamma.
$$
In particular, it is decreasing in $\gamma$.
\item[(b.2)] If $g^0=G$, then
$
\forall x\in [-\chi + 1-\gamma,1-\gamma], \ \ r(g^0)(x)= g^1(x) 
$ where 
\begin{equation}
\chi(\gamma,\beta) := \gamma - \sum_{j=1}^k(1-j\beta)(1-\gamma), 
\end{equation}
which is strictly positive on $(\gamma_c,1)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.083448+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9bbbcf13c6a961d1f6e68676d7ee86f004068d3f51d9b7bcc01c4a41acc6ec06","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defini 7 of arXiv:2608.27934, 'An example of biquandle and an invariant of long virtual knots defined through it'] {\it A long virtual knot diagram} is a smooth immersion $\phi : \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{2}$ such that 
