{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:40:22.404405+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9d7d95bf0b9f0a837f4bd583571702036f99a9d57cd2766d10f0725ad56b6c5e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem-le-tv of arXiv:2506.18827, 'Random walk reflected off of infinity, with applications to uniform spanning forests and s'] Let $\{\mathcal G_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ be an increasing family of connected subgraphs of $\mathcal G$ whose union is all of $\mathcal G$.
Also let $A\subset \mathcal V\mathcal G$ be a finite set of vertices and let $z \in \mathcal V\mathcal G$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Let $X$ be the random walk on $\mathcal G$ reflected off of $\infty$ started at $z$ and let $\tau$ be the smallest $t$ for which $X_t \in A$. Let $\operatorname{LE}$ be the loop erasure of $X|_{[0,\tau]}$ (Definition~(def-le-infty of the paper)). 
\item For $n\in\mathbbm N$ such that $A \cup \{z\} \subset\mathcal G_n$, let $Z^n$ be the random walk on $\mathcal G_n$ started at $z$ and let $J_n$ be the smallest $j$ such that $Z^n_j \in A$. Let $\operatorname{LE}^n$ be the loop-erasure of $Z^n|_{[0,J^n]}$ (Definition~(def-le of the paper)).
\end{itemize}
Fix $k\in \mathbbm N$ such that $A \cup \{z\} \subset\mathcal G_k$.
As $n\to\infty$, the total variation distance between the following two random variables goes to zero. The random variable which equals $\operatorname{LE}^n$ on the event $\operatorname{LE}^n \subset \mathcal G_k$ and equals $\emptyset$ otherwise; and the random variable which equals $\operatorname{LE}$ on the event that $\operatorname{LE} \subset \mathcal G_k$ and equals $\emptyset$ otherwise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.864130+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9d7e5bd9cd5b1422f59c13be89e3f00ae3b74323b2eb63f1b95c33a749ff3224","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Lower bound on relaxation time) of arXiv:2511.23453, 'Convergence rates of self-repellent random walks, their local time and Event Chain Monte C'] Let $(P_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the transition semigroup of a reversible Markov process and $(\hat P_t)_{t\geq 0}$ an arbitrary second-order lift. Then
\begin{equation*}
    t_\rel(\hat P)\ \geq \ \frac{1-e^{-1}}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{t_\rel(P)}\,.
\end{equation*}
In particular, for the self-repellent random walk,
   $$ t_{\rel}(\hat P)\ \geq \ \frac{1-e^{-1}}{2\pi} n^{3/2}  \,.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.625197+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9d833c01b4de00501718df46134eec902da4a7019d60839a7d403e4bb00c2857","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm: 0127 of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] Let $\{X_k\}_{k=1}^n$ ($n>1$ is finite) be a d-dimensional identical sequence on a sublinear expectation space $(\Omega,\mathcal{H},\hat{\mathbb{E}})$ such that $\hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X_1|^{1+\beta}]<\infty$ for some $\beta\in (0,1]$. Let $v\in C_b^{1,1+\beta}(\mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}^d)$ with some $\beta\in (0,1]$ be the solution to equation (equation PDE of the paper) with $p(a)=\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\langle a, X_1\rangle]$ and $S_n=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^nX_i$. Then
		\begin{align}
				v(1,0)-\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\phi(S_n)]&\geq -\sum_{i=2}^n\bigg|\hat{\mathbb{E}}\Bigl[v\Bigl(1-\frac{i}{n}, \frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^iX_j\Bigr)\Bigr]-\hat{\mathbb{E}}\Big[\hat{\mathbb{E}}\Bigl[v\bigl(1-\frac{i}{n}, x+\frac{X_i}{n}\bigr)\Bigr]\Big|_{x=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{i-1}X_j}\Big]\bigg|\notag\\
				&\ \ \ \ -4n^{-\beta}\int_0^1|\nabla_xv(s,\cdot)|_{\beta}ds\times \hat{\mathbb{E}}[|X_1|^{1+\beta}]+\underline{f},
		\end{align}
		where 
		$
			\underline{f}:=\inf_{\{0\leq t\leq 1,x\in \mathbb{R}^d\}}f(t,x)
	$, in which $f$ is from (equation PDE of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:23:58.622399+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9d882f7199b95cfb3587bb3d64c91b3ffd8d11888ab0f1b0a88a3767c4adf638","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 188 (The flat elliptic-hyperbolic current) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] Let $\SigmatTan$ be a $\Sigma_t$-tangent vectorfield.
	We define the characteristic current
	to be the vectorfield $\flatcurrent = \flatcurrent[\SigmatTan,\pmb{\partial} \SigmatTan]$
	with the following components, $(\alpha = 0,1,2,3)$:
	\begin{align} 
		\flatcurrent^{\alpha}[\SigmatTan,\pmb{\partial} \SigmatTan]
		& \eqdef 
		\SigmatTan^{\beta} 
		\partial_{\beta} \SigmatTan^{\alpha}
