{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.329948+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ed2dea85d3606a5f828c21c57343b67996c19157f33fde6d187b4dd09252810","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2506.22020, 'Norm-dependent Lamperti-type MAP representations of stable processes and Brownian motions '] An $E$-valued strong Markov process $Z=(Z_t)_{t\geq 0}$ defined on some (filtered) probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},(\mathcal{F}_t)_{t\geq 0},\mathbb{P})$, with probabilities $(\mathbb{P}_x, x\in E)$, is said to be a \textit{self-similar Markov process} if it satisfies the \textit{self-similarity property}: there exists an $\alpha>0$ such that for every $c>0$ and $x\in E$,
	\begin{equation}

		(\{cZ_{c^{-\alpha}t},t\geq0\},\mathbb{P}_x)\stackrel{\text{d}}{=}(\{Z_t,t\geq0\},\mathbb{P}_{cx}).
	\end{equation}
	We then say that $Z$ is a ssMp with \textit{index of self-similarity} $\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.257949+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ed2f47a10c0ab5287451ae1b6fb80027cb6de06d7c35c559e8f9f4fb6ea017c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.19308, '\textbf{On McKean--Vlasov SDEs with polynomial drifts for SIS epidemic models}'] In the setting of Example~\ref{ex:a representative SIS epidemic model} let~\eqref{a.1} be valid and~\eqref{a.2} be false and suppose that if $\eta_{0} = \frac{1}{2}$, then $g_{1,1} = 0$ or $g_{1,2} = 0$. If $u_{1},u_{2}\in\R$ satisfy~\eqref{eq:extinction in the epidemic models 4} and
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{t\uparrow\infty} c_{1,2}(t) - \frac{1}{2}\big((g_{1,1} + g_{1,2}\mathbbm{1}_{\{1\}}(\eta_{0}))^{2} + g_{2,1}^{2}\mathbbm{1}_{\{1\}}(\eta_{0})\big) \leq u_{2},
