{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.323984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9ef8a8022b4b9e7f1a7462d678c7263ea24e40290991cff5475d3b041f7ba3b4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:TransferCyl of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Assume that~$\msR\subset \msS(\mbbI)$. Then,
\[
\mathrm{em}^*u\eqdef u\circ \mathrm{em} \in \msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{} \quad \text{for every} \quad u\in \hCylP{}{0} \,\mathrm{.}
\]
\begin{proof}
Since~$\msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$ is an algebra, it suffices to show that~$\mathrm{em}^*u \in \msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$ for every~$u\in \hCylP{}{0}$ of the form~$u=(\varphi\otimes f)^\star$.
For every such~$u$, and since~$u\in \hCylP{}{0}$, there exists~$\epsilon=\epsilon_u>0$ such that~$\varphi\in\msR_\epsilon$.
Setting~$n\eqdef \left\lceil1/\epsilon\right\rceil\in {\mathbb N}$, we then have
\begin{equation}
(\varphi\otimes f)^\star\big({\mathrm{em}(\mbfs,\mbfx)}\big)= \sum_{k=1}^\infty s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)= \sum_{k=1}^n s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)
\,\mathrm{.}
\end{equation}
Since~$\msR\subset \msS(\mbbI)$, the function~$\mbfs\mapsto s_k\varphi(s_k)$ is an element of~$\msS(\mbfT)$ for every~$k$, by continuity (Borel measurability) of the coordinate projections.
Furthermore, since~$f\in\msA$, the function~$\mbfx\mapsto f(x_k)$ is an element of~$\Cyl{}{}$ for every~$k$, for the choice~$A=\left\{k\right\}$.
Thus,~$(\mbfs,\mbfx)\mapsto s_k\varphi(s_k) f(x_k)$ is an element of~$\msS(\mbfT)\otimes\Cyl{}{}$, hence so is~$u\circ \mathrm{em}$ being a finite a sum of functions therein.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

