{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.036386+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fa494bc4d2dd8d52fdd0611e699e1c3bc7d1a854cce49895d0810075b198653","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 9 of arXiv:2605.03182, 'Exponential integrability of the solution to the stochastic Burgers equation driven by whi'] With the same hypotheses and notation of Theorem \ref{theorem:apriorigamma}, suppose that we consider the random variable
\begin{equation}
	\alpha_0(\omega)=\frac{1}{\varepsilon'}\left( c_T\left({K}_{\varepsilon_1, 4}(\omega)\right)^{\left(\frac{1}{4}-\varepsilon_1-\gamma\right)^{-1}}  + 1+ F(\omega) \right),
\end{equation}
	where $0 < \varepsilon' \leq 1$ is small enough such that $C_P \delta - c_{\varepsilon} \bar{c}_T (\varepsilon')^{8 \theta_{\gamma}/3} - \frac{\varepsilon}{2} =: \delta' > 0$ (the ``upper bound'' on $\varepsilon'$ does depend only on $\gamma$ and $\varepsilon_1$) and $F:\Omega \rightarrow \R_+$ is a square integrable random variable.	

	Then there are some constants $c_{1,\varepsilon',T},c_{2,\varepsilon',T}>0$ (depending on $\varepsilon'$ and $T$ but not on $F$) and a constant $c_{\gamma, \varepsilon_1} > 0$ (depending on $\gamma$ and $\varepsilon_1$ but not on $F$) such that, for any $\omega$ $\P$-a.s.,
\begin{equation}
\| Y_t^{n,x_n,u,\alpha_0(\omega)} (\omega)\|_{L^2_{\xi}}^2 \leqslant e^{-\delta_1 t} \|x_n\|_{L^2_{\xi}}^2+ c_{1,\varepsilon',T}\Big (1+F^{2}(\omega)+({K}_{\varepsilon_1,  4}(\omega))^{\theta}+ \| u(\omega)\|_{L^2_tL^2_{\xi}}^2\Big),
\end{equation}
\vskip -6mm 
\begin{multline}  
\int_0^T\|\nabla Y_s^{n,x_n,u,\alpha_0(\omega)} (\omega)\|_{L^2_{\xi}}^2\mathd s \\
\leqslant	c_{\gamma, \varepsilon_1} \|x_n\|_{L^2_{\xi}}^2 + c_{2,\varepsilon',T} \Big(1+  F^{2}(\omega)+ (	{K}_{\varepsilon_1,  4}(\omega))^{\theta}+ \| u(\omega)\|_{L^2_tL^2_{\xi}}^2 \Big). 
\end{multline}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:45:10.083409+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fa8bd27872d72645881d0324b6da59c2a2c65f56c06deaad20002258168abae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:MartTrans of arXiv:2504.18739, 'discrete analogues of second-order Riesz transforms'] Let $A$ be a matrix-valued function with $\|A\|<\infty$. Then 
\begin{align} 
    \mathbb{E}_{(0, T)}| A\star M_T^f|^p& \leq (p^\ast -1)^p\|A\|^p\mathbb{E}_{(0, T)} |M_T^f|^p\\
    &=(p^\ast -1)^p\|A\|^p\mathbb{E}_{(0, T)}|f(X_T)|^p\nonumber\\
    &=(p^\ast -1)^p\|A\|^p\sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}^d}|f(n)|^p\frac{P_T(n)}{H_T(0)}.\nonumber
\end{align}
Suppose that $b, B$ are constants such that $-\infty<b<B<\infty$ and $A$ has the property that for all $\xi\in \mathbb{R}^d$,
\begin{equation} 
    b|\xi|^2\leq A(\xi, t)\xi\cdot \xi \leq B|\xi|^2,\quad \text{for all} \,\, (x, t)\in\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}_+.
\end{equation}
Then, 
\begin{align}
    \mathbb{E}_{(0, T)}| A\star M_T^f|^p\leq C_{p,b,B}^p\|A\|^p \mathbb{E}_{(0, T)}|M_T^f|^p=C_{p,b,B}^p\|A\|^p\sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}^d}|f(n)|^p\frac{P_T(n)}{H_T(0)}, 
\end{align}
where $C_{p,b,B}$ is the Choi ~[Cho] constant. In particular, when $b=0$ and $B=1$, $C_{p,0,1}=\gamma(p)$ is the constant in (equation RieszSharp2 of the paper) satisfying the basic asymptotics in (equation ChoiAsym of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.420636+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9faa533a64d091709bb48c4b1d9c436c6cd94cc969638fe0f6ffb83a85d4a7b5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conjecture conj:barcode_limit_via_F of arXiv:2507.22011, 'Random Lozenge Waterfall:\\Dimensional Collapse of Gibbs Measures'] The following limit exists for all
	$s,t\in \mathbb{Z}$,
	$s\ge t$:
	\begin{equation}

			\lim_{M\to\infty}
			q^{(M+1)(s-t)}\times
			(-1)^{t-s}
			\sqrt{\frac{-\kappa^2q^{1-s}}{(1-\kappa^2 q^{2-t})(1-\kappa^2 q^{1-s})}}
			\sum_{n=0}^{M+1/2}
			\frac{\bigl(-q^{n}\bigr)^{t-1-s}\mathcal{F}_n^{s}(0)\mathcal{F}_n^{t-1}(0)}
			{\|\mathcal{F}_n\|^2_{\ell^2(\mathbb{Z})}},
	\end{equation}
	where $M\in \mathbb{Z}$, and the sum is over $n\in \frac12\mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}$.
	The limit (equation eq:sum_regularization_of_badly_diverging_series of the paper)
	coincides with $\mathcal{K}^{\mathrm{barcode}}(s,t)$.

	Moreover, if we take the sum until $M$ instead of $M+1/2$,
	and correspondingly replace the regularization factor $q^{(M+1)(s-t)}$
	by $q^{(M+\frac{1}{2})(s-t)}$, then the limit
	also exists, and coincides with
	the shifted kernel $\mathcal{K}^{\mathrm{barcode}}(s+1,t+1)$. (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.970033+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9fae20b3adf8932cd270feaac5ad3faaa68e1fde061f3fd15ca9a8fab2c1dc5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 21 of arXiv:2606.13459, 'Symmetric Cooperative Motion in Higher Dimensions'] For any $\Ga\in (0, \sfrac{1}{3})$, there exists $R=R(d,m)>0$ such that $\bar{u}=(\bar{u}_{n})_{n\geq 0}$ as defined in \eqref{hformula} is an approximate subsolution of \eqref{timeevol} on $[0, \infty)$ with initial condition $\bar{u}_{0}$,  i.e., for $\mathcal{S}$ as in \eqref{e.bbSdef}, we have
