{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.333832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0072fdd4a2329668d826a54a06cfb7983b7edf63d485aecc1af77dc9feff273","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem-kmsw-boltzmann of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let $  r \in \mathbbm N$. Let $\mathcal Z = (\mathcal L , \mathcal R) : \mathbbm N_0 \to\mathbbm Z^2$ be a walk started from $(0,0)$ with i.i.d.\ increments sampled uniformly from $\{(1,-1), (-1,0) , (1,0)\}$. Let $\tau$ be the smallest $n\in\mathbbm N$ for which $\mathcal R(n) = -r$ and let
