 =\frac2{\sqrt\pi}
 \|\partial_tF_t\|_{L^2(\Sigma,\dd\mu_t)} .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.308692+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0cc38782b2feb9d5e0e23b8782444264f0117044a7666428fb5d1ad699f2d5a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 (Tropp, Chretien \& Darses) of arXiv:2606.24323, 'Conditioning of incoherent sub-dictionaries sampled from a coherent dictionary'] Let $\hat \P$ be a (discrete) probability measure on $\Delta$, $I$ the set-valued random variable with $I(\delta) = \{i:\delta_i=1\}$ and $\hol \in \R^{K\times K}$ a matrix with zero diagonal, then there exists a subset $T\subseteq [K]$, such that for all $\theta \in \R$
\begin{align*}
    \hat\P( \| D_I \hol D_I \| \geq r )&\leq  36 \cdot \hat\P\left( \max \left\{\| D_I \, D_T \hol D_{T^c} \, D_I \|, \| D_I \, D_{T^c} \hol D_{T} \, D_I \| \right\}\geq \frac{r}{2}\right) . 

\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.889711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0ced84e4ca891e0daf482ae603d539094fa52751c86683ccfab8a6ae412ad64","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:Spectral_NS+NStable of arXiv:2606.29829, ''] Let $\{(X_n(t),\Omega_n,\pi_n)\}_n$ be a sequence of reversible and irreducible continuous-time Markov chains. Here $\Omega_n$ is the state space, and $\pi_n$ is the stationary distribution of the $n$th chain $X_n(t)$. Also, let $t_{\rel}^n$ be the \emph{relaxation time} (will be defined) of the $n$th chain $X_n(t)$. A sequence $\{\mathfrak{f}_n\}_n$ of Boolean functions,
 	$\mathfrak{f}_n : \Omega_n \longrightarrow \{0, 1\}$, is said to be
 	\begin{itemize}
 		\item \emph{Noise sensitive} with respect to $(X_n(t),\Omega_n,\pi_n)$ if for all $\alpha>0$,
 		\begin{equation}
 			\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\cov\left(\mathfrak{f}_n(X_n(0)),\;\mathfrak{f}_n(X_n(\alpha t^n_{\rel}))\right)=0,\text{ where }X_n(0)\sim \pi_n.
 		\end{equation}
 		\item \emph{Noise stable} with respect to $(X_n(t),\Omega_n,\pi_n)$ if
 		\begin{equation}
 			\lim_{\alpha\rightarrow0}\sup_n\mathbb{P}\left(\mathfrak{f}_n(X_n(0))\neq \mathfrak{f}_n(X_n(\alpha t^n_{\rel}))\right)=0,\text{ where }X_n(0)\sim \pi_n.
 		\end{equation}
 	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:01:56.343617+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0cef410d8edad293e309e42964ba8860b67f7ba86033ac5a61dd7ce7e13a78a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:single-edge-map of arXiv:2609.01499, 'On the rigidity of sloped height functions in $d\ge 3$ \\ and non-crossing surfaces'] For a configuration $\phi$ with $\nabla_e \phi >0$, 
    \begin{itemize}  
        \item \emph{Stage 1}: for all $\mathfrak{e}_1$-oriented edges $f= \{w,w+\mathfrak{e}_1\}$ internal to $\mathcal C_e(\phi)$, in $\Phi_1(\phi)$, set 
        $$ \Phi_1(\phi)_w   = \lfloor \bar \phi_f\rfloor  \qquad\text{and} \qquad \Phi_1(\phi)_{w+\mathfrak{e}_1} = \lceil \bar \phi_f\rceil \quad \text{where} \quad \bar \phi_f = \frac{1}{2} (\phi_w + \phi_{w+\mathfrak{e}_1})$$
        (Note that this changes the gradient to zero if $\bar \phi_f \in \mathbb{Z}$ and to $1$ otherwise.)
        \item \emph{Stage 2}: In the second stage, there are two options for the operation to perform: 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\Phi^{2,-}$ takes all $\mathfrak{e}_1$-oriented edges internal to $\mathcal C_e(\phi)$ with gradient exactly equal to $1$ and lowers the height on all right-vertices by one;
            \item $\Phi^{2,+}$ takes all such edges and raises the height on all left-vertices by one.
        \end{itemize} 
        Let $\Phi_2$ be the choice among $\{\Phi^{2,-},\Phi^{2,+}\}$ which lowers the energy more (and if there is a tie, pick arbitrarily).  
    \end{itemize}
    Let $\Phi = \Phi_2 \circ\Phi_1$ be the composition of the two operations.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:49:21.127710+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d222a29e82440bdaa186873ff4fa402564082f25ca068814dcbfe34f735a00","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 of arXiv:2608.26692, 'Many Facets in Random Polytopes from Product and Log-Concave Measures'] For every fixed integer $m\geq1$,
