{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.202256+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d3653d21e848805347d59b61c01b637581527848421798205582915e090c3d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor order OmegamN of arXiv:2511.05223, 'Kac's Program and Relative Entropy Decay for Nonlinear Spin-Exchange Dynamics'] Let \(\nu \in \mathcal{P}(\Omega)\) be irreducible and assume that $\r\in\ensuremath{\mathcal D}_{\ensuremath{\mathcal A},N}$ satisfies the condition
\begin{align}
\langle \r - \r_\mathcal{A}(\nu), \r - \r_\mathcal{A}(\nu) \rangle = O\left( 1/N \right),
\end{align}
where $\r_\mathcal{A}(\nu)=(1+m_\ensuremath{\mathcal A}(\nu))/2$ is the density profile associated to $\nu$. 
Then,
\begin{align*}
\nu^{\otimes N} \left( \Omega_{N,\r}\right) \ge \frac{c}{N^{|\mathcal{A}|/2}},
\end{align*}
where $c=c(\nu)>0$ is a constant independent of $N$. In particular, 
\begin{align*}
\lim_{N \to \infty} \frac{1}{N} \log \left( \nu^{\otimes N} \left( \Omega_{N,\r}\right) \right) = 0.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.338938+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d3b7b3841d8701e362c701948f642c6a321cf6ca2bad39da341142508fb523","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2506.15044, '2506.15044'] \normalfont
Let $X$ be a $\mathbb{B}$-process.
If for all $(\omega,t)\in \mathbb{B}$ with $t>0$, the left-hand limits $X(\omega,t-)$ exist, then the {\bf left-limit process} of $X$, denoted by $X_{-}$, is defined by
  \[
   X_{-}(\omega,t)=\left\{
   \begin{aligned}
    &X(\omega,0),&&\quad \omega\in \Omega,\;t=0,\\
    &X(\omega,t-)=\lim\limits_{s<t,s\uparrow t}X(\omega,s),&&\quad (\omega,t)\in \mathbb{B},\; t>0.
    \end{aligned}
    \right.
  \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.283482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d3d74da21a5bb767fb33503755dfdb3b47188ee5a5f9a11140db56dfba5637","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:polybound of arXiv:2604.20327, 'Persistent Homology of the Wiener Sausage II: A Central Limit Theorem for Drifted Planar B'] There exists $C < \infty$ such that for every $t \geq 0$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}[H_t^2] \leq C(1 + t^3).
  \end{equation}
  Equivalently, for every bounded Borel $\psi$ supported in $[r_0, r_1]$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}\!\left[\left(\int_{r_0}^{r_1} \beta_1^t(r)\, \psi(r)\, dr\right)^{\!2}\right]
    \leq C_\psi (1 + t^3).
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.651057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d55c9693e6a13dfd17af72b16cfc1575049983a1440d1439281284e89f35c8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] A set $A$ is a thin for the field $\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}$ if for any continuous, bounded function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})= 0,
    \end{equation*}
    where the limit is in probability. The same definition applies to the fields $:\! \cos(\alpha\phi) \!: $ and $:\! \sin(\alpha\phi) \!: $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.201921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d61440c0de1eed9416e8d0380537f4252d9ffb23f39ec418d2a30433844bbe","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2604.26819, 'Sharp One-\!Dimensional Sub-Gaussian Comparison in Convex Order'] Let $ n \in\bbZ_{\ge2} $ and $s\ge0$. 
Define 
\begin{align}
    K &\coloneqq \brace{ y\in\bbR^n : \inprod{y}{1_n} = 0 , \, \normtwo{y}^2 = n } .  
\end{align}
Then 
\begin{align}
    \max_{y\in K} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i = 1}^n e^{s y_i} &= \frac{1}{n} e^{s \sqrt{n-1}} + \paren{ 1 - \frac{1}{n} } e^{-s / \sqrt{n-1}} .  
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.116239+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d61dc8656863fda67549bce362d1330f2268f23cc93e20d6fcac41a3436055","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2604.04823, '2604.04823'] Let~$U\colon \T^d \to \R$ be a regular double-well potential with wells of equal depth (but not necessarily the same shape). 
Then there exist constants $\eta, c_1, c_2, \bar C_\mathrm{BV}, c_d > 0$ such that the following holds.

For any~$0 < \underline \epsilon < \bar \epsilon < 1$, set
\begin{equation}
    N = \ceil*{\frac{1}{\underline \epsilon}}, 
    \quad \epsilon_0 = \bar \epsilon, 
    \quad \epsilon_N = \underline \epsilon,
\end{equation}
and choose~$\paren*{1/\epsilon_k}_{k=0}^N$ to be linearly spaced. For each~$k$, define
\begin{equation}
    h_k = \min\set*{\eta \epsilon_k^2, 1}\,.
\end{equation}
Then the corresponding parallel tempering chain~$P_\pt$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \gap(P_\pt) \geq D \min\set*{c_2,\, c_1 \underline\epsilon^7} \underline\epsilon^4\,,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    D(d, \epsilon_0) = c_d \exp\paren*{-\bar C_\mathrm{BV} - 2\paren*{1 + \frac{1}{\epsilon_0}}\norm{U}_{L^\infty}} h_0^2\,.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d6a4e2238a3e2a0ef28002261d52c984fb6181857cd6f9bb166e0eee9a73aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm:FractionalStab of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] There exists a positive constant $C = C (n,\gamma) > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
         \int_{\mathbb{R}^n } w (-\Delta )^{\gamma}w  dx - \frac{1}{S(n,\gamma) }\left( \int_{\mathbb{R}^n } |w|^{2^{*}} dx  \right)^{\frac{2}{2^{*}}} \geq C \text{dist}(w,\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n} )^2 \quad \forall w\in \dot{H}^{\gamma } (\mathbb{R}^n),
    \end{equation*}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        \text{dist}(w,\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n} ) := \inf \{\|w-u \|_{\dot{H}^{\gamma}(\mathbb{R}^n)} : u \in \mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n}  \},
    \end{align*}
    and $\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n}$ is the set of extremal functions for the fractional Sobolev inequality. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

