{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.536417+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1e33e9a765deafae2bb7a24324a8c8eee8e6cb7e8537665af04ae7e5ca250dc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2609.02610, 'The frame set of the first Hermite function'] Let $N \in \N$, $N\geq1$, let $\Omega\subset\C$ be a connected open set, and let
  $f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1}$ be holomorphic on $\Omega$. Their Wronskian matrix is
  \begin{equation}
    \begin{pmatrix}
      f_0 & \ldots & f_{N-1}\\
      f_0' & \ldots & f_{N-1}'\\
      \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
      f_0^{(N-1)} & \ldots & f_{N-1}^{(N-1)}
    \end{pmatrix}
  \end{equation}
  The \emph{Wronskian} is the determinant of the matrix in
  \eqref{eq:general-wronskian-matrix}, i.e., the holomorphic function
  \begin{equation}
    \Wr(f_0,\ldots,f_{N-1})(z)
    =\det\left(f_j^{(r)}(z)\right)_{0\leq r,j<N}
    \quad\text{for all }z\in\Omega.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.709903+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1e3abd361cdcc12ed0e5cd731ad8702ddd61bbb0ad510eed8c8759cbe0a9bdb","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 11 (Lifted flow) of arXiv:2609.01143, 'The voter model on the hyperbolic graph'] The first-coordinate and  second-coordinate lifts of a flow $f$ and a measure $\lambda$ are respectively
\[
\mathsf L_1(f,\lambda)((x,y),(x',y'))=
 \begin{cases}
 f(x,x')\lambda(y), & y=y',\\
0, & \text{otherwise}
 \end{cases}
\]
and
\[
\mathsf L_2(\lambda,f)((x,y),(x',y'))=
 \begin{cases}
 f(y,y')\lambda(x), & x=x',\\
0, & \text{otherwise.}
 \end{cases}
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:15:15.513490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1e6e39588311d8e2c79a2880ba9d028d8297f601d541d1ad477097d0950a4fc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Phi-AA-subseq of arXiv:2507.01406, '{\Large On Iterated Lorenz Curves with Applications: The Multivariate Case }'] Let $\{n_{k}\}_{k\geq 0}$ be any strictly increasing
