{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:02:34.835255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a26d1f4d6a86493e6e1163962d2c6051df3384bc833ee274b7fafbf4ec11aec2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 (C-S posterior identification) of arXiv:2607.20373, 'Embedding martingale diffusions as binary posteriors in sequential inference'] $P$ is a C-S posterior if and only if $\delta$ satisfies 
\begin{equation}
  \delta'(x)+2\mu_0(x)\delta(x)+\delta(x)^2=-\kappa,
  \qquad x\in J,
\end{equation}
for some $\kappa\in\mathbb{R}$.
In this case, the local $\widetilde{\mathbb{Q}}^1/\widetilde{\mathbb{Q}}^0$--likelihood-ratio process before $\zeta$ is
\begin{equation}
  L_t=\exp\left\{
    A(X_{t})+\kappa t/2
  \right\},
  \qquad t<\zeta, \qquad 
  A(x):=\int_{x_0}^x\delta(u)\,du, \quad \text{and}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
 P_t=\Gamma(t,X_t), \ \ \text{where} \ \ \Gamma(t,x)=H\left(\logit p+A(x)+\kappa t/2\right),
  \ \
  H(r):=1/(1+e^{-r}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

