{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.782337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2cf738128bf6ad09a03d147b8eb335d651d0fb2791b0ddabd2e63b0e767f38c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main-general of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] Fix $d\ge 2$. Suppose Assumption~(assump:uniform-mixing-time of the paper) holds. There exists $p_0(d)<1$ such that for all $p>p_0$ and all $\beta > \beta_0$, if $X_t$ is continuous-time Glauber dynamics on $\mathbb T^d_n = (\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)^d$, with probability $1-o(1)$, $x_0 \sim \bigotimes_{\mathbb T_n^d}\text{Rad}(p)$ is such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \|\mathbb P_{x_0} (X_t \in \cdot) - \pi^+_{\mathbb T^d_n}\|_{{\textsc{tv}}} \le n^{-10} \qquad \text{ for all $t\in [n^{o(1)}, e^{ \Omega(n^{d-1})}]$}\,.
    \end{align*}
    If $X_t$ is the infinite volume Glauber dynamics on $\mathbb Z^d$, with probability going to $1$ as $R\to\infty$, $x_0\sim \bigotimes _{\mathbb Z^d} \text{Rad}(p)$ is such that for $\Lambda_R = \{-\frac{R}{2},...,\frac{R}{2}\}^2$, once $t \ge R$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \|\mathbb P_{x_0}(X_t(\Lambda_R)\in \cdot) - \pi^+_{\mathbb Z^d}(\sigma(\Lambda_R)\in \cdot)\|_{{\textsc{tv}}} \le \exp( - \Omega((\log t)^{10}))\,.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:17.758685+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2d0a8c8d280dfc48dbbbd9da066f5bc0059855cd40bb82f1370e0e20e9dccd6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2511.05386, 'CLT for $\beta$-ensembles with Freud weights, application to the KLS conjecture in Schatte'] Let $p>2$.
For any compact set $K\subset \C$, any $\delta>0$ and any $\phi\in \mathcal{C}^\infty_c(\R)$ supported in $[-1-\delta,1+\delta]$, there exists $C>0$ such that the following holds.
For any $N,q \geq 1$ and $\alpha \in [0,1]$, if there exists $M>0$ such that, for any $x \in [-1-\delta,1+\delta]$ and $y \in (0,\delta]$, with $z = x+\ii y$,
\begin{equation} 
	\E_\alpha\left[ \abs{s_N(z)-s_{V_\alpha}(z)}^q \right] 
	\leq \frac{M^q}{N^q}\left( \frac{1}{y}+\frac{1}{\abs{z}^2} \right)^q,
\end{equation}
then, for any $w\in K$,
\begin{equation} 
	\E_\alpha\left[\abs{L_N(f_{\alpha,w}\phi)}^q\right]
	\leq C^qM^q.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.030150+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2d26b206bf4e038ad329be261d42817fd622a3168a2f2a166028d846f62a981","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2605.02713, '2605.02713'] Let $q \in \N \backslash \{1 \}$ and ${r \in \left\{ 1 , \ldots , q-1 \right\}}$. Define
	\begin{align*}
		s(n,r):= \sum_{i,j,k,l=1}^n     \alpha_n^{r|i-j|}   \alpha_n^{r|k-l|}   \alpha_n^{(q-r)|i-k|}   \alpha_n^{(q-r)|j-l|}  .
	\end{align*}
	Then it holds $\displaystyle \max_{r \in \left\{ 1 , \ldots , q-1 \right\}} s(n,r) = s(n,1) = s(n,q-1) $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.644652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2d53c0a68712030fe434d7221f5f61b171b116dfaf965e59b579e6e0a5b3379","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (Vlassis--Thomas) of arXiv:2607.23980, 'Sharp small-deviation inequalities for sums of independent\\nonnegative random variables'] If $Y_1,\ldots,Y_n$ are independent nonnegative random variables with $\E Y_i\le1$, then
