{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.204707+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a34c1d8f0168d18b33dcc5667b4cc416c6c0e051a550398414f22504e62e7e91","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop dissiBanach of arXiv:2506.08202, 'Stochastic dissipative systems in Banach spaces driven by L\'evy noise'] Let $f:{\rm Dom}(f)\subseteq \mathcal{K}{\rightarrow}\mathcal{K}$. $f$ is dissipative if and only if, for every $x,y\in {\rm Dom}(f)$ there exists $z^*\in\partial{\left\|x-y\right\|}$ such that
\begin{equation}
\dscal{f(x)-f(y)}{z^*}{\mathcal{K}}\leq 0.
\end{equation}
If $\mathcal{K}$ is a Hilbert space (equation disban of the paper) reads as
\begin{equation*}
\scal{f(x)-f(y)}{x-y}_{\mathcal{K}}\leq 0.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.988770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3519cf78ecb273a860f326c2b11d443b9d8c390094700921210f87e87768a93","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 71 of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] Assume $\omega$ is such that $\cC_1(p_0) \subseteq \cC_1(p_1)$. Denote by $\nu_1$ and $\nu_2$ the laws of $\ClCp{1}{p_0}$ and $\cT_{p_1 \mid p_0}$, respectively. Then
    \begin{equation} 
        \Vert \nu_1 - \nu_2\Vert_{\mathrm{TV}} \leq n^4 e^{-\beta(p_1 - p_0)},
    \end{equation}
    where $\Vert \cdot \Vert_{\mathrm{TV}}$ is the total variation norm. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.481292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3528aab74ed24c4a5167b97192ab98a6d3f4df9b1c226e88e810d39a0be8cb2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:johnson-decomp of arXiv:2606.22636, 'Spectral Gap for the Binary Fixed-Margin Swap Chain'] For each \(a\), the functions on \(a\)-subsets of \(E\) decompose orthogonally as
		\[
		L^2\!\left(\binom{E}{a}\right)
		=\bigoplus_{0\le\ell\le\min(a,N-a)}
