		\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.521690+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3548886a520f2213043c98c6b3422b154f3e8f44bcdd27f417339761427ea6f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop reduction to fixed plaquette config of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] Suppose that for some constants $C_1, C_2$, we have that for all $P \sse \mc{P}_\Lambda^+$,
\begin{equs}
\bigg|\int dU W_{\ell}(U) \prod_{p \in \mc{P}_{\Lambda}^+ \backslash P} &\exp_B(2 \beta \mathrm{Re}\mathrm{Tr}(U_p))\prod_{p \in P} \tau_B(2 \beta \mathrm{Re}\mathrm{Tr}(U_p)) \bigg| \\
& \leq C_1 \exp((B/40) M(P)) \exp(-C_2 (\mrm{area}(\ell) - 20dM(P))_+)  \\
&\quad \quad \quad \quad ~\times\int dU \prod_{p \in \mc{P}_{\Lambda}^+ \backslash P} \exp_B(2 \beta \mathrm{Re}\mathrm{Tr}(U_p))\prod_{p \in P} \tau_B(2 \beta \mathrm{Re}\mathrm{Tr}(U_p)).
\end{equs}
Here, $(\mrm{area}(\ell) - 20dM(P))_+ = \max(\mrm{area}(\ell) - 20dM(P), 0)$. Then we have that
\begin{equs}
|\langle W_\ell \rangle_{\Lambda, \beta, N}| \leq C_1 e^{2d|\ell|} 50^{d \cdot \mrm{area}(\ell)} 2^{\mrm{area}(\ell)} \max(\exp(-C_2), \exp(-d^{-1} 10^{-3} B))^{\mrm{area}(\ell)}.
\end{equs} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.829831+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35765bb8bc70f0272afcebb2846ee8c81555b9ac0cc0b2ff252888c3de4d76a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm exponentialconvergence of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] Let us suppose that (A4 of the paper),(A7 of the paper)--(A12 of the paper) hold true. Furthermore, suppose that the constants given in the hypotheses satisfy
\begin{align}
\kappa \coloneqq m_x + m_\lambda - 2L_\mu - \sigma_{\lambda}C_{Q}> 0,
\end{align}
and consider $\mu_{\cdot}$ as in (equation misurasol of the paper). Then there exists a unique invariant probability measure $\mu_\infty \in {\mathscr{P}}_{2}({\mathscr Y})$ such that for every initial condition $(Y(0),\Lambda(0))\in L^{2}(\Omega,{\mathscr Y})$ of (equation eq:limitMV_detailed of the paper),
\begin{align}
{\mathcal W}_{2}(\mu(t), \mu_\infty)
\le
e^{-\kappa t} {\mathcal W}_{2}(\mu_0,\mu_\infty),
\end{align}
where $\mu_{0}={\mathcal L}((Y(0),\Lambda(0)))$ is the law of $(Y(0),\Lambda(0))$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.163722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35850217818382415a51567d52b3dae11bdf69a5742d5c8080a15290b7c6842","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 (Effective sample size) of arXiv:2604.17526, '2604.17526'] Let~$w^1_K$, \dots, $w^N_K$ be the normalized weights defined in~\eqref{e:ais-small-error-and-wki-def}.
  Then the sum of the squared weights satisfies
  \begin{equation}
    \E \sum_{i = 1}^N (w^i_K)^2 
      \leq \frac{8\paren*{4C_w +1}}{N}
    \,.
  \end{equation}
  Consequently, the expected effective sample size is bounded by
  \begin{equation}
    \E \ESS(w^1_K, \dots, w^N_K)
      \geq \frac{N}{8\paren*{4C_w +1}}
    \,.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:20:30.573892+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a358f3b574a05dcb06fe9242fc3131a1a62a64630adb7167e4cd4d00e4b69f78","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2609.01826, 'An extension theory for fully fractional Schr\"odinger equations with memory'] Let $0<s<1, t>0$, and $z>0$. Then the following oscillatory identity holds:
\begin{equation}
\int_0^t
\frac{e^{i \frac{z^2}{4\sigma}}}{(t-\sigma)^{1-s}\sigma^{1+s}}\,d\sigma =
4^s\,\G(s)\,e^{i\frac{\pi s}{2}}\,t^{s-1}\,z^{-2s}
e^{i \frac{z^2}{4t}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.433664+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35a6d0dddd8f2c3ebd13e0afb919ab4550b4a667cc06529d0df0f1260c7ba41","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Define Definition 4.1 of arXiv:2601.09990, 'Remarks on the convex integration technique applied to singular stochastic partial differe'] Given initial data $u^{\text{in}} \in L^{n} (\mathbb{T}^{d})$, a scalar field $u \in L^{n} (0, \infty; L^{n} (\mathbb{T}^{d}))$ is a weak solution to (equation odd power of the paper) if 
\begin{align}
&\int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(t,x) \psi(t,x) dx - \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u^{\text{in}} (x) \psi(0,x) dx - \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(s,x) \partial_{s} \psi(s,x) dx ds  \nonumber \\
& \hspace{30mm} + \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u^{n}(s,x) \psi(s,x) dx ds = \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(s,x) \Delta \psi(s,x) dx ds 
\end{align} 
for all test function $\psi$ in $C_{c}^{\infty} ( (-\infty, T) \times \mathbb{T}^{d})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.594328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35d2f2c77693534b65117799c452ee3a4d795a02e3f4722d5d3bbfda182ad18","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Eq:1.12 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Abbreviate $\beta_k:=\beta\sigma_k^{-2}$. For all~$k=0,\dots,n$,
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
E\bigl(\phi_k^2\,\big|\,\mathcal F_{k+1}\bigr) =\phi_{k+1}^2+\frac1{\beta_k}+\frac1{\beta_k^2}\bigl[v_{k}'(\phi_{k+1})^2-v_{k}''(\phi_{k+1})\bigr]-\frac2{\beta_k}\phi_{k+1}v_{k}'(\phi_{k+1})
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
and, for all $k=1,\dots,n$, also
\begin{equation}

E\bigl(\phi_kv_{k-1}'(\phi_k)\,\big|\,\mathcal F_{k+1}\bigr) = \frac1b\phi_{k+1}v_{k}'(\phi_{k+1})-\frac1{\beta_k}\,\frac1b\bigl[v_{k}'(\phi_{k+1})^2-v_{k}''(\phi_{k+1})\bigr].
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.006984+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35dc2e1a7dac6d194dd50274a258d61b748d271fd3dcab2d0a7445fac59a002","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1path of arXiv:2606.27844, 'Critical percolation on preferential attachment graphs with infinite variance'] \text{}\\[-5mm]
\begin{itemize}
