{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.279644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a39fda0b7394afe8d3550f030560cbab7dd6336ea57ced62c364a8a8639da8ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro: zero probability sum for JSQ of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] \textnormal{(Probability Mass at Zero)}

    Under heavily overloaded assumption (ass:heavy_overload of the paper), the probability mass at zero for JSQ model is bounded as
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbb{P}(\mathbf{q}_{i} = 0) &\leq \exp\left( -\frac{C}{2n\lambda} \cdot\frac{\lambda-\mu}{\sqrt{\gamma}} \right) \\
        \exp\left( -\frac{\lambda}{\gamma} \right) \leq \mathbb{P}(\sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathbf{q}_{i} = 0) &\leq C_{\lambda,\mu}' \sqrt{\gamma/\mu} \exp\left( -\frac{1}{\gamma/\mu} (\lambda/\mu - 1 - \ln (\lambda/\mu)) \right) 
    \end{align*}
    $C_{\lambda,\mu}'$ is the same as in Lemma (lem: tight bound on P(q_infty = 0) of the paper), $C$ is from the heavily overloaded assumption (ass:heavy_overload of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.190400+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3a0bf4375a8c143ae7ac76e0964fe7ff54806824d29d171be34d2c70dc7f259","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 (Subadditivity and orientation reversal) of arXiv:2609.03916, 'Halfspace Theorems for Anisotropic Minimal Surfaces and Perimeter Minimizers'] Let $\mu$ and $\eta$ be vector-valued Radon measures.  Then, as Radon
measures,
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal F_\Phi(\mu+\eta;\cdot)
 \le\mathcal F_\Phi(\mu;\cdot)+\mathcal F_\Phi(\eta;\cdot).
\end{equation}
Consequently, for any locally integral $n$-currents $T$ and $S$,
\begin{equation}
 \mu_{T+S}^\Phi\le\mu_T^\Phi+\mu_S^\Phi.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for $\Phi^-$ defined in
\eqref{old-eq:reversed-integrand-definition},
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal F_\Phi(-\mu;\cdot)=\mathcal F_{\Phi^-}(\mu;\cdot),
 \qquad
 \mu_{-T}^\Phi=\mu_T^{\Phi^-},
 \qquad
 \Mphi(-T_E;A)=P_{\Phi^-}(E;A)
\end{equation}
for every Borel set $A$.
No evenness of $\Phi$ is required.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.313255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3a15ab8b0c7d37e2480219d99c0da8099cab4e13298fba0eb79b99d70476b3b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:883 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] For all $0<u\le 1$, there exists $K_1(u)>0$ such that the following holds. Consider the collection of spheres $\mathscr{S}:=\bigcup_{t\in\mathbb Z}\{\partial\mathcal B_{t/p}\}$ with a real parameter $p\geq1$. Then, for all $n\geq 1$ and $z\in\mathbb R^3$ with $|z|\ge2^{-n}$, we have
