{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.142927+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3b365c50ab26499181b64388e3afbc28a5c186a018a25631175376cf9708f64","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem symmetricmaintheorem of arXiv:2507.05417, 'Invertibility for non-Hermitian and symmetric random band matrices with sublinear bandwidt'] Fix $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and let $d_n\leq n$ be a sequence of integers such that $d_n\geq n^\alpha$ when $n$ is sufficiently large. 


    Let $S_n=(s_{ij})_{1\leq i,j\leq n}$ be a family of square \textbf{symmetric} random matrices such that $s_{ij}=s_{ji}$ and $(s_{ij})_{1\leq i\leq j\leq n}$ are mutually independent random variables. We assume that 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item When $|i-j|\leq d_n$ or $|n-i+j|\leq d_n$, then for each $1\leq i\leq j\leq n$ we can write $s_{ij}=u_{ij}+c_{ij}$ where $\{u_{ij}\}_{i\leq j}$ are i.i.d. and uniformly distributed on $\{-1,1\}$, and $c_{ij}$ are arbitrary integer-valued random variables that are independent of $u_{ij}$.
        \item All the other random variables $s_{ij}$ where $\min(|i-j|,|n-i+j|)>d_n$   are either identically zero or are arbitrary integer-valued random variables.\end{enumerate}
    Then whenever $\alpha>\frac{2}{3}$, we have 
    $$
\mathbb{P}(S_n\text{ is singular})\leq \exp(-Cn^{\alpha/2}),
    $$ for some universal constant $C>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:55:37.878318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3ba9186eb7b80a5d84b87d0cd83200935df427a72289ecf68c8b7e99721816a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Coro cor:cdfbounding of arXiv:2605.07920, 'Primitive Sequences for Probability Measures on Compact Intervals'] Suppose that $c_1, \ldots, c_m$ are the first $m \geq 1$ terms of an admissible sequence for $[a,b]$. Then for every $x_0 \in [a,b]$,
    \begin{equation*}
    \bar{F}_m(x_0; c_{1:m}) := \sup_{\mu \in \pazocal{F}_m(c_{1:m})} \mu([a,x_0]) 
    \quad \text{and} \quad 
    \underline{F}_m(x_0; c_{1:m}) := \inf_{\mu \in \pazocal{F}_m(c_{1:m})} \mu([a,x_0))
\end{equation*}
are attained by $(m+1)$-atomic measures. Equivalently, if $X$ is any random variable on $[a,b]$ with given moments $\mathbb{E}[X^k]$ for $k=1,\ldots,m$, then sharp bounds on $F_X(x_0) = \mathbb{P}(X \leq x_0)$ and on $F_X(x_0-) = \mathbb{P}(X < x_0)$ are attained by discrete random variables supported on at most $m+1$ points.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.785702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3bc50aab54e73c07b18bf5bdc88091913fc8261a52ea8c9d4d2a9bd4b63798c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem uniform-in-beta-bounds-on-surface-tension-and-stiffness of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] There exists $\beta_0$ such that for all $\beta>\beta_0$, 
