{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.355485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3e93421239b3066cace2f8c536acc472603d64ca5cbdc11b10cf6b1a5d205e0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:almost_Linfty of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] Fix $0<T<\infty$. Let $p,q\in (2,\infty)$ be such that 
\begin{equation}

\frac{2}{p}+\frac{d}{q}<1.
\end{equation}
Then for all $r\in (1,\infty)$ there exists $C_0>0$ such that for any $N$, any interval $I=(t_0-T,t_0)\subset (-1/4,\infty)$ with length $T<\infty$, and
\begin{equation}

F,G\in L^r_{\mathscr{P}}(\Omega; L^p(I;L^q(\mathbb{T}^d;{\mathbb R}^{d\times d}))),
\end{equation}
satisfying
\begin{equation}

\mathrm{Tr} (G)=0 \text{ a.e.\ on }\mathbb{T}^d \qquad \text{ and }\qquad \nabla \cdot G =0\text{ in }\mathscr{D}'(\mathbb{T}^d),
\end{equation} 
a.e.\ on $I\times \Omega$, it holds that 
\begin{align}

\sup_{ I\times \mathbb{T}^d}\big( {\mathbf E}|v^N|^r \big)^{1/r}
&\leq C_0  \big({\mathbf E}\|F\|_{L^p( I;L^q(\mathbb{T}^d))}^r\big)^{1/r}+ C_0 \big({\mathbf E}\|G\|_{L^p( I;L^q(\mathbb{T}^d))}^r\big)^{1/r},
\end{align}
where $v^N$ is the unique energy solution to (equation eq:turbulent_Stokes_scaling_uniform_estimates_modified of the paper).
In particular, for all $r_0\in (1,\infty)$, there exists $C_1>0$ independent of $N,t_0,F$ and $G$ for which  
\begin{align}

\big( {\mathbf E}\|v^N\|_{L^{r_0}( I\times \mathbb{T}^d)}^{r} \big)^{1/{r}}
\leq C_1  \big({\mathbf E}\|F\|_{L^p( I;L^q(\mathbb{T}^d))}^{r}\big)^{1/r}+ C_1 \big({\mathbf E}\|G\|_{L^p( I;L^q(\mathbb{T}^d))}^{r}\big)^{1/{r}}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.675089+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a3e9c077039e300b9d279dd9b6128b8fc711006d723b3f35cdf250dd323a5e72","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 40 of arXiv:2608.09700, 'A general paracontrolled ansatz for singular SPDEs'] We have the following two identies  
