{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.943247+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a433fbe2b60e8236654b4f8ffd822c3b1bafc77efa39125e28e403fee97f3047","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop hall_projection of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Let $G$ be a finite bipartite graph with bipartition $(B,W)$. Label every vertex $v\in G$ with a multiplicity $N_v$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in W} N_v = \sum_{v\in B} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    There exists an ${\mathbf N}= (N_v)_{v}$-dimer cover of $G$ if and only if there is no set $C\subset W$ such that 
    $$N(C) = \{v\in B : \exists\, c\in C, (c,v)\in E\}$$
    satisfies 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in N(C)} N_v < \sum_{v\in C} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    We call $U:=C \cup N(C)$ satisfying these conditions an \emph{${\mathbf N}$-counterexample} in $G$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.963652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4375474f4dbd4de30998676e10f45a52fba5ea75ae42014f60b631a28cdd97b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: merge increment of arXiv:2509.11384, 'The Horton-Strahler number of butterfly trees'] Let $\mathcal T_1,\mathcal T_2$ be rooted binary trees with $m$ nodes. Then 
    \begin{equation}
        \max(\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1),\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2)) \le \operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1 \oplus \mathcal T_2)\le \max(\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1),\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2)) + 1,
    \end{equation}
    and the upper bound is tight if and only if all of the following increment conditions are met:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item \textbf{Max parent}: $\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1) \ge \operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2)$.
        \item \textbf{Full profile}: The top right edge of $\mathcal T_1$ has nodes with HS numbers $\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2),\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2) + 1, \ldots, \operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1)$.
        \item \textbf{Escape paths}: For each $t \in \{\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_2),\ldots,\operatorname{HS}(\mathcal T_1)\}$, the top right edge of $\mathcal T_1$ has a node $v$ with value $\operatorname{HS}(v) = t$ that has a child node $w$ located off of top right edge with $\operatorname{HS}(w) = t$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Identical results hold for $\mathcal T_1 \ominus \mathcal T_2$ when replacing the top right edge with the top left edge in the above increment conditions. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.734551+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a438cfde093293fb5107eb82a98b2d38e10492dd871257d5c6daf6c976239cfa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:L1-contraction of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] For $f\in L^1(\mathbf{S}_N)$, let $f_t=P_tf$, then $P_t$ is contractive in $L^1$, i.e. 
