\end{equation}
We call ${\cal E}_h \mu$ the \emph{$h$-erasure of $\mu$}. We also adopt the convention that ${\cal E}_0 \mu \! :=\!   \mu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.712486+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4c4af42d29f6f9b22dc19bba4c399a8a36e580b4ed5965828c83caed7851fc7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 (Polynomial-degree stationary potentials) of arXiv:2609.01143, 'The voter model on the hyperbolic graph'] At $\varphi=2-2\alpha$, for every fixed $\gamma>0$, with high probability uniformly over vertices with $h(v)\geq\gamma R$,
