{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.614996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4cf97854952061b6df0397c2675707bf2b7eee1c88da1dc6477a1c3a4fe0557","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop-TaylormadeIto-1 of arXiv:2512.13200, 'Martingales on a Euclidean manifold with a boundary and reflected BSDEs in non-convex doma'] Let {Assumption (ass:3 of the paper) hold.}

 Consider a $C^1$ $\Gamma$-convex function $\psi : \bar{\mathcal{D}} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ (resp., $\psi : \bar{\mathcal{D}} \times \bar{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$) and a (Euclidean) semimartingale $(X_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ with values in $\bar{\mathcal{D}}$ (resp., $\bar{\mathcal{D}}\times \bar{\mathcal{D}}$). Then,
 \begin{align*} 
 \psi(X_t) \geqslant \psi(X_s) +\int_s^t \nabla \psi (X_u) \mathrm{d} X_u, \quad \forall \, 0 \leqslant s \leqslant t \leqslant T.
 \end{align*}
 More generally, consider any continuous function $S: \bar{\mathcal{D}} \rightarrow \mathcal{S}^+$ (resp. $S: \bar{\mathcal{D}}\times \bar{\mathcal{D}} \rightarrow \mathcal{S}^+$), where $\mathcal{S}^+ \subset \mathbb{R}^{2 \times 2}$ (resp., $\mathcal{S}^+ \subset \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$) is the set of all symmetric positive semidefinite matrices, such that the following holds for all geodesics $\gamma$:
 \begin{equation}

    \psi(\gamma_t) \geqslant \psi(\gamma_0)+\langle \nabla \psi(\gamma_0),\dot \gamma_0 \rangle t + \int_0^t (t-s) \dot \gamma_s^\top S(\gamma_{s})\dot \gamma_s \mathrm{d} s.
 \end{equation}
 Then, we have
 $$\psi(X_t) \geqslant \psi(X_s) +\int_s^t \nabla \psi (X_u) \mathrm{d} X_u + \frac12 \int_s^t  \langle \mathrm{d} X_u, S(X_u) \mathrm{d} X_u \rangle, \quad \forall 0 \leqslant s \leqslant t \leqslant T.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.440466+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a4d0f27a9ae9693095692f56dacc68553c464989bb38a52ba6032a76e4f62fe4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Endpoint mixed derivative estimate on finite intervals) of arXiv:2606.15425, 'Well-posedness of stochastic parabolic equations with gradient nonlinearities and applicat'] Let $\mathcal B$ be isomorphic to a closed subspace of an $L^q$-space for some
