{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.502336+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a5d52aef32b1c41847e4548a6e0ff28159f259a212f61d5906a8425d1b3c85d0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 48 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $\xi \in W$ be arbitrary. A path $(Y,Y') \in \scrd_X^{2 \alpha}([0,T_0],W)$ for some $T_0 \in (0,T]$ is called a \emph{local solution} to the RDE \eqref{RDE} with $Y_0 = \xi$ if $Y' = f(Y)$ and
\begin{align}
Y_t = \xi + \int_0^t f_0(s,Y_s) ds + \int_0^t f(s,Y_s) d\mathbf{X}_s, \quad t \in [0,T_0].
\end{align}
If we can choose $T_0 = T$, then we also call $(Y,Y')$ a \emph{(global) solution} to the RDE \eqref{RDE} with $Y_0 = \xi$.","status":"unverified"}

