{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.832640+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a5f048ddce94f69176b79fbbbb0bc3123b653781f9b40862adb86781f9ca85dc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm RT of arXiv:2609.01751, 'Orthogonal polynomials which are eigenfunctions of a partial differential operator'] For $\mu > -\frac12$, define the fourth-order differential operator 
\begin{align} 
  {\mathfrak D}_{\mu}^{{\mathbb R} \rtimes \triangle} u: = \,& -\frac14 (1-|{\bm y}|)^3 \Delta_{{\bm x}}^2 + (1-|{\bm y}|) {\langle} {\bm x},\nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle} \Delta_{\bm x} 
   - ({\langle} {\bm x},\nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle} + {\langle} {\bm y},\nabla_{\bm y}{\rangle}) ^2  \\
   & +  \left(1+ \frac{1}{2} ({\kappa}_{d_2+1} + d_1)\right) \Delta_{\bm x} - 
     \sum_{i=1}^{d_2} \left[ y_i \partial_{y_i}^2 + ({\kappa}_i+1) \partial_{y_i} \right] \notag \\
    & + (|{\bm k}|+d_1+d_2) \big( {\langle} {\bm x},\nabla_{\bm x}{\rangle}+ {\langle} {\bm y},\nabla_{\bm y}{\rangle}\big). \notag
\end{align}
Then ${\mathcal V}_n({\mathbb R}^{d_1}  \rtimes \triangle^{d_2}, \bm W_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb R} \rtimes \triangle})$ is an eigenspace 
of $ {\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb R} \rtimes \triangle}$ for each $n \in {\mathbb N}_0$. More precisely, 
\begin{align} 
  {\mathfrak D}_{{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}}^{{\mathbb R} \rtimes \triangle} u =  - n (n+ |{\boldsymbol{\kappa}}|+  d_1 + d_2) u, \qquad 
         \forall u \in {\mathcal V}_n\big({\mathbb R}^{d_1}  \rtimes \triangle^{d_2}, \bm W_{\mu}^{{\mathbb R} \rtimes \triangle}\big).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.364920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a5f439d121de9c6b097a99f81e544466bd7fa2727f2f47cf01cd9cf6bc397992","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 9 (Lemma 6.3, \cite{univ_euclidean}) of arXiv:2607.27535, 'Universal topological statistics on \\ triangulated singular spaces'] Let $K_\bullet$ be a filtered simplicial complex and let $\sigma$ be a simplex that enters $K_\bullet$ at some point. Then for every $\alpha$, following is true:
