{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:04:55.802263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a61651a3f69cfc541846e10d7519906a86b9ee4479cf3c972ed6ed6fae00e91e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:mesh-path of arXiv:2607.18194, 'Temperature chaos in directed polymers'] We say that a continuous path $\pi:[0,\delta^{3/2}]\to \mathbb{R}$ is a \emph{mesh path} if
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\pi(t_k) \in \varepsilon^{50}\mathbb{Z}$ for all $k\in\llbracket 0,n+1\rrbracket$,
        \item $|\pi(t_{k+1})-\pi(t_k)| < (t_{k+1}-t_k)^{2/3}\log(1/\varepsilon)$ for all $k\in\llbracket 0,n\rrbracket$, and
        \item  
        $\pi|_{[t_k,t_{k+1}]} = \Pi_{(t_k,\pi(t_k)), (t_{k+1}, \pi(t_{k+1}))}$ for all $k\in \llbracket 0,n\rrbracket$.
    \end{itemize}
    In other words, a mesh path is obtained by selecting a mesh point $v_k\in \varepsilon^{50}\mathbb{Z}$ corresponding to time $t_k$ for every $k\in\llbracket 0,n+1\rrbracket$, connecting $(t_k,v_k)$ and $(t_{k+1},v_{k+1})$ with a directed landscape geodesic, and then concatenating these geodesics across $k$ (as the reader may have already anticipated, the exponent 50 is an arbitrary large number and any other such choice would have sufficed too).
    Note that we impose a hard constraint on a mesh path's local transversal fluctuations.
    \footnote{
        This is just for technical convenience, and should be viewed as a mild constraint:
        we will be interested in the longest mesh path, which is approximately given by rounding the $\mathcal{L}$-geodesic to $\varepsilon^{50}\mathbb{Z}$, and the $\mathcal{L}$-geodesic satisfies the stated local transversal fluctuation bounds with high probability (Proposition (p:DOV-geodesic-holder of the paper)).
    }
    See Figure (fig:noise-rounding-eps-times of the paper) for a depiction of the spatial mesh $\varepsilon^{50}\mathbb{Z}$ and times $t_0,\dots,t_{n+1}$ involved in this definition.
    Since any mesh path $\pi$ is piecewise geodesic, its length is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \int_0^{\delta^{3/2}} d\mathcal{L}\circ \pi 
        = \sum_{k=0}^n \mathcal{L}(t_k,\pi(t_k); t_{k+1},\pi(t_{k+1})).
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.314762+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a616fa04e2bf0f863d981b501036ab762bc302ad49d4ff5e1343b1da5aa65e2c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop LDP-recursive-equation of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For all $x\in\mathbb Z$ and $y\in\mathbb Z_{\le -1}$,
