{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.636028+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a66166b45a6d6bf7c63154cb56f8486656d32ee46e4e4c90d0a34f9d86fa1148","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma terms_in_signature_second of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] \[
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (4(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})+(\xi_i,\xi_i,\xi_j,\xi_j)\Big ) .
\]
\begin{proof}
Representation (equation eq:expansion of the paper) combined with (cor:parity of the paper) gives,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)=\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big )+\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big ).
\end{equation}
Expanding the symmetric product as a pure tensor and applying linearity we get,
\[
\mathrm{e}^{\star k}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big )=\mathrm{e}^{\star k}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2\Big )=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\mathrm{e}^{\star k} (\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2 ).
\]
For \(k=2\):
\[
\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)=2(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_j,\xi_{iij})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij}) .
\]
Substituting \(\xi_{iij}=\xi_{jii}\) (reversal property) before re-indexing the middle term \((i,j)\mapsto (j,i)\) we get,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)&=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (2(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_j,\xi_{jii})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})\Big )\\
&=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (4(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})\Big ) .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
For \(k=4\):
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)=(\xi_i,\xi_i,\xi_j,\xi_j) .
\end{equation}
Substituting (equation eq:k2_iijj of the paper) and (equation eq:k4_iijj of the paper) into (equation eq:proof_ei2_ej2 of the paper) we obtain the required result.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.358682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a664b9dd185b2413fb7cad46b46c92d559ddec93a4c21d6fb1ca9da4d455e56f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem clt for negative graphons of arXiv:2411.13062, 'Graphon-Theoretic Approach to Central Limit Theorems for $\epsilon$-Independence'] Let $(Q_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be a sequence of symmetric $n\times n$ matrices with entries in $[-1,1]$. Assume that $w_{Q_n} \to w$ in the graphon sense. Let $s_1,\ldots,s_n \in \mathcal{A}$ be a mixed $q$-Gaussian system, for every $n \ge 1$. Then 
