{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:47:12.039142+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7103c709507eee56f55628ee423d2dc0c87d1650e6c0edad7479a238bd2956a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop-weak derivative of arXiv:2512.17727, 'Stochastic transport equation with L\'evy noise'] Suppose that $u:\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}$ is a locally Lebesgue essentially bounded function  and $b:\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}^d$ is a locally Lebesgue integrable vector field  such that the distributional derivative  $\operatorname{div} b :\mathbb{R}^d \to  \mathbb{R}$ is locally Lebesgue integrable.\\
 Then the   distribution $b\cdot D u $ defined in Definition (def-weak derivative of the paper) satisfies the following identity
\begin{equation}
 b\cdot  D u :=  -  u \,\operatorname{div} b +\operatorname{div}( u  b),
\end{equation}
where \\
(i) $u \,\operatorname{div} b$ is a point-wise product of $u$ and  $\operatorname{div} b $ and so is a locally Lebesgue integrable function,\\
(ii)  $u  b$ is a locally Lebesgue integrable vector field,\\
(iii) $\operatorname{div}( u  b)$ is the distributional divergence of $ u  b$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

