{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.037977+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a78cdf89c1d5105472e692a9d501323292dfe91bb3394501c3fa6c5834a528ed","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 (Semicircular element) of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] A self‑adjoint operator $W \in \mathscr{A}$ is called a \emph{semicircular element} with mean $0$ and variance $\sigma^2 > 0$ if its moments are given by the semicircular law $\mu_{\mathrm{sc}}$, i.e.,
