{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.906377+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7a0d708f7d8852f8059cbfc6992ecabc5859d693ba3b6b26ae5727ac9126a84","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:main1 of arXiv:2511.08479, '\Large From disordered systems to the Critical 2D Stochastic Heat Flow'] The Critical 2D SHF $(\mathscr{Z}_{s,t}^{\vartheta}(\mathrm{d} x , \mathrm{d} y))_{0 \le s \le t <\infty}$ is translation invariant in law:
\begin{equation*}
	(\mathscr{Z}_{s+\mathsf{a}, t+\mathsf{a}}^\vartheta(\mathrm{d} (x+\mathsf{b}) , \mathrm{d} (y+\mathsf{b})))_{0 \le s \le t <\infty}
	\stackrel{\rm dist}{=}
	(\mathscr{Z}_{s,t}^{\vartheta}(\mathrm{d} x , \mathrm{d} y))_{0 \le s \le t <\infty}
	\quad \ \forall \mathsf{a} \ge 0, \ \forall \mathsf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^2 \,,
\end{equation*}
and it satisfies the following scaling relation:
\begin{equation}
	(\mathscr{Z}_{\mathsf{a} s, \mathsf{a} t}^\vartheta(\mathrm{d} (\sqrt{\mathsf{a}} x) , \mathrm{d} (\sqrt{\mathsf{a}} y)))_{0 \le s \le t <\infty}
	\stackrel{\rm dist}{=}
	(\mathsf{a}\, \mathscr{Z}_{s,t}^{\vartheta+ \log \mathsf{a}}(\mathrm{d} x , \mathrm{d} y))_{0 \le s \le t <\infty}
	\quad \ \forall \mathsf{a} > 0 \,.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.932041+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7a40936f6808a762ce0cf0a099b719498b9d662892aee99ebbe7e546c5e5c5f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 25 (Fast merging of $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] Let $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$ be defined as the decreasing chains started from $k$ and $k'$, respectively, under the black-golden coupling $\mathbb{P}_{k,k'}$ of~\cref{defn:black-golden coupling}. 
    Then, the following assertions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item  There exists a constant $c \in (0,1)$ such that for all $k'\geq k\geq 1$ and all $A\geq 1$,
    \begin{equation*} 
        \Ppp{k,k'}{\xmer < A} \leq (1-c)^{s(A,k)}.
    \end{equation*}
        \item Furthermore, the total times $\mathtt{T} - \stmer$ and $\mathtt{T}' - \stmerpr$, representing the durations between merging and dying, tend to infinity in probability as $k, k' \to \infty$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.339576+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7acce55664090d3dda8783afb0e4edd3e2977c3feb6a6484df0b91c8c4a3fe5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:worst-case-md of arXiv:2411.09075, 'Weak Poincar{\'e} Inequalities, Simulated Annealing, and Sampling from Spherical Spin Glas'] Let $\pi$ be a distribution over $\Omega \subseteq \R^N$, and $\pi = \E_{\bz \sim \rho} \pi_{\bz}$ a measure decomposition of $\pi$ such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item for all functions $f$, $\E_{\bz \sim \rho} \Var_{\pi_{\bz}}[f] \ge \Cvar \Var_{\pi}[f]$, and
        \item Every $\pi_{\bz}$ satisfies a $\CPoi$-\Poincare~inequality with respect to Langevin diffusion.
    \end{itemize}
    Then, $\pi$ satisfies a $\CPoi\Cvar$-\Poincare inequality. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.690543+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7ae410829d3619de4f9eb490c6aea0ef477a70d14307e723d7903ffca1555b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop cont_image_jump of arXiv:2605.29073, 'Volterra clocks and their pure-jump limits: \\hitting times of curved boundaries'] Let $d_\mathrm{H}$ be the Hausdorff distance on $\mathbb{R}$. Consider $x^n=\phi^n\circ \tau^n$ and $x=\phi\circ \tau$, and suppose $(\phi^n,\tau^n) \rightarrow (\phi,\tau)$ in $D^\circ[0,T]$ with $\sup_{t\in D(\tau^n)}\Delta \tau^n(t)\rightarrow 0$. Then, for every $t\in D(\tau)$, we have
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty} d_{\mathrm{H}}\bigl(\hspace{1.2pt}x^n\bigl(B(t,\delta)\bigr) ,\hspace{1.2pt}\phi\bigl([\tau(t-),\tau(t)]\bigr)\bigr) \rightarrow 0\quad \text{as} \quad \delta \rightarrow 0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:43:16.147640+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7afca9215c4ac26985d41781814940d1b4f3a40da83f05bfcbc3c1e5be239d9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1 of arXiv:2604.13387, 'Multiradial Schramm-Loewner evolution: \\ Infinite-time large deviations and transience'] Fix a hull $K \subset \overline{\bD}$.  For any $x \in \bR$ such that $\dist(e^{\ii x}, K) > 0$, we have
\begin{align*}
\tfrac{1}{4} \, \sin \Big(\pi\min\Big\{\hmeas{\bD\smallsetminus K}(I^+_x; 0),\hmeas{\bD\smallsetminus K}(I^-_x; 0)\Big\}\Big)
\, \le \, | h_K'(x) |  \, \le \, e^{-\rcap(K)}  \, = \, \tfrac{1}{g_K'(0)} ,
\end{align*}
where $I^\pm_x$ are the two components of $\partial\bD \smallsetminus (K\cup\{e^{\ii x}\})$ adjacent to $e^{\ii x}$,
and $D_K := \hat{\bC}\smallsetminus (K \cup K^*)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:48:24.617911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7b1b942e6a1725f9fbef088c5cfff106d91463878585b209a97b0931aaa2899","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:general_OU of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] Let $\mathfrak{A}_\theta:\mathcal{S}_\theta\to\mathcal{S}_\theta$, $\mathfrak{B}_\theta:\mathcal{S}_\theta\to L^{2, \theta}([0, 1])$ {and let $\{\mathscr{W}_t, t\in[0, T]\}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_\theta'$-valued Brownian motion.} We say that an $\mathcal{S}_\theta'$-valued stochastic process $\{\mathscr{Y}_t, t\in[0, T]\}$ with continuous trajectories is a \textit{solution to the generalised Ornstein-Uhlenbeck equation}
\begin{equation}
    \partial_t\mathscr{Y}_t=\mathfrak{A}_\theta \mathscr{Y}_t +\mathfrak{B}_\theta \mathscr{\dot W}_t
\end{equation}
if, for any $H\in\mathcal{S}_\theta$,
the processes $\{\mathscr{M}_t(H), t\in[0, T]\}$ and $\{\mathscr{N}_t(H), t\in[0, T]\}$ defined by
\begin{align}
    &\mathscr{M}_t(H):=\mathscr{Y}_t(H)-\mathscr{Y}_0(H)-\int_0^t\mathscr{Y}_s(\mathfrak{A}_\theta H)\de s,
    \\&\mathscr{N}_t(H):=\mathscr{M}_t(H)^2-t\|\mathfrak{B}_\theta H\|_{L^{2, \theta}}^2\nonumber
\end{align}
are $\mathcal{F}_t$-martingales, where $\mathcal{F}_t:=\sigma\{\mathscr{Y}_s(H): s\le t, H\in\mathcal{S}_\theta\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7b36ad9df2f9a92f9fbad448e245cda230b1dcfbe1f4d309cec37fa60bf3ee5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 (\citeauthor{F10}, \citeyear{F10}, Eq.~(5.101)) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] For $\bm m=(m_1,\ldots,m_k)$ with $m_1\ge\cdots\ge m_k\ge1$, $\bm n=(n_1,\ldots,n_l)$ with $n_1\ge\cdots\ge n_l\ge1$, $k,l\in{\bf N}$, $m=|\bm m|$, and $n=|\bm n|$,
\begin{align}
\frac{\int_{\overline{\nabla}_\infty}P_{\bm m}P_{\bm n}\,d\text{PD}_\theta}{\int_{\overline{\nabla}_\infty}P_{\bm n}\,d\text{PD}_\theta}
&=C(\bm m)\sum_{r=0}^{k\wedge l}\bigg[\sum_{\substack{I\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}:\\ |I|=r}}\;\sum_{\substack{\gamma:I\mapsto\{1,\ldots,l\}\\ \text{\rm one-to-one}}}\nonumber\\
&\quad\;{}\dfrac{\prod_{i\in I}(m_i+n_{\gamma(i)}-1)!\,\prod_{i\in I^c}(m_i-1)!}{\prod_{i\in I}(n_{\gamma(i)}-1)!}\bigg]\frac{\theta^{k-r}}{(n+\theta)_{(m)}},
\end{align}
where $I^c:=\{1,\ldots,k\}-I$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.713097+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7b4c3a82aea0af72e60bb115463471484258625402c883d7e2d2603ccfe9756","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma SNDM of arXiv:2604.11761, 'The smallest singular value of signed random combinatorial matrices'] {MR4361906}}]
Consider any set  $S \subseteq \mathbb{S}^{n-1}$ , and fix any $ \alpha \in (0, \frac{1}{2}) $, $ \beta \in (0, \frac{\alpha}{10}) $. Assume $ n \geq 1/\alpha^2 $. Then there exists a (deterministic) net $ \mathcal{N} \subseteq S + \frac{4\beta}{\alpha} B_2^n $ such that
\begin{align*}
|\mathcal{N}| \leq N(S, \beta B_2^n) \cdot e^{C_{11} \alpha^{0.08} \log(1/\alpha) n},
\end{align*}
where $ C_{11} > 0 $ is an absolute constant. Moreover, for any (deterministic) $ n \times n $ matrix $ A $, the following holds: for every $ x \in S $, there exists $ y \in \mathcal{N} $ such that
\begin{align*}
\|(x - y)^{\top} A\|_2 \leq \frac{2\beta}{\alpha \sqrt{n}} \|A\|_{\mathrm{HS}}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.792609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7b73767122724268eacab5f90b1f8ccda451c508935e4eef27adad1b7b73542","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2607.10486, 'Weak martingale solutions to stochastic Navier-Stokes-Cahn-Hilliard system with transport '] If $\phi_{1,n},\, \psi_{1,n} \in H_{1,n}$ then 
