{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.329844+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7c45b46df700bae902386009472856a123d8c11dbb4c16d6f7d39106190ee9d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:2BG-Principle of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] Let $f:\Omega\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}$ be a local function such that $\psi_f(\rho ,\mathcal{E})=0$ and $\nabla\psi_f(\rho ,\mathcal{E})=0$ for fixed $\rho\in [0,1]$ and $\mathcal{E}\in [\rho ,\kappa\rho]$. Then, there exists a constant $C_{12}=C_{12}(\kappa ,\rho ,\mathcal{E} ,f)>0$ such that for any $\ell\ge 1$, any $t\ge 0$ and any measurable function~$v:[0,T]\longrightarrow\ell^2(\mathbb{Z} )$, we have
    \begin{multline}
        \mathbb{E}_{\rho ,\mathcal{E}}\left[\left( \int_0^t\sum_{x\in\mathbb{Z}}\tau_x\left\{ f(s)-\frac12 \overline{\mathbf{u}}^\ell (s)^\top\mathrm{Hess}(\psi_f)(\rho ,\mathcal{E})\overline{\mathbf{u}}^\ell (s)\right.\right.\right. \\
        \left.\left.\left. + \frac{1}{2\ell}\mathrm{Tr}(\chi\mathrm{Hess}(\psi_f))(\rho ,\mathcal{E})\right\}v_s(x)\,\mathrm{d} s\right)^2\right] 
        \le C_{12}\frac{\ell}{N^a}\int_0^t \|v_s\|^2\,\mathrm{d} s.
    \end{multline}
    where we recall that $\overline{\mathbf{u}}^\ell$ is the column vector whose coordinates are the averages $\xi_0^\ell -\rho$ and $\eta_0^\ell -\mathcal{E}$ defined in (equation eq:averages of the paper), and the compressibility matrix $\chi$ has been given in (equation eq:compressibilitymatrix of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:44.948915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7c5d8c44f7a1f9657ac9f5054484decc9e29e38c77be90308fb78f99f1836b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 5 (Conditional Local Law for $H(w)$) of arXiv:2606.24887, '2606.24887'] Let $ 0 < \delta \le 1/2$ be fixed and $X$ be a sparse matrix as in  \cref{defiX}. 
		Let $\# \in \{\mathrm{l}, \mathrm{b}, \mathrm{e}\}$.    
