{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.388391+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8244f27652a4a2fdf54022099add5e46223edaa0d5c8308e3037e675da74df4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi $K$-fuzz structure of arXiv:2607.28817, 'Limiting spectral distribution for the adjacency matrix of the Watts--Strogatz random grap'] We need a few definitions to be able to formally introduce the full $K$-fuzz graph model, denoted $F(K,p)$. See Figure~(fig:fullkfuzz of the paper) for an illustration.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Full $K$-path.} A full $K$-path is the infinite rooted graph with vertex set $\mathbb{Z}$ in which each vertex is adjacent to its $K/2$ nearest neighbors on either side.\\
        \item \textbf{Reduced $K$-path.} A reduced $K$-path is a rooted full $K$-path from which one of the $K/2$ edges connecting the root to its rightmost neighbors chosen uniformly at random is removed.\\
        \item \textbf{Full $K$-fuzz and reduced $K$-fuzz.} A full $K$-fuzz is obtained by starting from a full $K$-path and, for each node, independently rewiring each of its rightmost edges with probability $p$. Each rewired edge becomes a shortcut to a new full $K$-fuzz, producing an independent recursive copy. In addition, each node receives $\poisson(\frac{pK}{2})$ incoming shortcuts, each connecting to the root of a reduced $K$-fuzz. The reduced $K$-fuzz is defined analogously but starts from a reduced $K$-path.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:20:55.702465+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a825d19a69362e0c05a9d9615116ddccb6b85d7b2ab28a0aa91464013023389e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition PROP:REDUC of arXiv:2411.07840, 'Central limit theorem for the focusing $Φ^4$-measure in the infinite volume limit'] Let $F$ be a bounded, continuous function. Then, we have 
\begin{align}
&\int F\big(L(\phi-\pi_L(\phi)) \big) \rho_L(d\phi) \notag \\
&=\overline Z_{L}^{-1}\iiint\limits_{\mathcal{S}_{Q,x_0}\cap \mathcal{K}^L_\delta } F(L^\frac 12h(L\cdot))e^{L^3\mathcal{E}(Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, L^{-\frac 32}h)}
e^{\Lambda_L L^{\frac{3}{2}}\langle Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, \Re h\rangle} e^{\frac 12 \jb{Q^{\eta_L }_{x_0}, 3|\Re h|^2+|\Im h|^2 } } \notag \\
&\hphantom{XXXXXXXXXXXXX}\cdot \mathrm{Det}_L(h) \, \mu^\perp_{x_0,L^2}(\mathrm{d}h) \,\mathrm{d} \theta \, \mathrm{d}x_0+O(e^{-cL^{0+}})

\end{align}

\noindent
for some constant $c>0$. Here, the partition function $\overline Z_{L}$ represents the integral $\iiint$ over $S_{Q,x_0}\cap \mathcal{K}^L_\delta$ without $F$. The measure $\mu^\perp_{x_0, L^2}$ is the free field  as defined in (equation GFFNOR of the paper), corresponding to the mass term $m=\frac 1{L^2}$ \textup{(}see (equation massl2 of the paper)\textup{)}, 
and $\Lambda_L=\Lambda-\frac 1{L^2}$, where $\Lambda$ is the Lagrange multiplier from (equation Lagmul of the paper). The terms  $L^3\mathcal{E}(Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, L^{-\frac 32}h)$ and $\mathrm{Det}_L(h)$ are given by
\begin{align}
L^3\mathcal{E}(Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}, L^{-\frac 32}h)&=\frac 1{L^\frac 32} \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2} }  Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0}  |h|^2 \Re h \, \mathrm{d}x  +\frac {1}{L^3} \int_{\mathbb{T}_{L^2}} |h|^4 \, \mathrm{d}x  \\
\mathrm{Det}_L(h)&=\det\big( \Id-W_{x_0,\theta,L^{-\frac 32}h}\big).  

\end{align}

Here, the integral is taken over 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{S}_{Q,x_0}&=\big\{h\in L^2(\mathbb{T}_{L^2}):-\frac{1}{2}\|h\|_{L^2(\mathbb{T}_{L^2})}^2-L^{0+} < L^{\frac{3}{2}}\langle Q^{\eta_L}_{x_0},\Re h\rangle < -\frac{1}{2}\|h\|^2_{L^2(\mathbb{T}_{L^2})}\big\} \notag \\
\mathcal{K}^L_\delta&=\big\{ (x_0,\theta,h)\in \mathbb{T}_{L^2}\times [0,2\pi]\times V^{L^2}_{x_0,0}: \|h\|_{L^2(\mathbb{T}_{L^2}) }<\delta L^{\frac 32}  \big\}.

\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.256249+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8268847580d18f60144cf8ac99b6545d523dfb4cfbd83ea99429f22107ca584","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma spacetimeaverageagain of arXiv:2507.10545, 'KPZ equation from a class of nonlinear SPDEs in infinite volume'] \fsp Fix any {\small$\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}$} in (equation eq:qnformI2 of the paper). We have the following (with notation to be explained afterwards):

\begin{align}
N\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{0}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}[\tau_{\cdot}\bphi_{\t}]\mathbf{Z}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}&=\sum_{\substack{{m}=0,\ldots,\mathrm{M}\\0<|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\leq\mathfrak{n}_{\mathbf{Av}}}}\tfrac{1}{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|\ldots|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|}\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{1}}\ldots\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star(\mathbf{Av}^{\mathbf{T},\mathbf{X},\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}}_{\t,\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot})]_{\x}\\
&+\sum_{\substack{{m}=0,\ldots,\mathrm{M}\\0<|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\leq\mathfrak{n}_{\mathbf{Av}}}}\tfrac{1}{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|\ldots|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|}\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}\grad^{\mathbf{T},\mathrm{av}}_{\mathfrak{t}_{\mathbf{Av}}}\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{1}}\ldots\grad^{\mathbf{X}}_{\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}[\mathscr{S}^{N}\star(\mathbf{Av}^{\mathbf{T},\mathbf{X},\mathfrak{q}_{\n_{2}}}_{\t,\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot})]_{\x}\\
&+\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}.
\end{align}

\begin{itemize}
\item The parameter {\small$\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{O}(1)$} is a positive integer.
\item The {\small$\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}$} are deterministic and {\small$\mathrm{O}(N)$}. Moreover, {\small$\mathrm{b}'_{N,{m},\mathfrak{l}_{1},\ldots,\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}}=0$} if {\small$\min\{|\mathfrak{l}_{1}|,\ldots,|\mathfrak{l}_{{m}}|\}\geq2$}.
\item The term {\small$\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}\mathbf{S}^{N}]_{\t,\x}$} is a linear operator in {\small$\mathbf{S}^{N}$} evaluated at {\small$(\t,\x)$}, and it satisfies the deterministic estimate below as long as {\small$\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}(1)$} is sufficiently large:

\begin{align}
|\mathrm{Err}_{2}[\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\cdot}]_{\x}|\lesssim N^{-\frac32}\cdot N^{-1+\delta_{\mathbf{S}}}\sum_{|\w|\lesssim N^{1-\delta_{\mathbf{S}}}}\Big(1+\sum_{|\z|\lesssim1}|\bphi_{\t,\x+\w+\z}|^{\mathrm{C}}\Big)\cdot|\mathbf{R}^{N}_{\t,\x+\w}|\cdot|\mathbf{S}^{N}_{\t,\x+\w}|.
\end{align}

\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

