If $\mathbb{P} \in \mathscr{M}_{\gamma,\delta}$ and $\mathbb{P}(Y_0 = x) = 1$ for some $x \geq 0$, we write $\mathbb{P} \in \mathscr{M}_{\gamma,\delta}(x)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:49.905904+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8955ea9aabfacd0ba2908f651135fc682cc75a4a07d057001678d8686a530b5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:main0 of arXiv:2511.08479, '\Large From disordered systems to the Critical 2D Stochastic Heat Flow'] Let $\beta_N$ be chosen in the critical window (equation eq:sigma of the paper) for some $\vartheta\in \mathbb{R}$. As $N\to\infty$, the process of random measures
${\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}} ^{\beta_N}_N = ({\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}} ^{\beta_N}_{N;\, s,t}(\mathrm{d} x, \mathrm{d} y))_{0\le s \le t}$ converges in finite dimensional distribution
to a {\it unique} limit $\mathscr{Z}^\vartheta = (\mathscr{Z}^\vartheta_{s,t}(\mathrm{d} x, \mathrm{d} y))_{0 \le s \le t < \infty}$
called the \emph{critical $2d$ Stochastic Heat Flow},
which does not depend on the law of the disorder $\omega$ (assuming ${\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} [\omega]=0$, ${\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} [\omega^2]=1$, with finite exponential moments).
The first and second moments are
\begin{align}

	{\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} [\mathscr{Z}^\vartheta_{s,t}(\mathrm{d} x , \mathrm{d} y)]
	&= g_{t-s}(y-x) \, \mathrm{d} x \, \mathrm{d} y \,, \\

	\bbcov[\mathscr{Z}^\vartheta_{s,t}(\mathrm{d} x ,\mathrm{d} y),
	\mathscr{Z}^\vartheta_{s,t}(\mathrm{d} x' , \mathrm{d} y')]
	&= K_{t-s}^\vartheta(x,x'; y, y') \, \mathrm{d} x
	\, \mathrm{d} y \, \mathrm{d} x' \, \mathrm{d} y' \,,
\end{align}
where $g_t(\cdot)$ is the heat kernel on $\mathbb{R}^2$ and $K^\vartheta_{t-s}$ is defined by
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
	K_{t}^{\vartheta}(x,x'; y,y')
	&\,:=\, 4\pi \: g_{\frac{t}{2}}\big(\tfrac{y+y'}{2} - \tfrac{x+x'}{2}\big)
	\!\!\! \iint\limits_{0<a<b<t} \!\!\! g_{2a}(x'-x) \,
	G_\vartheta(b-a) \, g_{2(t-b)}(y'-y) \, \mathrm{d} a \, \mathrm{d} b \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $G_\vartheta(t) := \int_0^\infty \frac{e^{(\vartheta-\gamma)s} \, s \, t^{s-1}}{\Gamma(s+1)} \,\mathrm{d} s$
is a so-called \emph{Volterra function}, linked to the \emph{Dickman subordinator}
[CSZ19a]. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.751931+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a896f6e2b713ea78a3c0900bf938bbeb7e1762349f027c3f0ea24d0adc825768","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2609.00448, 'Equivariant formality and representation theory'] \begin{enumerate}
