        \end{align*}
        Above, $D_{L}$, respectively $D_{R}$, is the connected component on the left, respectively right, of $\gamma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.901260+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a8fc7d9cb30ce0398169cb94ad9e0185fadd2f87c66dc41b5ba22d8116bbbde7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2606.20908, 'On It\^o-Stratonovich formula for rough sheets'] For any $1$-increment $g\in\cac_{2}(V)$ such that $\delta g\in\cac_{3}^{1+}$,
  set $\delta f:=(\id-\Lambda\delta)g$. Then the increments of $f$ can be written as limits of Riemann type sums:
  \begin{equation*}
