{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.406030+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9cbc08cdf3f2cbbfc77f833cbdd6fd2f687d7ad53b254fcfefa93d5d58ebdb5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T: comparison of arXiv:2511.11036, 'Analysis of a class of Recursive Distributional Equations Including the Resistance of the '] Assume that $\mathcal{F}$ and $Q$ satisfy assumptions (equation A: elliptic of the paper) and (equation A: lipschitz of the paper).  If $F, G : \mathbb{R} \times [0,+\infty) \to \mathbb{R}$ are respectively a bounded upper and a bounded lower semicontinuous function such that $\partial_{t} F - \mathcal{F}( \partial_{x} F, \partial_{x}^{2} F ) + Q(F) \leq 0$ and $\partial_{t} G - \mathcal{F} ( \partial_{x} G, \partial^{2}_{x} G ) + Q(G) \geq 0$ in the viscosity sense in $\mathbb{R} \times (0,+\infty)$ and if $M$ is the constant defined by
		\begin{align*}
			M = \lim_{\delta \downarrow 0} \sup \left\{ ( F(x,0) - G(y,0) )_{+} \, \mid \, x, y \in \mathbb{R} \, \, \text{such that} \, \, |x - y| \leq \delta \right\},
		\end{align*}
	then, for any $t > 0$,
		\begin{align} 
			\sup \left\{ F(x,t) - G(x,t) \, \mid \, x \in \mathbb{R} \right\} \leq M e^{ L t}.
		\end{align}

	In particular, if $F$ and $G$ are respectively bounded viscosity sub- and supersolutions of (equation E: initial value problem appendix of the paper) for some fixed initial datum $F_{\text{in}}$, then $F \leq G$ pointwise in $\mathbb{R} \times [0,+\infty)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:07:35.010730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9cbc39c6624631aac5b9757fe0523e6b28e6b3054fe5aa7678e0356941d8204","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2609.02850, 'Canonical-row Chern flow on Bott--Samelson towers:\\ realizable-volume models for Schubert'] Let $Y$ be a finite-dimensional scheme and let $\Gamma$ be a finite directed multigraph.  Each
vertex $v$ carries a globally generated vector bundle $F_v$.  For every
ordinary edge $e:v\to w$, fix a short exact sequence
\begin{equation}
 0\longrightarrow E_e\longrightarrow F_w\longrightarrow F_v
 \longrightarrow0
\end{equation}
and an integral multiplicity $\kappa_e\in\NN$.  Fix also root multiplicities
$r_v\in\NN$, and define the weighted divergence
\begin{equation}
 b_v:=r_v+\sum_{e:\,t(e)=v}\kappa_e
          -\sum_{e:\,s(e)=v}\kappa_e.
\end{equation}
Assume $b_v\ge0$ for every vertex.  Then there is an equality in $K^0(Y)$,
\begin{equation}
 \left[\bigoplus_vF_v^{\oplus b_v}\right]
 =
 \left[
 \bigoplus_vF_v^{\oplus r_v}
 \oplus
 \bigoplus_eE_e^{\oplus\kappa_e}
 \right].
\end{equation}
Consequently the total Chern classes agree:
\begin{equation}
 c\!\left(\bigoplus_vF_v^{\oplus b_v}\right)
 =
 \prod_vc(F_v)^{r_v}
 \prod_ec(E_e)^{\kappa_e}.
\end{equation}
Taking the component in the common top degree gives
\begin{equation}
 \ctop\!\left(\bigoplus_vF_v^{\oplus b_v}\right)
 =
 \left(\prod_v c_{\rk F_v}(F_v)^{r_v}\right)
 \left(\prod_e c_{\rk E_e}(E_e)^{\kappa_e}\right).
\end{equation}
The bundle on the left is globally generated and is determined canonically
by the vertexwise divergence.  Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_v b_v\rk F_v
 =
 \sum_v r_v\rk F_v+\sum_e\kappa_e\rk E_e.
\end{equation}
No acyclicity assumption is required.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.018245+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9cd8a9b866ae5e3cd4fe2eba50551d67bed79259ae434c4a810ba14a9c29a0d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:massive_limit of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d \ge 1$. Suppose $D(x)$ has finite support $\mathcal{U}$.
Let $x \in {\mathbb R^d}$ be on the boundary of $\mathrm{Conv}(\mathcal{U})$.
Then as $z \to 0$, we have
\begin{equation}
\abs x_{z} = \mathrm{rad}(\mathcal{U}) + O(m_z^{-1}) .
\end{equation}
In particular, $\lim_{z\to 0} | \cdot |_z$ defines a norm on ${\mathbb R^d}$, with its unit ball being $\mathrm{Conv}(\mathcal{U}) / \mathrm{rad}(\mathcal{U})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:50:44.870353+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9cf4cdc96b1f1d2c64b5c6eb70a0e9ec4f92520e80ac45193e18f53ba5db65c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 (Composition law) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Cut-factorizable maps form a category.  If $\Theta$ and $\Psi$ are
