{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.339673+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9d24c6117fdc639418a584647f45515ff68c4dab6f8c3f6e6d000dd59441790","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: Airy bounds of arXiv:2606.19207, 'Extrema of cooling branching Brownian motion and related Gaussian fields'] There is a constant $C_{\mathrm A}<\infty$ such that, for every $n\geq 1$, $q>0$, and $x\geq 0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\lvert\psi_n^q(x)\rvert\leq C_{\mathrm A}\sqrt{q}\,x.
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, $\alpha_nn^{-2/3}\longrightarrow(3\pi/2)^{2/3}$. Consequently, for every $m>0$,
	\begin{equation}}
		\varrho(m)^2:=\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}
		\exp\{-2(\alpha_n-\alpha_1)m\}<\infty,
		\qquad \varrho(m)\longrightarrow 0\quad(m\to\infty).
	\end{equation}
	Finally, for every $L>0$, there is $C_L>0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		\psi_1^q(x)\geq C_L\sqrt{q}\,x,
		\qquad x\in[0,Lq^{-1/3}].
