{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.185710+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a9d5b4b0d04ea5df6eb5645b70d832dcc7495a62e36f4c886a0add2a1bc19240","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2606.30364, 'Distributional comparison for non-commutative infinitely divisible probability measures'] Let $\circledast \in \{ \ast, \boxplus ,\uplus, \rhd \}$. 

A probability measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}_{\infty}(\R)$ is said to be \emph{a compound $\circledast$-Poisson distribution with rate $ \lambda \geq 0 $ and jump distribution $\Poiss \in \mathcal{P}_{\infty}(\R)$} if for every integer $ {\ell} \geq 1$ we have that

