{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:04.859251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa0fd56d7df7b060fa748b7b04bf7e918cfe69331265ee7e3d3d741beefdb89b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 9 of arXiv:2511.21496, 'Optimal local central limit theorems on Wiener chaos'] Let $F, G \in \bD^{1,2}$. 
Let $\psi \colon \R \to \R$ be a continuously differentiable function with a bounded derivative. 
Then 
\begin{equation}
    \E[F\psi(G)] = \E[F]\E[\psi(G)] + \E[\psi'(G)\abra{DG, -DL^{-1}F}_{\fH}].
\end{equation}
Here, $L^{-1}$ is extended to a contraction operator on $L^2(\Omega)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.256830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa11192aa67057dfe2b0af1363ec29e64f46719002576f321ee2b5caa8e3ce1c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 55 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] For any $\delta>0$ and $n\in\N$,
there exists a constant $\g{c}{sle,n}=\g{c}{sle,n}(\delta)\in (0,\infty)$ such that
\begin{align*}
  \g{c}{sle,n}\alpha\leq \inf_{\substack{x,y\in F\\d(x,y)\geq \delta}}E_{x}[1-e^{-\alpha \sigma_{x,y}}]\wedge \inf_{\substack{z,w\in F_n\\d(z,w)\geq \delta}}E_{z}^n[1-e^{-\alpha \sigma^n_{z,w}}],\quad \alpha\in (0,\alpha_n).
\end{align*} 
Here, $\sigma_{x,y}$ and $\sigma^n_{z,w}$ are the commute time of $X$ between $x$ and $y$, 
and that of $X^n$ between $z$ and $w$ (recall its definition from \Cref{resis}, respectively).
Moreover, $\g{c}{sle,n}$ is given by
\begin{align*}
  \g{c}{sle,n}=\int_{0}^{T}(Ae^{-Bs}-1)^2e^{-\alpha_n s}ds.
\end{align*}
The definitions of $A,\;B,$ and $T$ are given below at \eqref{abtdef}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.048099+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa1167546c8e8d704ee3182d1262a8726d3b799eddec0f2a8f8e7e539a56c040","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 (Lemma 7.1 in \cite{BayrWu2022}) of arXiv:2608.19576, 'Convex order preservation for graphon mean-field systems'] Let $y: [0,\infty) \rightarrow [0, \infty)$ be a non-negative differentiable function. Suppose 
    \begin{equation*}
        y(t) - y(r) \leq -a_1\int_r^t y(s) \mathrm{d}s + a_2 \int_r^t \sqrt{y(s)} \mathrm{d}s + \int_r^t a_3(s) \mathrm{d}s, \quad \forall t>r \geq 0,
    \end{equation*}
    for some $a_1 >0$, $a_2 \in \mathbb{R}$ and non-negative and continuous function $a_3$. Then,
    \begin{equation*}
        y(t) \leq \max \Bigg\{y(0), \Bigg(\frac{a_2}{2 a_1}+\sqrt{\frac{\sup_{0\leq s\leq t} a_3(s)}{a_1}+\frac{a_2^2}{4 a_1^2}}\Bigg)^2\Bigg\}, \quad \forall t\geq 0.
    \end{equation*}
    In particular, $y(t) \leq \max \Big\{y(0), \frac{\sup_{0 \leq s\leq t}a_3(s)}{a_1}\Big\}$ if $a_2=0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.640523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa17291c8a9a6ed7e2057de3e26a92b8c3902d731ce86d794e4d66866455e09f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2605.31344, 'Subcritical sharpness for real-valued spin models'] Let $\beta>0$ and let $\Lambda\subset V$ be finite with boundary conditions $(\xi, \sfb)$ on $\Lambda$. For every increasing function $f: (\mathbb{R}^{+})^{\overline{\Lambda}} \times \{0,1\}^{\overline{E}(\Lambda)}    \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ and every $xy\in \overline{E}(\Lambda)$, we have
\begin{equation}

    \mathrm{Cov}_{\Lambda, \beta}^{(\xi, \sfb)} \left(f, \frac{2\sfa_x \sfa_y \omega_{xy}}{p_{xy}} - \sfa_x \sfa_y \right) \geq \frac{1}{2 \beta} \mathrm{Cov}_{\Lambda, \beta}^{(\xi, \sfb)}(f, g_{xy} \, \omega_{xy}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.687846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa1c583c161f5e92075c7e2f273ffba7704f07c1b860ab7cf0ce92a0a57846cd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 26 (Geometric axis and gate decomposition) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space, let $0\neq v\in V$, and let $g,a\in\Sl(V)$ satisfy
\[
g=a\e^v a^{-1}.
\]
Equip $V$ with an auxiliary Euclidean norm, denoted by $\norm{\cdot}_{\mathrm{Euc}}$, and set
\[
\mathcal{A}=a\mathcal{A}_v
 =\set{a\e^{tv}:t\in\R},
\qquad L_g(x)=gx,
\]
and let $p$ be the metric projection of $1$ onto $\mathcal{A}$. Then, for every $x\in\Sl(V)$,
\begin{align}
 d_{\infty}(x,gx)
 &=2d_{\infty}(x,\mathcal{A})+\norm{v}_{\mathrm{Euc}},
 \\
 \mathcal{A}
 &=\set{x\in\Sl(V):d_{\infty}(x,gx)=\norm{v}_{\mathrm{Euc}}}.

\end{align}
Thus $\mathcal{A}$ is the unique minimum-displacement axis of $L_g$ and is independent of the chosen factorisation of $g$. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
[1,g]=[1,p]\cup[p,gp]\cup[gp,g],
\end{equation}
where the three pieces meet only at $p$ and $gp$, and
\begin{equation}
gp=p\e^v,
\qquad
g=p\e^v p^{-1}.
\end{equation}
Finally,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}\cap[1,g]=[p,gp].
\end{equation}
The first and third pieces in \cref{eq:conjugacy-geodesic-decomposition} degenerate simultaneously.","status":"unverified"}

