{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.586024+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa539aa3347954624c5ad2cf4e253a9ed53e710c748b10b3def7478bf88136d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem charac of arXiv:2602.12713, 'GIG random matrices \\ and \\ a Yang-Baxter extension of the Matsumoto-Yor property'] Assume that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random matrices valued in $\Omega_+$ having strictly positive twice differentiable densities on $\Omega_+$. Let $\alpha,\beta\ge 0$ be two distinct numbers. Let 
		\begin{equation}
		\begin{cases} U=(\alpha X+Y)^{-1}(\beta X+Y)Y^{-1}
		\\
			V=(\alpha X+Y)^{-1}(\beta X+Y)X^{-1}.
			\end{cases}
		\end{equation}

		\bigskip
		If $U$ and $V$ are independent then there exist $\lambda\in\mathbb R$ and $a,b\in\Omega_+$ such that
			\begin{equation}
			X\sim\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\alpha a,b)\quad\mbox{and}\quad Y\sim\mathrm{MGIG}(-\lambda,a,\beta b)
		\end{equation}
		and
		\begin{equation}
		U\sim\mathrm{MGIG}(\lambda,\alpha b,a)\quad\mbox{and}\quad V\sim\mathrm{MGIG}(-\lambda,b,\beta a).
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

