{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.116032+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa8510d02ab5b5b601187b14b4790a815a30d43b4c480e0a9340d6c4d9c8102f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma-continuity-crossing-events of arXiv:2606.13147, 'Scaling limits of the single-curve interface and outermost loops in the planar random fiel'] For $Q$ a topological rectangle, denote by $\mathcal{C}^{\pm}(Q)$ the event that there is a $\pm$-interface joining two opposite sides of $Q$, without hitting $\partial Q$ otherwise. Then, for any $Q \subset \Lambda$,
