{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.298872+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aaa1003e7a54f106824c7666c960709bc6457e170dc6dcb1fed8a519a72ec4d2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Th:Catastrofe_aleatoria of arXiv:2605.24671, 'Immigration Processes with Binomial Catastrophes and Random Survival Parameters'] Consider the process $C_{\mathrm{cat}}(\lambda,\nu)$ with $\lambda>0$, and suppose that the probability distribution $\nu$ on $[0,1]$ is not concentrated at $1$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The process dies out almost surely.
    \item The expected number of catastrophes until extinction (which equals the expected time to extinction, see Remark~(E(T)=E(M) of the paper)) is finite and given by
    \begin{equation}
    \mathbb E(M_{C_{\mathrm{cat}}}) = -\frac{1}{\lambda} \frac{\mathcal{S}_\nu(\lambda)}{1 + \mathcal{S}_\nu(\lambda)} < \infty,
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathcal{S}_\nu(\lambda)$ is the analytic continuation of the alternating series
    \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{S}_\nu(\lambda) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{(-\lambda)^n}{\prod_{j=1}^n (1-\mathbb{E}[X^j])},
    \end{equation}
    with $X \sim \nu$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

