{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:43:43.468635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aac75ce7c03d15fa1f520890285e235b887501f5a6554d7ddb0b3ff5150c7361","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:CE(Dir) of arXiv:2509.04155, 'An Approach to Sub-Gaussian Heat Kernel Estimates via Analysis on Metric Spaces'] For a given $\delta > 0$, we say that $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ satisfies the \emph{cutoff energy condition} (eq:CE(Dir) of the paper) if there is a constant $C \geq 1$ satisfying the following. For all $x \in X$ and $R \in (0,\diam(X))$ there is a cutoff function $\xi \in \mathcal{F}$ for $B(x,r) \subseteq B(x,2r)$ such that
