{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.971522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab50968bc3732ed6fa93988a84982cf3f904a3d926e73e98c1176faf5d3ceb00","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:A of arXiv:2509.17319, 'Non-directed polymers in random environments with range penalties: the high-dimensional ca'] Let $\alpha\in (0,d)$ and $(\zeta,\gamma)\in R_3$ (see Figures (diagram2 of the paper) and (diagram3 of the paper)). Then $(S_n)_{0\leq n \leq N}$ has end-to-end fluctuations of order $N$ under ${\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} _N$, and as $N\to\infty$, we have the following convergence in distribution to a well-defined random variable, 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{ \beta_N N^{d/\alpha}} \log {\ensuremath{\mathbf Z}} _N  \stackrel{(d)}{ \longrightarrow} \hat{\ensuremath{\mathcal T}} _\infty:= \sup_{s\in {\ensuremath{\mathcal D}} , \hat{\mathrm{E}}\mathrm{nt}(s) <+\infty}\pi(s).
\end{equation}
Here
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathrm{E}}\mathrm{nt}(s):=\inf_{\varphi\in\Phi}\int_0^1 J_d((s\circ\varphi)'(t)){\ensuremath{\mathrm d}}  t,
\end{equation}
where $J_d(\cdot)$ is a rate function obtained by
\begin{equation}
J_d(x):=-\lim\limits_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{N}\log{\ensuremath{\mathbf P}} (S_N=\lfloor xN\rfloor)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\Phi:=\{\varphi:[0,1]\overset{\rm onto}{\longrightarrow}[0,1]: \varphi~\text{is non-decreasing and a.e. differentiable}\}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.206073+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab51cf8f165c0d55b9a11825da9d066c95e7e6ed9eaff257011105957af7daec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Propn 17 of arXiv:2604.27205, 'An Analysis of the Diaconis-Holmes-Neal Markov Chain Sampler Under Generalized Unimodal Un'] Suppose $\{R_l\}_{l = 1}^{r(n)}$ is the sequence of return times (in steps) of the intermediate returns contained in any overshoot. Then, with probability bounded below by a positive constant and for some constant $\tilde{c} > 4$,
