{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.146807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_acab3add971c75a99f9a2f42ceb091cc17e289c1cd70556ce17c378d9290868a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:jordan of arXiv:2606.24465, 'History estimation in random recursive trees:\\Pointwise approach via iterated Jordan cent'] For the Jordan-based estimator $\widehat{\sigma}_{J}$, there exist constants $c, C, \delta > 0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$, any $v \in [n]$ and any $S \in [1, \delta n/v]$, the upper tail satisfies
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{c}{S} 
        \ \leq\ \mathbb{P} \big( \widehat{\sigma}_{J}(v) \geq S v\big) 
        \ \leq\ \frac{C}{S}.
    \end{equation}
    The upper bound holds for all $S \geq 1$.

    Moreover, there exist constants $c', c'', C', C'', \delta' > 0$ such that for all $n \ge 1$, any vertex $v \in [n/2]$, and any $S \in [1, \min(\sqrt{v},\delta'v)]$, the lower tail satisfies
