{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.720317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ad944308e7d040c1f97fc2b682ef766dba2c0580adc28a398e5048cd30cd5dc0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:finite-rational of arXiv:2604.25108, 'Terminal Defects, Growing Multiplicity, and Variance Extremality\\in the Double Dixie Cup '] Let $A$ range over nonempty subsets of $[N]$, put $p_A=\sum_{i\in A}p_i$, and for $\alpha=(\alpha_i)_{i\in A}\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}^A$ write
\[
        |\alpha|=\sum_{i\in A}\alpha_i,
        \qquad
        \alpha!=\prod_{i\in A}\alpha_i!,
        \qquad
        p^\alpha=\prod_{i\in A}p_i^{\alpha_i}.
\]
Then for every integer $r\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
        \mathbb E\bigl[T_{m,p}^{(r)}\bigr]
        =r\sum_{\emptyset\ne A\subseteq[N]}(-1)^{|A|+1}
        \sum_{\alpha\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}^A}
        {\frac{p^\alpha}{\alpha!}}
        {\frac{(|\alpha|+r-1)!}{p_A^{|\alpha|+r}}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.962754+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ad95912dbfaad5d018df3de48be7c2dd089627c6187b8b4689855dd1b4b044df","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] The following holds for the optimal stopping problem in \eqref{eq:InfHorizonProblem_hom}: 
