{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:11:35.820846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ada88c49be2a529c5e530c30532967e401777e3db37ff62b35fe2ef2561745c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:vertical_decoupling of arXiv:2607.22908, 'Epidemic Phase Transitions in the Zero-Range Process'] There exist positive constants $C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:vertical_decoupling of the paper)}}$, $c_{\textnormal{\tiny (c:vertical_decoupling of the paper)}}$ and $C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:V of the paper)}}$ depending only on the dimension and $\Gamma_+$ and $\Gamma_-$, such that, for any two space-time boxes $B^1,B^2$ of side length $w>0$ with vertical distance $\dist_V = \dist_V(B^1, B^2) \ge C_{\textnormal{\tiny (C:V of the paper)}}$, any two non-decreasing bounded measurable functions $f_1, f_2: \mathbb{N}_0^{\mathbb{Z}^d \times \mathbb{R}_+} \to [0, 1]$ with supports in $B^1$ and $B^2$, any $\rho >0$, and any sprinkling increment $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$, we have
  \begin{equation}

    \begin{split}
