{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.882056+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_adb8a59268a93598975bd48faf3232374fab54efea8df9c04dbdf3f46d921923","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 42 (Conditional maximal identity~\cite{Nikeghbali2006essay}) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] Let $(M_t)_{t\ge0}$ be a nonnegative continuous-time \emph{local} martingale --- a martingale up to each of a sequence of stopping times increasing to infinity --- with $M_0=m>0$ and $\lim_{t\to\infty}M_t=0$ a.s., whose running supremum $M^\ast_t:=\sup_{u\le t}M_u$ has continuous paths, and write $M^\ast_\infty:=\sup_{u\ge0}M_u$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=2em]
\item For every $a>0$, $\PP\bigl(M^\ast_\infty>a\bigr)=\frac{m}{a}\wedge1$, and $m/M^\ast_\infty$ is uniform on $(0,1)$.
\item Suppose in addition that $M$ is strictly positive with no positive jumps, and let $\tau$ be an a.s.\ finite stopping time.
Write $M^\ast_{\ge\tau}:=\sup_{u\ge\tau}M_u$ for the \emph{future supremum} from $\tau$.  Then for every $a>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \PP\bigl(M^\ast_{\ge\tau}>a \,\big|\, \cF_\tau\bigr)=\frac{M_\tau}{a}\wedge1
  \qquad\text{a.s.}
\end{equation}
The ratio $M_\tau/M^\ast_{\ge\tau}$ is uniform on $(0,1)$ and independent of $\cF_\tau$, and $\log\bigl(M^\ast_{\ge\tau}/M_\tau\bigr)$ is a standard exponential variable independent of $\cF_\tau$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.060830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_adbb152759476cb6c2181f66cceb36444f4385a13299a84e45e99dba68f6b4db","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2607.02019, 'Critical branching processes in random environment with immigration and an application to '] In the critical randomised reproducing graph with $\beta>0$ and $\alpha=\delta$,
