{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.027216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_adeb83c2156cc02c5547920176144dc36cba3886a863dfca07ee91590fdfe522","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2606.06859, 'The rectangular finite free heat flow'] For monic degree-$d$ polynomials
    \[
    p(x) = x^d + \sum_{i=1}^d a_{2i} x^{d-i}, \qquad q(x) = x^d + \sum_{i=1}^d b_{2i} x^{d-i},
    \]
    their $(n,d)$-rectangular finite free convolution is
    \begin{equation} 
    (p \boxplus^n_d q)(x) = x^d + \sum_{k=1}^d x^{d-k} \sum_{\substack{i,j\ge 0\\i+j=k}} \frac{(d-i)!(d-j)!}{d!(d-k)!} \frac{(n+d-i)!(n+d-j)!}{(n+d)!(n+d-k)!} a_{2i} b_{2j},
    \end{equation}
    where we set $a_0 = b_0 = 1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

