Thus $W_k^{\mathrm{hom}}(h)=\Theta_k(h^{k-2})$ for fixed $k$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.872480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae19b73272b2dcfc286d834e878cddee9e4d73d2cde669436b76d077da06e88b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop transf_pant_to_alph of arXiv:2411.00629, '\Large Transformation of Stochastic Recursions and Critical Phenomena in the Analysis of t'] Suppose $\mathscr{X}(t)$ is a nonnegative binary-tree solution process of (equation markovsspantographeq of the paper), i.e.\ satisfying
\begin{equation}
\mathscr{X}(t)=\left\{ \begin{array}{*{35}{l}}
   0 & \text{if} & T_\theta\ge t  \\
   \mathscr{X}^{(1)}(\alpha (t-T_\theta))+\mathscr{X}^{(2)}(\alpha (t-T_\theta)) & \text{if} & T_\theta<t  \\
\end{array} \right.
\end{equation}
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item $w(t)=\mathbb{E}(\mathscr{X}(t))$, if finite for all $t$, satisfies (equation markovsspantographeq of the paper).
\item For any $\lambda\ge 0$, $X(t)=e^{-\lambda\mathscr{X}(t)}$ satisfies (equation alphrecursion of the paper) and 
$u(t)=\mathbb{E}\left(e^{-\lambda\mathscr{X}(t)}\right)$ satisfies (equation diffalpha-ricc of the paper).
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.557140+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae1b0d11a39c6d34f622a155e1b4f60e1ad4d72840500cc22964bf77deaeba3e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Dn_limit of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $w$ be a word of exponent $d\geq 1$, i.e. $w=\pi^d$ for some primitive word $\pi$. For any $g\geq 1$ and any tuple $(z_0,\dots,z_{g-1})\in \mathbb{C}^g$ we have the convergence in distribution
\begin{align}
		\lim_{L\rightarrow \infty}
	\lim_{n\rightarrow \infty}
		\frac{1}{\log L} D_n^w(L,g; z_0,\dots,z_{g-1})
=
		\rho(d,g; z_0,\dots,z_{g-1}).
	\end{align}
	Moreover, the convergence also holds in expectation, and in probability in the sense of Definition~(def:bivariate of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.919221+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae1c4bb84ccfe02cc9cdee0e12d720f67e08dabb8eb785743d469e2dac391416","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:KLLY of arXiv:2510.18392, 'Hyperuniformity of random measures, transport and rigidity'] {  [KLLY]}

   Let $  \mathscr  P = \{B_{ i};i\geqslant 1\}$ an equivariant fair partition of $ \mathbb{R}^{ d}$ with unit volume, and conditionally to $  \mathscr  P$, let $ X_{ i},i\geqslant 1$ independent points where $ X_{ i}\sim  \mathscr  U_{ B_{ i}}$. Then $ \mathsf{P}: = \{X_{ i};i\geqslant 1\}$ is a stationary hyperuniform point process.  Furthermore, its spectral measure is
\begin{align}

 \mathsf{S}(du) = (1- \mathbf E  | \widehat{ 1_{ B_{ *}}}(u) | ^{ 2})du
\end{align}
where $ B_{ *}$ is the a.s. unique cell containing $ 0$, and $ \mathsf{P}$'s  hyperuniformity exponent is at most $ 2.$ $ \mathsf{P}$ is $ 2$-hyperuniform if furthermore 
\begin{align*}
 \mathbf E \left[
\displaystyle\int_{B_{ *}}\|x\|^{ 2   }dx<\infty 
\right].
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:10.966420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae1edd8b67a4cf4e3e90d4c07d97389461102a594cdcadc3c70a2158f83a9275","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2606.12720, 'On McDiarmid's Inequality under Dependence \\ via Approximate Tensorization of Entropy'] {Concentration of the Maximum Cut}

    For a fraction $\epsilon \in (0,1)$ the maximum cut of the dependent ERG $G_{\mu^p, \Sigma}(X)$ fulfills 
    \begin{align*}
        \P\Parent{\Abs{\operatorname{MaxCut}(G_{\mu^p, \Sigma}(X))- \E[\operatorname{MaxCut}(G_{\mu^p, \Sigma}(X))]} \geq \epsilon \binom{n}{2}}
        \leq 2\exp\Parent{-\frac{2 \epsilon^2 \binom{n}{2}}{\kappa^\star(\Sigma)}}, 
    \end{align*}

    where $\kappa^\star(\Sigma) := \eigmax{D^{1/2} \Sigma D^{1/2}}$ with $D := \diagonal{\Sigma\inv}$ is the ATE constant for $\Sigma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.142084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae2033aed5c2d16141772c3724ff61811f1e11de4351dfdf796fb38f98840a0c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2604.05910, '\textbf{Well-posedness and Hurst parameter estimation for fluid equations driven by fracti'] The map $\Lambda$ is a contraction on $V^T$ choosing $T$ small enough.
That is \begin{equation}
  \Lambda: B_R(T) \rightarrow B_R(T)  
\end{equation}
and there exists $L < 1$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|\Lambda(\omega^{n,1}) - \Lambda(\omega^{n,2}) \|_{V^T} \leq L \|\omega^{n,1} - \omega^{n,2}\|_{V^T}, \quad  \hbox{where} \quad \omega^{n,1}, \omega^{n,2} \in B_R(T).
\end{equation}
i.e.
\begin{eqnarray}
\left\|\Lambda\left(\omega^{n,1}\right)-\Lambda\left(\omega^{n,2}\right)\right\|_{\left.C(0, T] ; \mathcal{B}_a\right)}+\left\|\Lambda\left(\omega^{n,1}\right)-\Lambda\left(\omega^{n,2}\right)\right\|_{C^{\gamma}\left([0, T] ; \mathcal{B}_{a-\gamma}\right)} \leq L\left\|\omega^{n,1}-\omega^{n,2}\right\|_{V^T}.
\end{eqnarray}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.905327+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae209720a552d1a3612d5182b0c8c63cb9372607217d05681a25132a08dc4fef","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:scale_shift_invar of arXiv:2411.11425, 'Non-equilibrium steady states with a spatial Markov structure'] The family $\{\Lambda^n\}_{n \geq 1}$ is 

 \begin{enumerate}
     \item  \textbf{Scale invariant} if for all $a,b, \gamma \in \mathbb{R}^+$, $n \geq 1$ and $\theta_1 \in I_{a,b}, \theta_2 \in I_{\theta_1,b},..., \theta_n \in I_{\theta_{n-1},b}$, 

     \begin{equation}
        \Lambda^n_{a,b}(\theta_1,...,\theta_n) = \gamma^n \cdot \Lambda_{\gamma \cdot a, \gamma \cdot b}^n (\gamma \cdot \theta_1,...,\gamma \cdot\theta_n).
    \end{equation}

    \item \textbf{Shift invariant} if, for all $a,b \in \mathbb{R}^+$, $\gamma > -(a \wedge b)$, $n \geq 1$ and $\theta_1 \in I_{a,b}, \theta_2 \in I_{\theta_1,b},..., \theta_n \in I_{\theta_{n-1},b}$

     \begin{equation}
        \Lambda^n_{a,b}(\theta_1,...,\theta_n) = \Lambda_{ a + \gamma, b + \gamma}^n (\theta_1 + \gamma,...,\theta_n + \gamma).
    \end{equation}
 \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.057223+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae2254051d2106f6ef9772e7487d3b5b9cd5ec623ff550a66969fbe1ca1a74f5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 32 of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] A function $\bar\xi:\R^\D_+\to\R$ is called a \textbf{regularization} of $\xi:\R^\D\to\R$ if the following conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  The function $\bar\xi$ agrees with $\xi$ on the intersection of $\R^\D_+$ with the closed unit ball in $\R^\D$.
    \item  The function $\bar\xi$ is Lipschitz and \textbf{proper} in the following sense: $\bar\xi$ is $\R^\D_+$-increasing and, for every $b\in\R^\D_+$, the map $\R^\D_+\ni a\mapsto \bar\xi(a+b)-\bar\xi(a)$ is also $\R^\D_+$-increasing.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.724704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae228990d86507e3d346e54b639f1b54aa7bea15da39b04cac2418a4b5c7ae69","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:many-PDEs_new_intro_summ of arXiv:2604.25851, '{Non-uniqueness of nonlinear Markov processes in the sense of McKean associated with parab'] Let $(u(t,\cdot))_{t>0} \subseteq  \mathcal P_* $ be a solution to the FPE (equation intro:nl-PDE of the paper) (in the sense of (def:FPE-sol of the paper)) with initial condition $\zeta \in \mathcal P$ and coefficients $(a, b): \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal P_* \to \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^d$ for some $\mathcal P_* \subseteq \mathcal P_{\textup{ac}} $ satisfying the integrability condition (equation eq:integrability_our_paper of the paper). Let $f: \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathcal D(f) \to \mathbb{R} $ with $\mathcal D(f) \subseteq \mathcal P_{\textup{ac}}$, be such that $(u(t,\cdot))_{t >0} \subseteq\mathcal D(f)$, and 
        $f(x,u(t,\cdot)) + a(x,u(t,\cdot))u(t,x) \geq 0 \,  u(t,x)dxdt\text{-a.e.,}$
        and that (equation eq:assumption_f_strong of the paper) holds.
        Then $(u(t,\cdot))_{t> 0}$ also solves
        \begin{equation}
            \partial_t u = \Delta \Big( \big(a(x,u) + \frac{f(x,u)}{u} \big)  u \Big) - \nabla \cdot \Big( \big( b (x,  u ) + \frac{\nabla f(x,u)}{u} \big) u\Big),
    \end{equation}
    where the new coefficients are defined on $\hat{\mathcal P}_* \coloneqq \mathcal P_* \cap \{ u \in \mathcal D(f): \, f(\cdot,u) \in W^{1,1}_{\textup{loc}}(\mathbb{R}^d)\}$, with the convention $\frac {f(\cdot,u)} {u} := 0, \,\frac{\nabla f(\cdot, u)}{u} := 0$ on $\{u = 0\}$, and there exists a weak solution $(X_t)_{t\geq 0}$ to the corresponding MV-SDE 
        \begin{equation}
        \begin{dcases}
