{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.680249+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae530a421471e2f46a6dc43d7507211cb6035ebbcf614c20efda085810a65e6c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma anisotropic:caff of arXiv:2412.09235, 'A semiconcavity approach to stability of entropic plans and exponential convergence of Sin'] Assume $\Sigma\succ 0$,  $\nabla^2U_\rho\succeq \alpha_\rho$ and $\nabla^2U_\nu\preceq\beta_\nu$ for some $\alpha_\rho>0$ and $\beta_\nu\in (0,+\infty)$. If $\nabla^2\varphi^{0}_\varepsilon\succeq -\Sigma+A_0\,\Sigma$ for some matrix $A_0\succeq 0$ commuting with $\Sigma$, then for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$ we have
    \begin{equation}
    \nabla^2\varphi^{n}_\varepsilon\succeq -\Sigma +A_n\,\Sigma\quad\text{ and }\quad\nabla^2\psi^{n}_\varepsilon\preceq -\Sigma+ B_n\,\Sigma\,,
    \end{equation}
    where $(A_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ and $(B_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ are two sequences of positive semidefinite symmetric matrices iteratively defined via
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
        B_{n}=\Sigma (A_n\Sigma+\varepsilon\alpha_\rho)^{-1}\,,\\
        A_{n+1}=        \Sigma(B_n\Sigma+\varepsilon\beta_\nu)^{-1}\,,
    \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    and converging to 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
A_\infty =&\,-\frac{\varepsilon\alpha_\rho}2\Sigma^{-1}+\biggl(\frac{\varepsilon^2\alpha_\rho^2}{4}\Sigma^{-2}+\frac{\alpha_\rho}{\beta_\nu}\biggr)^{\nicefrac12}\,,\\
B_\infty=&\,-\frac{\varepsilon\beta_\nu}2\Sigma^{-1}+\biggl(\frac{\varepsilon^2\beta_\nu^2}{4}\Sigma^{-2}+\frac{\beta_\nu}{\alpha_\rho}\biggr)^{\nicefrac12}\,.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:59.504980+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ae551e6abda28599caedc54a06afecbfc7fd4021b4b81f5b8fc11e91f9beaf1d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 88 of arXiv:2605.10150, 'Rough path theory and an introduction to rough partial differential equations'] Let $y \in D(A^2)$ be arbitrary. Then we have
