{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:04:39.212776+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aeb1866b161b6b5fed274d78d7fd1d0319dfb18c738855c22eaf45b831cd6f8e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.13687, 'Multiplicative chaos from random multiplicative functions'] There exists a constant $C>0$ such that for all $n\in\N$, we have 
\begin{align}
  V_n \leq C/n\,,
\end{align}
and the number of blocks in $I_n$ is bounded by $C \cdot \cut_n^{-1}$.
\begin{proof}
Let $F(x) \coloneqq \sum_{p\leq x} |f(p)|^2$.
Applying Riemann--Stieltjes integration by parts, we get:
\begin{align*}
    V_n &= \int_{(e^n,e^{n+1}]} \frac{1}{x} \d F(x)
    = e^{-(n+1)} F(e^{n+1}) - e^{-n} F(e^n) + \int_{e^n}^{e^{n+1}} \frac{F(x)}{x^2} \d x\,.
\end{align*}
Now, since $|f|^2 \in P_{\theta}$ (see \eqref{eq:PNT}), we have $|F(x)| \ll x/\log x$. The triangle inequality then implies
\[
    V_n \ll \frac{1}{n+1} + \frac{1}{n} + \int_{e^n}^{e^{n+1}} \frac{1}{x \log x}\d x \ll \frac{1}{n}\,.
\]
We can then bound the number of blocks in $I_n$ using this and \eqref{eq:complete-light-block-variance-bound}:
\[
1+ \frac{V_n}{\cut_n} \ll 1+ \frac{1}{n \cdot \cut_n} \ll  \cut_n^{-1}
\]
where the $+1$ comes from the possible existence of an incomplete block in $I_n$.
\end{proof}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.717921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aeb43c4484671905967f59d1cfdae0e79ca41c2b6ef1de3d6a2e8316672f0643","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2609.01166, 'Nonparametric inference for density-dependent McKean--Vlasov diffusions'] Assume \(Y_1,\ldots,Y_n\) are i.i.d. from \(\pi_0^M\) and the likelihood ratios satisfy, for all \(\xi\in\cX_m\), 
\(
 \gamma_M \le \pi_0^M/\pi_\xi^M \le \gamma_M^{-1}
\)
with some 
\(\gamma_M\in(0,1)\). If a data-dependent \(\widetilde\Xi_{n,M}\in\cX_m\) satisfies
\begin{equation}
\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\log\pi_{\widetilde\Xi_{n,M}}^M(Y_i)
 \geq
\sup_{\xi\in\cX_m}\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\log\pi_\xi^M(Y_i)-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{opt},M},
\end{equation}
then with probability at least $1-\delta$,
\begin{align}
\KL(\pi_0^M\Vert\pi_{\widetilde\Xi_{n,M}}^M)
 \leq
C_1\inf_{\xi\in\cX_m}\KL(\pi_0^M\Vert\pi_\xi^M)
 &+C_2\frac{B_{\gamma_M}(\mathcal H_m(\ve,M)+\log(2/\delta))}{n} \nonumber\\
 &\quad +C_3\omega_m(\ve,M)+C_4
 \varepsilon_{\mathrm{opt},M}
\end{align}
with some positive constants \(C_i\), \(i\in\{1,2,3,4\}\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.505961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aeb4ddc46ebf01f293577a8b63e8c9e6fdce79383d9eecca75a07aaf19aaa212","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 39 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] Let $u=Kw$ be the velocity corresponding to the vorticity solution, with the
unit viscosity rescaling used above.  There is a number $\eta_0>0$ such that,
for every $T<\infty$, $m<\infty$, $C_0<\infty$, and $2<\zeta<3$, there are
$R<\infty$ and $C<\infty$ for which
\begin{align}
 &\mathbf E_{u_0}\left[
  \exp\left(C_0\int_0^T\|u_t\|_{H^\zeta}\,\dd t\right)
  \sup_{0\le t\le T}(1+\|u_t\|_{H^{b+1}}^2)^m
  \right]                                                      \notag\\
 &\hspace{25mm}\le C(1+\|u_0\|_{H^{b+1}}^2)^R
                 \exp(\eta_0\|u_0\|_{H^1}^2).

\end{align}
Equivalently, in the original vorticity variables, for every
$m,r\in[1,\infty)$,
\begin{align}
 \mathbf E_w\left[1+\|w\|_{C([0,T];H^b)}^m\right]^r
 \le C_{T,m,r}(1+\|w\|_{H^b}^2)^R
                    \exp(\eta_0\|w\|_2^2).

\end{align}
For $s_*<\gamma<\alpha_{\rm H}/2$ and every $q<\infty$,
\begin{align}
 \mathbf E\left[
  \sup_{0\le t\le1}(1+\|u_{S+t}\|_{C^\gamma})^q
  \;\middle|\;\mathcal F_S\right]
 \le C_q(1+\|u_S\|_{C^\gamma})^{R_q}

\end{align}
after every finite stopping time $S$ for which $u_S\in C^\gamma$.
Moreover, for every fixed $t_{\rm v}>0$,
\begin{align}
 \mathbf E\left[
  (1+\|u_{S+t_{\rm v}}\|_{C^\gamma})^q
  \;\middle|\;\mathcal F_S\right]
 \le C_{q,t_{\rm v}}
  (1+\|u_S\|_{H^{b+1}}^2)^{R_{q,t_{\rm v}}}
  e^{\eta_0\|u_S\|_{H^1}^2}.

\end{align}
The same estimate holds for the supremum over a compact interval bounded
away from $S$.  The maximal and exponential estimates also hold
conditionally after a finite stopping time, with the state at that time on
the right hand side.","status":"unverified"}

