{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.236680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af310c4323235d5900e49b922d8544a6314aaf9d48c55f50ed775db38aab5208","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma scaling limit of the homogeneous partition functions of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	Recall the definitions~(equation eq: def of the continuum homogeneous partition function of the paper) of the (free and conditioned) continuum partition functions~\({\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{t,\hat{h}}\) and \({\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{t,\hat{h}}^{c}\).
	If $\lim_{N \to +\infty} \frac{M_N}{N} = t \in (0,1]$, then 	
	\begin{equation*}
		\lim_{N \to +\infty} Z_{M_N,h_N} = {\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{t,\hat{h}} := E_{\alpha}(\hat{h} t^{\alpha}),
		\qquad \text{and} \qquad
		\lim_{N \to +\infty} \frac{Z_{M_N,h_N}^c}{u(N)} = {\ensuremath{\mathcal Z}}_{t,\hat{h}}^{c} := \alpha t^{\alpha - 1} E_{\alpha}'(\hat{h} t^{\alpha}),
	\end{equation*} 
	These convergences hold uniformly in $t$ on every compact subset of $(0,1]$.
	Furthermore there exists a constant $C=C_{\hat{h}}$ such that for every $1 \, \leqslant\, M \, \leqslant\, N$, 
	\begin{equation}{}
		Z_{M,h_N} \leq C \quad \text{and} \quad Z_{M,h_N}^{c} \, \leqslant\, C u(M). 
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:22:20.952422+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af31748898fa09d03ff5967211334a6685ee10d323368e2f8a0670360a138cc7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Suppose $X$ is a weak solution to \eqref{eq: intro quenched dynamics} with Gaussian disorder $\bfJ=\bfG$. Then $X$ satisfies the following SDE, which admits a unique strong solution:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
dX_t^i &= - U'(X_t^i) dt + \frac{\beta^2}{N} \bigg[X_t^{\top} \int_0^t Q_s X_s dW^i_s \bigg] dt + dW^i_t, \quad i =1,\ldots, N, \quad t \in (0, T), \\
Q_t&=\Big(I+\frac{\beta^2}N \int_0^t X_sX_s^\top \,ds\Big)^{-1},\\
    X_0 &\sim P_0^N,
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
where $W = (W^i)_{i = 1}^N$ is a standard $N$-dimensional Brownian motion with respect to the filtration generated by $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.161170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af331fe5b0cfda3fbd837f68a57e089657878b669cea2f539cb9ebe8ec56d357","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma quotient-we of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $G$ be a compact Lie group, and let $f\colon X\to Y$ be a $G$-equivariant map of $G$-free Hausdorff spaces. Then $f$ is a (non-equivariant) weak homotopy equivalence if and only if the induced map $f/G\colon X/G\to Y/G$ on orbits is so.
    \begin{proof}
        By Lemmas~(lemma:quotient-map-is-fib of the paper) and~(lemma:quotient-pb of the paper), the commutative square
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                X\arrow[d,"f"']\arrow[dr,pullback]\arrow[r] & X/G\arrow[d,"f/G"]\\
                Y\arrow[r] & Y/G
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        is a pullback in $\textbf{\textup{Top}}$ and the horizontal maps are Serre fibrations. Thus, this square defines a homotopy pullback in the $\infty$-topos $\Spc$. As $Y\to Y/G$ is surjective on $\pi_0$, i.e.\ an effective epimorphism, the claim follows from [HTT]*{Lemma 6.2.3.16}.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.336961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af34fed68bf679c2054f633ac8fb9755f455ed1412b7df03d9f4a2fb0561aa1a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:relEntropyDensity of arXiv:2601.19630, 'Mass generation for the two dimensional O(N) Linear Sigma Model in the large N limit'] The following bound holds
 \begin{equation}
    \sup_{N,L \in \mathbb{N}}\frac{1}{L^{2}}\mathcal{H}(\nu^{N }_{L} \mid \mu^{\otimes N}_{m,L} ) \lesssim C_{r.e.}(\lambda,\beta).  \nonumber 
