{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.471929+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af9b28096fcca927dc7c0f71fd4dc7909bcae777309d87b1b9ce9be2cfb31503","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 42 (Same-field Wick completion) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Let $\cH$ be a separable real Hilbert space, let $\cH_\C$ be its
complexification, and let $W$ be a real isonormal Gaussian process over
$\cH$.  Scalar multiple integrals on $\cH_\C^{\otimes m}$ are defined by
complexification as in Definition~\ref{def:scalar-multiple-integral}.  Define
the algebraic symmetric kernel space
\begin{equation*}
  \mathcal W_{\mathrm{alg}}^{(m)}
  :=\operatorname{Sym}_m\bigl(\cH_\C^{\otimes_{\mathrm{alg}}m}\bigr)
    \otimes_{\mathrm{alg}}\conj{\cC}
    \otimes_{\mathrm{alg}}\cE.
\end{equation*}
Through $\Theta_{\cC,\cE}$, its elements are precisely the symmetric
operator-valued kernels of finite-dimensional support from
Definition~\ref{def:finite-rank-multiple-integral}.

Choose labelled copies $\cH^{(1)},\ldots,\cH^{(m)}$ of the real
Hilbert space $\cH$ and orthogonal isomorphisms
$J_\nu:\cH\to\cH^{(\nu)}$.  Denote their complex-linear extensions to
$\cH_\C\to\cH_\C^{(\nu)}$ by the same symbols.  On
$\mathcal W_{\mathrm{alg}}^{(m)}$, define
\[
  \operatorname{Dec}_m
  :=J_1\otimes\cdots\otimes J_m
    \otimes\Id_{\conj{\cC}}\otimes\Id_{\cE}.
\]
For $2\le r<\infty$, set
\begin{equation}

  \|K\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}}
  :=\|\operatorname{Dec}_mK\|_{\mathfrak T_{m,r}}
  =\max_{S\subset[m]}
   \|\Flat_S(\operatorname{Dec}_mK)\|_{\Sch_r}.
\end{equation}
Denote the Banach completion of
$(\mathcal W_{\mathrm{alg}}^{(m)},\|\cdot\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}})$ by
\begin{equation*}
  \mathfrak W_{m,r}(\cH;\cC,\cE).
\end{equation*}
Proposition~\ref{prop:completion-functoriality} shows that different choices
of labelled copies produce canonically isometric completion spaces.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:39:11.681219+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_af9e15c00481bc7eb2bfc33221537722936b6f5df8e8fb56c7b0d751293d2495","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 46 (Least-state theorem for norming quadratic responses) of arXiv:2608.04993, '2608.04993'] Let \(\tau=(\eta;\mathfrak I,\mathcal S_1)\) be accessible, let \(Z_\tau\) be
its canonical group-valued state, and let \(L=(L_A,L_Q)\) satisfy
\eqref{eq:norming-lower-bounds}.  For every \(\cP\)-causal state
\((\mathsf S,S)\),
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 S\text{ realizes the restarted step-two signature}
 &\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Z_\tau\preceqsc S,\\
 S\text{ jointly realizes }\mathfrak T_{\tau,L}
 &\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Z_\tau\preceqsc S.
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
If \(\Psi_{\rm sig}\) is a signature realization, then
\(\mathcal D_\tau^{\rm sig}\Psi_{\rm sig}\) is the corresponding factor to
\(Z_\tau\).  If \(\Psi_L\) is a triangular realization, the factor is
\(\mathcal D_{\tau,L}\Psi_L\).  Conversely, a factor \(R:S\to Z_\tau\)
produces the two realizations \(\mathcal J_\tau R\) and
\(\mathcal O_{\tau,L}R\).  Thus the signature and the norming triangular
bundle carry the same realized second-order information, and \(Z_\tau\) is the
unique least sufficient state up to stable-causal equivalence.","status":"unverified"}

