{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.437830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_afb61e1aba110cde90f1af6ea0a83f391680261095678f7eb5e579b038872d66","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition mixingdef of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] Let $t_N$ be a non-decreasing sequence of positive integers. A Markov chain $([N], P_N)$  is said to be \emph{Short-to-Long Mixing} at time \(t_N\) if its $n$-step transition probability $p_n(x,y)$ satisfies the following two conditions:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,label=(B\arabic*)]
    \item  \textbf{(Short-time average mixing)}  
    There exists a constant $\gamma \ge 1$ and $N_0 > 0$ such that for all $N \ge N_0$ and all $x, y \in [N]$,
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{1}{t_N} \sum_{n=1}^{t_N} p_n(x, y) \le \frac{\gamma}{N}.
    \end{equation}

    \item  \textbf{(Long-time uniform mixing)} 
    There exists  $\delta \in (0, 0.1)$, such that for all $n \ge t_N$ and all $x, y \in [N]$,
    \begin{equation}
        \left| p_n(x,y) - \frac{1}{N} \right| \le \frac{\delta}{N}.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
The additional assumption that  $\delta = \delta_N \to 0$  may be imposed   when necessary.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:13:22.519212+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_afb6b36be44b70e4017b52c049ecc72af6d0a3e63eba26d8d07cc76249fcbdbf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Plaquette count of arXiv:2505.16585, 'Expanded regimes of area law for lattice Yang--Mills theories'] We will refer to functions $K : \mc{P}_\Lambda^+ \ra \N$ or $J : \mc{P}_\Lambda \ra \N$ as plaquette counts. Our convention will be that the letter $K$ will denote an upper bound on the number of copies of each plaquette in our system, in that for each $p \in \mc{P}_\Lambda^+$, $K(p)$ is an upper bound on the total number of copies of $p$ and $p^{-1}$. Whereas when we use the letter $J$, we will think of $J(p)$ as specifying the exact number of copies of each oriented plaquette $p \in \mc{P}_\Lambda$. 

Given plaquette counts $K : \mc{P}_\Lambda^+ \ra \N$ or $J : \mc{P}_\Lambda \ra \N$, we define $\mrm{supp}(K) := \{p \in\mc{P}_\Lambda^+ : K(p) \neq 0\}$, and similarly for $\mrm{supp}(J)$. We refer to these sets as the supports of $K, J$.

Additionally, we will often abuse notation and write $B$ for the plaquette count $\mc{P}_\Lambda^+ \ra \N$ which assigns every plaquette $p \in \mc{P}_\Lambda^+$ the value $B$. It should always be clear from context whether $B$ represents a number or a plaquette count.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.406209+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_afb70d68512fd9c864c52f029ad980a91ba2104bce2047959ebdba75dd66adba","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2604.06516, 'From stochastic individual-based models to free-boundary Hamilton-Jacobi equations'] Let us consider $t\geq 0 $ and $f\in AC[0,t]$ such that $I_t(f)<+\infty$ and such that for all $s\leq t$, $F_s(f)>0$. Almost surely, for all sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$, 
