{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.530581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0217695437b4478ce385924d05236f3445e9ad363751f8d7bd90729c27571b6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_PRM_filtration of arXiv:2505.24043, 'Weak solutions of Navier-Stokes Equation with purely discontinuous L\'evy Noise'] Let $(Y,\mathscr{Y})$ be a measurable space and let 
\begin{equation}
    E = \mathbb{R}_+ \times Y\mbox{ and }\mathscr{E} = \mathscr{B}(\mathbb{R}_+) \otimes \mathscr{Y}.
    \end{equation}
    Let $(\Omega , \mathscr{F} , \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space and $\mathbb{F}=(\mathscr{F}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a filtration on $(\Omega,\mathscr{F}, \mathbb{P})$ satisfying the usual conditions.
A measurable function
\begin{equation}
\eta\colon \Omega \to M_{\overline{\mathbb{N}}}(E)
\end{equation}
is called a Poisson random measure over
$(\Omega , \mathscr{F} , \mathbb{F} , \mathbb{P})$  if and only  it satisfies the conditions  (i) and (ii) in Definition (def_PRM of the paper) as well as  the following two  additional  conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(iii)] For all $A\in \mathscr{Y}$ and $t \geq 0$ the
function
\begin{equation*}
\eta((0,t] \times A)\colon \Omega \ni \omega \mapsto \eta(\omega)((0,t] \times A) \in \overline{\mathbb{N}}
\end{equation*}
 is $\mathscr{F}_t$-measurable.
\item[(iv)] For all $A\in \mathscr{Y}$ and $(s,t)\in [0,\infty)^2_+$ the random variable  $\eta((s,t] \times A)$ is independent of the $\sigma$-field $\mathscr{F}_s$.
\end{enumerate}

A Poisson random measure $\eta$  as in (equation eqn-PRM-02 of the paper) will be called a time homogeneous Poisson random measure on  $(\mathbb{R}_+ \times Y, \mathscr{B}(\mathbb{R}_+) \otimes \mathscr{Y} )$ over $(\Omega , \mathscr{F} , \mathbb{F} , \mathbb{P})$ if and only if  its intensity measure $\mu$ is of the form $\Leb \otimes \nu$, where  $\Leb $ is the Lebesgue measure on 
the $\sigma$-field $ \mathscr{B}(\mathbb{R}_+)$ and $\nu$ is a  $\sigma$-finite measure on the $\sigma$-field $\mathscr{Y} $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.025373+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0232c85b721889f86e1e16faa004d53a7bc047ffd283835d92c30b16a7bae61","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2608.26501, 'Explicit Bounds on the Entropy of Piecewise H\"{o}lder Graphon Models'] Let $W$ be piecewise H\"{o}lder with polytope domain, and write
\[W(x,y) = \sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x,y) \1_{A_t}(x,y),\]
where for each $t$, $f_t$ is H\"{o}lder continuous with constant $L_t$ and exponent $\alpha_t$. Define $L=\max_{t\in\set{1,\dots,T}}L_t$ and $\alpha = \min_{t\in\set{1,\dots,T}}\alpha_t$. Further, let 
\begin{equation}\rho = \max_{t,t'\in\set{1,\dots,T}}\sup_{\substack{(x,y)\in A_t\\ (x',y')\in A_{t'}}}|f_t(x,y)-f_{t'}(x',y')|.\end{equation}
Define
\[\Delta_n = \frac{1}{\binom n2}H(G(n,W)) - \iint_{\Omega^2}h\big(W(x,y)\big)\dd x\dd y.\]
Then
\begin{equation}0\leq\Delta_n\leq\frac{dn\log m}{\binom n2} +a_1 \frac{\log m}{m^\alpha}+a_2 \frac{1}{m^\alpha}+a_3\frac{1}{m}+a_4\frac{1}{m^2}\end{equation}
where $m = Cn^\gamma$ for $\gamma>0$ with $C\geq L^{1/\alpha}\ell\sqrt{2d}$ and the constants $a_i$ are given by 
\begin{align*}
a_1 &= \alpha L (\ell\sqrt{2d})^{\alpha}\ell^{2d}, & a_2 &=L(\ell\sqrt{2d})^{\alpha}(1/\ln 2 - \log(L(\ell\sqrt{2d})^{\alpha}))\ell^{2d},\\
a_3 &= 4d(T-1)\ell^{2d}\theta+2|\partial\K|\ell, & a_4 &= |\partial\K|^2\ell^2,
\end{align*}
where $\theta = -\rho\log\rho+\rho/\ln 2$. In the special case that $\Omega = [0,1]$, we can take $a_3 = 4d(T-1)\ell^{2}\theta$ and $a_4 = 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.831222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b02421d828e1a0a7413cb8763ca67b9d85ff4040ea25ec06719c6b6eea729938","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:correlation_init of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] For all $H > 0$ large enough,
