{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:49:06.311420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3012f5521276b5478fd3c4dd2608166ab1bb06cd21bbbafcec6183589fa116b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: prelim of arXiv:2601.07759, 'The value of random zero-sum games'] Let $M\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times m}$ be a random matrix with i.i.d. standard Gaussian entries (cf. Section (sec:main of the paper)), or $M\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ be a uniformly random orthogonal matrix with $m=n$ (cf. Section (sec:orth of the paper)). With probability one, the solution $({\boldsymbol x},{\boldsymbol y},v)$ of Equation (equation eq:vonNeumann of the paper) is unique and the following equalities hold: 
\begin{align*}
    C &= \{j\in [m] : y_j>0\} = \{j\in [m] : ({\boldsymbol x}^TM)_j = v\},\\
    R &= \{i\in [n] : x_i>0\} = \{i\in [n] : (M{\boldsymbol y})_i = v\},
\end{align*}
where $C$ the set of supporting columns and $R$ the set of supporting rows have the same cardinality $|C|=|R|$.  We also have, denoting by $(M_{RC}, {\boldsymbol x}_R,{\boldsymbol y}_C)$ the restrictions of $(M,{\boldsymbol x},{\boldsymbol y})$ to rows $R$ and columns $C$, that $M_{RC}$ is almost surely invertible and that
\begin{equation}
    {\boldsymbol y}_C = v M_{RC}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{1}}\quad\text{and}\quad  {\boldsymbol x}_R = v \boldsymbol{\mathbf{1}}^T M_{RC}^{-1} \quad \text{with} \quad v = \frac{1}{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{1}}^T M_{RC}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{1}}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

