{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.324583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b33105eae937e064313d816b663eee30f7f8f82906b844943266dba518782e81","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop-conjugate of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] Let $X = \{X(n) \}_{n\in\mathbbm N_0}$ be a positive regular, non-singular $d$-type branching process (Definition~(def-bp-basic of the paper)) with generating function $f$. Assume that the extinction probability started from any state is one. 
Let $A \in [1,\infty)^d$ solve $f(A) = A$. Define a new branching process $\widehat X = \{\widehat X(n)  \}_{n\in\mathbbm N_0}$ with offspring distribution determined by
\begin{equation} 
\widehat{\mathbbm E}_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ F(X_1(1) , \dots, X_d(1))   \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] 
 = \frac{1}{A_k} \mathbbm E_k\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft[ F(X_1(1),\dots,X_d(1)) \prod_{j=1}^d A_j^{X_j(1)} \aftergroup\egroup\originalright] ,\quad\forall k\in \{1,\dots,d\}    
\end{equation}  
for every bounded measurable function $F : \mathbbm N_0^d \to \mathbbm R$. Then the extinction probability for $\widehat X$ started from an individual of type $k$ is $1/A_k$, and the $\widehat{\mathbbm P}_k$-conditional law of $\widehat X$ given the event that it eventually becomes extinct is the same as the $\mathbbm P_k$-law of $X$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.883379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b334b06bca723e920f7037c85c72de4ea146646956a911eafe15e5caef57d124","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:ATmetric of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] If $\mathcal{X}$ is non degenerate, the AT metric is given for every $x\in M$, $v\in T_xM$ by 
