{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.814480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3c1882c84e616d2a1680abab08b4a5e55fb99576a80cbd6824a36bd965be20f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:type_path_induced of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Fix $s_0,s_1 \in S$. Consider any rooted tree $\mathbb T$ with vertex types in $S$. For any vertex $v \in \mathbb T$ with $T_{p(v)}=s_0, T_v=s_1$, and a type path/ray $\pi=(s_0\rightarrow s_0s_1 \rightarrow s_0s_1s_2\rightarrow \dots)$, we define the subtree $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ of $\mathbb T$ to be spanned by the vertices
    \begin{align*}
        \{v\}\cup \left(\cup_{i=2}^{|\pi|}C(e;\pi_{(i)})  \right),
    \end{align*}
    where $e$ is the directed edge $(p(v),v)$. We naturally think of $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ to be a rooted tree, rooted at $v$. We call $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ the subtree of $v$ in $\mathbb T$ induced by $\pi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.718132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3c3706859fdbb5cd3aa1323a7fb1b3dc0b0bf36c75b13921f4490af8e81599c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition l_corollary of arXiv:2604.16206, 'Extrapolation of max--stable random fields with Fr\'echet marginals'] The optimal weight vector ${\hat{\mathbf{\lambda}}} =(\hat{\lambda}_1,\ldots,\hat{\lambda}_n) $ from Definition (Xp_definition of the paper) has the form 
