{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.002251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b4261ffea8690aa1b73c25cee8b5432de00f057fef0642a67b85b625b05c74e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem_typical_distances of arXiv:2606.27357, '\textbf{Typical distances in high-genus triangulations}'] Fix $\theta \in (0,\frac{1}{2})$ and $\displaystyle \frac{g_n}{n} \to \theta$ as $n \to +\infty$. Let $e_n^1,e_n^2$ be two independent oriented edges chosen uniformly at random on $T_{n,g_n}$. Let $x_n^1$ and $x_n^2$ denote their respective starting points. Then, we have
    \begin{align}
       \frac{d_{T_{n,g_n}}(x_n^1,x_n^2)}{\log(n)} \xrightarrow[n \to +\infty]{(\mathbb{P})} D_{\theta},
