{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.308616+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b48f5f92c1131f60c947a29d3233cb9cffd79d45e031fb9c9a9d95d49290bf09","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:jakubowski of arXiv:2607.13748, 'Load Balancing with Individually Heterogeneous Servers'] The sequence of probability measure-valued processes $(\eta^n:n\in\mathbb{N})$ with sample paths in $\mathbb{D}_{\mathcal{P}(E)}[0,T]$, where $E$ is a metric space, is tight provided that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(J1)] for every $\bar{\rho}>0$, there exists a compact set $\mathcal{K}_{\bar{\rho}}\subset\mathcal{P}(E)$ such that
$$\liminf_{n\to \infty}\mathbb{P}(\eta^n(t)\in\mathcal{K}_{\bar{\rho}}\ \text{for all }t\in[0,T])\ge 1-\bar{\rho}.$$
\item[(J2)] there exists a family $\mathcal{F}$ of real-valued maps on $\mathcal{P}(E)$ that separates points, is closed under addition, and such that $(H(\eta^n):n\in\mathbb{N})$ is tight in $\mathbb{D}_\mathbb{R}[0,T]$ for every $H\in\mathcal{F}$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:08.998694+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b492368ae3c84f3198a9749ea555a234608d2d87abdc0a15a876a909fa9e02b0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defn:wallblock of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] A \emph{walk} of $(\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_m}^{N_m})^{k_m} \cdots (\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_1}^{N_1})^{k_1}$ consists of $\vec r=\{r_j\}_{j=1}^N$, with each $r_j:\llbracket 0, Q_m\rrbracket\to \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}$, satisfying the following conditions.
Denote $\mathscr{H}(t)=\sum_{j=1}^N r_j(t)$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $|\mathscr{H}(t)-\mathscr{H}(t-1)|=1$ for each $t\in \llbracket 1, Q_m\rrbracket$, $\mathscr{H}(t)\ge 0$, and  $\mathscr{H}(0)=\mathscr{H}(Q_m)=0$.
        \item For each $\ell\in \llbracket 1, m\rrbracket$ and $t\in \llbracket Q_{\ell-1}+1, Q_\ell\rrbracket$, we must have one of the two cases:
        \begin{itemize}
        \item Either, $r_{i_\ell}(t)=r_{i_\ell}(t-1)\pm 1$ and $r_j(t)=r_j(t-1)$ for all $j\ne i_\ell$;
        \item Or there exists one and only one index\footnote{This corresponds to the third option, i.e., $O(t)=\top$, in (defn:tie of the paper).} $j\ne i_\ell$, such that $r_j(t)\neq r_j(t-1)$ .
    Besides, in this case, necessarily $\mathscr{H}(t)=\mathscr{H}(t-1)-1$, and $j\le N_\ell$, and the differences $r_j(t)-r_j(t-1)$ and $r_j(t)-r_{i_\ell}(t-1)+1/2$ are of opposite signs.

        \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

