{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.240632+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b55b2fa23d99a45c51af9244a9128a87f005b6d45d307ce4ea8c08141d5c4f35","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:positive-cells of arXiv:2608.29719, 'Persistence Pairing Graphs: Tracking Changes of Selected Homology Bases'] A cell $\sigma_i$ is called \emph{positive} if the $i$-th reduced column satisfies $R_i=0$, and \emph{negative} if $R_i\neq0$. If $\sigma_i$ is a positive $q$-cell, its \emph{selected birth cycle} is the $q$-chain
