{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.902659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56a8ab9eeef23405b476698fa5429d07d4239131e7008cc5aba3703998c1a47","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:optimalweightoperator of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\mathbf{X} \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ be a matrix with the full singular value decomposition 
$\mathbf{X}= \mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}} \diag (\boldsymbol{\sigma}) \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^\top$, 
where $\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}  \in \R^{d_1 \times d_1}$ and $\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}} \in \R^{d_2 \times d_2}$ are orthogonal
and $\diag (\boldsymbol{\sigma}) \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ is the rectangular diagonal matrix with the extended vector of singular values $\boldsymbol{\sigma} \in \R^{D}$ of $\mathbf{X}$ on the diagonal.
Then the  \emph{weight operator}  $\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}: \R^{d_1 \times d_2} \to \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ 
is defined by
\begin{equation} 
	\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{Z}) = \mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}} \left[\mathbf{H}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon} \circ (\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{Z} \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}})\right] \mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
\end{equation}
for each $\mathbf{Z} \in \R^{d_1 \times d_2}$.
Here $\mathbf{H}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon}$ is the 
weight operator core matrix of (def:weightcore of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.456292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56b58b5dbc35d337f410f3ef22826797b0d58016a42cdad60758169a94ef453","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 15 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Assume Hypothesis \ref{hyplin}. Then, there exist some $\delta_1 \in (0, \;1]$ and some function $g: \mathbb{N} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ satisfying $\lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} g(n)=0$, such that
	\begin{align} 
\forall \alpha,\beta \in S, \; \forall n \in \mathbb{N}_+, \; \forall	0 \leq s \leq t \leq T, \quad	\mathbb{E}_{\nu_{\rho}} \big[ \big\{ \mcb{B}_t^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) - \mcb{B}_s^{n, \alpha , \beta}(H) \big\}^{2} \big] \leq  C (t-s)^{1 + \delta_1} g(n). 
	\end{align}
	In the last line, $C$ is some constant independent of $n$, $s$ and $t$.

	In particular, the sequence of stochastic processes $\big\{ \big( \mcb{B}_t^{n}(H) \; \big)_{0 \leq t \leq T}, \; n \in \mathbb{N}_+ \big\}$ is tight with respect to the uniform topology of $\mathcal{C} \big( [0, T], \mathbb{R} \big)$, due to the Kolmogorov-Centsov Theorem. Moreover, any of its limit points is identically equal to zero.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.188987+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b56fd0e110513926f14c0d57ec019b11b6273fb744128e0a38fc949527226645","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2606.30938, 'Poissonian potential measures for refracted-reflected L\'evy processes'] Let $p,q \geq 0$ and $\lambda,\gamma> 0$. Then, for $x,b \in [0,a]$, we have that 
			\begin{align}
				\E_x \Bigl( \ee^{-q T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda} - p \int_0^{T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda}} 1_{\left\{U_s<b\right\}} \mathrm{d} s} \; \1_{\{T_{a,U}^{+,\lambda} < T_{b,U}^{-,\gamma}\}}\Bigr) &=  \frac{\overline{z}_b^{(q+\gamma_p, -\gamma_p)}(x)}{\varphi_{q+\lambda} \int^\infty_0 \ee^{-\varphi_{q+\lambda}y}\overline{z}_b^{(q+\gamma_p, -\gamma_p)}(y+a) \dd y},  

			\end{align} 
			where $\overline{z}_b^{(\cdot, \cdot)}$ is defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:SecondGenFunc-RefractedZTilde1}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.158367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b57049059fcb247d9f2af110084cec2f43d422dec28b83f0a86d038f8fd430e9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor uniqueness-maps-to-O(n) of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $f_1,f_2\colon {\textup{O}}(m)\to {\textup{O}}(n)$ be homomorphisms. Assume that for every block diagonal matrix
    \begin{equation}
        A=\begin{pmatrix}
            A_1\\
            &\ddots\\
            &&A_r
        \end{pmatrix}
    \end{equation}
    where $A_i\in {\textup{O}}(m_i)$, $1\le m_i\le 2$, $m_1+\cdots+m_r=m$, the two matrices $f_1(A)$ and $f_2(A)$ are conjugate in ${\textup{GL}}(n,{\mathbb{C}})$. Then $f_1$ and $f_2$ are homotopic in $\textup{Glo}$.
    \begin{proof}
