{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.160895+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b59f700b735aec5aafaa6d349c2811b3216be6422bee37f0eb39fb47f25ee0df","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Brown-eagleson theorem 7 of arXiv:2604.16002, 'Normal approximation for iterated inner functions'] Consider the following triangular array of real-valued random variables on a probability space $(\Omega, \cal F, \mathbb P)$:
		\begin{align}
		\begin{pmatrix}
 			X_{1,1} & 0 & 0 & \cdots \\
			X_{2,1} & X_{2,2} & 0 & \cdots \\
			X_{3,1} & X_{3,2} & X_{3,3} & \cdots \\
			\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots
		\end{pmatrix}
	\end{align}
		Let $\{\mathcal{F}_{N,n}: 0\leq n \leq N\}$ be a family of $\sigma$-algebras such that for each $N\geq 1$ fixed, 
		\begin{align}
			\cal F_{N,0} \subset \cal F_{N,1} \subset \cdots \subset \cal F_{N,N} \subset \cal F, 
		\end{align} 
		and for any $1\leq n\leq N$, 
		\begin{align} 
			X_{N,n} \text{ is $\cal F_{N,n}$-measurable}, \quad \E\big[ X_{N,n} | \cal F_{N,n-1}\big]=0.
		\end{align}
		Define
		$$
			V^2_N:= \sum_{n=1}^N \E\big[X_{N,n}^2| \cal F_{N,n-1}\big],\quad  m_N:= \max_{1\leq n\leq N} \E\big[X_{N,n}^2| \cal F_{N,n-1}\big].
		$$ 
		If the following three conditions are satisfied as $N\to \infty$: 
		\begin{itemize}
			\item [(C1)] $m_N \stackrel{p}{\longrightarrow} 0$;
			\item [(C2)] $V^2_N \stackrel{p}{\longrightarrow} \sigma^2$ (constant);
			\item [(C3)] For any $\varepsilon>0$, $$\sum_{n=1}^N \E[X_{N,n}^2 I\big(|X_{N,n}| \geq \varepsilon\big)| \cal F_{N,n-1}] \stackrel{p}{\longrightarrow}  0,$$
		\end{itemize}
		 then the $N$-th row sum of \eqref{eq-martingale-array} converges in law to $\cal N(0,\sigma^2)$ as $N\to \infty$: $$X_{N,1}+ \cdots + X_{N,n}\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} \cal N(0,\sigma^2).$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.346406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5a2d10196b9dd5a9f93c80b148463295527cb9a4267e202dbf5930199995709","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2607.15125, '2607.15125'] (Flajolet - Odlyzko \cite{flajolet1990singularity})  
Assume that, with the sole exception of the singularity $z=1$, $F(z)$ is analytic in the domain $\Delta(\epsilon,\theta) \coloneqq \{z : \abs{z} \le 1 + \epsilon,  \abs{\mathrm{Arg}(z-1)} \ge \theta \}$ where $\epsilon > 0$ and $\pi/2 <\theta < \pi$. Assume further that as $z$ tends to 1 in $\Delta$,
     \begin{equation*}
         F(z) \sim G(z),
     \end{equation*}
     Then, as $n\to\infty$, the nth Taylor coefficient of $F(z)$ satisfies 
     \begin{equation}
         [z^n]F \sim [z^n]G
     \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.212428+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5aa4b722ef97d3d8bf10c044bc6a04d3b41b5787ab86d8c11fb41b8ce21ea32","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 ({\cite[Lemma 5) of arXiv:2604.27519, 'Quantitative homogenization of the maximal action of curves in a Brownian potential'] {OPW}}]
