{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.596059+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5d8853f6e67b5d1e0bf6a05a03bf2dde096875227b55676f1100729e17d4f57","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition EHI:d:ehi of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] We say that $X$ satisfies the \emph{elliptic Harnack inequality} ($\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}$) if there exist constants $C \geq 1$ and $\kappa \in (0, 1)$ such that for all $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and $r>0$, if a function $h$ is non-negative everywhere and harmonic with respect to $X$ on $B(x_0, r)$, then
    \begin{equation}
        C^{-1} h(y) \leq h(x) \leq C h(y)
    \end{equation}
    for all $x, y \in B(x_0, \kappa r)$.

    Given a set $S \subseteq (0, \infty)$, let us say that $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \in S)$ holds if there exist $C$ and $\kappa$ such that (equation EHI:e:ehiDef of the paper) holds for all $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^d$, $r \in S$, all functions $h$ that are non-negative everywhere and harmonic on $B(x_0, r)$, and $x, y \in B(x_0, \kappa r)$. Let us also write $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \leq 1)$ for $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \in (0, 1])$, $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \geq 1)$ for $\hyperref[EHI:d:ehi]{\mathrm{EHI}}(r \in [1, \infty))$, etc.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:53.182301+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5da768bcd942640c5815f844163ea9ceeadeb6a542fd901f8a5186ad3208f5a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 3 (Proposition 5.2 in \cite{BV93}) of arXiv:2606.29683, 'Scaling limit theorem for mixed free and Boolean convolution powers'] For any $\mu\in\calP$, there exist unique $b\in \R$ and a finite measure $\tau$ on $\R$ such that 
    \begin{align}
        F_{\mu}(z) 
        =  b+ z - \int_\R \frac{1+xz}{z-x}\,\tau(\mathrm{d} x).
    \end{align}
    Conversely, if an analytic function $F:\C^+\to\C^+$ admits a representation of the form \eqref{PN}, then there exists a unique $\mu \in \calP$ such that $F=F_{\mu}$ on $\C^+$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:02:17.907564+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5dbb679d24284c61c75376f53c7413699eabb5fb49552d4363595173f64ace2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:prodWF of arXiv:2602.08591, 'The Yang--Mills measure on compact surfaces as a \\ universal scaling limit of lattice gau'] Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\Gamma_1,\Gamma_2$ be two closed conic sets in $T^*M\setminus \underline{0}$,   assume that the convex sum $\Gamma_1+\Gamma_2:=\{(x;\xi_1+\xi_2); (x;\xi_1)\in \Gamma_1,(x;\xi_2)\in \Gamma_2\}$ does not meet the zero section $\underline{0}$. 
Then the wedge product
$$ T_1,T_2\in \mathcal{D}^\prime_{\Gamma_1
 } \times \mathcal{D}^\prime_{\Gamma_2} \mapsto T_1\wedge T_2 $$
 is bilinear hypocontinuous. In particular, it is sequentially continuous. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.026070+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5dc6de3f6ae4e666b06a23b092d082c5e6a84da05e1510013b462afa374f8cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2608.26509, '2608.26509'] Let \(H\) be a real or complex Hilbert space. Suppose that
