{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:11.411203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5de99f31720bc0fa295bee576c21231ad907a65eed9b5020657bb79f7705a6b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 (Selected phase subfamily versus global class-wide cover) of arXiv:2606.07931, 'Pointwise Complexity for Gaussian Fields:\\Upper Envelopes, Algorithmic Lower Bounds, and '] In the coarse charts of Theorem~\ref{thm:phase-atlas-rg}, let \(\mathcal T_{\rm full}\) be a finite observable class and let \(\mathcal T_a\subseteq\mathcal T_{\rm full}\) be the subfamily relevant to phase \(a\).  Define
\[
\ell_a(x)^2:=\langle v_x,(H_a^{\rm RG})^{-1}v_x\rangle,
\qquad
L_a:=\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_a}\ell_a(x),
\qquad
L_{\rm glob}:=\sup_{b\in\mathcal A}\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_{\rm full}}\ell_b(x).
\]
For an atlas prior \(q\in\Delta(\mathcal A)\), with \(q(a)>0\), the selected phase validation is
\begin{equation}
\nu_{C,a}\!\left\{
\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_a}|Z_{a,x}|
\le
C L_a\sqrt{\log\frac{2|\mathcal T_a|}{\delta q(a)}}
\right\}
\ge 1-\delta q(a).
\end{equation}
If the phase and subatlas structure is ignored, the full class-wide singleton cover gives only
\begin{equation}
\nu_{C,a}\!\left\{
\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_{\rm full}}|Z_{a,x}|
\le
C L_{\rm glob}\sqrt{\log\frac{2|\mathcal T_{\rm full}|}{\delta}}
\right\}
\ge 1-\delta .
\end{equation}
There are non-log-concave Gaussian-mixture phase atlases for which, on a selected phase with \(q(a)\) bounded below,
\[
\mathbb E\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_a}|Z_{a,x}|\asymp\sqrt{\log K},
\qquad
\mathbb E\sup_{x\in\mathcal T_{\rm full}}|Z_{a,x}|\asymp\sqrt{\log(NK)}.
\]
Hence the full-cover scale can be strictly larger.  If \(q\) is uniform over all \(N\) phases, the selected bound already pays \(\log N\); in that case this particular atlas advantage disappears.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:34:45.615092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5e03a35e89198a45677df08400cd39d537f4ad205387717b8bed5b8a4e6e241","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2609.01791, 'Minimum $b_2$ of symplectic fillings of lens spaces'] A \textit{left-right sequence} is a (possibly empty) finite string of ``L''s and ``R''s. A left-right sequence associated with a sequence of deep blowups is the string of ``L''s and ``R''s recording whether the blowup happened at the left side or right side of the trough for each blowup.

We read a left-right sequence from left to right in chronological order. Thus $RRL$ means: first perform a deep blowup on the right of the trough, then another on the right, and finally one on the left.

Given a left-right sequence $S$, we define the tuple $apply(S)$ to be the adjusted value tuple obtained by applying the corresponding sequence deep blowups on the trough in the adjusted value tuple $(1,-1,1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:46:00.357335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5e1d285723b8eff43bf84045476bb0fd8ed15a901237c0d16583308d6eb488b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:slepian of arXiv:2607.20956, 'Sharp moduli of continuity for Gaussian fields and stochastic PDEs via correlation bounds'] Let $Y$ and $Z$ be centered $\mathbb{R}^m$-valued Gaussian random variables.
