{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:44:17.355177+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b70df2692ae7f699483a81cf376e70b70a7a19cfe1c6e1ed309679e4a8a7aacd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:def-ftb of arXiv:2608.23554, 'Provably adaptive sampling with uniform and remasking \\ discrete diffusion models'] Let \(t \geq 0\) and \(b \in \cV\). Define \(\cF: \bR_{\geq 0} \times \cV \to [0, 1]\) as follows:
    \begin{equation*}
    \cF(t, b) \coloneqq \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t} \Pr(X_t^i = b) + \Pr(X_t^i = b) = \sum_{a \in \cV} \cQ(a, b) \Pr(X_t^i = a).
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.557329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b70effab5e04a5d005c93888f94c60c97589f4964afbd76e0d1c739a9e3a04fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm: prob of A_alpha of arXiv:2411.10296, 'Parking on a random rooted binary tree'] Let $T_\infty$ be a BGW(2,1/2) tree, conditioned on non-extinction, which is constructible as in Subsection 2.1. Assume that an independent Po$(\alpha)$ number of cars arrive at each of the vertices of $T_\infty$. Let $A_\alpha$ be the event that all of the arriving cars can park on the $T_\infty$. Then
    \[\mathds{P}(A_\alpha) = \begin{cases}
        e^{\alpha/2} \sqrt{\frac{\alpha^2 - 4\alpha + 2}{2(1-\alpha)}} & 0 \leq \alpha \leq 2-\sqrt{2} \\
        0 & \alpha > 2-\sqrt{2}.
    \end{cases} \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.123580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7105f1bd8b6f1e004b70043fd7ff5dc848c47a5ba320c025bcdca838d7c81ca","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 10 of arXiv:2605.27713, 'Lower path regularity in all dimensions'] Suppose that the process $X=(X_t)_{t\in [0,T]}$ satisfies Assumption \ref{a1} (i), let $q'\geq \max(Hd,1)$ and $\gamma<\frac{d}{q'}$. Then for any $0 < u < v\leq T$ and any $n \geq 1$, we have
		\begin{multline}
			\int_{[u, v]^n} \Big(\int_{(\mathbb{R}^{d})^n}  \prod_{k=1}^n\psi_\gamma(\Vert \xi_k\Vert)^{q'}\Big\lvert \mathbb{E} \exp \Big( i \sum_{j = 1}^n \langle \xi_j, X_{t_j}\rangle \Big) \Big\rvert^{q'} d\xi\Big)^{\frac{1}{q'}} dt  \\
			\leq c_7^n n^{\left(H\theta  + \frac{Hd}{q'}\right)n}(v-u)^{\left(1-\frac{Hd}{q'}\right)n}[\log(e+(v-u))]^{2 n d}
		\end{multline} 
		with a constant $c_7 > 0$ depending only on $T$, $d$, $\gamma$, $c_0$, $H$ and $q'$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.342801+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7118d1e44c22c5d7f1feaa54e3d7c1fa20ca5aafc84a74fc2f6824ec230e8d5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] \begin{enumerate}
        \item Let $\eps > 0$. Then, the subgraph $K[{V_{\leq(1-\eps)}}]$ is a union of connected components of $K$, each of which is a tree supported on a vertex set of size at most $\frac{1}{\eps}$.
        \item Let $k \geq 2$, and denote by $\Ker_k (K)$ the set of vertices inside the $k$-core of $K$. Then, $V_{\geq k} \subseteq \Ker_k(K) \subseteq V_{\geq \frac{k}{2}}$.

        \item Let $\rho_m = \max_{X \subseteq V} \dfrac{|K[X]|}{|X|}$. Then, $\rho_m = \max_{v \in V} w_K(v)$, and the set $X = V_{\geq \rho_m}$ maximises $\dfrac{|K[X]|}{|X|}$.

    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.300500+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b7121feb2131b3b09b0045c44f0f653774058df4fa39e4b44df758b283ede324","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:interchange_sups of arXiv:2605.26048, 'Classification of the eternal solutions and multiple coalescing shocks in the KPZ fixed po'] Let $(f_\alpha)_{\alpha \in \mathcal I}$ be any indexed collection of functions $f_\alpha:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty)$, and define the function $f:\mathbb{R} \to [-\infty,\infty]$ by
    \[
    f(y) = \sup_{\alpha \in I} f_\alpha(y).
    \]
    Then, for all $t > s$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}$, we have 
    \begin{equation} 
    \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f,s) = \sup_{\alpha \in \mathcal I} \mathfrak{h}_t(x;f_\alpha,s).
    \end{equation}
    Consequently, if $\varphi:\mathfrak{X} \to [-\infty,\infty)$ is such that $b(x,t) := (W \star \varphi)(x,t) < \infty$ for all $(x,t) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, then $b$ is an eternal solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.691049+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b71a5207ea82c539f007733f59763772a4778432e8d20ca5e8348e035cb635d5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 of arXiv:2607.29534, 'Expected signatures via partial integration, coordinate change and symmetrization'] We recursively define a graded sequence $(\Psi_w)_{w \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{A}}}\subset \mathbb{R}\langle \mathcal{A} \rangle $ by
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{dcases}
        \bigg.\Psi_{w} = w, &\text{for } w \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}}, \\   
     \Psi_w= \left (\Psi_{w_0\blue{j_1}\cdots \blue{j_{k-1}}w_{k-1}} \right )\blue{j_k} \shuffle w_k, & \text{for } w \in \mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{A}}\setminus\mathcal{W}_{\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}},
    \end{dcases}
    \end{equation}  
    with $((w_0, \dots, w_{k}), \blue{j_1 \cdots j_k}) = \decomp{\mathcal{A}^\prime}(w) $.
    Then, for any $Y=(A,X)\in C^{1-var}([s,t]; \R^d)$, we have \begin{equation}
        \langle w, \Sig{Y} \rangle_{\Psi} = 
        \langle w_k, \Sig{X}\rangle\int_{\Delta^{k}_{s,t}}\prod_{j=1}^{k}  
        \langle w_{j-1}, \Sig{X}_{s,t_j}\rangle \dd A^{\blue{i_{j}}}_{t_{j}},
    \end{equation} where we recall $\langle w,\cdot \rangle_\Psi:=\langle \Psi_w,\cdot \rangle$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.645884+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b71ea37bc1d718414e6148052118cf0d0f751a66c1c1c779bb0ccb838b171269","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:useful-bound of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] For any real number $\alpha\in\mathbb R$ and any two strictly positive numbers $\beta,\gamma>0$, consider the function
    \begin{align*}
        F_t(x,y)=\frac{t^\alpha}{x^\beta}\exp\left(-\frac{t}{y^\gamma}\right)\,.
    \end{align*}
    Let $a_+=a_+(t)$ be strictly positive and bounded, and $\epsilon=\epsilon(t)$ converging to $0$ as $t$ diverges. 
    Then there exists $k=k(t)$ an integer depending on $t$ and a sequence of increasing values $(a_0\leq\ldots\leq a_k)$ (implicitly depending on $t$) such that $a_0=\epsilon$, $a_k=a_+$, and satisfying
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^kF_t(a_{i-1},a_i)=0\,.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.420991+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b71f1bcdad639713c63560c0ad87453998996d58f631d1781a7f5ffd66e7e58c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] The \emph{traffic distribution} of a family $\mathbf{A}_{N}$ of random matrices is the map
        \begin{equation}
            \Phi_{\mathbf{A}_{N}}:g\in\mathbb{C}\mathcal{G}\langle\mathbf{x},\mathbf{x^*}\rangle\rightarrow\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{N}\tr\left(g(\mathbf{A}_{N})\right)\right].
        \end{equation}
        We say that the family $\mathbf{A}_{N}$ \emph{converges in traffic distribution} if $\Phi_{\mathbf{A}_{N}}$ converges point-wise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.268103+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b71fd5af8cd7e1340206a51f0535bd28d639452a850a8a551d5ff644fad844ee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:drift_y of arXiv:2509.08430, 'One-dimensional particle clouds with elastic collisions'] Let $x\in{\mathbb X}_N$ and assume that $y\in{\mathbb X}'_N$
is such that $y_k=y_{k+1}$ for all~$k \in [N]$
such that $x_k=x_{k+1}$. Then 
for ${\mathbf e}$ as defined at~(equation eq:e-def of the paper), it holds that
\begin{equation}

{\mathbb E}_x \langle y,{\mathbf e} \rangle_{N} 
= R_x^{-1} \langle  y , u \rangle_N .
\end{equation}
More generally, for $\ell, r \in [N]$, 
with $\ell \leq r$ and 
 $\ell_\pm, r_\pm$ as defined in~(equation eq:k-plus-minus of the paper),
\begin{align}

 R_x {\mathbb E}_x \langle y,{\mathbf e} \rangle_{\ell,r} 
  & = \langle y ,u \rangle_{\ell , r} 
  + \2 { \{ \ell > \ell_- \}} \langle y, b \rangle_{\ell_-, \ell - 1} 
  + \2 { \{ \ell > \ell_- \}} \langle y, a \rangle_{\ell_-, \ell_+}  - \2 {\{ r < r_+ \}} \langle y , a \rangle_{r+1, r_+} \nonumber\\
& {} \qquad{}  
  - \2 {\{ r < r_+ \}} \langle y ,b \rangle_{r_- ,r_+}.
\end{align}
In particular, if $\min_{k \in [N]} y_k \geq 0$, we have the inequalities
\begin{align}

{\mathbb E}_x \langle y,{\mathbf e} \rangle_{1,r} 
& \leq R_x^{-1}\langle y , u \rangle_{1, r}, \\

{\mathbb E}_x \langle y,{\mathbf e} \rangle_{\ell,N} 
& \geq R_x^{-1}\langle y ,u \rangle_{\ell, N}.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.331557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b72292b91783bbc8f9290e3eb3a8f1c3b5cdaa7f7ffa1fa9db0988b9e7084514","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.03959, 'The combinatorial Hopf algebra of signed graphs'] Given a signed graph $\Sigma = (V,E,\sigma)$, define the \textit{chromatic (unsigned) symmetric function of $\Sigma$} as
