{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.057353+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8326804445de46eba35010f4e4eeb00e98a911d3ee3c46d6ea3587009a9fc5b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:exp-euler-gaussian of arXiv:2608.25279, 'Provable Non-Acceleration of Standard Strang\\ Splittings of Kinetic Langevin Dynamics'] Let $\kappa>1$ and let $h,\gamma>0$. For
$U_\lambda(x)=\lambda x^2/2$ on $\mathbb R$, let $M_\lambda$ be the
matrix in (equation eq:exp-euler-matrix of the paper). If $M_\kappa$ is not Schur
stable, then the left-endpoint exponential integrator for
$U_\kappa$ has no invariant probability law. If $M_\kappa$ is
Schur stable, then $M_\lambda$ is Schur stable for every
$\lambda\in[1,\kappa]$, and
\begin{equation}
 \rho(M_1)\ge1-\frac{64}{\kappa}.
\end{equation}
In this case, the chain for $U_1$ has a unique invariant law
$\pi_1$. Let $P$ denote the transition kernel of the chain for
$U_1$. For every initial state $y\in\mathbb R^2$,
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \lVert \delta_yP^n-\pi_1\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}^{1/n}
 \le\rho(M_1),
\end{equation}
and the limit exists and equals $\rho(M_1)$ for a suitable $y$.
Moreover, there are initial states $y,\widetilde y\in\mathbb R^2$ such
that
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}
 \norm{\delta_yP^n-\delta_{\widetilde y}P^n}_{\mathrm{TV}}^{1/n}
 =\rho(M_1)\ge1-\frac{64}{\kappa}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.766591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8338c84342a35f56b94d78a6f0b7f9e581a72248388ae24c0282554cf4067b3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2609.03003, 'Causal Foundation Models'] A DGP satisfies the \textbf{stable unit treatment value assumption} (SUTVA) if: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item (No interference) The individual-level potential outcome $Y_n(t_n)$ for individual $n$ does not depend on the treatment assigned to any other individual $m$, and;
    \item (No hidden versions of treatments) The treatment $t$ is a well-defined intervention without multiple versions that have different effects.
\end{enumerate}

Without the first part of the SUTVA, $Y_n(t_n)$ is not well-defined from $t_n$ alone; $Y_n(t_n)$ would instead need to express dependence on other treatment assignments as $Y_n(t_1, ..., t_n, ..., t_{N})$. No interference implies $Y_n(t_1, ..., t_n, ..., t_{N}) = Y_n(t_n)$. Without the second part there are different versions of $t_n$ that produce different outcomes, so $Y_n(t_n)$ does not uniquely specify an outcome. The SUTVA ensures that the potential outcome $Y_n(t_n)$ is a well-defined object which can then be linked to the corresponding factual outcome $Y_n$ under $t_n$ via consistency.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.595568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b833fe8bf3cca7ae2cd6d5b2cd3cf0af05f3401ee336e0fa68394ba70b99fcc2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 (Weak Law of Large Numbers) of arXiv:2607.07022, 'Coordinate-wise Elephant Random Walk'] Assume $\max_{1\le i \le k } p_i<1$. Then, for every bounded function $f:Q_k\to\R,$ we have the weak law of large numbers 
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=0}^{n-1}f(S_t)\xrightarrow[n \to \infty]{\PP}\pi(f) . 
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.747384+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b834a1d2cbd6e5b55d1c931539a71abfa7f799e7fedb707c67fefcf1b7f174c3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 4 of arXiv:2607.28865, 'First-Passage Percolation on Spread-out line graphs: Microscopic Regime'] Let $a>0$ be a fixed constant satisfying Assumption \textnormal{B.\ref{ass:B1}}.
A vertex $v$ is called a \emph{pivot} if
