{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.136322+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b85b2392d6040c8f56f2ab2378a2334d0d8d5ccc41d3d45282ad7816239cdf9e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn ofn of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Assume that $\gamma:[0,T]\to R^d$ is a bounded variation path and $(0,0)=\gamma_0\neq\gamma_T$. We define the associated (with $\gamma$, $T$) normalization operator $\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\gamma,T}$ by
$$
\mathcal{A}(\alpha)_t:=k^{-1}A\alpha_t,~\forall~t\in[0,S],
$$
where $\alpha:[0,S]\to R^d$ is a bounded variation path, the rotation $A$ and the scaling $k$ are given by  
\begin{equation}
A
=\left(
\begin{array}{cc}
\cos(\theta) & \sin(\theta) \\
-\sin(\theta) &\cos(\theta)\\
\end{array} 
\right)
=\frac{1}{|\gamma_T|}\left(
\begin{array}{cc}
 x_T & y_T \\
-y_T & x_T\\
\end{array} 
\right),\quad\quad k=|\gamma_T|.
\end{equation}
For any one-form $f(\cdot):=\left(
\begin{array}{c}
f_1  \\
f_2 \\
\end{array} 
\right)(\cdot)$, we define its adjoint operator $\mathcal{A}^*$ by 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{A}^*(f)(\cdot)=k^{-1} A^{-1}\left(
\begin{array}{l}
f_1  \\
f_2 \\
\end{array} 
\right)\left(k^{-1}A\cdot\right)
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.415673+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b85b2d4df305d7f726e2b6b949dba3b49d1e8a5c78f1f0373762f9417aa7de00","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2605.16072, 'Stochastic integration \\with respect to a L\'evy basis'] Let $\Theta\colon \RR \to L_P^0(\Omega;\R)$ be an infinitely divisible random measure. Then, for every $A\in\RR$, the characteristic function of $\Theta(A)$ is given by
	\begin{align*}
		\phi_{\Theta(A)} (\beta)
		=\exp\left(
		i\beta a_0(A)
		-\tfrac12 \beta^2 q_0(A)
		+ \int_{\R} \big(e^{i\beta\alpha} - 1 - i\beta \tau(\alpha)\big)\, \lambda_0(A,\d \alpha)
		\right),
	\end{align*}
	for every $\beta\in\R$, where $a_0\colon \sigma(\RR)\to \R$ is a signed measure,
	$q_0\colon \sigma(\RR)\to [0,\infty)$ is a measure, and
	$\lambda_0\colon \sigma(\RR)\otimes \Borel(\R)\to [0,\infty]$ is a $\sigma$-finite measure. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.202337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8612edd0e227d2ad543eaa7299203afb5a8da4e113c87364598484dff6ad755","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (THP dichotomy) of arXiv:2608.03575, '2608.03575'] Let $P$ be a tame, infinite, ergodic, unimodular random trivalent hyperbolic polyhedron satisfying (Z3) and (Z4), and let $f$ be its root face. If $\mathbb{E}[\deg(f)]<\infty$, then
