{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.251015+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9c64eeb460055af8817a59d70800dd8d4815d32d8a8fba8c48a8674b66a805a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 58 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Fix integers $j_0 \in [0, \mathfrak{j}]$ and $i_0 \in [0, a_{j_0}]$. Assume that, for any integers $j \in [j_0, \mathfrak{j}]$ and $i \in [0, a_j]$ such that either $j > j_0$ or $i < i_0$, the following holds w.o.p.: 
\begin{flalign}

	\begin{aligned}
		& H_{\mathsf T_{i,j},j} (w) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i}} (w) + N^{\delta-1}, \qquad \text{for each $w \in \mathfrak{X}$}; \\
		& H_{\mathsf T_{i,j},j} (w) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i}} (w), \qquad \qquad  \quad  \text{for each $w \notin \mathfrak{L}_{q_i}^+ (\delta)$}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{flalign}

\noindent Then w.o.p., we have for any $t \in [\mathsf{T}_{i_0, j_0}, N^5]$ that 
\begin{flalign}

	\begin{aligned}
		& H_{t,j_0} (z_0) \le  \mathcal{H}_{q_{i_0}} (z_0) + N^{\delta-1}, \qquad \text{for each $z_0 \in \mathfrak{X}$}; \\ 
		& H_{t,j_0} (z_0) \le \mathcal{H}_{q_{i_0}} (z_0), \qquad \qquad \quad    \text{for each $z_0 \notin \mathfrak{L}_{q_{i_0}}^+ (\delta)$}.
	\end{aligned} 
\end{flalign} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.580662+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9c9aa641c4fd49611be125945f434ca516388dbdd795bfd060e0bc2925b0a8d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm.dl-convergence of arXiv:2606.30517, '\textsc{Convergence towards Ideal Poisson--Voronoi tessellations with a focus on Diestel--'] The Diestel--Leader graph ${\rm DL}(p,q)$ satisfies the conditions of (thm.ctfg of the paper). If $p\geq q$, then $\restrict{F}{\mathbb N}$ is log-regularly-varying with index $\ln p$. In addition, the measure $\nu_0$ on $\partial {\rm DL}(p,q)$ has the following description:
		\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
			\item If $p>q$, then $\nu_0$ is concentrated on $\partial T\subseteq \partial {\rm DL}(p,q)$ and is the harmonic measure on $\partial T$. In this case, {${\rm IPVT}_{\xi}({\rm DL}(p,q)) = \pi^{-1}({\rm IPVT}_{\xi'}(T))$ for some $\xi'$}, where $\pi:{\rm DL}(p,q)\to T$ denotes the projection on the first coordinate.
			\item If $p=q$, then $\nu_0$ is the average of the harmonic measures on $\partial T$ and $\partial T'$. 
		\end{enumerate}
		In addition, the edge-measured version of ${\rm DL}(p,q)$ satisfies (thm.ctfemg of the paper) with the same growth index $\ln p$, and the measures $\nu_t$ in (thm.ctfemg of the paper) are identical to the measure $\nu_0$ of the vertex-measured version. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.596078+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9ceb7ffe1083728a25f261c3296c6adbab774c2e9cc2e9307dd00c78cb670c9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:JumpHKE of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] Let $\phi_j$ be a scale function.
  For a  regular Dirichlet form $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ of pure-jump type, 
  we say that HK($\phi_j$) holds if there exists a heat kernel $p(t,x,y)$ of $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$
  and 
  there exist a properly exceptional set $\mathcal{N}$ and 
  constants $c_1 ,c_2 > 0$ such that
  for all $t > 0$ and $x,y \in M \backslash \mathcal{N}$,
  \begin{align}
    c_1 p^{(j)}(t,x,y) \le p(t,x,y) \le  c_2p^{(j)}(t,x,y).

  \end{align}
  Here, $p^{(j)}(t,x,y)$ is defined by
  \begin{equation}
    p^{(j)}(t,x,y) := \frac{1}{V(x,\phi_j^{-1}(t))} \wedge \frac{t}{V(x,d(x,y))\phi_j(d(x,y))}, \quad t > 0,x,y \in M.

  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.086712+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9cf6ec4663ada069dc97d875137eb21a758f2d30fabab47a9cb299fc949d2e7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary co-atomic decomposition of arXiv:2411.12623, 'Random signed measures'] Let $\xi$ be a random signed measure on $S$, then
	\begin{equation}
		\xi=\alpha_+-\alpha_-+\sum_{k\leq\kappa_+}\beta_{+,k}\delta_{\sigma_{+,k}}-\sum_{k\leq\kappa_-}\beta_{-,k}\delta_{\sigma_{-,k}}
	\end{equation}
	where $\alpha_+$ and $\alpha_-$ are diffuse random measures, $\kappa_+$ and $\kappa_-$ are random variables in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{0\}\cup\{\infty\}$, $\beta_{+,1},\beta_{-,1},\beta_{+,2},\beta_{-,2},...$ are positive random variables, and $\sigma_{+,1},\sigma_{-,1},\sigma_{+,2},\sigma_{-,2},...$ are almost surely distinct random points in $S$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:15:09.012641+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9d2fa1a0da9421b205a2397922584872dcb47260dadac4421ecd80fb848c96b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defn:hDeps of arXiv:2411.10829, 'Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble and its Laplace transform'] Take any $\epsilon$ with $\hat{C}^{-1}N^{-2/3}<\epsilon<\hat{C}$ for a universal constant $\hat{C}>0$; any $C, c$ can depend on $\hat{C}$. We let $\mathscr B_\epsilon=\mathscr B_\epsilon[i_1,\ldots,i_m]$ be the set of all walks $\vec r$ of $(\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_m}^{N_m})^{k_m} \cdots (\hat{\mathfrak D}_{i_1}^{N_1})^{k_1}$, with the following requirements:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $J_\mathrm {VI.1}=J_\mathrm {VI.2}=J_\mathrm {VI.3}=J_\mathrm C=\emptyset$.
    \item For each $j\in J_\mathrm I\cup J_\mathrm {II}\cup J_\mathrm {IV}$, $\vartheta_j\wedge \dot\vartheta_j > \epsilon N^{2/3}$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.011732+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9d4462a279c485894eaf2682d41b929e1fecd61ae3f28b33a61559b00881005","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition T rho eta of arXiv:2605.13479, '\LARGE Dean-Kawasaki Equation with Biot-Savart and Keller-Segel Interactions: Existence an'] Under the same setting as in Proposition (entropy estimate of the paper) and for every $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ fixed, the laws of $\left\{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right\}_{\eta ,\gamma \in(0,1)}$ are tight on $L^1_{[0,T]}L^1_{\mathbb{T}^2}$ with the strong topology. Furthermore, for any $\alpha \in(0,1 / 2)$ and $\psi \in \mathrm{C}_c^{\infty}(\mathbb{T}^2\times(0, \infty))$, the family of laws of the martingales $(M^{\gamma,\eta})_{\eta,\gamma\in(0,1)}$ and $(M^{\gamma,\eta}_{\text{entropy}})_{\eta,\gamma\in(0,1)}$ defined by
 \begin{align}
 M_t^{\gamma,\eta}:=\sqrt{\varepsilon}\int_0^t \int_{\mathbb{T}^2} \psi\left(x, \rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right) \nabla \cdot\left[s_{\eta}\left(\rho^{\gamma, \eta}\right) \xi_{K}\right],  
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
M^{\gamma,\eta}_{\text{entropy},t}:=\sqrt{\varepsilon}\int_0^t\int_{\mathbb{T}^2}
\frac{s_{\eta}(\rho^{\gamma, \eta})}
{\sqrt{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}}}\nabla\sqrt{\rho^{\gamma, \eta}}\cdot\xi_{K}	
\end{align}

is tight on $\mathcal{C}_{[0, T]}\mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.844637+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9d7e0e20a37dbda3d556c2264d8cc774997ae2a1934e0774d629275206fa39c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition Prop: recursive form remainder of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] For any forest $w=\tau_1\cdots\tau_k\in{\mathcal F}$ and any rooted tree $\tau_0\in{\mathcal T}$ we have
		\begin{equation}
			\sum_{i=1}^k\frac{1}{\pi(\tau_i; w)}H_{z_1z_2}(w\setminus\tau_i;\tau_0) \, \Upsilon_{\tau_i}(z_1)-\frac{1}{\pi(w)}\Upsilon_{w\curvearrowright\tau_0}(z_1)=H_{z_1z_2}(w;\tau_0)\cdot z^{[1]}\,,
\end{equation}
		where $\pi(\tau_i;w)$ is the number of times the tree $\tau_i$ appears in the forest $w$ and $w\setminus\tau_i$ is the forest obtained by removing an instance of $\tau_i$ in $w$, see Definition (Def: symmetry factor of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.717481+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9dba2380a67edcca7a758c72d0d9c63326a2c0617f49c2429a9832ae1d8f14a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2609.01166, 'Nonparametric inference for density-dependent McKean--Vlasov diffusions'] Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:potential} and \ref{ass:coefficient}, there exists a unique \(C^1\) zero-flux stationary density for $\xi$ given by
\begin{equation}
 \pi_\xi(x)=g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-\V(x)),
\end{equation}
where $\mu_\xi$ is the unique constant satisfying \( \int_{v_\star}^{\infty}g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-s)m_\V(s)\,\dd s=1 \). Equivalently, $q_\xi(s):=g_\xi^{-1}(\mu_\xi-s)$ solves
\begin{equation}
 q_\xi'(s)=-q_\xi(s)\xi(q_\xi(s)) \quad \text{for} \quad s>v_\star,
\end{equation}
where $a_\xi:=q_\xi(v_\star)$ is chosen to satisfy 
\begin{equation}
\int_{v_\star}^{\infty}q_\xi(s)m_\V(s)\,\dd s=1.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.634773+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9dd0007cd2fba20d8543629f72643e1d655aba9de767f19b841d02afe6ba426","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:no-hit-sde of arXiv:2607.00800, 'Brownian motion in Minkowski normed spaces'] Let $s> 0$ and $X$ be a weak solution of (equation SDE-linear of the paper) with initial datum $f_Q^z(s,x)\,\mathrm d x$, defined on a stochastic basis $(\Omega, \mathcal F, (\mathcal F_t)_{t\geq 0}, \mathbb{P})$.
