{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.796838+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9ddbdfb0fd28ce352233f4846498fcca727f415b5b1cab74c255fe3eff3bb5c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop p.mainber of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] Let $h$ be the Gaussian random field with covariance $K$ as in (equation eq.generalKernel of the paper). For a given $f\in C\left(\overline{D}\right)$, define 
    \begin{equation*}
        I_{\varepsilon,\varepsilon_0} = \int_D f(x) \1_{G_{\alpha, \varepsilon}(x)} \exp\left(\gamma h_\varepsilon(x) - \frac{\gamma^2}{2}\mathbb E\left[h^2_\varepsilon(x)\right]\right)dx.
    \end{equation*}
    If $I_{\varepsilon}$ is uniformly bounded in $L^2$ and $\mathrm{cov}(h_{\varepsilon}(x), h_{\delta}(y))$ converges punctually to a function $G(x,y)$ when $\varepsilon$ and $\delta$ go to $0$ for $x\not =y$, then, $\mu_{\varepsilon}^{\gamma}$ given by (equation eq.generalGMC of the paper) converges in probability to a non-trivial random measure $\mu^{\gamma}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.055629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9deb4c72fc4fe661d47c4c0286fb99110ac9cd9f64a16a4c19d90b6df1d8f8f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 (Convergence of the drift term) of arXiv:2607.01629, 'Quadratic fluctuations of speed-change Kawasaki dynamics'] For every function $f\in C^\infty\left(\mathbb{T}^{2d}\right)$, the sequence of $\{\mathcal A^N(f)\}_{N \in \N}$ admits a limit $\mathcal A(f)$ as $N\to\infty$ in $D\left([0,T],\R\right)$. Every limit $\mathcal A(f)$ is in $C\left([0,T],\R\right)$ and satisfies that 
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathcal A_t(f)=\int_0^t \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf D(\rho)Q_s(\partial^2 _{1,2}f)\right)\d s,
		\end{equation*}
        where $Q$ is the associated limit of $\{Q^{N}\}_{N \in \N}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:16:46.142755+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e0ce1a9a3dbe755fc0938513c68369842e59dbf94bf8b9919409cdafbec844","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:steins-gibbs-uncentered of arXiv:2605.03718, 'Potential Hessian Ascent III:\\ Sampling the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick Model at $\beta < 1/'] {panchenko2013sherrington}}]
    Let $f: \mathbb R \to \mathbb R \in C^1$ be a function with slower than exponential growth. Let $\{X(\sigma)\}_{\sigma \in \{-1,1\}^n}$ and $\{Y(\sigma)\}_{\sigma \in \{-1,1\}^n}$ be (possibly uncentered) Gaussian processes with a Gibbs measure $G_{n,\beta,Y}$ defined over the process $Y$ at inverse temperature $\beta$.

    Then,
    \begin{align*}
        &\mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\langle f\big(X(\sigma) - \mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}[X(\sigma)]\big)\right\rangle_{G_{n,\beta,Y}}\right] = \mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\langle f\big(\mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}\left[X(\sigma)Y(\sigma)\right] - \mathop{{}\mathbb{E}}\left[X(\sigma)Y(\sigma')\right]\big)\right\rangle_{G^{\otimes 2}_{n,\beta,Y}}\right]\,. 
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.735317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e42b02561017988c53724622ce993a4062e68c62ecd564e85a93783197897f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: general renormalised potential of arXiv:2503.24372, 'A criterion on the free energy for log-Sobolev inequalities in mean-field particle systems'] For any $\psi \in \bbH$, the renormalised potential is 
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal V_T(\psi)
= \inf_{\bf m} \Big\{ \hat  \cF_T({\bf m}) + \frac{1}{2 T} \| \psi - {\bf m} \|_{\bbH}^2   \Big\} ,

\end{equation}
where the projection $\hat  \cF_T$ of the mean-field functional was introduced in 
(equation eq: projection F_T of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:37:08.322002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e5dc71bb709770eae21e9c31b78ad6526549950c9347376ef3c7453e1091a4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:higher_regularity of arXiv:2608.31071, 'Global smooth solutions to the inhomogeneous Landau--Fermi--Dirac equation'] Suppose $f:[0,T]\times \mathbb T^3 \times \mathbb R^3 \to [0,1]$ is a weak solution to (equation e.LFD of the paper) with nonvacuum initial data in the sense of (d.nonvacuum of the paper) and an element of $L^\infty_m$ for some $m > 5$. Then, $f$ is a classical solution to (equation e.LFD of the paper). Moreover, for any fixed $k \in \mathbb N$, there is $m_0$ such that, if $m \geq m_k$, then
        \begin{equation*}
            D^k_{t,x,v} f \in L^\infty.
        \end{equation*}
        The constant $m_k$ depends on $k$, $T$, $\|f_{\rm in}\|_{L^\infty_{m_0}}$, and the nonvacuum parameters $r$, $\delta$, and $v_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.667812+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e63080bca7e5d858e061cc7d31aee84298758252f18005c5a8cc75da98e114","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary corollary_bound_on_last_point_if_close_to_mu_c_appendix of arXiv:2605.26804, '\Large Large Deviation Principle for the Empirical Measures of Simple Random Walks on $\ov'] If $\mu_0 \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Z})$ and $R > R_{\mu_0}(\varepsilon)$ then for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $w \in \Omega_{n}^{(0)}(\mu_0,2^{-R}\varepsilon)$,
\begin{equation*}
|w_n| \leq R+2 \varepsilon n.
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:19:00.858165+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e6ad57d02d2e0d7835df0650ae59be35df3197182168d7e2da1bfe29e8aff7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 (Event identity) of arXiv:2608.20337, 'Information on trajectories:\\ martingales and random times'] In the setting of Proposition~\ref{prop:supermart-relax}(iii), for $A \in \cF_t$ with $P(A) > 0$,
\begin{equation}
  -\log\PP(A)
  = \lambda\,\E[Y_t \mid A] - \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A]
    + \KL\bigl(P(\cdot \mid A)\,\big\|\,\overline Q^\star_t\bigr)
    - \log\E_P[E_t(\lambda)],
\end{equation}
for $\dd\overline Q^\star_t/\dd P \propto E_t(\lambda)$.
Both residuals are nonnegative, and discarding them gives the event bound
\begin{equation}
  \PP(A) \leq \exp\!\bigl(-\lambda\,\E[Y_t \mid A] + \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A]\bigr);
\end{equation}
if in addition $\lambda \geq 0$ and on $A$ one has $Y_t \geq x$ and $\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \leq c$,
then $\PP(A) \leq e^{-\lambda x + c}$, at the further cost of the event relaxation $\lambda(\E[Y_t \mid A] - x) + (c - \E[\overline\Psi_t(\lambda) \mid A])$.
Since $E_t(\lambda) > 0$ a.s., the first residual is strictly positive whenever $\PP(A) < 1$: equality in~\eqref{eq:event-bound} holds only if $\PP(A) = 1$ and $\lambda Y_t = \overline\Psi_t(\lambda)$ a.s. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.601263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b9e6ade2906dbeb9e79aacbecace4dddcba767ac352647dd163be3bb2304a781","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm6.2 of arXiv:2602.23124, '\textsc{ Necessary and Sufficient Conditions for the Lacunary/Hereditary Laws of Large Num'] {\bf Necessity and Sufficiency in the  Classical  WLLN for I.I.D. Sequences:}
  For   real-valued I.I.D. functions $\,h_1, h_2, \cdots,$  the following are equivalent: 

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  {\bf (i)} {\rm WLLN:} There exist ``correctors"     $\,d_1, d_2, \cdots\,$ in $\mathbb R\,,$ so that 
  $ \,
\lim_{N \to \infty} \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum_{n=1}^N h_n - d_N \right) = 0\,$ holds in $\,{\mathbb P}-$probability; i.e., for each $\varepsilon >0$ we have
\begin{equation} 

\pi_N^\star( \varepsilon)\,:= \,  {\mathbb P} \bigg( \, \bigg| \frac{1}{\,N\,} \sum_{n=1}^N h_n - d_N \bigg| > \varepsilon  \bigg)\, \longrightarrow \, 0\,, \quad \text{as}~~ N \to \infty\,.
\end{equation}
 {\bf (ii)} With the notation of (equation 6.7 of the paper), for every $\varepsilon >0$ we have 
