{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.320593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_badeafe7e7a7aa7e81495d54dfa8ea7b0be81cbd8cfe32f3e2c72bad3e8b3347","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:IntroMeasRep of arXiv:2411.14936, 'Massive Particle Systems,\\Wasserstein Brownian Motions,\\ and the Dean--Kawasaki Equation'] Under Assumptions~(ass:IntroSetting of the paper) and~(ass:QPPIntro of the paper), the process~$\mu_\bullet$ is
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item properly associated with a quasi-regular recurrent (in particular: conservative) symmetric Dirichlet form on~$L^2(\mcQ_{\pi,\nu})$;
\item the solution ---unique in law--- to the martingale problem for the form's generator;
\item a diffusion (i.e., with a.s.\ continuous sample paths) if and only if so is~$\mssW_\bullet$;
\item ergodic if and only if~$\pi$ is concentrated on a singleton in the $N$-simplex.
Otherwise, Borel $\mu_\bullet$-invariant sets (up to $\mcQ_{\pi,\nu}$-equivalence) are in one-to-one correspondence with Borel subsets of the $N$-simplex (up to $\pi$-equivalence).
\end{itemize}
Furthermore, the form, its generator, and the predictable quadratic variation for the martingale problem are all computed explicitly on an explicit core. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.821823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_badf25af71d5bf0e275e1ca9f63567d12bdffdacbc8f0e1cd3962104765cec30","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th: a priori branched0 of arXiv:2607.18635, 'Remainders of generalised Taylor expansions and a priori bounds for rough differential equ'] Assume that $\Upsilon_\tau$ is globally Lipschitz continuous for all $\tau\in{\mathcal T}^{\leq N}$. Then, for sufficiently small $\varepsilon:=T\wedge\mu$ and $Z$ any solution of (equation eq:davie of the paper), 
