{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:28.472263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb57db14549acc561bcbc6042c4e7d1022ea98e266e729c2fdef532579b3ca05","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 45 (Evaluation bounds on the intersection branch) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] For every $m\ge1$ there are constants $C_m,A_m<\infty$, depending only on
$m$, such that for $1\le p<\infty$ and $2\le r<\infty$,
\begin{align}

 \|\cT_K^{(m)}\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 &\le C_m(p+r)^{m/2}\Prof_{m,r}(K),\\

 \|I_m(K)\|_{L^p(\Omega;\Sch_r)}
 &\le A_m(p+r)^{m/2}\|K\|_{\mathfrak W_{m,r}}.
\end{align}
One may take $C_m\le C_0^m$ and $A_m=m^{m/2}C_m$.
The first assertion is Theorem~\ref{thm:m-finite} together with the completed
map of Proposition~\ref{prop:completed-decoupled-chaos-map}; the second is
Proposition~\ref{prop:wick-completion}.  The range $1\le p<2$ follows from
$p=2$ and monotonicity of probability norms.  Since $r\ge2$,
$r+2\le\frac43(r+p)$ in this range, so the displayed $(p+r)^{m/2}$ form is
retained after enlarging the absolute exponential base $C_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.785398+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb583762f607c11694b69edef53bdb9d79e20019c0f76eab2b2519dbc24e1715","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2604.00474, 'Trap behaviors for Brownian motions'] Let $Z^\dagger =\{Z_t, \, t < T_D(Z)\}$ be the part process on the bounded domain $D$ of $Z= \{Z_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ with $Z_0=x \in D$. If there exists $\beta \in (0, 1)$ such that
\begin{align}
\int_{D} \mathbf{P}_x(T_D(Z^\dagger) \leq t) \, dx   \simeq   t^\frac{\beta}{2} \quad \textrm{as } t \to 0+ 
\end{align}
then we say that $Z$ exhibits a trap behaviour on $D$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.573996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb58fa4fb03646fe14175502515a13b229588f93a2e79157381a285185e827dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:critgauge of arXiv:2412.04607, 'Asymptotics of the partial \(n\)-fold dimer model'] For any \(\vb*{\alpha} \in \mathcal{M}_\mathbb{R}\) and weight function \(w\) there is a unique gauge-equivalent weight function \(w'\) with the property that for all \(v\) the sum of weights of tiles containing vertex \(v\) (counted with multiplicity) is \(\alpha_v\), that is 
\(\sum_{t} t_v w'(t) = \alpha_v\).
The corresponding gauge transformations \(f\colon V\to \mathbb{R}_{>0}\) are exactly those which solve the "criticality equations" 
\begin{equation} 
\frac{x_v \partial_{x_v} P}{P} = \alpha_v
\end{equation}
with \(x_v = f(v)\). The growth rate of \(Z_{w,\vb{n},N}\) is 
\begin{equation}
\sigma(w, \vb*{\alpha}) \coloneq \lim_{n_v, N\to \infty} \frac1N \log Z_{w,\vb{n},N} = \log P(\vb{x}) - \sum_v \alpha_v \log(\mathrm{x}_v) 
\end{equation}
for any solution \(\vb{x}\) to ((eqn:crit of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:10:43.855076+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb59970265540acb204e3514bf27d67ae6b10edb202ad43129c490237902aa19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor22 of arXiv:2412.04334, 'Fractional counting process at L\'{e}vy times and its applications'] If $\{Y_n, n\geq 1\}$ are integer-valued discrete random variables, we set
    \begin{equation*}
        b_s^{*m} = \mathbb{P}\left[Y_1+Y_2+\cdots +Y_m= s\right],\;\; s\in\mathbb{N}_{0}.
    \end{equation*}
    Then, for $s\in \mathbb{N}_{0}$, we have that
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}\left[Z(t)=s\right]= &\;\mathbb{I}_{\{ s= 0\}} \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\zeta )_k\mathit{\Gamma } ( \vartheta )(-\lambda_{\theta})^k}{k!\mathit{\Gamma } (\mu k+ \vartheta )} \mathbb{E}\left[(H(t))^{\theta k}\right]\\ & + \mathit{\Gamma } ( \vartheta )\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{\lambda_{\theta}^m(\zeta )_m}{m!}b_s^{*m} \sum_{l=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\zeta  + m)_{l}(-\lambda_{\theta})^l}{l! \mathit{\Gamma } (\mu (l+m)+ \vartheta )}\mathbb{E}\left[(H(t))^{\theta(l+m) }\right].\nonumber
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.766771+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb59baa258211a713447a25e6bfd7a0df02e0f3a8a4e308916e307ba1ca0a1af","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (Covariance-existence criterion) of arXiv:2607.09994, '2607.09994'] Suppose Assumptions~\ref{ass:stationarity}, \ref{ass:innovations} and \ref{ass:perron-excitation} hold. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item the stationary solution is square-integrable, \( \mathbb E\|Z\|^2<\infty \);
    \item the second-order spectral radius satisfies \( \rho(\Top)<1 \).
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E\|Z\|^2<\infty
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\rho(\Tmat)<1.

\end{equation}

When \eqref{eq:main-equivalence} holds, the regime-weighted second moments \(M_1,\ldots,M_M\) are the unique finite solution of
\begin{equation}
M_j
=
\sum_{i=1}^M p_{ij}A_jM_iA_j^\top+\pi_jQ_j,
\qquad j=1,\ldots,M.

\end{equation}
Equivalently, in vectorised form,
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{vec}(M)
=
\Tmat\operatorname{vec}(M)+q,

\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{vec}(M)
=
(I-\Tmat)^{-1}q.

\end{equation}

Moreover, the following second-order quantities are well-defined.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The covariance matrix is
    \begin{equation}
    \Sigma
    =
    \operatorname{Var}(Z)
    =
    \mathbb E[ZZ^\top]
    =
    \sum_{j=1}^M M_j.

    \end{equation}

    \item For components \(Z^a\) and \(Z^b\),
    \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Var}(Z^a)=\Sigma_{aa},
    \qquad
    \operatorname{Cov}(Z^a,Z^b)=\Sigma_{ab}.

    \end{equation}

    \item If \(\Sigma_{aa}>0\) and \(\Sigma_{bb}>0\), then
    \begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Corr}(Z^a,Z^b)
    =
    \frac{\Sigma_{ab}}{\sqrt{\Sigma_{aa}\Sigma_{bb}}}.

    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:23:27.793788+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb5bb4c7aa597e28d903b840e6dd48f637a60cf2bf2d3c8fae3f1b629cec5a05","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn tfd of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] Let $\varPhi$ be a realization of the OU-GFF.
    For $x\in D$ we define 
    \begin{align}
        \begin{aligned}

        &\gamma_x^{+}(t): = 
        \limsup_{\varepsilon\to0}
        \frac{\Phi_{\varepsilon}(x,t)}{\ln(\frac{1}{\varepsilon})}, \\
        &\gamma_x^{-}(t):= \liminf_{\varepsilon \to 0 } \frac{\Phi_{\varepsilon}(x,t) }{\ln\left(\frac{1 }{\varepsilon }\right )  }, \\
        &\gamma_x(t): = \begin{cases}
         \gamma_x^+(t) & \text{ if } \gamma_x^+(t)=\gamma_x^-(t),\\
        \dagger& \text{else}.\end{cases}
        \end{aligned}
    \end{align}
   We call them the sup-thickness function, inf-thickness function and thickness function respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:38:04.517968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb5bcd84cc50782b8f3f587709132c136e94f8528935bc833a1e4e986f22cd80","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:useful_bound_1 of arXiv:2607.18011, 'From local giants to locality in long-range percolation'] \thlabel{lem:useful_bounds}
    Let $\alpha \in(0,2)$ and $\delta \in (\max(0, 1-\alpha),2-\alpha)$.

    For all $c > 0$ we can choose $r_0$ sufficiently large such that  for all $i \geq 1$

    \begin{align}

        \varepsilon_i 
        \kappa_{i,j} 
        r_{i-1}^{d(2 - \alpha - \delta)} 
        & \geq 
        c 
        r_{i}^{d(2 - \alpha - \delta)},
        \\

        (r_i / r_0)^{d(2 -\alpha - \delta)} 
        & \geq 
