{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.390077+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb86aadaa4456ad8fa2a3990139d474450e2a2e66abadf3b3b59100afc793fff","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:weak-conv of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] (i) For $n\ge 1$, let $\mu,\mu_n\in \Lambda(\mathbb R^d)$. We say that $\mu_n$ \emph{converges L\'evy weakly, or $\Lambda$-weakly, to $\mu$}, written $\mu_n \xrightarrow{\Lambda} \mu$, if
	\begin{align}
		\lim_{n \to \infty} \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(x) \, d\mu_n(x) = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(x) \, d\mu(x)	
	\end{align}
	for all bounded continuous test functions \( \varphi \in C_b(\mathbb{R}^d) \) satisfying \( |\varphi(x)| \le C\min\{1,|x|^2\} \) for some constant \( C \ge 0 \).

	(ii) A family \( \mathcal{M} \subset \Lambda(\mathbb{R}^d) \) is said to be \emph{$\Lambda$-tight} if the following conditions holds: (1) it holds
	\begin{align*}
		\sup_{\mu\in \mathcal{M}} \int_{\mathbb R^d} \min\left\{1,|x|^2\right\}d\mu(x)<\infty;
	\end{align*}
	(2) for every \( \varepsilon > 0 \), there exist a compact set $K\subset \mathbb R^d\setminus \{0\}$ such that 
	\[
	\sup_{\mu \in \mathcal{M}} \int_{K^c} \min\{1, |x|^2\} \, d\mu(x) < \varepsilon.
	\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:24:34.431136+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb87330fbeec089891f5fc7a5417c20b248cd2181ec7e8ff700bf149576ed206","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 (Universal construction in the regime $q\ge1$) of arXiv:2608.31157, 'Sharp Approximation Rates for Neural Networks with Affine Latent Parameterizations'] Let $d,S,D\in\N^+$ with $S\le D$, and let $q,K,\widehat{L}_{d,D}$ be the
quantities in \eqref{eq:qK}.  Assume $q\ge1$.  There exist an architecture
$\Phi\in\Arch(N_d,\widehat{L}_{d,D})$ with input dimension $d$ and an affine map
\[
 \calA_0\in\aff(3S+1,P_\Phi),
\]
both independent of $f$.  For every $f\in C([0,1]^d)$, there exists
$\bmxi_f\in\R^{3S+1}$ such that, with $\bmtheta_f:=\calA_0(\bmxi_f)$, one has
\begin{equation}
 \left\lVert f-\Phi_{\bmtheta_f}\right\rVert_{L^\infty([0,1]^d)}
 \le(d+2)\omega_f\bigl(\tfrac{\sqrt d}{K}\bigr).
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 P_\Phi\le B_dD.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.852214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb882a2fc1c0306a1c46f739c91bb1bc74ff62df2c1b2e1599d89f0f477783fb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mart of arXiv:2607.10708, 'A class of Markov processes with resetting and applications to cybersecurity'] Let $X=(N,\Lambda,C)$  be a self-exciting PDMP with resetting. Then, the following processes are $({(\mathcal F_t)}_{t \ge 0}, \mathbb P^{\,\alpha,x})$-martingales for each $x\in S$:
\begin{align}
M^{N}_t&= N_t -\int_0^t\Lambda_s ds + \int_0^t (N_s+1)\Lambda_s {\bar{G}_{\ell}(N_s+1, A-\Lambda_s)}ds; \\
M^{\Lambda }_t&= \Lambda_t- \int_0^t \big(\alpha(\beta-\Lambda_s)+  L^o(N_s+1, \Lambda_s)\big)ds,\\
M^{C}_t&= C_t-\mu_Z \int_0^t\Lambda_s ds,
\end{align}
where $\bar{G}_\ell=1-G_\ell$,  and
\begin{equation} 
L^o(n,\lambda):= \lambda ({\lambda^o} - \lambda) \bar{G}_{\ell}(n, (A-\lambda)\textrm{-}) +  \lambda \int\mathds{1}_{\{y<A-\lambda\}} \, y \,dG_{\ell}(n, y).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.244767+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb8851d311df25b5b966044ac4429220c304de5417982fda412c998628fb217f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (Discrete height function) of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Given a simply-connected, tilable domain $D$, a tiling $\eta\in\Omega_D$, and a point $z_0\in D\cap \mathbb T_N$, we define $H_{\eta} : D \cap \mathbb{T}_N \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ as follows. First set $H_\eta(z_0)=0$; then, $H_{\eta}$ is determined by the relations 
  \begin{eqnarray}

    H_\eta(z')-H_\eta(z)=N^{-1}\cdot
    \begin{cases}
      -1+{\bf 1}_{(z,z') \text{ is the edge of a lozenge in $\eta$}} & \text{if } z'-z=(N^{-1},0) \\
      {\bf 1}_{(z,z') \text{  is the edge of a lozenge in $\eta$}} & \text{if } z'-z=(0,N^{-1})
    \end{cases}.
  \end{eqnarray}

\noindent We further extend $H_{\eta}$ to $D$ by linearity. In the specific case $D=\mX_{a,b,c}$ we always choose $z_0=(0,0)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.327963+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb8ab80f5de5c5696648bce6f58033386718f85b3e09725a3164e4e98c96825a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defin:OUsolution of arXiv:2606.01937, 'Stationary fluctuations for an exclusion process with mass and energy conservation'] We say that a process $(\mathcal{Z}_t)_{t\in [0,T]}$ with continuous $\mathscr{S}'(\mathbb{R} )$-valued trajectories is a solution to the \emph{Ornstein-Uhlenbeck equation}
    \begin{equation}
    \partial_t\mathcal{Z}_t = \mathfrak{a}\Delta\mathcal{Z}_t +\sqrt{2\mathfrak{a}\sigma^2}\nabla\dot{\mathcal{W}}_t
    \end{equation}
    if it satisfies conditions (energysol_item1 of the paper) and (energysol_item3 of the paper) with $\mathfrak{g}=0$ in (defin:energysolution of the paper). The existence and uniqueness of solutions to the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck equation is a classical result, see [holley_generalized_1978].","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.589619+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb8e2a86aee7c358d72b43442c324cfb3c424aa3cc74ddada6711475d1e9907c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm lower alpha of arXiv:2603.15323, '\Large \bf Small-time heat decay for stable processes on fractal drums'] Let $G$ be an open set given as in (equation eqn:SS of the paper), $\alpha\in(0,d-\mathfrak{b}]$, and Condition (cond:lower alpha of the paper) is satisfied. Then, 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{t\to 0}\frac{m(G)-Q_{G}^{(\alpha)}(t)}{t}=\textup{Per}^{(\alpha)}(G)=\int_{G}\int_{G^c}\frac{c(\alpha, d)}{|x-y|^{d+\alpha}}dydx,
\end{equation}
where the constant $c(d,\alpha) $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
c(\alpha, d) = \frac{\alpha \Gamma\big(\frac{d +\alpha} 2\big)} {2^{1 - \alpha} \pi^{d/2}\Gamma\big(1 - \frac \alpha 2\big)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:23:25.622747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb8f8bf0cd00893c5d803bc844007f1d87c9992ab5e1f42f2fac732a0e8ee327","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn chordchordarcarc of arXiv:2609.01250, 'Global regularity for 2D gravity water waves with two retreating point vortices'] Assume that $z(\alpha)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \beta_0|\alpha-\beta|\leq |z(\alpha)-z(\beta)|\leq \beta_1|\alpha-\beta|, \quad \quad \forall \alpha,\beta\in \mathbb{R},
\end{equation}
where $0<\beta_0<\beta_1<\infty$ are two absolute constants.
We define the Hilbert transform associated to a curve $z(\alpha)$ as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}f(\alpha):=\frac{1}{\pi i}p.v.\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{z_{\beta}(\beta)}{z(\alpha)-z(\beta)}f(\beta)d\beta.
\end{equation}
We use the notation $\mathfrak{H}$ to denote the Hilbert transform associated with $\zeta$:
\begin{equation}\mathfrak{H}f(\alpha,t):=\frac{1}{\pi i}p.v.\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\zeta_{\beta}}{\zeta(\alpha,t)-\zeta(\beta,t)}f(\beta,t)d\beta.
\end{equation}
The standard Hilbert transform is the Hilbert transform associated with $z(\alpha)=\alpha$, which is denoted by
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{H}f(\alpha):=\frac{1}{\pi i}p.v.\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{1}{\alpha-\beta}f(\beta)d\beta.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.569473+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb92e8b2ae318892fa217ab3507996aba5d5291df98de65937a20e1bbb5eda31","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2606.14170, 'Uniform-in-time error estimates for McKean-Vlasov SDEs with common noise and stochastic al'] Suppose that Assumptions \ref{A1}, \ref{S1}-\ref{N2-1} hold with $\overline\lambda_1>2\overline\lambda_2$. Let
\begin{align*}
h^*:=1\wedge \frac{1}{\overline\lambda_1}\wedge\frac{4\alpha^2(\overline\lambda_1-2\overline\lambda_2)^2}{9\hat K^4(1+2\alpha)^2},
\end{align*}
where $\hat K=2K+\lambda_2+|b(0,\delta_0)|$ was defined in \eqref{b_growth}. Then, for any $p\ge 1$ and $h\in(0,h^*]$, the solution of \eqref{TEM} satisfies
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}|\overline X_{nh}^{i,N, h}|^p\le C(1+\mathbb{E}|\overline X_0^{i,N,h}|^p), ~n\in\mathbb{N},
\end{align}
in case of $\mathbb{E}|\overline X_0^{i,N,h}|^p<\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:26.965160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb92ff19f618c6c1278d080aede80f78a2fbc3fece2d1881eea7f3d90f59348f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:KPZ_preserve_lim of arXiv:2505.09604, 'Exceptional force, uncountably many solutions in the KPZ fixed point'] [Busa-Sepp-Sore-22a] 
The following holds simultaneously for all initial data and all $t > s$ on the event of probability one from Lemma~(lem:Landscape_global_bound of the paper). Let $\mathfrak h \in \operatorname{UC}$ (the space of upper semi-continuous functions $\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$) be initial data for the KPZ fixed point, sampled at time $s$. 
For $t > s$, let 
\[
h_t(y;\mathfrak h) = \sup_{x \in \mathbb{R}}\{\mathfrak h(x) + \mathcal{L}(x,s;y,t)\}.
\]
Then, simultaneously for all $t > s$,
\begin{equation} 
\liminf_{x \to +\infty} \frac{h_t(x;\mathfrak h)}{x} \ge \liminf_{x \to + \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x},\qquad\text{and}\qquad \limsup_{x \to - \infty} \frac{h_t(x;\mathfrak h)}{x} \le \limsup_{x \to -\infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x}.
\end{equation}

Furthermore, assuming that $\mathfrak h:\mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is \textbf{continuous} and satisfies
\begin{equation} 
\liminf_{x \to \pm \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x} > -\infty \qquad\text{and}\qquad \limsup_{x \to \pm \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x} < +\infty,
\end{equation}
then also
\begin{equation} 
\limsup_{x \to + \infty} \frac{h_t(x;\mathfrak h)}{x} \le \limsup_{x \to +\infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x},\qquad\text{and}\qquad \liminf_{x \to -\infty} \frac{h_t(x;\mathfrak h)}{x} \ge \liminf_{x \to - \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x}.
\end{equation}

In particular, for \textbf{continuous} initial data $\mathfrak h$ satisfying~(equation liminfsupfinite of the paper), if either \rm{(}or both\rm{)} of the limits
$
\lim_{x \to \pm \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x}
$
exist \rm{(}potentially with different limits on each side\rm{)}, then for $t > s$,
\[
\lim_{x \to \pm \infty} \frac{h_t(x;\mathfrak h)}{x} = \lim_{x \to \pm \infty} \frac{\mathfrak h(x)}{x}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:20:15.584180+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb93ac65bdbef715e5598bc9e3f5b1bb8db1968575b0c05b2f6878b5e3e30137","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition propAllPrimePowersAverage of arXiv:2609.00777, 'Universal murmuration and Hecke augmentation'] Assume, in each case, the hypotheses and parameter range of the corresponding cited theorem.
For the holomorphic weight family, set $\mathcal C=\mathcal N(K)$ and
\begin{equation*}
\mathscr W_{K,H,\delta}
=\{(k,f):k\equiv2\delta\pmod4,\ |k-K|\leq H,\ f\in H_k(1)\},
\end{equation*}
where $H_k(1)$ is the normalized Hecke eigenbasis of $S_k(1)$ used in~[BBLLD2026].
For the Maass family, set $\mathcal C=\mathcal N(R)$ and
$\mathscr M_{R,H}=\{j:|r_j-R|\leq H\}$.
Each assertion below uses the corresponding choice of $\mathcal C$, $M_{\mathcal C}$, and $T_{\mathcal C}$.
In the corresponding case, define
\begin{equation*}
M_{\mathcal C}=
\begin{cases}
\#\mathscr W_{K,H,\delta},& \mathcal C=\mathcal N(K),\\
\#\mathscr M_{R,H},& \mathcal C=\mathcal N(R),
\end{cases}
\qquad
T_{\mathcal C}(n)=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\sum_{(k,f)\in\mathscr W_{K,H,\delta}}\lambda_f(n),
& \mathcal C=\mathcal N(K),\\[6pt]
\displaystyle\sum_{j\in\mathscr M_{R,H}}\varepsilon_j a_j(n),
& \mathcal C=\mathcal N(R).
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Define the aggregate contribution from proper prime powers by
\begin{equation}

R_{\geq2}
=\sum_{\substack{m\geq2,\ P\ \mathrm{prime}\\P^m/\mathcal C\in E}}
(\log P)T_{\mathcal C}(P^m).
\end{equation}
As $K\to\infty$ in the holomorphic case and $R\to\infty$ in the Maass case, the all-prime-power average has the same leading term as the corresponding prime-only average if and only if
\begin{equation}

R_{\geq2}
=o_E\left(M_{\mathcal C}\mathcal C^{1/2}\right).
\end{equation}
Under this equivalent condition, the holomorphic weight family satisfies
\begin{equation}

\frac{
\displaystyle\sum_{\substack{m\geq1,\ P\ \mathrm{prime}\\P^m/\mathcal C\in E}}
(\log P)\sum_{(k,f)\in\mathscr W_{K,H,\delta}}\lambda_f(P^m)
}{
\displaystyle\Psi_E(\mathcal C)
\sum_{(k,f)\in\mathscr W_{K,H,\delta}}1
}
=\frac{(-1)^\delta}{\sqrt{\mathcal C}}
\left(\frac{\nu(E)}{|E|}+o_{E,\varepsilon}(1)\right).
\end{equation}
The Maass family satisfies
\begin{equation}

\frac{
\displaystyle\sum_{\substack{m\geq1,\ P\ \mathrm{prime}\\P^m/\mathcal C\in E}}
(\log P)\sum_{j\in\mathscr M_{R,H}}\varepsilon_j a_j(P^m)
}{
\displaystyle\Psi_E(\mathcal C)
\sum_{j\in\mathscr M_{R,H}}1
}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\mathcal C}}
\left(\frac{\nu(E)}{|E|}+o_{E}(1)\right).
\end{equation}
The measure $\nu$ is the same measure that occurs in the prime-only statements.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:38.362124+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb946a59a1f6c26b69bb2c1d5b708f98bb791a122c9bb41ffaa0f9688b2dd86b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2604.21669, 'Discontinuous transition in 2D Potts:\\ II. order-order interface convergence'] The modified ATRC model \(\matrc_{n,m} \equiv \matrc_\mathsf{K}\) is a probability measure on \(\{0,1\}^{\bar{\mathsf{E}}}\times\{0,1\}^{\bar{\mathsf{E}}}\) defined by
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{multlined}
\matrc_\mathsf{K}(\omega_\tau,\omega_{\tau\tau'})\\
\propto\mathds{1}_{\omega_\tau\subseteq\omega_{\tau\tau'}}\,\mathds{1}_{\omega_{\tau\tau'}\setminus\omega_{\tau}\subseteq \mathsf{E}}\,
2^{|\omega_\tau\cap\mathsf{E}|}\,\big(\tfrac{2}{\svcb-1}\big)^{|\omega_\tau\cap\mathsf{E}_\rmb|}\,(\svc-2)^{|\omega_{\tau\tau'}\setminus\omega_\tau|}\,2^{\clusters_{\mathsf{K}}(\omega_\tau)+\clusters_{\mathsf{K}^1}(\omega_{\tau\tau'})}.
	\end{multlined}
	\end{equation}	
	where~\(\clusters_{\mathsf{K}}(\omega_\tau)\) and~\(\clusters_{\mathsf{K}^1}(\omega_{\tau\tau'})\) are the numbers of clusters of~\(\omega_\tau\) and~\(\omega_{\tau\tau'}\) viewed as spanning subgraphs of~\(\mathsf{K}\) and~\(\mathsf{K}^1\), respectively.
	We say that a cluster of~\(\omega_\tau\) or~\(\omega_{\tau\tau'}\) is an {\em inner cluster} if it is entirely contained in~$\mathsf{B}$ and a {\em boundary cluster} otherwise.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.364629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb94b9e9b571aa9abad92fd5a9c2009568d52c5e37364adb4da426d8939f576b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Jacobi-Malliavin-Derivatives of arXiv:2412.01645, 'Regularisation by Gaussian rough path lifts of fractional Brownian motions'] For all $(s, t) \in [0, 1]^2_{\leq}$, $x \in {\mathbb{R}}^d$, we have that $J^{s, x}_t \in \mathbb{D}^{m,p}$ for all $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $p \geq 1$.   In addition, for any $ n   \in  \mathbb{N}$, there exists a random variable $\Theta_n$ such that for all  $(s, t) \in [0, 1]^2_{\leq}$,  $x \in {\mathbb{R}}^d$, with probability one,  we have 
\begin{equs}
    \|D^n J^{s, x}_t\|_{{\mathbb{R}}^{d\times d} \otimes \mathcal{H}^{\otimes n}} \leq \Theta_n. 
\end{equs}
 Moreover, for any $p \geq 1$, there exists a constant $C=C(d, n, p, \|\sigma\|_{\mathcal{C}^{n+5}}, H_-)$ such that  $\|\Theta_n\|_{L_p(\Omega)}\leq C$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:01.899727+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb986633366784a1bd231e07039f8a16d5bf48b846ca8fc643c1697c0b7d199a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.local0 of arXiv:2512.19897, 'On the convoy of the ASEP speed process'] Under the condition of $Q_1 = 0$, the local limit theorem for $\mathbb{P}\left(Q_{n+1} = \lfloor y \sqrt{n} \rfloor\right)$ holds. We have
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n \to \infty} \sqrt{n}\mathbb{P}\left(Q_{n+1} = \lfloor y \sqrt{n} \rfloor | Q_1 = 0\right) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{c\pi}} e^{-\frac{y^2}{4c}}.
    \end{equation}
    Thus, 
    \[
    \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{Q_{n+1}}{\sqrt{n}}|_{Q_1 = 0} \overset{\left(d\right)}{=} |\mathcal{N}\left(0,2c\right)|.
    \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:24:25.932468+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb98cab12d5a0b60d7c6ba36356bb433e740684863b5235303532bdcfa8d97f9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 31 (Spatial Markov property at level $7\ell$) of arXiv:2506.19123, 'The longest increasing subsequence of Brownian separable permutons'] Fix $\ell\in\mathrm{GoodScales}$ and $k,k'\geq 7\ell+1$. Let $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$ be defined under the black-golden coupling $\mathbb{P}_{k,k'}$ of~\cref{defn:black-golden coupling}. Then the following assertions are true:
\begin{enumerate}
\item 
For $m\in\intervalleentier{7\ell +1}{k}$ and  $m'\in\intervalleentier{7\ell +1}{k'}$, the $\Gf_\ell$-conditional distribution of $(\DMC(H_{7\ell}), \DMC'(H'_{7\ell}))$ on the event $\{\DMC(H_{7\ell}-1)=m, \DMC'(H'_{7\ell}-1)=m'\}$ is given by
\begin{align}
\Law\left((\DMC(H_{7\ell}), \DMC'(H'_{7\ell})) \ | \  \Gf_\ell \right) = \Law((B_m,B_{m'}) \ | \ B_m \leq 7\ell, B'_m \leq 7\ell).
\end{align}
\item Conditionally on $\sigma(\Gf_{\ell}, (\DMC(H_{7\ell}),\DMC'(H'_{7\ell}))$, the chains $(\DMC(H_{7\ell}+t))_{t\geq 0}$ and $(\DMC'(H'_{7\ell}+t))_{t\geq 0}$ have the distribution of $\DMC$ and $\DMC'$ under the black-golden coupling $\bbP_{\DMC(H_{7\ell}),\DMC'(H'_{7\ell})}$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.224747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bb9b4edf17a41fdcd065f45cd77053fc5379c860d32d6a3952608b6f1e011b0c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2607.17943, 'Coadjoint averaging and cross-scale fluxes in a fast-slow stochastic Euler-Arnold system o'] For the quadratic observable $Q$ introduced in \emph{(\ref{Eq:QuadraticObservableGeneralizingEnergyEnstrophy})}, any $z$ in the interior of $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{regular}}$, and the coadjoint orbit invariant measure $dm_z(\Omega)=dm_{\text{KKS},z}(\Omega)$, assume $\mathcal{O}^*(z)=\mathcal{O}^*(\Lambda)$ for $\Lambda=\text{diag}(i\lambda_1,...,i\lambda_N)\in \su(N)$, then we have
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ll}
\overline{j_Q^\alpha}(z) & = \dfrac{\alpha \|\Lambda\|^2}{c_N(N^2-1)}\sum\limits_{k\in \mathcal{K}_N} b_{k, -k} \ ;
\\
\overline{j_Q^\nu}(z) & = \dfrac{\nu \|\Lambda\|^2}{c_N(N^2-1)}\sum\limits_{k\in \mathcal{K}_N}|k|^2 b_{k, -k} \ ;
\\
\overline{j_Q^\delta}(z) & =-\dfrac{\delta^2}{2}\sum\limits_{k\in \mathcal{K}_N^+} b_{k, -k} \ ;
\\
\overline{j_Q^f}(z) & = -\dfrac{1}{2}\sum\limits_{k\in \mathcal{K}_N^+, |k|\approx k_f}q_k^2 b_{k, -k} \ .
\end{array}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615269+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bba1aeec0f235a4040c669626a1dc29e5f36d90f626f7c3ee805f405320ea356","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For all $t > 0$, there exists an a.s.-finite random Borel measure~$Z_t$ on~$[0,1]$ such that the following holds:
\begin{equation}

\lim_{n\to\infty}P^\varrho(\tau_{\BbbL_n}<t) = \BbbP\bigl(Z_t([0,1])>0\bigr)\in(0,1)
\end{equation}
and, for~$L_t$ sampled under the conditional law $P^\varrho(\cdot\,|\tau_{\BbbL_n}<t)$, 
\begin{equation}

\sum_{x\in\BbbL_n}\delta_{\theta_n(x)}\otimes\delta_{\sqrt{L_t(x)}-m_n}\,\,\,\underset{n\to\infty}\lawarrow\,\,\,\sum_{i\ge1}\sum_{j\ge1}\delta_{x_i}\otimes\delta_{h_i+d_j^{(i)}}.
\end{equation}
Here~$\{(x_i,h_i)\}_{i\ge1}$ enumerates points in a sample of the Poisson point process
\begin{equation}

\PPP\bigl(\,Z_t(\textd x)\otimes\texte^{-2h\slb}\textd h\bigr)
\end{equation}
with~$Z_t$ sampled conditional on~$\{Z_t([0,1])>0\}$
and~$\{d_j^{(i)}\colon j\ge1\}_{i\ge1}$ denoting i.i.d.\ samples from~$\DD$ in Theorem~\ref{thm-1}, drawn independently of ~$\{(x_i,h_i)\}_{i\ge1}$ and~$Z_t$. 
Moreover, with $\wt C_\star \in (0,\infty)$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm-1} and~$L_t$ sampled under~$P^\varrho$,
\begin{equation}

\wt C_\star\,b^{-2n} \sum_{x \in \BbbL_n} \Bigl(n\slb - 
\sqrt{L_t(x)}\,\Bigr)^+ L_t(x)^{1/4}\,
	\texte^{2\slb\,\sqrt{L_t(x)}}\delta_{\theta_n(x)}\,\,\underset{n\to\infty}\lawarrow\,\,Z_t.
\end{equation}
A.e.\ sample of~$Z_t$ is diffuse (i.e., does not charge singletons). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:27:32.360468+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bba355f76e155b4aa14e34f2bcbb1431dad1bc7bf316a6d2dd16b7a4c0a59347","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:coup-char of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] Let $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}\in\mathcal{G}(\Pi)$ and $\mathcal{J}\in \mathcal{G}(\Pi^2)$. The following are equivalent.

	(i) $\mathcal{J}$ is a Feller coupling generator of $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}$;

	(ii) for all $\varphi\in \bar D(\mathcal{A}),\psi\in \bar D(\mathcal{B})$, it holds $\varphi\otimes 1 ,1\otimes \psi\in \bar D(\mathcal{J})$ and
	\begin{align*}
		\mathcal{J}[\varphi\otimes 1]=\mathcal{A}\varphi\otimes 1,\quad \mathcal{J}[1\otimes \psi]=1\otimes \mathcal{B}\psi;
	\end{align*}	

	(iii) for all $\varphi\in D(\mathcal{A}),\psi\in D(\mathcal{B})$, it holds $\varphi\otimes 1 ,1\otimes \varphi\in \bar D(\mathcal{J})$, and the marginal condition from (ii) holds. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.727542+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bba362b37c509e0432ba3e216713b58490dba4752606d7ccd3653c32f1e5f467","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2605.17494, 'Generalized intersection exponents and local cut points for three-dimensional Brownian loo'] Let $\alpha_*\in I_0$. Then there exist constants $c_1,c_2>0$, depending only on
$\alpha_*$, such that for every $\alpha\in(0,\alpha_*]$, every $0<\epsilon<\frac12$,
and every pair $V,V^-$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:BM stays in a cone}, we have
\begin{equation}
