{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:24:45.890435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bcd7395c7e804a251c3121840d41f9ffe3135d75e10d9603e0c047f5df0bd368","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:larger group is better of arXiv:2509.02996, 'Group-averaged Markov chains: mixing improvement'] Assume $P\in \mathcal{S}(\pi)$. Let $G_1\leq G_2$ be two locally compact topological groups with Harr measure $\mu_1$ and $\mu_2$ respectively that act on $\mathcal{X}$. Assume $\pi$ is $G_2$-invariant (and hence $G_1$-invariant). For $i=1,2$, denote $(P_{la})_{ra}(G_i)$ as the independent-double-average of $P$ under group $G_i$. Then we have 
