{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:21:45.336562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd45840f2d6c98f62e9fedf62a3e3ce4049819d9fbb891cd2b52fad96ef15463","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 ({\cite[Theorem 4.12) of arXiv:2607.27150, 'Modelling Anomalous Diffusion: The Role of CTRWs and Non-Local Dynamics'] {savtoa}}]
		Let  $\alpha: \mathbb{R}\mapsto (0,1)$, with $\alpha^*=\min_{x\in \mathbb{R}}\alpha(x)>0, \max_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\alpha(x)<1$ and \[1>\lim_{x \to +\infty}\alpha(x)=\alpha_I>\alpha^*,\,\,1>\lim_{x\to -\infty}\alpha(x)=\alpha_J>\alpha^*.\] Also, let there exist $\beta_0>0$ small enough such that for all $\beta_0\geq \beta$, the set $A_\beta=\{x\in\mathbb{R}:\alpha(x)<\alpha^*+\beta<1\}$ is bounded and satisfies $0<\mathpzc{l}({A_\beta})<\infty$ and $\mathpzc{l}(\partial A_\beta)=0$. Then,
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item if $2\alpha^*<\min\{\alpha_I,\alpha_J\}$, then for all $0\leq \beta\leq \beta_0$
			\begin{equation}
				\lim_{t \to +\infty}\frac{\int_{0}^{t}\mathds{1}_{[B(L(s))\in A_\beta]}ds}{t}=1,\,\mathds{P}^{(x,0)}\text{-a.s.};
			\end{equation}
			\item  if $2\alpha^*>\min\{ \alpha_I,\alpha_J\}$, then for any $K>0$,
			\begin{equation}
				\lim_{t \to +\infty}\frac{\int_{0}^{t}\mathds{1}_{\big[B(L(s))\in A^c_\beta\cap [-K,K]^c\big]}ds}{t}=1,\,\mathds{P}^{(x,0)}\text{-a.s.}.
			\end{equation}
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.816629+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd4697a71902d8106d3c0dfab297f15efb2ed293e1efe6979790912974c0d40a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:howardlieblemma of arXiv:2411.09516, 'Sharp Matrix Empirical Bernstein Inequalities\footnote{NeurIPS 2025 Poster}'] Let $\{  \mathbf{Z} _n \}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued, adapted martingale difference sequence. Let $\{ \mathbf{C} _n\}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued adapted process, $\{ \mathbf{C} _n'\}$ be an $\mathcal{S}_d$-valued predictable process. If 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb E  (\exp(  \mathbf{Z} _n -   \mathbf{C} _n) | \mathcal{F}_{n-1}) \preceq \exp(   \mathbf{C} _n'  ),
\end{equation}
holds for all $n$, then the process
\begin{equation}
  \textstyle  L_n = \mathrm{tr} \exp \left( \sum_{i=1}^n  \mathbf{Z} _i -  \sum_{i=1}^n  (  \mathbf{C} _i +  \mathbf{C} _i' )  \right)
\end{equation}
is a nonnegative supermartingale. Further,
\begin{equation}
\textstyle   L_n \geqslant \exp \left( \lambda_{\max}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n  \mathbf{Z} _i \right)-   \lambda_{\max}\left( \sum_{i=1}^n (  \mathbf{C} _i +  \mathbf{C} _i' ) \right)  \right).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.837667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd47207eb4abdba709aa8183c81825c17db923ecbef4cfc195a0944a54d6ac74","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2608.13359, 'Fractional moments of the Stochastic Heat Flow and\\2D Directed Polymers'] Define \(\cV_{N}^{\beta} \coloneqq \sigma^2(\beta) \EE\bigl[ (Z_N^{\beta,\w})^2 \bigr]\).
  Then, recalling the definition~\eqref{def:theta-N-beta} of \(\th(N,\beta)\), we have
  \begin{equation}

    \cV_N^{\beta}
    = \pi\cV\Bigl( (1+o(1))\e^{\th(N,\beta)-\gamma} \Bigr)  \quad \text{ as } N\to\infty, \beta\downarrow 0\,.
  \end{equation}
  In particular \(\cV_N^{\beta}\) converges to \(\pi\cV(\e^{\th-\gamma})\) if \(\lim_{N\uparrow\infty,\beta\downarrow 0}\th(N,\beta) = \th \in \R\).
  More generally, there are constants \(c_1,c_2\) \(c_3,c_4\) such that, for any \(N\in \N\) and \(\beta \in (0,1)\),
  \begin{equation}

    c_1\,\cV\bigl( c_2\, \e^{\th(N,\beta)} \bigr)
    \leq \cV_{N}^{\beta}
    \leq c_3\,\cV\bigl( c_4\, \e^{\th(N,\beta)} \bigr) \,.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.663689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd4a51a69f68ab9dc33e5de1d8ee3a0f9e13d286bc3098c93fe53d81a745d6a7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor derivative bound on ratio of G_n of arXiv:2603.17858, 'Decay of correlations and zeros for the hard-core model'] Given $\lambda_+>\lambda_->0$, $C>0$ and $\alpha\in (0,1)$ there exists a constant $A>1$ such that the following holds: if $(\lambda_n)$ and $(\widehat\lambda_n)$ in $[\lambda_-,\lambda_+]$  satisfy $|\lambda_n-\widehat \lambda_n|<C\alpha^n$ for all $n$, then 
