{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.346584+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdc9ee9cd0f4add6bf597349fb8d7ed0cf254b6cbfa9110aa282d242c3bf8751","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 50 of arXiv:2506.15044, '2506.15044'] Fix an integer $d\in \mathbf{N}^+$, and define $Z=(X_{1},X_{2},\cdots, X_{d})$, where $X_{1},X_{2},\cdots, X_{d}$ are $\mathbb{B}$-inner semimartingales. Suppose that $F$ is a $C^2$-function on $\mathbf{R}^d$ (i.e., $F$ possesses continuous partial derivatives up to the second order). Then
\begin{align}
F(Z)=F(Z(0))\mathfrak{I}_\mathbb{B}+\sum_{k=1}^d D_kF(Z_{-})_{\bullet}(X_k-X_k(0)\mathfrak{I}_\mathbb{B})+\eta+
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k,l=1}^d D_{kl}F(Z_{-})_{\bullet}\langle X_k^c,X_l^c\rangle,
