{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:22:21.905189+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdf6c20e99399ca9cdf54eaa1d8d4a6d1e57ef93f0f138547471e74dfe98a2e6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:generating_recursion of arXiv:2411.11425, 'Non-equilibrium steady states with a spatial Markov structure'] $\Lambda^1$ is called a generating density if it is of the form in (equation eq:form_generating of the paper) for all $z_1,z_2 \in \mathbb{R}^+$ such that $z_1 < z_2$ and $a,b \in (z_1,z_2)$. The unique symmetric two-sided Markov family $\{\Lambda^n\}_{n \geq 1}$ with first marginals

    \begin{equation}
        \Lambda_{a,b}^{n,1}(x) = f_n(a,b) \cdot g_n(b,x) \cdot h_n(x,a) \cdot \mathds{1}(x \in I_{a,b})
    \end{equation}

    \noindent
    is called the generated family. The functions $f_n,g_n$ and $h_n$ on $\mathbb{R}^+$ are defined via the recursion

    \begin{equation}

        \begin{cases}
            f_{n+1} (a,b) = \big( \int_{a \wedge b}^{a \vee b} [f_{n}(b,x)]^{-1} \cdot h_1(x,a) dx \big)^{-1} \\[2ex]
            g_{n+1} (b,x) = [f_n(a,x)]^{-1} \\[2ex]
            h_{n+1}(x,a) = h(x,a) = g(x,a)
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    \noindent
    with $f_1 = f$ and $g_1 = g$. For fixed $z_1,z_2$ we call $(f_n,g_n,h_n)$ the $n$-th order factors generated by $\Lambda^{1}$. The functions $f_n$ and $g_n$ are symmetric for all $n \geq 1$, as is shown in the proof of theorem (thm:characterization_two-sided_symmetric of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.117571+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bdf7af6eb85b4cce13fc074fa6b095c13e7a9f280a2688c3c711484f4c81622a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:neumann of arXiv:2608.17022, 'Sharp hypocoercive convergence estimates for underdamped Langevin dynamics with specular r'] Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ be a bounded convex domain and $U\in C^2(\overline\Omega)$, and let $-\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}}$ be the nonnegative self-adjoint operator on $L^2(\mu_x)$ associated with the closed form $\mathcal E$ on $H^1(\Omega)$. Then,
\begin{align}
D(\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}})=\{h\in H^2(\Omega):\ \partial_nh=0\ \text{$\sigma$-a.e.\ on }\partial\Omega\},\qquad
\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}} h=\Delta_xh-\nabla_xU\cdot\nabla_xh,
\end{align}
and there is $C=C(\Omega,U)$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|h\|_{H^2(\Omega)}\le C\big(\nrm[\mu_x]{\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}} h}+\nrm[\mu_x]{h}\big),\qquad h\in D(\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

